; SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
;
; checker — the type half of the reference's internal/checker.
;
; ── What this module is FOR, and what a slice of it is ──────────────────────
;
; The checker answers two questions about a bound tree, and the fifth yardstick
; compares both (tests/oracle/types.go, TESTPLAN.md's *The fifth yardstick*):
;
;   C  the SEMANTIC DIAGNOSTICS of one file — GetDiagnostics, which runs the
;      whole check of the file and answers out of the checker's own sorted list
;   T  the TYPE of every node the reference's own baseline walker may ask about —
;      GetTypeAtLocation plus TypeToString
;
; Neither is reachable through any earlier artifact, which is why the yardstick is
; a fifth one rather than a section of the fourth: a type is not in the tree and
; not on a symbol. It is computed on demand, memoised inside the checker and never
; written back, so a port that computes NO types and a port that computes WRONG
; ones compare exactly equal on the first four, in both directions.
;
; ── The dimension's size, and what that decides ─────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ internal/checker is 59 971 lines against the 12 540 of scanner + parser +
; binder — measured at slice 46, which projects 88 000–141 000 lines of Scaly, two
; to three times the whole port to date. So this file will be built over many
; slices, and the SKELETON's job is to make every one of them an ARM: the type
; representation, the intrinsic types, the printer's keyword arms, the file-level
; check, the diagnostic list with the reference's sort, and — the part that earns
; its keep — a REPORT at every arm that is not ported, so tests/triage.py names
; the arm that unlocks the most units next. That is the binder's arrangement
; (§3.5bw) one dimension over.
;
; ── What is NOT ported, and why each omission is a decision ─────────────────
;
; ★★★ THE FLOW GRAPH PARAGRAPH THAT STOOD HERE IS SPENT, AND IT IS KEPT AS A
; POINTER RATHER THAN DELETED BECAUSE IT NAMED BOTH OF ITS READERS CORRECTLY. It
; read *the flow graph is not ported, and it is the checker that needs it*, and
; listed the two dependants: NARROWING, which the narrowing engine closed, and
; REACHABILITY, which stayed open twenty-nine slices longer than the graph did —
; `is_reachable_flow_node` has been in this file since that engine landed and
; nothing CALLED it from the statement walk. Slice 184 wrote the caller
; (check_source_element_unreachable). What the paragraph got wrong is the price it
; put on reachability: it priced the chapter at TS7027, which no yardstick can see
; (0 of 64 148 reference C lines), and the chapter's real product is a SECOND VISIT
; to the nodes under a flow-call node. See within_unreachable_code.
;
; ★★ THE RELATER AND THE NODEBUILDER ARE NOT PORTED EITHER, and each is a
; dimension rather than a gap. relater.go (5 006 lines) is assignability, which
; even `var x: string = 1` needs; nodebuilderimpl.go (3 585) is the type PRINTER,
; of which this file ports the keyword arms only — the twenty lines of
; typeToTypeNodeHelper that answer an INTRINSIC — because those are the only type
; shapes anything here can produce.
;
; ★★★ AND THE COUPLING THE SKELETON MEASURED, because it decides how this
; dimension advances: THE C SECTION GATES THE T SECTION. One dump is one
; comparison, so a unit counts as matched only when both halves agree — and the C
; half is complete only when the check of EVERY statement in the unit is ported.
; Measured over the whole corpus: 7 300 of 17 810 units — 41 % — have an EMPTY C
; section, and not one of them can be claimed without porting the checks that PROVE
; it empty. So the
; 1 230 663 type answers of the corpus wait behind its 63 127 diagnostics, and the
; checker dimension advances at the speed of checkSourceElement. ★The escape hatch
; is named here rather than taken: the ORACLE's output would not change by one
; byte if the comparison were SPLIT into two yardsticks (C and T), because the two
; sections are already separate lines — it is compare.py, triage.py, run.sh and
; ctl.sh that would each gain a column (slice 46's rule: all four in one commit,
; or the first battery lies). That is a harness decision with a measured argument
; behind it, and it belongs to whichever slice finds the coupling is actually
; holding the type machinery back.
;
; ── What the harness fixes, and what our side therefore inherits ────────────
;
; The oracle builds a STUB program: no module resolution, the per-case
; @target/@lib/@module directives ignored, and the lib set fixed to lib.es5.d.ts
; (priced at slice 46 as a 3 % diagnostic delta against the reference's default
; closure). Two of those three are symmetric for free here, because this port has
; no program layer at all. The third is not: this side reads NO lib, so every
; global name is unresolved — which is invisible while no arm resolves a name, and
; is the first thing the slice that ports name resolution has to answer. It is
; written down here so that slice does not discover it as a diff.
;
; ★ compilerOptions: the harness sets SkipDefaultLibCheck and NoErrorTruncation
; and nothing else, so every option this file would read is at its default —
; strictNullChecks false, exactOptionalPropertyTypes false, noImplicitAny false,
; NoUnusedLocals/NoUnusedParameters false (hence checkUnused false at
; getDiagnostics). Each place that matters says which option it is reading and
; what its default answers, so that an options record arriving later is a
; substitution rather than a search.

use scaly.memory.Page
use scaly.containers.Array
use scaly.containers.Vector
use scaly.containers.String
use scaly.containers.StringBuilder
use tscaly.TsOptionDecls.TsOptionDecls
use tscaly.addressindex.AddressIndex
use tscaly.addressindex.HashChain
use tscaly.ast.AstNode
use tscaly.ast.Symbol
use tscaly.ast.FlowNode
use tscaly.ast.FlowList
; ★ Slice 94 is the first slice in which the CHECKER mints an AST node — the
; synthetic `this.p` of isPropertyInitializedInConstructor — so the payload union
; and the one arm it builds have to be visible here as well as the node itself.
use tscaly.ast.NodeData
use tscaly.ast.ExportAssignmentData
use tscaly.ast.ImportOrExportSpecifierData
use tscaly.ast.TokenData
use tscaly.ast.PropertyAccessData
; slice 117: the synthetic element access getFlowTypeOfDestructuring builds.
use tscaly.ast.LiteralData
use tscaly.ast.ElementAccessData
use tscaly.ast.WrappedTypeData
; slice 120: the synthetic access NAME getFlowTypeInConstructor builds — an
; Identifier or a PrivateIdentifier, which share this payload.
use tscaly.ast.IdentifierData
; slice 134: newCallSignature's synthetic FunctionType declaration — the node
; getOrCreateTypeFromSignature asks the KIND of.
use tscaly.ast.FunctionTypeData
; slice 199: the synthesized `children` PropertySignature
; createJsxAttributesTypeFromAttributesProperty fakes up.
use tscaly.ast.PropertyDeclarationData
use tscaly.scanner.Scanner
use tscaly.parser.Parser
use tscaly.binder.Binder
use tscaly.program.ProgramFiles
use tscaly.program.ResolutionContext
use tscaly.program.JsonValue
use tscaly.buildinfojson.JsonText
use tscaly.program.ModuleResolver
use tscaly.Diagnostics.DiagnosticList
use tscaly.Diagnostics.RelatedInfo
use tscaly.modulespecifiers.SpecifierHost
use tscaly.emitter.NodeBuilder
use tscaly.xxh3.Xxh3
; ★ Slice 113: the Checker is this port's PROGRAM stub (see create's header), and
; a program is what reads the lib off the disk. The oracle's `main` does the same
; three steps — read, parse, bind — before it builds a checker at all.
use scaly.io.File

; getenv(3), for the one thing the lib loader cannot derive: WHERE the submodule
; is. The default is the path from the repository root, which is the directory
; every harness in tests/ runs from; TSCALY_LIB overrides it.
function getenv(name: pointer[const_char]) returns pointer[const_char] extern

; ── ObjectFlags: two bits, deliberately not a table ─────────────────────────
;
; ★★ The reference's ObjectFlags is a 40-entry uint32 enum in
; internal/checker/types.go, and it is NOT generated beside TypeFlags for a reason
; that is about the enum rather than about effort: its bit positions are REUSED.
; `1 << 22` is ObjectFlagsContainsSpread on a type with TypeFlagsObject,
; ObjectFlagsIsGenericTypeComputed on a union or intersection, and the comments in
; the source are what say which — so a flat table of names would read as 40
; independent bits and be wrong in exactly the way a reader cannot see. It also
; carries a FORWARD reference (ObjectFlagsObjectTypeKindMask names
; ObjectFlagsInstantiationExpressionType, declared below it), which gentypeflags.py
; would refuse rather than resolve.
;
; So the two bits the intrinsic block below actually SETS are declared here, with
; their positions from the source, and the table arrives with the slice that needs
; the object types — where the reuse discipline can be ported with it.
define ObjectFlagsNone: int 0

; ── the bits that require TypeFlagsObject, in the reference's own order ──────
;
; ★★ SLICE 66 BRINGS THE TABLE THE NOTE ABOVE PROMISED, and it stops where the
; reuse begins. Bits 0..21 mean one thing each; `1 << 22` and up are the reused
; positions the note names, and they are NOT declared, because the name would be
; the lie the note describes. A slice that needs ContainsSpread declares it beside
; the reader that establishes which TypeFlags it is reading it under.
define ObjectFlagsClass: int 1 << 0                   ; Class
define ObjectFlagsInterface: int 1 << 1               ; Interface
define ObjectFlagsReference: int 1 << 2               ; Generic type reference
define ObjectFlagsTuple: int 1 << 3                   ; Synthesized generic tuple type
define ObjectFlagsAnonymous: int 1 << 4               ; Anonymous
define ObjectFlagsMapped: int 1 << 5                  ; Mapped
define ObjectFlagsInstantiated: int 1 << 6            ; Instantiated anonymous or mapped type
define ObjectFlagsInstantiatedMapped: int ObjectFlagsMapped | ObjectFlagsInstantiated
define ObjectFlagsObjectLiteral: int 1 << 7           ; Originates in an object literal
define ObjectFlagsEvolvingArray: int 1 << 8           ; Evolving array type
define ObjectFlagsObjectLiteralPatternWithComputedProperties: int 1 << 9
define ObjectFlagsReverseMapped: int 1 << 10          ; Object contains a property from a reverse-mapped type
define ObjectFlagsJsxAttributes: int 1 << 11          ; Jsx attributes type
define ObjectFlagsJSLiteral: int 1 << 12              ; Object type declared in JS
define ObjectFlagsFreshLiteral: int 1 << 13           ; Fresh object literal
define ObjectFlagsArrayLiteral: int 1 << 14           ; Originates in an array literal
define ObjectFlagsPrimitiveUnion: int 1 << 15         ; Union of only primitive types
define ObjectFlagsContainsWideningType: int 1 << 16   ; Type is or contains undefined or null widening type
define ObjectFlagsContainsObjectOrArrayLiteral: int 1 << 17 ; Type is or contains object literal type
define ObjectFlagsNonInferrableType: int 1 << 18      ; Type is or contains anyFunctionType or silentNeverType
define ObjectFlagsCouldContainTypeVariablesComputed: int 1 << 19
define ObjectFlagsCouldContainTypeVariables: int 1 << 20
define ObjectFlagsMembersResolved: int 1 << 21        ; Members have been resolved
; Slice 85. Set for the length of resolveObjectTypeMembers' base loop and cleared
; after it — *member resolution in process*. Its only READER upstream is the cache
; key of getPropertiesOfType's index-signature variant, a chapter away; it is
; written here because the flag is what tells a re-entrant ask that the members it
; can see are half-built.
define ObjectFlagsUnresolvedMembers: int 1 << 29

define ObjectFlagsClassOrInterface: int ObjectFlagsClass | ObjectFlagsInterface

; ── the two REUSED positions slice 94 reads, each under its own TypeFlags ────
;
; ★★ THE TABLE ABOVE STOPS AT BIT 21 BECAUSE THE POSITIONS ABOVE IT MEAN
; DIFFERENT THINGS ON DIFFERENT TYPES, and its own rule is that *a slice that
; needs one declares it beside the reader that establishes which TypeFlags it is
; reading it under*. These are the first two to arrive under that rule, and the
; name carries the condition:
;
;   1 << 25 is ObjectFlagsContainsIntersections on a type with TypeFlagsUnion,
;           and ObjectFlagsIsNeverIntersection on one with TypeFlagsIntersection.
;   1 << 27 is ObjectFlagsIsConstrainedTypeVariable on a type with
;           TypeFlagsIntersection, and IsGenericTypeComputed elsewhere.
;
; ★★★ THE SECOND IS READ ONLY WHERE IT CANNOT FIRE, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS
; DECLARED RATHER THAN LEFT OUT. add_type_to_union's constrained-type-variable
; term is `flags & Intersection <> 0 and t.object_flags & this <> 0`. ★★★THE
; SENTENCE THAT FOLLOWED — *"and NOTHING IN THIS PORT MINTS AN INTERSECTION TYPE —
; new_object_type has no arm for one and there is no new_intersection_type"* —
; EXPIRED AT SLICE 121, when `get_intersection_type_ex` arrived, and it was still
; being read as live four slices later at eight sites (§3.5gk). Seven of them
; stop; the guard here is the harmless kind, because the SECOND term settles it
; too. The
; term is written with the real bit anyway: a version that dropped the second half
; would be a different function the day an intersection arrives, and the deletion
; would be invisible.
define ObjectFlagsContainsIntersections: int 1 << 25
; ★★★ BIT 25 IS SHARED, AND THE SHARING IS UPSTREAM'S. `ContainsIntersections`
; is a UNION's bit and `IsNeverIntersectionComputed` an INTERSECTION's, so no
; type can be asked both questions — exactly the way `ContainsSpread` shares bit
; 22 with an object flag (see its own note). Naming both is what keeps the next
; reader from "fixing" the collision.
define ObjectFlagsIsNeverIntersectionComputed: int 1 << 25
define ObjectFlagsIsNeverIntersection: int 1 << 26
define ObjectFlagsIsConstrainedTypeVariable: int 1 << 27
; ★★ AND BITS 26/27 ARE SHARED ONE MORE WAY, upstream's own: on a type with
; TypeFlagsUNION they are `IsUnknownLikeUnionComputed` and `IsUnknownLikeUnion`,
; where the two above are read on an INTERSECTION alone. is_unknown_like_union_type
; is the only reader and it tests `flags & Union` before it looks.
define ObjectFlagsIsUnknownLikeUnionComputed: int 1 << 26
define ObjectFlagsIsUnknownLikeUnion: int 1 << 27

; ── IntersectionFlags (slice 121) ───────────────────────────────────────────
;
; getIntersectionTypeEx's second parameter. Both non-zero members turn a
; REDUCTION off, and both are set by exactly one caller: the type node passes
; NoSupertypeReduction for `X & {}`, and NoConstraintReduction comes from the
; relation's own intersection construction.
; ── IndexFlags (slice 121) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; getIndexTypeEx's second parameter. ★NoReducibleCheck exists to break a
; recursion rather than to change an answer: getIndexType asks
; isGenericReducibleType, which asks getIndexType back.
define IndexFlagsNone: int 0
define IndexFlagsStringsOnly: int 1 << 0
define IndexFlagsNoIndexSignatures: int 1 << 1

; ── MappedTypeModifiers and MappedTypeNameTypeKind (slice 125) ──────────────
;
; Two of the reference's own enums, WHOLE for TypeSystemPropertyName's reason:
; the four modifier bits are one word read by one function and the three name-type
; kinds are the arms of one dispatch, so a partial declaration would invite a
; renumbering nothing here could catch.
;
; ★ The modifiers are what `+readonly` / `-readonly` / `+?` / `-?` on a mapped
; type mean, and the pairing is the point: INCLUDE wins over EXCLUDE at every
; reader, and the ABSENCE of both is *inherit from the modifiers type*, which is a
; third answer neither bit spells on its own.
define MappedTypeModifiersIncludeReadonly: int 1 << 0
define MappedTypeModifiersExcludeReadonly: int 1 << 1
define MappedTypeModifiersIncludeOptional: int 1 << 2
define MappedTypeModifiersExcludeOptional: int 1 << 3

; MappedTypeNameTypeKind — what the `as N` clause of `{ [P in K as N]: T }` does.
; FILTERING when N is assignable to P (the keys are a subset of K), REMAPPING
; otherwise, None when there is no clause at all. The distinction decides whether
; the synthesized property keeps the ORIGINAL declarations — a remapped key names
; a different property, so it must not.
define MappedTypeNameTypeKindNone: int 0
define MappedTypeNameTypeKindFiltering: int 1
define MappedTypeNameTypeKindRemapping: int 2
define IndexFlagsNoReducibleCheck: int 1 << 2

define IntersectionFlagsNone: int 0
define IntersectionFlagsNoSupertypeReduction: int 1 << 0
define IntersectionFlagsNoConstraintReduction: int 1 << 1

; ★ ObjectFlagsContainsSpread, slice 119, and it is the OTHER meaning of bit 22 —
; the one this block's header names first. It is set by getSpreadType on a type
; that already carries TypeFlagsObject, so the two readings never meet on one
; value; the pair is the clearest illustration of why the whole enum is written by
; hand rather than generated.
define ObjectFlagsContainsSpread: int 1 << 22

; ★ The four genericity bits, slice 117. IsGenericType is the OR of the other two
; and is written as a literal for ElementFlagsFixed's reason — a global's
; initializer folds inside ONE precedence level, and `1 << 23 | 1 << 24` mixes the
; shift with the `|`.
define ObjectFlagsIsGenericTypeComputed: int 1 << 22
define ObjectFlagsIsGenericObjectType: int 1 << 23
define ObjectFlagsIsGenericIndexType: int 1 << 24
define ObjectFlagsIsGenericType: int 25165824   ; IsGenericObjectType | IsGenericIndexType

; ── AccessFlags (slice 117) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; checker.AccessFlags — the ten bits getIndexedAccessType is steered by. The whole
; table is declared rather than the four this slice reads, because the flags are
; passed THROUGH getIndexedAccessTypeOrUndefined into getPropertyTypeForIndexType
; as one value: a partial table would make the next reader's `accessFlags & X`
; read as a new decision rather than as a transcription.
define AccessFlagsNone: int 0
define AccessFlagsIncludeUndefined: int 1 << 0
define AccessFlagsNoIndexSignatures: int 1 << 1
define AccessFlagsWriting: int 1 << 2
define AccessFlagsCacheSymbol: int 1 << 3
define AccessFlagsAllowMissing: int 1 << 4
define AccessFlagsExpressionPosition: int 1 << 5
define AccessFlagsReportDeprecated: int 1 << 6
define AccessFlagsSuppressNoImplicitAnyError: int 1 << 7
define AccessFlagsContextual: int 1 << 8
define AccessFlagsPersistent: int 1   ; IncludeUndefined

; ── UnionReduction ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; checker.UnionReduction, slice 94. Three values and the reference's own numbers,
; because `unionReduction & UnionReductionSubtype` is a BIT test on them rather
; than an equality — Literal is 1 and Subtype is 2, so Literal does not reduce
; subtypes and None does neither.
;
; ★ getUnionType passes Literal, which is the only value any caller in this port
; passes today; Subtype's callers upstream are inference and the array-literal
; chapter, and the arm that meets one carries `remove-subtypes`.
define UnionReductionNone: int 0
define UnionReductionLiteral: int 1
define UnionReductionSubtype: int 2

; ★ ObjectFlagsPropagatingFlags, whose only reader is getPropagatingFlagsOfTypes.
; ObjectFlagsObjectTypeKindMask is NOT declared here for the reason this line has
; carried since it was written — it NAMES two of the reused bits, so it cannot be
; written before they are. Slice 201 declared it at the first point where all nine
; of its members exist, beside ObjectFlagsIsClassInstanceClone.
define ObjectFlagsPropagatingFlags: int ObjectFlagsContainsWideningType | ObjectFlagsContainsObjectOrArrayLiteral | ObjectFlagsNonInferrableType

; ★ ObjectFlagsRequiresWidening arrives in slice 69, where getWidenedType is its
; reader — the note above listed it as having none. It is the gate that decides
; whether widening has anything to do at all.
define ObjectFlagsRequiresWidening: int ObjectFlagsContainsWideningType | ObjectFlagsContainsObjectOrArrayLiteral

; ★★ ObjectFlagsFromTypeNode is the FIRST of the reused positions to be declared,
; and it is declared the way the table's own note demands — beside its reader and
; with the TypeFlags it is read under. Its reader is getRecursionIdentity (slice
; 81), inside a branch guarded by TypeFlagsObject, which is what makes
; unambiguous there: upstream reuses the bit only for types that are not objects.
; Nothing in this port SETS it (createTypeReferenceEx and the tuple constructors
; do upstream, and both report here), so the guard reading it is written and always
; passes — which is stated rather than hidden, because a guard that cannot fail
; looks exactly like one that was ported wrong.
define ObjectFlagsFromTypeNode: int 1 << 30

; ★★ THE SECOND AND THIRD REUSED POSITIONS, declared on the same discipline and
; with the same reader: `could_contain_type_variables_worker` (slice 86) tests
; both inside a branch guarded by TypeFlagsObject, which is the condition under
; which upstream's reuse is unambiguous. Nothing here SETS either — the object
; rest declaration and the instantiation expression are both chapters away — so
; the test is written and always fails, which is stated rather than hidden for
; ObjectFlagsFromTypeNode's reason.
define ObjectFlagsObjectRestType: int 1 << 23
define ObjectFlagsInstantiationExpressionType: int 1 << 24
; `1 << 25` on a TypeFlagsObject type: a single signature type extracted from a
; potentially broader one. Slice 125 reads it twice in getObjectTypeInstantiation
; — once as part of the instantiation KEY and once to decide whether the fresh
; mapper is merged with the caller's — and nothing here SETS it
; (getSingleSignatureTypeForType has no producer), so both tests are written and
; always fail, on ObjectFlagsFromTypeNode's discipline.
define ObjectFlagsSingleSignatureType: int 1 << 25
; `1 << 26` is ObjectFlagsIsClassInstanceClone on a type with TypeFlagsObject —
; set by getTypeWithThisArgument's clone path. Read by isClassInstanceSide (slice
; 111) and by nothing else here; the clone path this port takes never mints an
; ANONYMOUS type, so the test is written and always fails.
define ObjectFlagsIsClassInstanceClone: int 1 << 26

; ★★★ ObjectFlagsObjectTypeKindMask, DECLARED HERE AND NOT BESIDE
; ObjectFlagsPropagatingFlags, because the note there is exact and has not
; expired: the mask names ObjectFlagsInstantiationExpressionType and
; ObjectFlagsSingleSignatureType, two of the reused positions, and a `define`
; folds at plan time against the declarations ABOVE it. Written at the first
; point where all nine of its members exist. Its one reader is compare_types'
; non-reference object arm (slice 201).
define ObjectFlagsObjectTypeKindMask: int ObjectFlagsClassOrInterface | ObjectFlagsReference | ObjectFlagsTuple | ObjectFlagsAnonymous | ObjectFlagsMapped | ObjectFlagsReverseMapped | ObjectFlagsEvolvingArray | ObjectFlagsInstantiationExpressionType | ObjectFlagsSingleSignatureType

; ── WideningKind ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's four-member iota, declared WHOLE because reportImplicitAny
; branches on GeneratorYield and every caller names one of the four. Slice 69
; passes Normal from its single call site; the other three arrive with the return
; type and the generator.
define WideningKindNormal: int 0
define WideningKindFunctionReturn: int 1
define WideningKindGeneratorNext: int 2
define WideningKindGeneratorYield: int 3

; ── thisAssignmentDeclarationKind (slice 120) ───────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's four-member iota, declared WHOLE because
; getWidenedTypeForAssignmentDeclaration branches on three of the four and the
; fourth is its own `nil` answer.
define ThisAssignmentDeclarationNone: int 0        ; not (all) this.property assignments
define ThisAssignmentDeclarationTyped: int 1       ; typed; use the type annotation
define ThisAssignmentDeclarationConstructor: int 2 ; at least one in the constructor; use control flow
define ThisAssignmentDeclarationMethod: int 3      ; methods only; look in base first

; ── CheckMode (slice 70) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's eight-member flag set, declared WHOLE for WideningKind's
; reason: `checkExpressionEx`'s two gates test Inferential and
; SkipGenericFunctions by NAME, `checkExpressionCachedEx` branches on Normal, and
; every caller in the reference names one of the eight — so a partial table would
; make the next arm's test read as a new decision rather than as a transcription.
;
; ★★ ONLY `Normal` IS EVER PASSED HERE, and that is a fact about the CALLERS
; rather than about this table: every call this port makes comes from a statement
; arm or from a declaration's initializer, i.e. from the reference's own
; `checkExpression` / `checkExpressionCached`, both of which pass Normal. The four
; modes that change an answer (Contextual, Inferential, SkipContextSensitive,
; SkipGenericFunctions) are set only by the contextual-typing and inference
; machinery, which reports. ★That is what makes the two gates in
; check_expression_ex provably inert rather than merely quiet — see there.
define CheckModeNormal: int 0
define CheckModeContextual: int 1 << 0            ; Explicitly assigned contextual type, therefore not cacheable
define CheckModeInferential: int 1 << 1           ; Inferential typing
define CheckModeSkipContextSensitive: int 1 << 2  ; Skip context sensitive function expressions
define CheckModeSkipGenericFunctions: int 1 << 3  ; Skip single signature generic functions
define CheckModeIsForSignatureHelp: int 1 << 4    ; Call resolution for purposes of signature help
define CheckModeRestBindingElement: int 1 << 5    ; Checking a type used to determine a rest binding element's type
define CheckModeTypeOnly: int 1 << 6              ; Called from getTypeOfExpression, diagnostics may be omitted
define CheckModeForceTuple: int 1 << 7

; ── TypeFacts (slice 90) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's twenty-seven bits and the forty-two COMBINATIONS built out of
; them, declared WHOLE. Every other flag table in this port is generated
; (tools/gentypeflags.py and its siblings) and this one is hand-written, because
; it does not live in a flags file at all: it sits inside internal/checker/
; checker.go, in the middle of the checker's own declarations, and the generators
; read the enum files. The whole table is transcribed rather than the members with
; a reader, on CheckMode's rule — `getTypeFactsWorker` names forty of the
; forty-two in its own switch, so a partial table would make the next arm read as
; a decision instead of as a transcription.
;
; ★★★ THE COMBINATIONS ARE WRITTEN SYMBOLICALLY AND THAT IS NOT A PREFERENCE — IT
; IS THE ONLY THING THAT MAKES THEM CHECKABLE. A reader can compare
; `TypeFactsBaseStringStrictFacts` against the reference line for line; a
; forty-two-row column of decimal numbers can only be trusted. The four masked
; members are the exception the rule has to state: the reference writes them with
; Go's `&^`, and a scalar `define` initializer in this language folds ONE
; precedence level (root CLAUDE.md), so `A & ^(B | C)` is a hard rc-4.
; **They are written as `xor` chains instead, which is EXACT rather than close**:
; every bit removed is set in TypeFactsAll and the removed bits are pairwise
; disjoint, so clearing them and toggling them are the same operation. Verified
; numerically against the reference's own values when this table landed —
; EmptyObjectStrictFacts 83427327, EmptyObjectFacts and UnknownFacts 83886079,
; AndFactsMask 134209471.
;
; ★ TypeFactsAll is the one member written as a NUMBER, for the same folding rule:
; `(1 << 27) - 1` is two levels. The formula is on the line.
define TypeFactsNone: int 0
define TypeFactsTypeofEQString: int 1 << 0
define TypeFactsTypeofEQNumber: int 1 << 1
define TypeFactsTypeofEQBigInt: int 1 << 2
define TypeFactsTypeofEQBoolean: int 1 << 3
define TypeFactsTypeofEQSymbol: int 1 << 4
define TypeFactsTypeofEQObject: int 1 << 5
define TypeFactsTypeofEQFunction: int 1 << 6
define TypeFactsTypeofEQHostObject: int 1 << 7
define TypeFactsTypeofNEString: int 1 << 8
define TypeFactsTypeofNENumber: int 1 << 9
define TypeFactsTypeofNEBigInt: int 1 << 10
define TypeFactsTypeofNEBoolean: int 1 << 11
define TypeFactsTypeofNESymbol: int 1 << 12
define TypeFactsTypeofNEObject: int 1 << 13
define TypeFactsTypeofNEFunction: int 1 << 14
define TypeFactsTypeofNEHostObject: int 1 << 15
define TypeFactsEQUndefined: int 1 << 16
define TypeFactsEQNull: int 1 << 17
define TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull: int 1 << 18
define TypeFactsNEUndefined: int 1 << 19
define TypeFactsNENull: int 1 << 20
define TypeFactsNEUndefinedOrNull: int 1 << 21
define TypeFactsTruthy: int 1 << 22
define TypeFactsFalsy: int 1 << 23
define TypeFactsIsUndefined: int 1 << 24
define TypeFactsIsNull: int 1 << 25
define TypeFactsIsUndefinedOrNull: int TypeFactsIsUndefined | TypeFactsIsNull
; (1 << 27) - 1 — see the header for why this one is a number.
define TypeFactsAll: int 134217727

; ── slice 128: THE ITERATION AND ASYNC PROTOCOL — IterationUse ──────────────
;
; The reference's `IterationUse`, a bit set the whole iteration chapter is keyed
; on. The eight FLAGS and the nine named COMBINATIONS, verbatim, because every
; one of them is read by name somewhere in this chapter and a caller that spelled
; the bits itself would be a second definition of the same thing.
;
; ★★★ IterationUseCacheFlags IS THE ONE THAT IS NOT A CONVENIENCE. It is the mask
; the iteration-types CACHE keys on — sync/async/for-of and nothing else — so two
; uses differing only in the string or destructuring bit SHARE a row, which is
; the reference's own statement about what the answer depends on. A cache keyed on
; the whole `use` would answer the same and never hit; one keyed on less would
; answer a for-await question with a for-of row.
define IterationUseAllowsSyncIterablesFlag: int 1 << 0
define IterationUseAllowsAsyncIterablesFlag: int 1 << 1
define IterationUseAllowsStringInputFlag: int 1 << 2
define IterationUseForOfFlag: int 1 << 3
define IterationUseYieldStarFlag: int 1 << 4
define IterationUseSpreadFlag: int 1 << 5
define IterationUseDestructuringFlag: int 1 << 6
define IterationUsePossiblyOutOfBounds: int 1 << 7

define IterationUseElement: int IterationUseAllowsSyncIterablesFlag
define IterationUseSpread: int IterationUseAllowsSyncIterablesFlag | IterationUseSpreadFlag
define IterationUseDestructuring: int IterationUseAllowsSyncIterablesFlag | IterationUseDestructuringFlag
define IterationUseForOf: int IterationUseAllowsSyncIterablesFlag | IterationUseAllowsStringInputFlag | IterationUseForOfFlag
define IterationUseForAwaitOf: int IterationUseAllowsSyncIterablesFlag | IterationUseAllowsAsyncIterablesFlag | IterationUseAllowsStringInputFlag | IterationUseForOfFlag
define IterationUseYieldStar: int IterationUseAllowsSyncIterablesFlag | IterationUseYieldStarFlag
define IterationUseAsyncYieldStar: int IterationUseAllowsSyncIterablesFlag | IterationUseAllowsAsyncIterablesFlag | IterationUseYieldStarFlag
define IterationUseGeneratorReturnType: int IterationUseAllowsSyncIterablesFlag
define IterationUseAsyncGeneratorReturnType: int IterationUseAllowsAsyncIterablesFlag
define IterationUseCacheFlags: int IterationUseAllowsSyncIterablesFlag | IterationUseAllowsAsyncIterablesFlag | IterationUseForOfFlag

; IterationTypeKind — which of the three slots a caller wants.
define IterationTypeKindYield: int 0
define IterationTypeKindReturn: int 1
define IterationTypeKindNext: int 2

; The members that encode facts about a KIND of type, in the reference's order.
; The presence of a fact means the test is true for some (and possibly all) values
; of that kind.
define TypeFactsBaseStringStrictFacts: int TypeFactsTypeofEQString | TypeFactsTypeofNENumber | TypeFactsTypeofNEBigInt | TypeFactsTypeofNEBoolean | TypeFactsTypeofNESymbol | TypeFactsTypeofNEObject | TypeFactsTypeofNEFunction | TypeFactsTypeofNEHostObject | TypeFactsNEUndefined | TypeFactsNENull | TypeFactsNEUndefinedOrNull
define TypeFactsBaseStringFacts: int TypeFactsBaseStringStrictFacts | TypeFactsEQUndefined | TypeFactsEQNull | TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsStringStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseStringStrictFacts | TypeFactsTruthy | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsStringFacts: int TypeFactsBaseStringFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsEmptyStringStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseStringStrictFacts | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsEmptyStringFacts: int TypeFactsBaseStringFacts
define TypeFactsNonEmptyStringStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseStringStrictFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsNonEmptyStringFacts: int TypeFactsBaseStringFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsBaseNumberStrictFacts: int TypeFactsTypeofEQNumber | TypeFactsTypeofNEString | TypeFactsTypeofNEBigInt | TypeFactsTypeofNEBoolean | TypeFactsTypeofNESymbol | TypeFactsTypeofNEObject | TypeFactsTypeofNEFunction | TypeFactsTypeofNEHostObject | TypeFactsNEUndefined | TypeFactsNENull | TypeFactsNEUndefinedOrNull
define TypeFactsBaseNumberFacts: int TypeFactsBaseNumberStrictFacts | TypeFactsEQUndefined | TypeFactsEQNull | TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsNumberStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseNumberStrictFacts | TypeFactsTruthy | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsNumberFacts: int TypeFactsBaseNumberFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsZeroNumberStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseNumberStrictFacts | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsZeroNumberFacts: int TypeFactsBaseNumberFacts
define TypeFactsNonZeroNumberStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseNumberStrictFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsNonZeroNumberFacts: int TypeFactsBaseNumberFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsBaseBigIntStrictFacts: int TypeFactsTypeofEQBigInt | TypeFactsTypeofNEString | TypeFactsTypeofNENumber | TypeFactsTypeofNEBoolean | TypeFactsTypeofNESymbol | TypeFactsTypeofNEObject | TypeFactsTypeofNEFunction | TypeFactsTypeofNEHostObject | TypeFactsNEUndefined | TypeFactsNENull | TypeFactsNEUndefinedOrNull
define TypeFactsBaseBigIntFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBigIntStrictFacts | TypeFactsEQUndefined | TypeFactsEQNull | TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsBigIntStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBigIntStrictFacts | TypeFactsTruthy | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsBigIntFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBigIntFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsZeroBigIntStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBigIntStrictFacts | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsZeroBigIntFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBigIntFacts
define TypeFactsNonZeroBigIntStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBigIntStrictFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsNonZeroBigIntFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBigIntFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsBaseBooleanStrictFacts: int TypeFactsTypeofEQBoolean | TypeFactsTypeofNEString | TypeFactsTypeofNENumber | TypeFactsTypeofNEBigInt | TypeFactsTypeofNESymbol | TypeFactsTypeofNEObject | TypeFactsTypeofNEFunction | TypeFactsTypeofNEHostObject | TypeFactsNEUndefined | TypeFactsNENull | TypeFactsNEUndefinedOrNull
define TypeFactsBaseBooleanFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBooleanStrictFacts | TypeFactsEQUndefined | TypeFactsEQNull | TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsBooleanStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBooleanStrictFacts | TypeFactsTruthy | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsBooleanFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBooleanFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsFalseStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBooleanStrictFacts | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsFalseFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBooleanFacts
define TypeFactsTrueStrictFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBooleanStrictFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsTrueFacts: int TypeFactsBaseBooleanFacts | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsSymbolStrictFacts: int TypeFactsTypeofEQSymbol | TypeFactsTypeofNEString | TypeFactsTypeofNENumber | TypeFactsTypeofNEBigInt | TypeFactsTypeofNEBoolean | TypeFactsTypeofNEObject | TypeFactsTypeofNEFunction | TypeFactsTypeofNEHostObject | TypeFactsNEUndefined | TypeFactsNENull | TypeFactsNEUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsSymbolFacts: int TypeFactsSymbolStrictFacts | TypeFactsEQUndefined | TypeFactsEQNull | TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsObjectStrictFacts: int TypeFactsTypeofEQObject | TypeFactsTypeofEQHostObject | TypeFactsTypeofNEString | TypeFactsTypeofNENumber | TypeFactsTypeofNEBigInt | TypeFactsTypeofNEBoolean | TypeFactsTypeofNESymbol | TypeFactsTypeofNEFunction | TypeFactsNEUndefined | TypeFactsNENull | TypeFactsNEUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsObjectFacts: int TypeFactsObjectStrictFacts | TypeFactsEQUndefined | TypeFactsEQNull | TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsFunctionStrictFacts: int TypeFactsTypeofEQFunction | TypeFactsTypeofEQHostObject | TypeFactsTypeofNEString | TypeFactsTypeofNENumber | TypeFactsTypeofNEBigInt | TypeFactsTypeofNEBoolean | TypeFactsTypeofNESymbol | TypeFactsTypeofNEObject | TypeFactsNEUndefined | TypeFactsNENull | TypeFactsNEUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsTruthy
define TypeFactsFunctionFacts: int TypeFactsFunctionStrictFacts | TypeFactsEQUndefined | TypeFactsEQNull | TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsVoidFacts: int TypeFactsTypeofNEString | TypeFactsTypeofNENumber | TypeFactsTypeofNEBigInt | TypeFactsTypeofNEBoolean | TypeFactsTypeofNESymbol | TypeFactsTypeofNEObject | TypeFactsTypeofNEFunction | TypeFactsTypeofNEHostObject | TypeFactsEQUndefined | TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsNENull | TypeFactsFalsy
define TypeFactsUndefinedFacts: int TypeFactsTypeofNEString | TypeFactsTypeofNENumber | TypeFactsTypeofNEBigInt | TypeFactsTypeofNEBoolean | TypeFactsTypeofNESymbol | TypeFactsTypeofNEObject | TypeFactsTypeofNEFunction | TypeFactsTypeofNEHostObject | TypeFactsEQUndefined | TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsNENull | TypeFactsFalsy | TypeFactsIsUndefined
define TypeFactsNullFacts: int TypeFactsTypeofEQObject | TypeFactsTypeofNEString | TypeFactsTypeofNENumber | TypeFactsTypeofNEBigInt | TypeFactsTypeofNEBoolean | TypeFactsTypeofNESymbol | TypeFactsTypeofNEFunction | TypeFactsTypeofNEHostObject | TypeFactsEQNull | TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull | TypeFactsNEUndefined | TypeFactsFalsy | TypeFactsIsNull
; TypeFactsAll &^ (EQUndefined | EQNull | EQUndefinedOrNull | IsUndefinedOrNull)
define TypeFactsEmptyObjectStrictFacts: int TypeFactsAll xor TypeFactsEQUndefined xor TypeFactsEQNull xor TypeFactsEQUndefinedOrNull xor TypeFactsIsUndefined xor TypeFactsIsNull
; TypeFactsAll &^ TypeFactsIsUndefinedOrNull
define TypeFactsEmptyObjectFacts: int TypeFactsAll xor TypeFactsIsUndefined xor TypeFactsIsNull
; TypeFactsAll &^ TypeFactsIsUndefinedOrNull
define TypeFactsUnknownFacts: int TypeFactsAll xor TypeFactsIsUndefined xor TypeFactsIsNull
define TypeFactsAllTypeofNE: int TypeFactsTypeofNEString | TypeFactsTypeofNENumber | TypeFactsTypeofNEBigInt | TypeFactsTypeofNEBoolean | TypeFactsTypeofNESymbol | TypeFactsTypeofNEObject | TypeFactsTypeofNEFunction | TypeFactsNEUndefined
; The two masks getIntersectionTypeFacts combines with — an intersection's facts
; are an OR over two bits and an AND over the rest.
define TypeFactsOrFactsMask: int TypeFactsTypeofEQFunction | TypeFactsTypeofNEObject
; TypeFactsAll &^ TypeFactsOrFactsMask
define TypeFactsAndFactsMask: int TypeFactsAll xor TypeFactsTypeofEQFunction xor TypeFactsTypeofNEObject

; ── PredicateSemantics, slice 88 ────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The three-valued answer of the two SYNTACTIC walks the logical and coalescing
; operators use — `getSyntacticTruthySemantics` and
; `getSyntacticNullishnessSemantics`. It is a bit PAIR and not an enum, and that
; is what makes the ConditionalExpression arm one line: a conditional is
; `semantics(whenTrue) | semantics(whenFalse)`, so two branches that disagree
; combine to Sometimes without a case for it.
;
; ★ `Sometimes` is the only value that reports NOTHING, at all four report sites.
; A walk that cannot decide must therefore answer Sometimes — which is why the
; two Identifier arms below answer it when this port cannot resolve the name.
define PredicateSemanticsNone:      int 0
define PredicateSemanticsAlways:    int 1 << 0
define PredicateSemanticsNever:     int 1 << 1
define PredicateSemanticsSometimes: int PredicateSemanticsAlways | PredicateSemanticsNever

; ── the type payloads ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ SLICE 66 IS THE SECOND PAYLOAD, so the union the note below promised
; arrives with it. The reference's hierarchy is four levels of Go embedding —
; `InterfaceType` embeds `TypeReference` embeds `ObjectType` embeds
; `StructuredType` — and §3.1's sum type flattens it: each arm is ONE record
; carrying the slots its own readers ask for, exactly as `NodeData`'s 134 arms do.
;
; ★★★ WHICH SLOTS ARE HERE IS A CLAIM ABOUT READERS, NOT ABOUT THE REFERENCE'S
; STRUCT. §3.5be's rule: a field nobody reads is a claim about the future. Every
; slot below is read by a function this slice ports; every slot NOT here is named
; with the reader that would make it observable, at the arm it belongs to.

; ObjectType.instantiations, as an ASSOCIATION LIST rather than a map — and the
; divergence is worth stating because it makes the port STRONGER than the
; reference here. Upstream keys `map[CacheHashKey]*Type` on `getTypeListKey`, an
; xxh3 hash of the type ids; a hash is what buys O(1) lookup and it buys a
; collision question with it. This slice's lists are one entry long for a
; non-generic class or interface, so a linear scan comparing the ARGUMENT LISTS
; THEMSELVES is both faster and exact — there is no key to collide.
;
; ★ The successor is named: the slice that instantiates generics is the one whose
; lists get long enough for the hash to be worth its collision argument, and it is
; the slice that must port `getTypeListKey` and `keyBuilder` with it.
; ★ The leading `hash` is a filter and not a key: these lists
; are PER TARGET and stay association lists, so the hash only lets the scan
; compare an int before walking two argument lists. Measured on
; `recursiveReverseMappedType`, where `type_lists_equal` under these two scans was
; the head of the profile once the union table was indexed.
define TypeInstantiation
(
    hash: int
    arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    instantiated: ref[Type]?
)

; The map upstream's `instantiations` is, over the port's association list: the
; hash's FIRST row through an address index, the rows of one hash chained in list
; order, caught up with the rows added since (catch_up_instantiation_index#). A
; generic interface (`Event<T>`, `Promise<T>`) collects thousands of rows, and the
; list scan per lookup was a leading item of a 2 800-file profile (2026-09-17).
; `list` names the list the index was built over. ★Since slice 326 the index is
; open-addressed slots of row + 1 probed by the row's own hash (the rows of one
; hash lie in list order along the probe path, so the first equal row still
; answers), made only for a list of ObjectRowChainMin rows or more: an
; AddressIndex per interface took an exclusive page even for one row.
; an instantiation list this long or longer is searched through its hash chain
define ObjectRowChainMin: int 8

define InstantiationIndex
(
    list: ref[Array[ref[TypeInstantiation]?]]
    slots: ref[Vector[int]]
    mask: int
    indexed: int
)

; TupleNormalizer, slice 115 — the reference's own struct, and its two methods are
; Checker methods here because `add` needs addOptionalityEx.
;
; ★ `last_required_index` / `first_rest_index` / `last_optional_or_rest_index` all
; start at -1, which is the reference's initialisation and is load-bearing for the
; tail: `firstRestIndex >= 0 && firstRestIndex < lastOptionalOrRestIndex` is how it
; asks *is there a run to fold*, and a 0 default would answer yes on a tuple with
; no rest element at all.
define TupleNormalizer
(
    types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    infos: ref[Array[ref[TupleElementInfo]?]]?
    last_required_index: int
    first_rest_index: int
    last_optional_or_rest_index: int
)
{
    init()
    {
        set types: null
        set infos: null
        set last_required_index: 0 - 1
        set first_rest_index: 0 - 1
        set last_optional_or_rest_index: 0 - 1
    }
}

; One row of c.tupleTypes, slice 115. The reference's map is keyed by a hash
; (getTupleKey); this is the same list-comparison shape TypeInstantiation uses, and
; the header on the `tuple_targets` field says why.
define TupleTarget
(
    element_infos: ref[Array[ref[TupleElementInfo]?]]?
    readonly: bool
    target: ref[Type]?
)

; The intrinsics, which carry no payload at all. It exists as an arm rather than
; as a null `data` so that every Type answers a `choose` — the shape ast.scaly's
; TokenData has, for the same reason.
; the intrinsic's name (`any`, `string`, …) lives in its own arm since slice 330:
; every other type carried an empty sixteen-byte slice for it
define NoTypeData (name: Slice[char])

; ObjectType, the reference's plain anonymous object — newObjectType's last arm
; and, in slice 67, the STATIC TYPE OF A CLASS. It carries no slots at all, which
; is a statement about readers rather than an omission: everything the
; reference's ObjectType holds (`members`, `properties`, `callSignatures`,
; `constructSignatures`, `indexInfos`) is written by resolveStructuredTypeMembers
; and read by getPropertiesOfType / getSignaturesOfType / getIndexInfosOfType,
; all three of which report here — and `target` / `mapper` belong to the
; INSTANTIATED anonymous type, whose reader is instantiateType.
;
; ★ It is an arm of its own rather than NoTypeData because the two are different
; answers to the same `choose`: NoTypeData means an INTRINSIC, which is what the
; reference's `AsObjectType()` would refuse to give. An empty arm that can be
; told apart is worth more than a shared one that cannot.
;
; ★★ SLICE 85 GIVES IT ITS ONE SLOT, and the line above named this slice as the
; reader.
;
; ★★★ SLICE 125 ADDS THE OTHER THREE, AND THE TWO NOTES THAT STOOD HERE ARE ITS
; EXPIRY. Both said `target` and `mapper` *belong to the INSTANTIATED anonymous
; type, whose reader is instantiateType* — true while getObjectTypeInstantiation
; reported, and this slice is that function. `instantiations` is the reference's
; `ObjectType.instantiations` map, held as an association list for the reason the
; interface arm's is (see ObjectInstantiation).
define AnonymousTypeData
(
    resolved: ref[StructuredMembers]?
    target: ref[Type]?
    mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?
    instantiations: ref[Array[ref[ObjectInstantiation]?]]?
)

; ── MappedTypeData (slice 125) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's `MappedType`, flattened: its embedded `ObjectType` gives the
; first four slots (the same four AnonymousTypeData carries) and its own eight
; follow. It is an arm of its own rather than a shared anonymous one because
; every one of those eight is a mapped type's alone — `AsMappedType()` upstream —
; and the port's rule for a downcast is that the arm answers null where the
; reference would panic.
;
; ★★ `declaration` is the MappedTypeNode, held as a plain `ref[AstNode]` the way
; every other declaration slot in this file is; the reference's typed
; `*ast.MappedTypeNode` buys nothing here because ast.scaly's accessors take a
; node and ask its kind.
;
; ★★ `contains_error` is written by get_type_of_mapped_symbol's circularity arm
; and read by nothing yet — it is the reference's own slot and the writer is
; ported, so it is carried rather than dropped: a slot with a writer and no
; reader is the shape §3.5be admits, where a slot with neither is not.
define MappedTypeData
(
    resolved: ref[StructuredMembers]?
    target: ref[Type]?
    mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?
    instantiations: ref[Array[ref[ObjectInstantiation]?]]?

    declaration: ref[AstNode]?
    type_parameter: ref[Type]?
    constraint_type: ref[Type]?
    name_type: ref[Type]?
    template_type: ref[Type]?
    modifiers_type: ref[Type]?
    resolved_apparent_type: ref[Type]?
    contains_error: bool
)

; ── ReverseMappedTypeData (slice 160) ───────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's `ReverseMappedType`, flattened the way MappedTypeData is: the
; embedded `ObjectType` gives the first four slots and its own three follow. It
; is the type a HOMOMORPHIC mapped type is inferred BACKWARDS through — the
; source object whose properties still have to be run through the mapping — and
; its members are resolved lazily, one reverse inference per property.
;
; ★ `source`, `mapped_type` and `constraint_type` are never null on a type this
; port mints: create_reverse_mapped_type writes all three in the line after
; new_object_type, which is the reference's own order. They are declared nullable
; because the arm answers null where `AsReverseMappedType()` would panic.
define ReverseMappedTypeData
(
    resolved: ref[StructuredMembers]?
    target: ref[Type]?
    mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?
    instantiations: ref[Array[ref[ObjectInstantiation]?]]?

    source: ref[Type]?
    mapped_type: ref[Type]?
    constraint_type: ref[Type]?
)

; ── the reverse-mapped memo rows (slice 160) ────────────────────────────────
;
; `c.reverseMappedCache` and `c.reverseHomomorphicMappedCache`, both keyed on the
; reference's `ReverseMappedTypeKey{sourceId, targetId, constraintId}`.
;
; ★★★ THE TWO TABLES ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE AND THE DIFFERENCE IS `ok`. The
; homomorphic one is read as `if cached := …; cached != nil`, so a null result is
; recomputed; the other is read as `cached, ok := …` and a PRESENT null answers
; `unknownType`. That is why the lookup here returns the ROW and not the type:
; absence and a null result are two answers, and folding them would make a failed
; reverse inference retry on every property of every arrival.
define ReverseMappedRow
(
    source_id: int
    target_id: int
    constraint_id: int
    result: ref[Type]?
)

; ── the three closure STATES of the mapped type (slice 125) ─────────────────
;
; §3.9's answer applied three times. `resolveMappedTypeMembers` and
; `getIndexTypeForMappedType` each build a closure over half a dozen locals and
; hand it to `forEachType` and to
; `forEachMappedTypePropertyKeyTypeAndIndexSignatureKeyType`; a Scaly closure
; cannot escape into a callee, so what the closure captured becomes a record and
; what it DID becomes a method taking that record.
;
; ★★ THE TWO STATES ARE NOT ONE. They share `mapped`, `type_parameter` and
; `name_type` and nothing else: one accumulates SYMBOLS and index infos, the other
; accumulates KEY TYPES, and merging them would make every reader ask which half
; is live.
define MappedMemberState
(
    mapped: ref[Type]
    members: ref[SymbolTable]
    index_infos: ref[Array[ref[IndexInfo]?]]
    type_parameter: ref[Type]?
    name_type: ref[Type]?
    template_type: ref[Type]?
    modifiers_type: ref[Type]?
    template_modifiers: int
    should_link_prop_declarations: bool
)

define MappedKeyState
(
    mapped: ref[Type]
    type_parameter: ref[Type]?
    name_type: ref[Type]?
    keys: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
    ; getIndexTypeForMappedType's callback widens a `string` key to
    ; `string | number`; getApparentMappedTypeKeys' callback does not (slice
    ; 202 — the two shared one state and `keyof { [K in keyof T as K & string]:
    ; … }` answered `string | number` against a target of `string`).
    widen_string: bool
)

; ── the map-over-a-union TAG and its captures (slice 125) ───────────────────
;
; §3.18's shape at the second site in this port. `mapType(t, f)` is called with
; three different closures in this chapter, each capturing something else — the
; mapped type and its homomorphic variable, a conditional root and its mapper, or
; nothing at all — so the mapping is a TAG plus the union of what they capture,
; and `apply_map_type_kind` is the one-line dispatcher.
;
; ★ The three callers: instantiate_mapped_type's `instantiateConstituent`,
; get_conditional_type_instantiation's distribution, and
; get_lower_bound_of_key_type's own recursion over a union.
define MapTypeKindLowerBoundOfKeyType: int 0
define MapTypeKindMappedConstituent: int 1
define MapTypeKindConditionalDistribution: int 2
; getTemplateLiteralType's own union arm (slice 126): it re-enters itself with ONE
; constituent substituted at `index`, so the three captures are the two lists and
; the position.
define MapTypeKindTemplateLiteralSpan: int 3
; getBaseTypeOfLiteralTypeForComparison's own union arm — it maps itself over the
; constituents and captures nothing, so it needs no MapTypeArgs at all.
define MapTypeKindBaseTypeForComparison: int 4
; getBaseTypeOfLiteralTypeUnion's mapper, which is get_base_type_of_literal_type
; itself. Captures nothing either.
define MapTypeKindBaseTypeOfLiteral: int 5
; extractDefinitelyFalsyTypes' mapper — getDefinitelyFalsyPartOfType, slice 135.
; Captures nothing.
define MapTypeKindDefinitelyFalsyPart: int 6
; getContextualTypeForElementExpression's mapper, slice 135 — the ARRAY LITERAL
; element's contextual type. Captures four INTEGERS (the element's index, the
; literal's length, and the first and last spread positions), which is why
; MapTypeArgs grew four int slots rather than reusing `index`: that one belongs to
; the template-literal span.
define MapTypeKindContextualElement: int 7
; getWidenedLiteralType's own union arm, slice 139 — it maps ITSELF over the
; constituents and captures nothing, exactly as the two base-type mappers above
; do. ★The stop it replaces read *mapType over getWidenedLiteralType, i.e.
; getUnionType again* — a note whose two premises (this dispatcher, and the union
; type) had both been in the file for a dozen slices (§3.5eo).
define MapTypeKindWidenedLiteral: int 8
; getMutableArrayOrTupleType's own union arm, slice 139. Captures nothing.
define MapTypeKindMutableArrayOrTuple: int 9

; getTupleElementTypeOutOfStartCount's distribution (slice 142) — two captures,
; the START INDEX as a jsnum bit pattern and the undefined-like type, which is
; nullable BY VALUE upstream (`core.IfElse(..., c.missingType, nil)`) and is the
; whole difference between including undefined and not.
define MapTypeKindTupleElementOutOfStartCount: int 10

; getRestType's own union arm (slice 145) — the OBJECT-REST recursion, which is
; the reference's `mapType(source, func(t) { return c.getRestType(t, properties,
; symbol) })`. Two captures, the omitted property NAMES and the symbol the
; anonymous result is named after.
define MapTypeKindRestType: int 11
; isValidSpreadType's head (slice 145) — `mapType(t, c.getBaseConstraintOrType)`.
; Captures nothing.
define MapTypeKindBaseConstraintOrType: int 13
; checkArrayLiteralDestructuringElementAssignment's rest arm (slice 145) —
; `mapType(sourceType, func(t) { return c.sliceTupleType(t, elementIndex, 0) })`.
; The captured index rides `index`, which is already a field.
define MapTypeKindSliceTuple: int 12
; checkObjectLiteral's SPREAD tail (slice 147) — *remap the raw emptyObjectType fed
; in at the top into a fresh empty object literal type, unique to this use site*,
; which is the reference's own comment. Its two captures are the LITERAL NODE and
; the accumulated state, because the callback calls `createObjectLiteralType()`.
define MapTypeKindEmptyObjectLiteralRemap: int 14

; MapTypeKindContextualIndexInfoValue (slice 151) — the closure of
; getContextualTypeForObjectLiteralElement's third arm, whose one capture is the
; NAME TYPE the index info is looked up with. ★The reference goes through
; mapTypeEx rather than getApplicableIndexInfo *"because it performs potentially
; expensive intersection reduction"*, which is a comment about cost and is
; transcribed as the shape it names.
define MapTypeKindContextualIndexInfoValue: int 15

; MapTypeKindThisTypeFromContextual (slice 158) — getThisTypeFromContextualType's
; closure, which captures nothing: it looks for a `ThisType<T>` in the constituent
; and answers its one type argument. ★The INTERSECTION arm returns the FIRST
; constituent that carries one and `nil` otherwise, so it is not a fold over the
; whole intersection — a `A & ThisType<X> & ThisType<Y>` answers X.
define MapTypeKindThisTypeFromContextual: int 16

; MapTypeKindJsxChildElement (slice 158) — getContextualTypeForChildJsxExpression's
; closure, whose one capture is the CHILD INDEX: an array-like `children` type is
; indexed at that position, anything else is the answer as it stands. ★It reuses
; MapTypeArgs' `index` field, which MapTypeKindTemplateLiteralSpan already carries
; — the two kinds never share a call.
define MapTypeKindJsxChildElement: int 17

; MapTypeKindSimplified (slice 166) — getSimplifiedIndexedAccessTypeWorker's
; mapped-object closure, whose one capture is the `writing` flag. ★It gets a
; field of its own rather than borrowing `for_constraint`: that one is the
; conditional distribution's `forConstraint` and the two kinds are asked from the
; same chapter, so a shared slot would read as one flag with two meanings.
define MapTypeKindSimplified: int 18

; MapTypeKindSpreadLeft / MapTypeKindSpreadRight (slice 170) — getSpreadType's two
; own distributions, `mapType(left, t => getSpreadType(t, right, …))` and its
; mirror. ★TWO kinds rather than one plus a side flag: the captured type is the
; OTHER operand, and which side that is decides the argument ORDER of the
; recursive call — a bit read at the call site would be a second thing to get
; right at every reader.
define MapTypeKindSpreadLeft: int 19
define MapTypeKindSpreadRight: int 20

; MapTypeKindStringMapping (slice 171) — getStringMappingType's own union arm,
; `mapType(t, func(t) { return c.getStringMappingType(symbol, t) })`. Its one
; capture is the intrinsic's SYMBOL, which is what decides which of the four
; mappings runs; it gets a slot of its own rather than borrowing `rest_symbol`
; or `spread_symbol` for the reason MapTypeKindSimplified did not borrow
; `for_constraint`.
define MapTypeKindStringMapping: int 21

; MapTypeKindConstraintFilter (slice 179) — getInferredType's return-type
; salvage: `mapType(inferredType, func(t) { return IfElse(compareTypes(t,
; constraintWithThis) != False, t, neverType) })`, i.e. every constituent that
; does not satisfy the instantiated constraint replaced by `never` so that the
; union reduction drops it. Its two captures are the CONSTRAINT and the
; inference context's COMPARER kind, and both get slots of their own: the
; comparer is an int rather than a func value for §3.18's reason, and the
; constraint cannot borrow `check_type` because that slot means the
; conditional's own check type at the arm two above.
define MapTypeKindConstraintFilter: int 22

; MapTypeKindInstantiationExpression (slice 181) — getInstantiationExpressionType's
; UNION arm, `c.mapType(t, getInstantiatedType)`. ★It re-enters the OUTER of the
; two closures, not the part-wise one, which is what gives every constituent a
; FRESH `hasSignatures`/`hasApplicableSignature` pair while the two
; `hasSomeApplicableSignature`/`nonApplicableType` slots stay shared. That
; asymmetry is the whole reason the state is split across two records here.
define MapTypeKindInstantiationExpression: int 23

; MapTypeKindNormalizedTupleVariadic (slice 201) — createNormalizedTupleTypeEx's
; own distribution, `[A, ...(X | Y | Z)]` into `[A, ...X] | [A, ...Y] | [A, ...Z]`.
; ★The three captures are the tuple TARGET, the element list and the INDEX of the
; variadic slot being distributed; the object flags ride along because the
; recursion is the same entry point. It cannot borrow `types` above — that slot
; is the template-literal span's — nor `index`, which the slice-tuple kind uses.
define MapTypeKindNormalizedTupleVariadic: int 24
; narrowTypeByTypeFacts' closure: `mapped` carries the implied type, `index` the facts.
; It goes through map_type on purpose — mapType walks a union's ORIGIN, so the enum
; `Color` narrowed out of `Color | string` comes back as the enum's own union object
; (slice 206; `TypeGuardWithEnumUnion`).
define MapTypeKindNarrowByTypeFacts: int 25
; getNarrowedTypeWorker's two inner closures over the CURRENT type: `mapped` carries the
; candidate constituent `n`, `for_constraint` carries checkDerived. Through map_type for the
; reason §3.5ik gives: mapType walks a union's ORIGIN, and `Array.isArray(expression)`
; narrows `Expression` back to its named member `BooleanLogicExpression` only that way.
define MapTypeKindPickMoreSpecific: int 26
define MapTypeKindIntersectIfGeneric: int 27

; IntrinsicTypeKind — the five names `type X<T> = intrinsic` is legal for. ★A
; TABLE upstream (`intrinsicTypeKinds`, a map from name to kind); five names
; here, for the reason type_alias_intrinsic_is_legal's header gives: the VALUE
; is read at four sites, all in the string-mapping chapter, and the fifth
; reader asks only for membership.
define IntrinsicTypeKindUnknown: int 0
define IntrinsicTypeKindUppercase: int 1
define IntrinsicTypeKindLowercase: int 2
define IntrinsicTypeKindCapitalize: int 3
define IntrinsicTypeKindUncapitalize: int 4
define IntrinsicTypeKindNoInfer: int 5

define MapTypeArgs
(
    mapped: ref[Type]?
    type_variable: ref[Type]?
    mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?
    root: ref[ConditionalRoot]?
    check_type: ref[Type]?
    for_constraint: bool

    ; MapTypeKindTemplateLiteralSpan's three (slice 126).
    texts: ref[Array[ref[String]?]]?
    types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    index: int

    ; MapTypeKindContextualElement's four (slice 135).
    element_index: int
    element_length: int
    first_spread_index: int
    last_spread_index: int

    ; MapTypeKindTupleElementOutOfStartCount's two (slice 142).
    start_index: u64
    undefined_like: ref[Type]?

    ; MapTypeKindRestType's two (slice 145). MapTypeKindSliceTuple adds none —
    ; its one capture is `index` above.
    rest_properties: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    rest_symbol: ref[Symbol]?

    ; MapTypeKindEmptyObjectLiteralRemap's two (slice 147).
    objlit_node: ref[AstNode]?
    objlit_state: ref[ObjectLiteralState]?

    ; MapTypeKindContextualIndexInfoValue's one (slice 151).
    key_name_type: ref[Type]?

    ; MapTypeKindSimplified's one (slice 166).
    simplify_writing: bool

    ; MapTypeKindSpreadLeft / MapTypeKindSpreadRight's four (slice 170) — the
    ; OTHER operand of the spread plus the three arguments getSpreadType carries
    ; unchanged through its recursion.
    spread_other: ref[Type]?
    spread_symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    spread_object_flags: int
    spread_readonly: bool

    ; MapTypeKindStringMapping's one (slice 171) — the intrinsic's symbol.
    string_mapping_symbol: ref[Symbol]?

    ; MapTypeKindConstraintFilter's two (slice 179).
    filter_constraint: ref[Type]?
    filter_comparer: int

    ; MapTypeKindInstantiationExpression's two (slice 181).
    inst_expr_node: ref[AstNode]?
    inst_expr_state: ref[InstantiationExpressionState]?

    ; MapTypeKindNormalizedTupleVariadic's four (slice 201).
    tuple_target: ref[Type]?
    tuple_element_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    tuple_variadic_index: int
    tuple_object_flags: int
)

; getInstantiationExpressionType's SHARED captures — the two the reference's two
; nested closures both write. §3.21's shape.
define InstantiationExpressionState
(
    type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    has_some_applicable: bool
    non_applicable: ref[Type]?
)

; …and its PER-CALL captures: `hasSignatures` and `hasApplicableSignature` are
; declared inside getInstantiatedType, so each constituent of a union gets its
; own pair while the record above is threaded through unchanged.
define InstantiationExpressionPart
(
    has_signatures: bool
    has_applicable_signature: bool
)

; addSpans' captured state (slice 126) — §3.21. The builder is a SLOT because the
; reference resets it when a span opens and a StringBuilder here has none: a fresh
; builder is put in its place instead.
define TemplateSpanState
(
    sb: ref[StringBuilder]
    new_texts: ref[Array[ref[String]?]]
    new_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
)

; inferFromLiteralPartsToTemplateLiteral's captured state (slice 126) — §3.21
; again, and this one is the reason that rule exists: `addMatch` captures THREE
; mutable locals and is called from four places in one loop.
define TemplateMatchState
(
    seg: int
    pos: int
    matches: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
)

; ── ConditionalInstantiation (slice 125) ────────────────────────────────────
;
; One row of `ConditionalRoot.instantiations`, whose upstream key is
; getConditionalTypeKey(typeArguments, alias, forConstraint). An ENTRY for
; ObjectInstantiation's reason.
define ConditionalInstantiation
(
    arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    alias_symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    alias_type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    for_constraint: bool
    instantiated: ref[Type]?
    hash: int
)

; ── ObjectInstantiation (slice 125) ─────────────────────────────────────────
;
; One row of `ObjectType.instantiations`, whose upstream key is
; getTypeInstantiationKey(typeArguments, alias, singleSignature). It is an ENTRY
; rather than a bare type for §3.16's reason: two thirds of the key are not on
; the answer — a mapped type's instantiation may be a UNION (the homomorphic
; distribution), which carries neither the argument list nor the alias the key
; was built from.
define ObjectInstantiation
(
    hash: int
    arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    alias_symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    alias_type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    single_signature: bool
    instantiated: ref[Type]?
)

; InterfaceType, flattened. The first three slots are the embedded ObjectType's
; and TypeReference's; the last three are InterfaceType's own.
;
; ★ NOT CARRIED, each with its reader: `mapper` (instantiateType) and `node`
; (getTypeArguments' deferred path, which is unreachable while
; resolved_type_arguments is set at construction).
;
; ★★ THE FIVE `declared*` SLOTS THIS LINE USED TO NAME ARRIVED IN SLICE 85, and
; the deferral had named its own expiry date — *"resolveDeclaredMembers, behind
; checkIndexConstraints"* — exactly as slice 66's base-type deferral did one slice
; earlier. They are ONE reference here rather than five slots (see
; DeclaredMembers), and `resolved` beside it is the embedded StructuredType's.
;
; ★★ THE THREE BASE SLOTS BELOW ARRIVED IN SLICE 67, AND SLICE 66 HAD NAMED THIS
; SLICE AS THEIR READER. The line above used to carry `baseTypesResolved` /
; `resolvedBaseTypes` / `resolvedBaseConstructorType` with the reader
; "getBaseTypes, which is where BOTH call sites of this slice now stop" — so the
; deferral names its own expiry date, and this is it. A limit that is fixed and
; still written down as a limit is worse than no note at all.
define InterfaceTypeData
(
    target: ref[Type]?
    resolved_type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    instantiations: ref[Array[ref[TypeInstantiation]?]]?
    instantiation_index: ref[InstantiationIndex]?

    ; allTypeParameters is outer + local + thisType, in that order, and the two
    ; counts below are what slice the three apart — which is why
    ; outer_type_parameter_count is carried even though only TypeParameters()
    ; reads the array in this slice.
    all_type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    outer_type_parameter_count: int
    this_type: ref[Type]?

    ; getBaseTypes' memo and its two answers. `base_types_resolved` is a BIT
    ; rather than "the list is non-null", because the reference's answer for an
    ; interface with no `extends` is an EMPTY list and asking again is what the
    ; bit prevents — a null list would make the memo miss for exactly the 97 % of
    ; the corpus that has no base type at all.
    base_types_resolved: bool
    resolved_base_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?

    ; getBaseConstructorTypeOfClass' memo. `undefined_type` is a real answer here
    ; (the class has no `extends`), which is why the reference tests it against
    ; nil rather than keeping a second bit.
    resolved_base_constructor_type: ref[Type]?

    ; resolveDeclaredMembers' memo and the embedded StructuredType, slice 85.
    declared: ref[DeclaredMembers]?
    resolved: ref[StructuredMembers]?

    ; ★★★ THE FIVE TUPLE SLOTS, SLICE 115, AND THEY ARE HERE BECAUSE THE
    ; REFERENCE'S STRUCT PUTS THEM HERE: `type TupleType struct { InterfaceType;
    ; elementInfos; minLength; fixedLength; combinedFlags; readonly }` EMBEDS the
    ; interface type, so a tuple target's field list is this one followed by these
    ; five. §3.5n's rule is *read the reference's struct*, and the reference's
    ; answer is an extension rather than a sibling.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ALTERNATIVE COSTS TWELVE ACCESSORS AND EACH MISS IS A SILENT NULL. A
    ; separate `TupleTypeData` arm would need a Tuple case added to `this_type_of`,
    ; `target_of`, `resolved_type_arguments_of`, `base_types_resolved_of`,
    ; `resolved_base_types_of`, `resolved_base_constructor_type_of`,
    ; `mark_base_types_resolved`, `set_resolved_base_types`,
    ; `set_resolved_base_constructor_type`, `type_parameter_count_of`,
    ; `all_type_parameters_of`, `declared_members_of` and `attach_declared_members`
    ; — and each of those answers null or false for an arm it does not name, which
    ; is the port's own choice for a downcast the reference would panic on. Sharing
    ; the arm makes every one of them correct for a tuple BY CONSTRUCTION, which is
    ; exactly the property the reference's embedding has.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE PRICE IS THAT AN ORDINARY INTERFACE CARRIES FIVE DEAD SLOTS, so the
    ; DISCRIMINATOR is the FLAG and never the payload: every reader below asks
    ; `object_flags & ObjectFlagsTuple` first, the same question the reference's
    ; `AsTupleType()` is guarded by at each of its call sites. A reader that asks
    ; the payload arm alone gets an interface's zeros and cannot tell them from a
    ; zero-arity tuple's.
    element_infos: ref[Array[ref[TupleElementInfo]?]]?
    min_length: int
    fixed_length: int
    combined_flags: int
    readonly: bool
)
{
    init()
    {
        set target: null
        set resolved_type_arguments: null
        set instantiations: null
        set instantiation_index: null
        set all_type_parameters: null
        set outer_type_parameter_count: 0
        set this_type: null
        set base_types_resolved: false
        set resolved_base_types: null
        set resolved_base_constructor_type: null
        set declared: null
        set resolved: null
        set element_infos: null
        set min_length: 0
        set fixed_length: 0
        ; 0 rather than `ElementFlagsNone`: that table is declared 2 100 lines
        ; BELOW this record, and a constant read before its own declaration is the
        ; silent-drop class the root CLAUDE.md names.
        set combined_flags: 0
        set readonly: false
    }
}

; TypeReference — createTypeReferenceEx's product. A SEPARATE record rather than a
; shared one, on §3.5n's rule: the slot lists are not identical, and an arm that
; borrowed InterfaceTypeData would carry three slots whose absence of a writer is
; exactly what tells a reader it is not an interface.
;
; ★★★ IT WAS CALLED `TypeReferenceData` UNTIL SLICE 86 AND THAT NAME IS TAKEN BY
; ast.scaly — see TypeParameterTypeData's header for what the collision costs. This
; one was HARMLESS by luck (both records are `{ptr, ptr}`, so the collapsed LLVM
; type fits either) and it is renamed anyway, because the two facts a reader needs
; are that the name is taken and that a shape agreeing today is not a rule.
; ★★★ THE `resolved` SLOT IS SLICE 96'S AND IT WAS FOUND BY AN exit 21 AT STAGE 2.
; Upstream every object type embeds `StructuredType`, so a TYPE REFERENCE holds a
; resolved-members table exactly as an interface does; here the arms were split and
; only two of them had the slot. Nothing had ever asked a REFERENCE for its members
; — `resolve_type_reference_members` existed, but every caller of
; resolveStructuredTypeMembers stood behind a guard that never sent one — so
; `structured_members_of` answered null for `B<string>`, set_structured_type_members
; unwrapped that null, and the process exited 21 with no source location.
; ★★It was invisible at stage 1 and on all seven fixtures: a GENERIC interface
; accessed through a property is the shape, and the corpus has it six times in
; 18 264 units. **A slice whose chapter reaches a new kind of type owes a stage-2
; run, and this is the entry that says why.**
define ReferenceTypeData
(
    target: ref[Type]?
    resolved_type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    resolved: ref[StructuredMembers]?

    ; ★★★ THE DEFERRED PAIR, SLICE 115. createDeferredTypeReference builds a
    ; reference whose type ARGUMENTS are not resolved yet and hangs the type NODE on
    ; it; getTypeArguments' second branch reads the node back and resolves them.
    ; They arrive together because a `node` without that branch is a slot nothing
    ; can answer from — which is exactly what the slot's absence used to mean.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND `type A = string[]` IS THE SHAPE THAT FORCED THEM. Every array or
    ; tuple type node written as the right-hand side of a type ALIAS is deferred by
    ; isDeferredTypeReferenceNode's FIRST disjunct (getAliasSymbolForTypeNode names
    ; it), so without this pair the whole alias-of-array family stopped — measured
    ; on slice 115's own array fixture, whose first line is one.
    ;
    ; ★ `mapper` is the deferred reference's own (create_deferred_type_reference_ex
    ; receives it from get_object_type_instantiation); the note that once called it
    ; nil for every reference expired with the deferred instantiation arm.
    node: ref[AstNode]?
    mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?

    ; ★ `ObjectType.instantiations` — EVERY object kind carries it upstream, a DEFERRED
    ; type reference included: getObjectTypeInstantiation keys the instantiations of a
    ; deferred reference's node on `links.resolvedType`, which IS a Reference. Without
    ; the slot every ask missed, built a fresh deferred reference with a fresh mapper,
    ; and the recursive units instantiated four to five times what the reference does
    ; (`recursiveIndexedAccessSimplification`: TS2589 36× against 11×; slice 215).
    instantiations: ref[Array[ref[ObjectInstantiation]?]]?
)
{
    init(tg: ref[Type]?, args: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?)
    {
        set target: tg
        set resolved_type_arguments: args
        set resolved: null
        set node: null
        set mapper: null
        set instantiations: null
    }
}

; TypeParameter. `constraint` is written by getDeclaredTypeOfClassOrInterface for
; the `this` type and, since slice 81, by getConstraintFromTypeParameter — which is
; also its first reader; `is_this_type` is IsThisType()'s slot.
;
; ★★ THE LAST TWO SLOTS ARE THE REFERENCE'S ConstrainedType AND TypeParameter
; MEMOS, and the first of them sits one level too low here on purpose:
; `resolvedBaseConstraint` belongs to ConstrainedType, which upstream is embedded by
; EIGHT type kinds (StructuredType — so every object, union and intersection type —
; plus TypeParameter, IndexType, IndexedAccessType, TemplateLiteralType,
; StringMappingType, SubstitutionType and ConditionalType). Seven of those eight
; cannot be MADE by this port (see the base-constraint chapter's header), and the
; eighth is this arm — so a shared slot would have to live on `Type` itself, which
; is a bigger claim than the one reader needs. is_constrained_type is where the
; distinction is written down, and it is the function to move when the second kind
; arrives.
;
; ★ NOT CARRIED, with readers: `target` and `mapper` (instantiateType). The
; `resolvedDefaultType` this note used to name among them arrived with slice 81.
;
; ★★★ IT WAS CALLED `TypeParameterData` UNTIL SLICE 86, WHICH IS THE NAME
; ast.scaly'S TypeParameterDeclaration PAYLOAD ALREADY HAD — AND THE COLLISION WAS
; SILENT, LOSSY AND FOUR SLICES OLD. A top-level `define` mangles WITHOUT its
; module (`_Z17TypeParameterData`), so the emitter's named-struct table keeps ONE
; of the two: the AST record, `{ptr, ptr, ptr, ptr, ptr}`. Under that type
; `mark_this_type`'s `store i1 true` went into the low byte of a POINTER slot, and
; `is_this_type_of`'s `return tp.is_this_type` extracted a `ptr` where an `i1` was
; wanted and was DROPPED — the emitted arm computes the value, branches to the tail
; and returns the literal `false`. **So the predicate answered false for every type
; in the tree, and nothing said a word.**
;
; ★★★ WHAT MADE IT SURVIVE IS THAT IT HAD ALMOST NO READER: `is_this_type_of`'s one
; caller before slice 86 is computeBaseConstraint's early exit, where a wrong
; `false` costs one extra recursion step and no visible answer. Slice 86's
; `MapsThisOnly` is the first reader that DECIDES something, and it decided wrong on
; every class in the corpus — every member minted, none of them by the reference's
; rule. §3.5be says a field with no reader is a claim about the future; **this is
; the sharper form: a field with no reader is not even known to WORK.**
;
; ★ The gate is `tests/run.sh`'s duplicate-`define` scan, added in the same commit.
; The compiler cannot see this today: two modules of one package may declare the
; same top-level concept and nothing is reported.
define TypeParameterTypeData
(
    constraint: ref[Type]?
    is_this_type: bool
    resolved_default_type: ref[Type]?

    ; ★★★ THE CLONE PAIR, SLICE 125. `target` and `mapper` are the reference's own
    ; slots and they are what makes a CLONED type parameter answer a constraint at
    ; all: instantiateAnonymousType's mapped arm mints a fresh parameter for the
    ; mapped type's `P`, and getConstraintFromTypeParameter's FIRST branch is
    ; `if tp.target != nil` — instantiate the ORIGINAL's constraint through the
    ; clone's mapper. Without them that branch cannot be written, and a clone
    ; answers *no constraint* for a `[P in keyof T]` whose constraint is the whole
    ; point.
    target: ref[Type]?
    mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?
)

; LiteralType — the payload of every string, number, bigint and boolean literal
; type, slice 71.
;
; ★★★ THE REFERENCE'S `value` IS A GO `any` AND IT IS THREE SLOTS HERE, NOT A
; FOUR-ARM UNION, and the precedent is this file's own: TypeResolution's
; `TypeSystemEntity` is likewise an `any` held as slots (see its note). The
; argument is the same and it is stronger here — the reference never *asks* the
; value what it is. Its four readers are `getStringLiteralValue`,
; `getNumberLiteralValue`, `getBigIntLiteralValue` and `getBooleanLiteralValue`,
; each an unchecked type ASSERTION whose correctness is the type's own FLAG. So
; the discriminator is `Type.flags`, exactly as upstream, and a second one in the
; payload would be a thing to keep in step for no reader.
;
; ★ `text` is BORROWED, never copied: every value that reaches here is an
; `AstNode.literal_text_of`, i.e. a run of bytes on the page the parse owns, and
; the checker does not outlive the parse. The bigint arm is the one exception in
; SPIRIT rather than in kind — its digits are computed by ParsePseudoBigInt into a
; buffer this file allocates on the checker's own page.
;
; ★★ `regular_type` and `fresh_type` are the reference's own two slots and they
; are what makes a literal type a PAIR. The regular type is created first and
; points at itself; the fresh type is minted lazily by getFreshTypeOfLiteralType,
; points BACK at the regular one, and points at itself as its own fresh type.
; Both booleans break the pattern by being built eagerly and in the other order
; (see initialize_intrinsic_types).
;
; ★★★ `regular_type` STILL HAS NO READER AND `fresh_type` HAS TWO — and slice 71
; wrote that pair down WRONG BY ONE, which slice 72 is what made visible. That
; slice said `regular_type` is read by getRegularTypeOfLiteralType AND
; isFreshLiteralType. It is not: isFreshLiteralType is
; `t.flags&Freshable != 0 && t.AsLiteralType().freshType == t` and reads the FRESH
; slot. ★A slot's reader list is the kind of claim that reads as checked and is
; not, so it is corrected here rather than left to be re-derived.
;
; ★★ SO THE DOOR SLICE 71 NAMED OPENED FOR HALF OF WHAT WAS BEHIND IT.
; getWidenedLiteralType is ported (slice 72) and asks isFreshLiteralType five
; times, which gives `fresh_type` its second reader beside
; getFreshTypeOfLiteralType. getRegularTypeOfLiteralType is STILL not ported, and
; its argument is unchanged: its only callers are getRegularTypeOfExpression and
; getBaseTypeOfLiteralType, its UNION arm carries a `record_unported`, and a
; work-list row in a function nothing calls is a row that can never appear — the
; product of these slices IS the work list, so a phantom entry in it is worse
; than a missing function.
define LiteralTypeData
(
    ; StringLiteral: the cooked text of the literal. BigIntLiteral:
    ; PseudoBigInt.Base10Value — the absolute value in base 10, no leading zeros,
    ; and EMPTY for zero, which is the reference's own representation and the
    ; reason pseudo_big_int_to_string has a length test rather than a value test.
    text: Slice[char]
    ; NumberLiteral: the IEEE-754 bits of the jsnum.Number.
    bits: u64
    ; BigIntLiteral: PseudoBigInt.Negative.
    negative: bool
    ; BooleanLiteral: the value.
    bool_value: bool
    regular_type: ref[Type]?
    fresh_type: ref[Type]?
)
{
    init(t: Slice[char], b: u64, neg: bool, bv: bool)
    {
        set text: t
        set bits: b
        set negative: neg
        set bool_value: bv
        set regular_type: null
        set fresh_type: null
    }
}

; ★★★ THE ORDER-OF-DECLARATION AUFLAGE that ast.scaly's NodeData carries applies
; here too and is stated now rather than when it bites: the tag IS the jump-table
; index, and AArch64 needs ≥14 contiguous tags per dispatch level. Five arms are
; far below that, so every `choose` over this union lowers to a compare chain
; today — which is correct and cheap at five. The arm that makes the union big
; enough to care is the one that must re-read the root CLAUDE.md's `Insn` note.
; One row of c.declaredTypeLinks. `interface_checked` is checkInterfaceDeclaration's
; own once-bit and lives here because the reference keeps it in the same links
; record — two questions about one symbol, asked from one place.
;
; ★★★ `index_signatures_checked` ARRIVES IN SLICE 77 AND RETIRES A NOTE SLICE 73
; LEFT STANDING. That slice called the enum's `enumChecked` bit *"a PER-SYMBOL side
; table this port has none of"* — true when it was written and false by the time
; slice 77 read it, because this table landed for `declared_type` in between and
; already carries two once-bits. **A blocker recorded as a property of the port
; rather than of a slice does not expire on its own**; the enum row is still open,
; but not for the reason written down.
define DeclaredTypeLink
(
    symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    declared_type: ref[Type]?
    interface_checked: bool

    ; links.indexSignaturesChecked — checkClassOrInterfaceForDuplicateIndexSignatures'
    ; ONLY-CHECK-ONCE bit. It is a REPORT memo and not a work memo, which is what
    ; makes it load-bearing rather than an optimisation: a merged interface has two
    ; declarations, both arms call the function, and diagcheck's relation is a
    ; SUBSEQUENCE — so a second visit's duplicate line is a failure. Same argument
    ; as ValueSymbolLink.function_or_constructor_checked, one dimension over.
    index_signatures_checked: bool

    ; links.typeParametersChecked — checkTypeParameterListsIdentical's
    ; ONLY-CHECK-ONCE bit, the third of exactly the same shape as the two above and
    ; load-bearing for the same reason: the function is called once per class-like
    ; DECLARATION and a merged interface has several, so without the memo TS2428
    ; would be reported once per declaration per declaration.
    type_parameters_checked: bool

    ; ★★★ `data.resolvedBaseConstructorType` AS A REPORT MEMO — slice 104, and the
    ; defect it closes is one only STAGE 2 could see. Upstream the field holds the
    ; base constructor TYPE, and its first job is the one every memo in this record
    ; has: `getBaseConstructorTypeOfClass` is called from six places here, and the
    ; second caller must not re-run `checkExpression(baseTypeNode.Expression())`.
    ; This port cannot hold the type — the line after that call is the
    ; `is-constructor-type` stop — so it holds the BIT, exactly as
    ; index_signatures_checked and ValueSymbolLink.function_or_constructor_checked
    ; do, and for the same reason: diagcheck's relation is a SUBSEQUENCE, so a
    ; second visit's duplicate line is a failure.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS KEYED ON THE SYMBOL WHERE UPSTREAM'S IS KEYED ON THE TYPE, which is
    ; the same key for a class: the type is `getDeclaredTypeOfSymbol(symbol)` and
    ; this record is that memo's own row.
    base_constructor_checked: bool

    ; ★★★ `links.enumChecked` — checkEnumDeclaration's per-SYMBOL once-bit, and it
    ; arrives in slice 116 to close a note this record's own header names. The two
    ; reports it gates (TS2473, TS2432) are about a MERGED enum, so the question is
    ; *has this symbol been checked* and not *is this the first declaration*; the
    ; merged-declaration head one screen up needs no table because its once-guard
    ; is a node identity test (GetDeclarationOfKind), which is exactly the
    ; difference.
    enum_checked: bool
)
{
    init(sym: ref[Symbol]?)
    {
        set symbol: sym
        set declared_type: null
        set interface_checked: false
        set index_signatures_checked: false
        set type_parameters_checked: false
        set base_constructor_checked: false
        set enum_checked: false
    }
}

; One row of c.flowTypeCache (slice 120) — see get_type_of_expression's header
; for why this memo is OBSERVABLE and not an optimisation.
define FlowTypeCacheLink
(
    node: ref[AstNode]?
    t: ref[Type]?
)

; The reference's `TypeDiscriminator`, as a record with a kind (slice 146) — see
; DiscriminatorKindRelation for the argument.
;
; ★★ THE OBJECT-LITERAL FORM CARRIES TWO LISTS AND THE INDEX SPANS BOTH, which is
; upstream's own `name(index)` / `matches(index, t)`: indices below
; `nodes.length` are the literal's own discriminant PROPERTIES and the rest are
; the contextual type's OPTIONAL members the literal does NOT carry — those match
; only `undefined`, which is what makes a missing discriminant exclude a
; constituent that requires one.
define Discriminator
(
    kind: int
    ; the RELATION form: the source type's discriminant property symbols, and the
    ; relation the caller is asking under.
    props: ref[Array[ref[Symbol]?]]?
    comparer_relation: int
    ; the OBJECT-LITERAL / JSX form.
    nodes: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    members: ref[Array[ref[Symbol]?]]?
)

; One row of c.discriminatedContextualTypes (slice 146) — keyed on the PAIR
; (node, contextual type), which is upstream's `DiscriminatedContextualTypeKey`.
;
; ★ The memo is not an optimisation: `discriminateTypeByDiscriminableItems` mints
; a filtered UNION, and two calls for one literal would produce two unions that
; are structurally equal and not pointer-equal — the same argument
; RegularObjectLiteralTypeLink's header makes.
define DiscriminatedContextualTypeLink
(
    node: ref[AstNode]?
    contextual: ref[Type]?
    discriminated: ref[Type]?
)

; One row of c.contextFreeTypes (slice 141) — FlowTypeCacheLink's shape at a
; second per-node memo.
;
; ★★★ TWO READERS ASK IT DIFFERENTLY UPSTREAM and this port collapses them, on
; the reading get_type_of_expression's cache already established:
; getContextFreeTypeOfExpression asks `cached != nil` while
; checkFunctionExpressionOrObjectLiteralMethod asks `cached, ok :=` — PRESENCE —
; and the difference would matter only for a row holding nil. Here a null answer
; means the check STOPPED, and a cached stop would answer a later ask without
; reporting one, so nothing null is ever put in and the two questions have one
; answer.
define ContextFreeTypeLink
(
    node: ref[AstNode]?
    t: ref[Type]?
)

; ── the FLOW REFERENCE KEY's part, slice 141 ────────────────────────────────
;
; `getFlowReferenceKey` + `writeFlowCacheKey` build the key of the flow-loop
; cache, and the reference's own comment says what it is: *the id of the symbol
; referenced by the leftmost identifier followed by zero or more property names
; separated by dots. The result is nonDottedNameCacheKey if the reference isn't a
; dotted name.*
;
; ★★★ THE KEY IS upstream's: the parts are written into a byte buffer in the
; order `writeFlowCacheKey` writes them and the 128-bit xxh3 of the buffer is the
; key (`keyBuilder.hash`, `Xxh3.hash128` being the port of the same Sum128). A
; symbol, a type and a node are written as their ADDRESS — the pointer is the
; identity upstream's ids stand for, and unlike an id it needs no counter.
;
; ★ Until slice 353 the port kept the PARTS as records and compared them one by
; one, on two arguments that have both expired: a symbol id was unique only per
; FILE (§3.10) — an address has no such boundary — and a structural comparison
; cannot collide where a hash can, which is the risk upstream accepts at 128 bits.
; The parts cost ~300 bytes a key (a 64-byte record per part plus its list) where
; the hash costs 16: 86 MB of the checker's region over the whole of VS Code. The
; buffer is the key-building function's own and goes back with its frame; a text
; part is consumed as it is written, so no key holds a view of anybody's bytes.

; One row of c.flowLoopCache — the reference's `FlowLoopKey{flowNode, refKey}`
; against the type computed for it. ★A row is written only where the loop
; junction COMPLETED; an in-process junction lives on the stack below instead.
define FlowLoopCacheLink
(
    flow: ref[FlowNode]?
    key: ref[Xxh3]?
    t: ref[Type]?
    ; the next row of the SAME flow node, in insertion order
    next_same_flow: ref[FlowLoopCacheLink]?
)

; c.flowLoopStack — the junctions currently being analyzed, with the antecedent
; types gathered so far.
;
; ★★★ IT IS A CONS LIST FOR ReduceLabelStack's REASON — upstream pushes before a
; recursion and truncates after it, and `Array` here has `add` and `clear` and
; nothing between them. A cons list pushes by minting a head and pops by USING
; THE OLD HEAD in the continuation.
;
; ★★★ AND `types` IS SHARED WITH THE CALLER'S LIST ON PURPOSE, which is the one
; place §3.5fs's rule does not apply: upstream pushes the SLICE
; `antecedentTypes`, keeps appending to the caller's variable, and the append
; happens only BETWEEN recursions — so during the recursion that reads the stack
; the length is fixed and a shared reference is exactly the snapshot Go took.
define FlowLoopInfo
(
    flow: ref[FlowNode]?
    key: ref[Xxh3]?
    types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    next: ref[FlowLoopInfo]?
)

; One row of c.cachedTypes at CachedTypeKindArrayLiteralType (slice 120) —
; WidenedTypeLink's idiom at the second kind, see create_array_literal_type.
define ArrayLiteralTypeLink
(
    source: ref[Type]?
    literal: ref[Type]?
)
{
    init(s: ref[Type]?)
    {
        set source: s
        set literal: null
    }
}

; One row of c.cachedTypes at CachedTypeKindEvolvingArrayType (slice 141) —
; ArrayLiteralTypeLink's idiom at the third kind this port reads. See
; get_evolving_array_type for why the memo is load-bearing rather than a saving.
define EvolvingArrayTypeLink
(
    source: ref[Type]?
    evolving: ref[Type]?
)
{
    init(s: ref[Type]?)
    {
        set source: s
        set evolving: null
    }
}

; One row of c.cachedTypes at CachedTypeKindWidened — see get_widened_type's
; header for why that memo is load-bearing here and why ONE kind of the
; reference's two-part key is one table rather than a general one.
define WidenedTypeLink
(
    source: ref[Type]?
    widened: ref[Type]?
)
{
    init(s: ref[Type]?)
    {
        set source: s
        set widened: null
    }
}

; One row of c.cachedTypes at CachedTypeKindRegularObjectLiteral (slice 146) —
; WidenedTypeLink's idiom at the sixth kind this port reads. The memo is the
; reference's own and it is not an optimisation here either: the regular twin is a
; FRESH anonymous type, so re-asking would mint a second one and two structurally
; equal object types that are not pointer-equal break every identity test
; downstream of them.
define RegularObjectLiteralTypeLink
(
    source: ref[Type]?
    regular: ref[Type]?
)
{
    init(s: ref[Type]?)
    {
        set source: s
        set regular: null
    }
}

; One row of c.cachedTypes at CachedTypeKindApparentType (slice 155) —
; WidenedTypeLink's idiom at the seventh kind this port reads.
;
; ★★★ THE KEY IS THE `this` ARGUMENT AND NOT THE INTERSECTION, which reads as a
; transcription error and is not one: upstream's key is
; `CachedTypeKey{kind: CachedTypeKindApparentType, typeId: thisArgument.id}` and
; the intersection `t` is nowhere in it. The row is therefore written as the
; reference wrote it — `source` is the thisArgument — because a port that
; "corrects" a cache key answers a different program on the input where the two
; disagree, and this one has never been observed to disagree.
define ApparentTypeLink
(
    source: ref[Type]?
    apparent: ref[Type]?
)
{
    init(s: ref[Type]?)
    {
        set source: s
        set apparent: null
    }
}

; THREE rows of c.cachedTypes on ONE record (slice 151) —
; CachedTypeKindPermissiveInstantiation, CachedTypeKindRestrictiveInstantiation and
; CachedTypeKindRestrictiveTypeParameter, all keyed on the same type id.
;
; ★★★ UPSTREAM'S KEY IS (kind, typeId) OVER ONE MAP, SO THREE KINDS ON ONE ROW IS
; THE SAME ASSOCIATION AND NOT A MERGE. WidenedTypeLink's idiom keeps one table per
; kind because each kind's table has a different reader; these three are read by
; one chapter, the scan is linear, and three scans of one list is three times the
; work for an identical answer. ★The slots are disjoint by construction anyway: a
; type parameter is the only thing that ever fills the third, and a type parameter's
; permissive instantiation is the wildcard rather than a row.
;
; ★★★ THE RESTRICTIVE SLOT IS WRITTEN TWICE PER ASK AND THE SECOND WRITE IS THE
; OBSERVABLE ONE. Upstream sets `cachedTypes[{RestrictiveInstantiation, result.id}]
; = result` after caching the answer, so a restrictive instance IS its own
; restrictive instantiation — and its own comment says what that row is FOR: it is
; how a comparison DETECTS a restrictive instance and disables the distributive
; constraint check for it, which would otherwise be *distinctly unsafe*. The reader
; is get_constraint_of_distributive_conditional_type, whose second disjunct asks
; exactly that row.
define InstantiationCacheLink
(
    source: ref[Type]?
    permissive: ref[Type]?
    restrictive: ref[Type]?
    restrictive_type_parameter: ref[Type]?
)
{
    init(s: ref[Type]?)
    {
        set source: s
        set permissive: null
        set restrictive: null
        set restrictive_type_parameter: null
    }
}

; TWO rows of c.cachedTypes on ONE record (slice 166) —
; CachedTypeKindIndexedAccessForReading and CachedTypeKindIndexedAccessForWriting,
; both keyed on the same type id, which is InstantiationCacheLink's argument at a
; smaller key: upstream reads the pair out of one map under two kinds, and one
; linear scan answering both is the same association rather than a merge.
;
; ★★★ THE MEMO IS OBSERVABLE AND IT IS ALSO THE RECURSION BRAKE. The reference
; writes `t` into the slot BEFORE calling the worker and only overwrites it when
; the worker answered something else, so a simplification that re-enters itself
; on the same type sees the identity and terminates — `{ [P in T]: { [Q in U]:
; number } }[T][U]` is the reference's own example of a nested access and the
; worker simplifies the inner one first.
;
; ★★★ AND THE SLOT IS CLEARED WHEN THE WORKER STOPS, which is a deliberate
; divergence and the only one in this chapter. Upstream a worker always answers,
; so the in-progress `t` either stays (nothing simplified) or is overwritten; here
; a stop answers null and would LEAVE the `t` behind — and the next ask of that
; same type would then read the identity out of the cache and record no stop at
; all. That is a silent wrong answer standing in for a wall, which is the one
; thing this port may not trade a memo for.
; One row of the relation's result cache: the reference's
; `map[CacheHashKey]RelationComparisonResult`, one map per relation, as ONE table
; whose key carries the relation. `key` is get_relation_key's hash; the relation is
; one of five ids and the result a bit set below 1 << 7, so the row is 16 bytes.
; It was six ints (48 bytes) with a stored hash and a source/target/state triple
; that every writer had filled with (key, 0, 0) since slice 216 — 126 MB of the
; checker's region over the whole of VS Code (slice 352, the census).
define RelationCacheRow
(
    key: int
    rel: u8
    result: u8
)

define SimplifiedIndexedAccessLink
(
    source: ref[Type]?
    for_reading: ref[Type]?
    for_writing: ref[Type]?
)
{
    init(s: ref[Type]?)
    {
        set source: s
        set for_reading: null
        set for_writing: null
    }
}

; ast.SyntheticExpression's payload, held BESIDE the node rather than in it
; (slice 134).
;
; ★★★ THE SLOT UPSTREAM IS `Type any` AND THAT IS WHY IT IS A SIDE TABLE HERE.
; NodeData lives in `ast.scaly`, which is declared BEFORE `checker.scaly`, so an
; arm carrying a `ref[Type]` would put a checker concept into the ast module's
; union — and the reference itself does not type that slot either, it holds an
; `any` and casts at each of its four readers. A table keyed on the synthetic
; NODE says the same thing without the module edge, and it is the idiom this file
; already uses eight times over.
;
; ★ `tuple_name_source` is declared and never written: its only writer upstream is
; getEffectiveCallArguments' SPREAD arm, which is a stop in this port
; (`get-effective-call-arguments-spread`), and its only reader is the tuple
; element's `labeledDeclaration`. It is declared rather than named because the
; record is the reference's node and a missing field would read as a slot nobody
; needed.
define SyntheticExpressionLink
(
    node: ref[AstNode]?
    resolved_type: ref[Type]?
    is_spread: bool
    tuple_name_source: ref[AstNode]?
)
{
    init(n: ref[AstNode]?, t: ref[Type]?, spread: bool, source: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        set node: n
        set resolved_type: t
        set is_spread: spread
        set tuple_name_source: source
    }
}

; One row of c.cachedTypes at the four DecoratorContext kinds (slice 134) —
; WidenedTypeLink's idiom at a THREE-part key.
;
; ★★ THE KEY IS THE REFERENCE'S OWN, spelled out. Upstream it is
; `CachedTypeKey{kind, typeId}` where the four kinds encode the two booleans;
; here the booleans are two columns beside the name type, which is the same key
; with the encoding removed. ★The memo is OBSERVABLE and not an optimisation: the
; override is an anonymous type intersected into every member decorator's context
; type, and two objects for one member would print as two intersections.
define DecoratorContextOverrideLink
(
    name_type: ref[Type]?
    is_private: bool
    is_static: bool
    override_type: ref[Type]?
)
{
    init(n: ref[Type]?, priv: bool, stat: bool)
    {
        set name_type: n
        set is_private: priv
        set is_static: stat
        set override_type: null
    }
}

; checker.WideningContext, whole since slice 154.
;
; ★★★ IT STOOD AS ONE SLOT AND FOUR NAMES, AND THE NOTE WAS RIGHT ON THE DAY.
; It read *four of its five slots are named rather than declared … `parent`,
; `propertyName`, `resolvedProperties`, `childContexts` and the `widenedTypes` memo
; are read by getSiblingsOfContext, getPropertiesOfContext and getChildContext —
; all three behind ONE stop in get_widened_type_of_object_literal — so a slot for
; them is a slot no instrument in this suite can reach*. That stop was the
; frontier's fourth row when this slice opened (+41 units over 55 arrivals), which
; is what a §3.5be name costs when the thing it defers is a chapter and not a line.
;
; ★★ THE TWO MAPS ARE LISTS, and the population is why rather than laziness:
; `childContexts` is keyed by the property names of ONE object literal and
; `widenedTypes` by the types widened under ONE child context, so both are a
; handful of entries and a linear scan is the whole implementation — the same
; shape TypeInstantiation's list already takes for the reference's hashed map.
; ★`widened_types` is written only in a context that HAS a parent, which is the
; reference's own condition and not a size argument: the root context never widens
; one object literal type twice.
define WideningChild
(
    name: Slice[char]
    context: ref[WideningContext]
)

define WidenedTypeEntry
(
    source: ref[Type]
    widened: ref[Type]
)

define WideningContext
(
    parent: ref[WideningContext]?
    property_name: Slice[char]
    siblings: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    resolved_properties: ref[Array[ref[Symbol]?]]?
    child_contexts: ref[Array[ref[WideningChild]?]]?
    widened_types: ref[Array[ref[WidenedTypeEntry]?]]?
)
{
    ; The root context — the union arm's `&WideningContext{siblings: types}`.
    init(types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?)
    {
        set parent: null
        set property_name: Slice[char]()
        set siblings: types
        set resolved_properties: null
        set child_contexts: null
        set widened_types: null
    }

    ; getChildContext's `&WideningContext{parent: w, propertyName: propertyName}`.
    init(p: ref[WideningContext], name: Slice[char])
    {
        set parent: p
        set property_name: name
        set siblings: null
        set resolved_properties: null
        set child_contexts: null
        set widened_types: null
    }
}

; checkAndAggregateReturnExpressionTypes' three locals, in one record because the
; visitor writes all three and the walk carries them down. Upstream they are
; captured by the closure; here the closure does not survive (§3.9d) and the
; state has to travel as an argument.
define ReturnAggregate
(
    types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
    has_no_expression: bool
    has_never: bool
)
{
    init(ts: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]])
    {
        set types: ts
        set has_no_expression: false
        set has_never: false
    }
}

; One row of c.valueSymbolLinks — the OTHER per-symbol links table, and the one
; getTypeOfSymbol memoises into. It is a second table rather than three more
; slots on DeclaredTypeLink because the reference keys them separately and the
; two answer different questions: a class symbol has BOTH a declared type (its
; instance side) and a resolved type (its static side), and conflating them is
; the shape of a bug rather than a saving.
;
; ★ NOT CARRIED, with its reader: `writeType` (getWriteTypeOfSymbol) and
; `nameType` (the late-bound symbol's own name, read by symbolToString).
;
; ★★★ `target` AND `mapper` ARRIVED IN SLICE 86 and the note above them used to
; say they belonged to *"one of the four CheckFlags arms this port cannot
; represent at all"*. instantiate_symbol mints a transient symbol now, so the arm
; is representable and is written; the other three CheckFlags arms (DeferredType,
; Mapped, ReverseMapped) still have no writer and get_type_of_symbol still leaves
; them out rather than reporting.
define ValueSymbolLink
(
    resolved_type: ref[Type]?

    ; getTypeOfInstantiatedSymbol's pair: the symbol this one was instantiated
    ; FROM, and the mapper it was instantiated through.
    target: ref[Symbol]?
    mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?

    ; links.functionOrConstructorChecked — the ONLY-CHECK-ONCE bit of
    ; checkFunctionOrConstructorSymbol (slice 68). It is a report memo rather than a
    ; work memo, which is what makes it load-bearing: the two call sites can reach
    ; one symbol, and diagcheck's relation is a SUBSEQUENCE, so a second visit's
    ; duplicate line is a failure.
    function_or_constructor_checked: bool

    ; ── the four slots the SYNTHESIZED property needs (slice 125) ───────────
    ;
    ; `containingType` and `nameType` are the reference's own two remaining
    ; valueSymbolLinks slots; `keyType` and `syntheticOrigin` are its SEPARATE
    ; `mappedSymbolLinks` table, merged in here.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE MERGE IS A DIVERGENCE AND IT IS BOUNDED BY ONE QUESTION. Upstream
    ; keeps mappedSymbolLinks apart so that `mappedSymbolLinks.Has(symbol)` can
    ; ask *is this a mapped property at all* — and both readers of that question
    ; are in `emitresolver.go` and `services.go`, neither of which this port
    ; builds. Every reader that IS ported asks `CheckFlagsMapped` instead, which
    ; is set on the same symbols by the same line. A second table would be a
    ; second lookup for a question nothing here asks.
    ;
    ; ★★ `name_type` RETIRES A NOTE ON get_literal_type_from_property that read
    ; *"the slot has no producer here and is not added for one reader"*. True
    ; until this slice: resolve_mapped_type_members writes it for every property
    ; it synthesizes, and the union of two enum members' name types is the shape
    ; that makes it observable.
    containing_type: ref[Type]?
    name_type: ref[Type]?
    ; the reference's SEPARATE mapped, deferred and reverse-mapped symbol links
    ; and the unique-ES-symbol memo, one record made on the first write (slice
    ; 322): 7.45 million value links on ganz VS Code, the nine slots empty on
    ; almost all of them
    extra: ref[ValueSymbolExtra]?

    ; ── slice 146: c.uniqueESSymbolTypes ────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; getESSymbolLikeTypeForNode's memo — upstream a `map[*ast.Symbol]*Type` of
    ; its own, merged here on the same test the four slots above passed: ONE
    ; reader and ONE writer, both inside that function, and nothing anywhere asks
    ; `Has(symbol)`. See get_es_symbol_like_type_for_node's header.

    ; ── slice 127: the SYNTHESIZED property's two remaining slots ───────────
    ;
    ; `writeType` is valueSymbolLinks'; the three `deferred*` are the reference's
    ; SEPARATE `deferredSymbolLinks` table, merged here on §3.22's test — its only
    ; question is *what are this symbol's constituents*, asked behind
    ; `CheckFlagsDeferredType`, and nothing ported asks `Has`.
    write_type: ref[Type]?

    ; ── slice 160: the REVERSE-MAPPED property's three slots ────────────────
    ;
    ; The reference's SEPARATE `ReverseMappedSymbolLinks` table, merged here on
    ; the same test the mapped four and the deferred three passed (§3.22): its
    ; only question is *what does this property have to be inferred back through*,
    ; asked behind `CheckFlagsReverseMapped`, and the one place upstream that asks
    ; `Has` is `emitresolver.go`, which this port does not build.
)

; ValueSymbolLink's rarely written half: mappedSymbolLinks' two slots, the
; uniqueESSymbolTypes memo, deferredSymbolLinks' three and
; ReverseMappedSymbolLinks' three
define ValueSymbolExtra
(
    key_type: ref[Type]?
    synthetic_origin: ref[Symbol]?
    unique_es_symbol_type: ref[Type]?
    deferred_parent: ref[Type]?
    deferred_constituents: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    deferred_write_constituents: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    reverse_property_type: ref[Type]?
    reverse_mapped_type: ref[Type]?
    reverse_constraint_type: ref[Type]?
)

; One row of c.typeAliasLinks — slice 105, and it is a table of its own rather
; than two more slots on DeclaredTypeLink because the reference keys it
; separately and the two answer different questions. A class symbol has a
; DECLARED type in one and nothing in the other; a type-alias symbol has its
; declared type HERE, and `typeResolutionHasProperty`'s DeclaredType arm reads
; this row and not that one.
;
; ★★★ `instantiations` IS CARRIED SINCE SLICE 125 AND THE NOTE THAT STOOD HERE
; NAMED THAT SLICE. It read *"its only reader anywhere is getTypeAliasInstantiation,
; which is this slice's stop … it arrives with the slice that instantiates"* —
; this is that slice. ★It borrows ObjectInstantiation for its rows: the alias key
; is (typeArguments, alias) where the object key is (typeArguments, alias,
; singleSignature), so the third field is written false here and read by nothing.
;
; ★ `isConstructorDeclaredProperty` is the reference's fourth slot and belongs to
; a JavaScript expando chapter that has no writer here either.
define TypeAliasLink
(
    symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    declared_type: ref[Type]?

    ; links.typeParameters — *undefined if non-generic* upstream, and an empty
    ; list is the same statement here. It is the slot getTypeFromTypeAliasReference
    ; forks on, which is why it is carried and `instantiations` is not: this one
    ; has a reader in this slice.
    type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?

    ; links.instantiations, slice 125 — see the note above the record.
    instantiations: ref[Array[ref[ObjectInstantiation]?]]?
)
{
    init(sym: ref[Symbol]?)
    {
        set symbol: sym
        set declared_type: null
        set type_parameters: null
        set instantiations: null
    }
}

; One row of c.varianceLinks — slice 130, and it is a table of its own for the
; reason the reference keys it separately: the variance of a generic type's type
; parameters is a property of the SYMBOL, not of any one instantiation of it.
;
; ★★★ `computing` IS THE RECURSION GUARD AND IT IS THE REASON THE EMPTY LIST MAY
; NOT DOUBLE AS *not measured*. The reference writes `links.variances = []` BEFORE
; the loop and overwrites it after, so a re-entrant call — which is the ordinary
; case, since measuring a variance compares instantiations of the very type being
; measured — reads the empty list and returns it. That empty answer is what makes
; `typeArgumentsRelatedTo` default to covariance for the recursive case, which is
; the reference's documented intent. Here the empty list is spelled as a filled
; row whose `variances` is an EMPTY array, and `computing` records that the row is
; the guard rather than a measurement, so `get_variances`' null (*could not
; measure*) stays a third answer distinct from both.
define VarianceLink
(
    symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    variances: ref[Array[int]]?
    computing: bool
)
{
    init(sym: ref[Symbol]?)
    {
        set symbol: sym
        set variances: null
        set computing: false
    }
}

; One row of c.typeNodeLinks — the first per-NODE links table in this file, and
; the key is what makes it a third table rather than a third slot: the two above
; are keyed on a SYMBOL, and a type literal's type belongs to the NODE. Two
; `{ a: number }` annotations in one file are two types, and the reference's
; `typeNodeLinks.Get(node)` is what says so.
;
; ★ ONE SLOT of the reference's row, per §3.5be. `resolvedSymbol`,
; `resolvedSignature`, `outerTypeParameters` and the rest are read by functions
; that report today, so they arrive with their readers.
;
; ★★ THE SCAN IS LINEAR, like its two neighbours, and the cost is a property of
; ONE FILE rather than of the corpus: the table holds one row per type literal,
; function type and constructor type of the unit being checked, and a fresh
; Checker is built per file.
; One row of c.assertionLinks (slice 120) — the EXPRESSION type checkAssertion
; computed, read back by checkAssertionDeferred. It is a table of its own because
; the reference keys it separately and because the slot means something different
; from TypeNodeLink's `resolved_type`: that one is the assertion's own answer (the
; TARGET type), this one is what was asserted FROM.
define AssertionLink
(
    node: ref[AstNode]?
    expr_type: ref[Type]?
)
{
    init(n: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        set node: n
        set expr_type: null
    }
}

; One row of c.mergedSymbols (slice 137) — the table `get_merged_symbol`'s own
; header has been owing since slice 113, and this is the slice that fills it.
;
; ★★★ THE KEY IS THE **SOURCE** AND THE VALUE IS THE TARGET, which is the one thing
; about this table that reads backwards: `recordMergedSymbol(target, source)` writes
; `mergedSymbols[source] = target`, so the row says *whoever still holds the symbol
; that was merged AWAY must be handed the survivor*. A row keyed the other way round
; would be an identity map with extra steps.
define MergedSymbolLink
(
    source: ref[Symbol]?
    target: ref[Symbol]?
)
{
    init(s: ref[Symbol]?, t: ref[Symbol]?)
    {
        set source: s
        set target: t
    }
}

define TypeNodeLink
(
    resolved_type: ref[Type]?
    expression_resolved: bool
    expression_stopped: bool

    ; links.outerTypeParameters (slice 125) — getObjectTypeInstantiation's memo of
    ; the type parameters in scope at a declaration. ★★It is a THIRD sentinel
    ; problem of the kind the note below describes and it is solved the same way:
    ; the reference's `nil` means *not computed* and an EMPTY list is a real
    ; answer, so the bit beside it is what says the walk has run.
    outer_type_parameters_computed: bool
    outer_type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
)

; ★★★ THE SECOND SLOT IS A PORT-LOCAL BIT AND SLICE 88 PAID FOR IT AT STAGE 2.
; Upstream `resolvedType == nil` means exactly *not computed*, because
; checkExpressionEx always answers a type. **Here nil has a SECOND meaning — a
; stop — so a node whose check stopped stayed uncached, and every node asked twice
; re-ran its check and RE-EMITTED every diagnostic it had raised before the stop.**
; Two declarations of one variable are enough: `var co2 = (3, co2); var co2: any;`
; answered TWO TS2695 where the reference answers one, on two units of the stage-2
; corpus and none of stage 1. ★The bit says *this expression has been checked*,
; which is the question the reference asks with its nil; the nil TEST is kept in
; front of it so that where the reference's memo answers, this answers identically.
; ★★A port that gives a memo's sentinel a second meaning owes it a second slot —
; otherwise the memo silently stops being a memo for exactly the inputs that
; needed it.

; One row of c.nodeLinks (slice 83) — the reference's OTHER per-node table, and it
; is a fourth table rather than a slot on TypeNodeLink because upstream keeps the
; two apart: `typeNodeLinks` is read by getTypeFromTypeNode and `nodeLinks` by the
; checker's own once-bits, and a row of one is never asked for the other's answer.
;
; ★★ THE FLAGS WORD IS ONE NAMED BOOLEAN, which is declared_type_links'
; `index_signatures_checked` convention. NodeCheckFlags has thirty bits upstream;
; this port carries none of them, and the only one anything here asks for is
; NodeCheckFlagsTypeChecked on an accessor's GETTER. A word would be thirty
; promises where the file can keep one.
define NodeLink
(
    type_checked: bool
    ; NodeCheckFlagsContextChecked (slice 100). The SECOND named boolean, on the
    ; convention above: contextuallyCheckFunctionExpressionOrObjectLiteralMethod
    ; reads and writes it twice in one call, and the reference's own comment says
    ; why the second read is not redundant.
    context_checked: bool

    ; NodeCheckFlagsEnumValuesComputed (slice 116) — the THIRD named boolean, and
    ; the first one whose job is to run a LOOP once rather than a report.
    ; computeEnumMemberValues is reached from two sides (checkEnumDeclaration
    ; walks the members, getEnumMemberValue asks for one), so without it the
    ; auto-numbering would run again per member and every report inside it would
    ; be duplicated — and diagcheck's relation is a SUBSEQUENCE, so a second
    ; visit's line is a failure.
    enum_values_computed: bool

    ; NodeCheckFlagsAssignmentsMarked (slice 123) — the FOURTH named boolean, on
    ; the same convention, and it guards a WALK the way the one above it does:
    ; markNodeAssignments runs once per function or source file and records the
    ; last assignment position of every parameter and mutable local under it.
    assignments_marked: bool

    ; NodeCheckFlagsInCheckIdentifier (slice 150) — the FIFTH named boolean, and
    ; the first RE-ENTRANCY guard rather than a once-bit. getNarrowedTypeOfSymbol
    ; narrows a binding PATTERN as a pseudo-reference, and the flow walk under it
    ; asks the checker for the types of the very binding elements it is narrowing;
    ; the bit is set on the pattern's parent for the duration and cleared after, so
    ; the recursion falls through to the symbol's own type instead of looping.
    ; ★It is CLEARED at the end of the arm and not left standing, which is what
    ; makes it a guard: the same declaration may legitimately be narrowed again at
    ; another location.
    in_check_identifier: bool

    ; NodeCheckFlagsInitializerIsUndefinedComputed and …IsUndefined (slice 175) —
    ; the SIXTH and SEVENTH named booleans, and the first PAIR: upstream writes
    ; both bits in one word, the computed one to say the question has been asked
    ; and the other to carry the answer. They are a pair because the answer FALSE
    ; and *not yet asked* are different states and one bit cannot hold both.
    initializer_is_undefined_computed: bool
    initializer_is_undefined: bool

    ; c.reportedUnreachableNodes (slice 184) — the EIGHTH named boolean, and
    ; upstream it is a `collections.Set[*ast.Node]` rather than a bit on the links;
    ; it is transcribed onto this table because the question it answers is keyed on
    ; a node and this is the node-keyed table.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS LOAD-BEARING FOR DIAGNOSTIC COUNTS AND NOT FOR THE REPORT IT
    ; GUARDS. The report it guards is TS7027, which is a SUGGESTION under this
    ; oracle's options and therefore invisible to every yardstick (measured: 0 of
    ; 64 148 reference C lines carry 7027). What the bit decides is whether
    ; `is_source_element_unreachable` is ASKED at all — the `Has(node)` test sits
    ; in FRONT of it — and that question runs the whole reachability walk, which
    ; reports through get_effects_signature. So dropping the set would have added
    ; reachability walks, not removed reports.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference CLEARS the set at the end of checkSourceFile. There is
    ; nothing to clear here: this port builds a Checker per unit and checks one
    ; file, so the table dies with it. Named rather than omitted, because the day a
    ; second file is checked through one Checker the clear becomes load-bearing.
    reported_unreachable: bool

    ; NodeCheckFlagsContainsClassWithPrivateIdentifiers and
    ; …ContainsSuperPropertyInStaticInitializer (slice 258): the markers the two
    ; generated-code collision checks read off a block-scope container
    contains_class_with_private_identifiers: bool
    contains_super_property_in_static_initializer: bool
    ; `declarationLinks.isVisible` (slice 362): 0 unknown, 1 visible, 2 not. It stood
    ; on the parser's node (NodeExtras) until parallel checkers shared the tree;
    ; the reference keeps it per resolver
    decl_visible: u8
)

; One row of c.markedAssignmentSymbolLinks (slice 123), keyed on the SYMBOL.
;
; ★ `last_assignment_pos` is 0 for *never assigned* and MARKED_ASSIGNMENT_MAX for
; *assigned somewhere this walk cannot order* — a nested function, or an export
; specifier. Both are the reference's sentinels and both are compared, never
; arithmetic.
define MARKED_ASSIGNMENT_MAX: int 2147483647

define MarkedAssignmentLink
(
    symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    last_assignment_pos: int
    has_definite_assignment: bool
)
{
    init(s: ref[Symbol]?)
    {
        set symbol: s
        set last_assignment_pos: 0
        set has_definite_assignment: false
    }
}

; ── slice 116: the enum's VALUE, and the two records it needs ───────────────
;
; evaluator.Result. ★★★ THE REFERENCE'S `Value any` IS SLOTS PLUS A TAG HERE, and
; the tag is what makes it different from LiteralTypeData's three slots one screen
; up. That record needs no discriminator because its readers ask `Type.flags`
; first; this one has no such carrier — `computeConstantEnumMemberValue` asks
; `result.Value.(jsnum.Number)` and `.(string)` DIRECTLY, and the whole point of
; the enum chapter is that the answer decides between a numeric member, a string
; member and a computed one. So the tag is a field, and it is the type ASSERTION
; the reference writes, made explicit.
;
; ★ A Go `nil` Value is EvalKindNone and is not the same as an empty string:
; `enum E { A = "" }` is a string member and `enum E { A = f() }` is not.
define EvalKindNone:   int 0
define EvalKindString: int 1
define EvalKindNumber: int 2

define EvalResult
(
    kind: int
    ; EvalKindString: the cooked text, BORROWED for LiteralTypeData's reason — a
    ; literal's bytes live on the page the parse owns. The one exception is a
    ; TEMPLATE, whose text is assembled and therefore allocated; see
    ; evaluate_template_expression.
    text: Slice[char]
    ; EvalKindNumber: the IEEE-754 bits, the representation jsnum uses.
    bits: u64
    is_syntactically_string: bool
    resolved_other_files: bool
    has_external_references: bool
)

; ── slice 128: IterationTypes and the resolver ──────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's `IterationTypes` struct — the *yield*, *return* and *next* type
; of one iterable or iterator — returned BY VALUE the way EvalResult is, for the
; same reason: three borrowed slots and no identity of its own. The reference's
; `noIterationTypes` sentinel is the zero value, i.e. all three null, and
; `has_types` is its test.
;
; ★★★ ALL THREE SLOTS ARE NULLABLE AND THE NULLS MEAN DIFFERENT THINGS. All three
; null is *could not find the iteration types* — the sentinel every caller forks
; on. A SINGLE null slot is *this kind was not resolvable*, which
; getIterationTypesOfIteratorResult produces on purpose (a yield result has no
; return type) and which getType hands straight back to the caller. So a reader
; must not collapse the two, and `has_types` is the only test that separates them.
define IterationTypes
(
    yield_type: ref[Type]?
    return_type: ref[Type]?
    next_type: ref[Type]?
)

; One row of c.iterationTypesCache, whose upstream key is the STRUCT
; `IterationTypesKey{typeId, use}`. Here the type half of the key is the type
; POINTER for declared_type_links' reason (a Type.id is drawn lazily and its
; numbers are observable), and the use half is the masked `use` — see
; IterationUseCacheFlags.
;
; ★★★ THE ROW HOLDS THE SENTINEL TOO, and that is not a detail of the encoding:
; getIterationTypesOfIterable caches a FAILURE (`noIterationTypes`) and then reads
; it back with `cached.hasTypes()` to decide whether a cached miss may be re-asked
; WITH error reporting. A table that stored only successes would re-run the whole
; resolution on every failing type and, worse, would report the diagnostic twice.
; ★★ `use` IS A DECLARATION KEYWORD (§3.5d's class, the one `operator` is in), so
; the slot cannot carry the reference's own field name — a `use: int` line makes
; the whole `define` a parse error at its FIRST field, which names neither the
; line nor the word. It is `iteration_use` here and at every parameter of this
; chapter.
define IterationTypesLink
(
    source: ref[Type]
    iteration_use: int
    result: IterationTypes
)

; One row of c.cachedTypes at CachedTypeKindAwaitedType — declared_type_links'
; idiom keyed on the TYPE pointer, like widened_type_links.
;
; ★★ IT IS A MINT-ON-MISS ROW WITH A NULLABLE PAYLOAD and the distinction matters
; here more than at the widened table: getAwaitedTypeNoAliasEx caches only
; SUCCESSES (every failure path returns before the store), so an absent row and a
; row holding null cannot both occur — but the recursion writes the row for a
; UNION before its constituents are done, so the reader must not treat a present
; row as a finished answer. It does not: the store is the last statement of each
; arm.
define AwaitedTypeLink
(
    source: ref[Type]
    awaited: ref[Type]?
)
{
    init(s: ref[Type])
    {
        set source: s
        set awaited: null
    }
}

; One row of c.cachedTypes at CachedTypeKindPromisedTypeOfPromise.
define PromisedTypeLink
(
    source: ref[Type]
    promised: ref[Type]?
)
{
    init(s: ref[Type])
    {
        set source: s
        set promised: null
    }
}

; One row of c.enumLiteralTypes, whose upstream key is the STRUCT
; `EnumLiteralKey{enumSymbol, value}`. Here the value half of the key is read off
; the TYPE the row points at — a literal type carries its own text or bits — so
; the row is the enum symbol plus the type, and get_enum_literal_type's scan asks
; the two halves separately. ★That is also what collapses the reference's second
; table (see that function's header).
define EnumLiteralRow
(
    enum_symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    t: ref[Type]?
)

; One row of c.enumMemberLinks. It holds the value BY VALUE rather than a
; reference to one: EvalResult is seven scalar slots, the table is written once
; per member by the loop that computes it, and a reference would need a page to
; live on for no reader that outlives the checker.
define EnumMemberLink
(
    node: ref[AstNode]?
    value: EvalResult
)
{
    init(n: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        set node: n
        set value: EvalResult(EvalKindNone, Slice[char](), 0 as u64, false, false, false)
    }
}

; ── c.symbolNodeLinks (slice 91) ──────────────────────────────────────
;
; The links idiom a SIXTH time, keyed on the NODE, carrying the one slot this
; port has a reader for: `resolvedSymbol`.
;
; ★★★ THIS MEMO IS NOT AN OPTIMISATION AND SKIPPING IT WOULD BE A DEFECT.
; getResolvedSymbol hands resolveName a nameNotFoundMessage, so the walk it
; performs REPORTS — the four in-loop steps below it, and a stop where the name is
; not found at all. A second ask about the same identifier would report a second
; time, and a diagnostic emitted twice is exactly what diagcheck's subsequence
; relation is red on. The reference's `core.OrElse(symbol, c.unknownSymbol)` is
; what makes the slot non-nil on a miss, which is why the miss needs a symbol of
; its own rather than a second boolean.
define SymbolNodeLink
(
    resolved_symbol: ref[Symbol]?

    ; ★★★ THE STOP'S HALF OF THE MEMO, AND IT IS NOT AN OPTIMISATION — SLICE 95
    ; FOUND OUT WHAT IT COSTS AT STAGE 2. `getSymbolFromTypeReference`'s memo is
    ; UNCONDITIONAL upstream, because `resolveTypeReferenceName` cannot answer nil:
    ; a miss becomes `getUnresolvedSymbolForEntityName`, a synthetic symbol. So the
    ; resolution runs ONCE PER NODE there — and `resolveName`'s class arm REPORTS
    ; TS2302 (*static members cannot reference class type parameters*) when it is
    ; given a message. This port's miss STOPS instead, so the memo was not written
    ; and every later visit to the same node re-resolved and re-reported: **twelve
    ; units at stage 2 carried the same span five and six times over, and stage 1
    ; showed 0 differing.**
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE RULE IT LEAVES: A STOP MUST NOT CHANGE HOW MANY TIMES A REPORTING
    ; ROUTINE RUNS. Storing unknownSymbol here instead would make the second visit
    ; ANSWER where the first honestly stopped, which is the invention the stop
    ; exists to avoid; the bit records *this node has been asked and could not be
    ; finished*, which is exactly what the reference's memo records for the case it
    ; can finish.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE STOP IS REPLAYED RATHER THAN SWALLOWED, WHICH IS THE HALF THE
    ; FIRST FIX GOT WRONG. Suppressing the second visit's stop as well took stage 1
    ; from 0 differing to 32 and added 66 diagnostics: every `unported_mark()`
    ; guard in the chain above reads *did anything stop under this call*, and the
    ; repeated stop is what those guards were firing on. So the tag and the detail
    ; are kept and re-recorded, which reproduces the stop log event for event while
    ; the RESOLUTION — and its report — runs once. **A memo may remove work; it may
    ; not remove a stop.**
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHAT THE MEMOISED VISIT DOES IS MOVE THE MARK AND NOT WRITE THE LOG, AND
    ; THE PRECEDENT IS `check_expression_cached_ex` — the one caller of
    ; `replay_stop_mark`, whose note says it in the words this case needs: *a
    ; memoised nil must still MOVE the mark, because every `unported_mark` guard in
    ; this file reads "did anything stop under this call" and a cached incomplete
    ; answer is still incomplete; but it must NOT append to the log, because the log
    ; is the WORK LIST and the stop it stands for was logged the first time the node
    ; was checked.* ★What is stored is the NUMBER of stops the resolution recorded,
    ; so the mark moves by the same delta a re-run would have moved it by.
    ;
    ; ★★★ TWO DRAFTS GOT THIS WRONG AND EACH FAILED IN ITS OWN DIRECTION, WHICH IS
    ; WHY BOTH ARE WRITTEN DOWN. Swallowing the stop entirely took stage 1 from 0
    ; differing to **32** and added 66 diagnostics, because the guards above were
    ; firing on the repeat. Re-appending the log SLICE kept stage 1 green and
    ; **trapped at exit 15 on 64 stage-2 units**: the range was indexed with
    ; `unported_mark()`, which is the COUNT, and `replay_stop_mark` is the one place
    ; where the count and the log deliberately disagree — the very mechanism this
    ; fix ended up using. **A number that is nearly the right index is a bounds
    ; trap two corpora away.**
    resolution_stopped: bool
    stopped_count: int
)

; ── c.aliasSymbolLinks and c.moduleSymbolLinks (slice 110) ──────────────────
;
; The links idiom a SEVENTH and EIGHTH time, and the two are separate tables
; because the reference keys them separately and they answer about different
; symbols: an ALIAS symbol has a resolution target and a type-only marker, a
; MODULE symbol has an export table. A file's `import { x } from "m"` mints a row
; in the first; the file's own symbol gets one in the second.
;
; ★★★ `alias_target` CANNOT BE A NIL-MEANS-UNCOMPUTED MEMO HERE and the reference
; is what says so: `resolveAlias` stores `core.OrElse(target, c.unknownSymbol)`,
; so a resolution that failed is recorded AS unknownSymbol and never re-run. That
; is load-bearing rather than a saving — `getTargetOfAliasDeclaration` REPORTS
; (TS2307 for every unresolvable module specifier under this harness), and
; diagcheck's relation is a SUBSEQUENCE, so a second resolution's second TS2307
; is a failure. The same argument SymbolNodeLink's header makes for
; `resolved_symbol`.
;
; ★ `type_only_declaration` is a NODE and its nil is genuinely *not marked*:
; markSymbolOfAliasDeclarationIfTypeOnly writes it at most once and every reader
; asks only whether it is there.
define AliasSymbolLink
(
    symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    alias_target: ref[Symbol]?
    type_only_declaration: ref[AstNode]?

    ; links.referenced — markAliasSymbolAsReferenced's ONCE bit. Its product is
    ; read by the emit resolver, which this port does not have; the bit is carried
    ; because the marking WALK has reporting side effects and must not run twice.
    referenced: bool

    ; links.immediateTarget — getImmediateAliasedSymbol's memo. It is a SECOND
    ; target beside `alias_target` and not a duplicate of it: `alias_target` is the
    ; END of the chain (resolveAlias follows an alias to an alias), this one is the
    ; ONE hop the symbol's own declaration names.
    immediate_target: ref[Symbol]?
)
{
    init(sym: ref[Symbol]?)
    {
        set symbol: sym
        set alias_target: null
        set type_only_declaration: null
        set referenced: false
        set immediate_target: null
    }
}

; One row of c.moduleSymbolLinks.
;
; ★★★ `exports_checked` IS THE BIT checkExternalModuleExports GUARDS ITSELF WITH,
; and it is a report memo of exactly the kind DeclaredTypeLink's
; `index_signatures_checked` is: the function is reached once per source file
; today, but `export = x` inside a namespace reaches it a second time through
; checkExportAssignment, and its two reports would then be doubled.
;
; ★★★ `type_only_export_star_map` IS A SLOT HERE SINCE SLICE 129, AND WHAT WAS
; WRONG WITH THE NOTE THAT STOOD HERE IS ITS DIRECTION. It read *a map nothing can
; fill would make getExportOfModule's read by NAME a claim rather than a lookup*,
; and so the WRITER reported and the READ passed nil — which left `export type *`
; unwritable AND every ordinary module-member lookup stopped at the read
; (`type-only-export-star-map`, 372 units at stage 2, the group's third-largest
; row). Both halves are ported now and the asymmetry is the whole finding: the
; WRITER arrives on NO unit of the 18 436 (an `export type * from "m"` whose
; specifier resolves), while the READER is on the path of every property lookup on
; a value module. **A map that is provably empty is still a lookup; a stop in its
; place is a wall in front of everything that shares the road.**
; typeOnlyExportStarMap's row — the name an `export type * from "m"` contributed
; and the `export *` declaration that carried it. A name→NODE map, so it is not a
; SymbolTable; and it is an Array of rows rather than a hash because its whole
; population is one export-star declaration's export list.
define TypeOnlyExportStar
(
    name: Slice[char]
    node: ref[AstNode]?
)

; ExportCollisionTable's row — the per-name state extendExportSymbols collects so
; that getExportsOfModuleWorker can report TS2308. Two `export *` contributing the
; SAME name from DIFFERENT symbols is what fills `dups`.
define ExportCollision
(
    name: Slice[char]
    specifier_node: ref[AstNode]?
    dups: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
)
{
    init(d: Slice[char], sn: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        set name: d
        set specifier_node: sn
        set dups: null
    }
}

; c.membersAndExportsLinks.Get(symbol) — the pair of RESOLVED tables
; getResolvedMembersOrExportsOfSymbol memoizes. Upstream it is one row indexed by
; a two-valued MembersOrExportsResolutionKind; here it is two named slots, because
; the index's whole job is to select between `symbol.Members` and `symbol.Exports`
; and a bool at the call site says which (§3.5e's shape: the callees are static).
define MembersAndExportsLink
(
    symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    resolved_members: ref[SymbolTable]?
    resolved_exports: ref[SymbolTable]?
)
{
    init(sym: ref[Symbol]?)
    {
        set symbol: sym
        set resolved_members: null
        set resolved_exports: null
    }
}

; c.lateBoundLinks.Get(symbol).lateSymbol — the late-bound symbol a computed
; member's EARLY symbol maps to. ★It is keyed on the early symbol and written by
; addDeclarationToLateBoundSymbol, which is what makes getLateBoundSymbol's forcing
; branch observable: the force runs the container's whole table and the answer
; arrives here rather than as a return value.
define LateBoundLink
(
    symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    late_symbol: ref[Symbol]?
)
{
    init(sym: ref[Symbol]?)
    {
        set symbol: sym
        set late_symbol: null
    }
}

define ModuleSymbolLink
(
    symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    resolved_exports: ref[SymbolTable]?
    type_only_export_star: ref[Array[ref[TypeOnlyExportStar]?]]?
    exports_checked: bool
)
{
    init(sym: ref[Symbol]?)
    {
        set symbol: sym
        set resolved_exports: null
        set type_only_export_star: null
        set exports_checked: false
    }
}

; ── JsxFlags and JsxReferenceKind (slice 119) ───────────────────────────────
;
; The two bits `getIntrinsicTagSymbol` sets on an element's links and
; `getIntrinsicAttributesTypeFromJsxOpeningLikeElement` reads back. ★The
; `IntrinsicElement` combination the reference also declares has no reader in
; jsx.go; it is not transcribed, on §3.5be's rule about a constant nobody can be
; wrong about.
define JsxFlagsNone: int 0
define JsxFlagsIntrinsicNamedElement: int 1
define JsxFlagsIntrinsicIndexedElement: int 2

; JsxReferenceKind — which of the three shapes a tag names. Only
; `checkJsxReturnAssignableToAppropriateBound` reads it, and only through
; `getJsxReferenceKind`.
define JsxReferenceKindComponent: int 0
define JsxReferenceKindFunction: int 1
define JsxReferenceKindMixed: int 2

; core.JsxEmit (slice 255: the checker reads the option since then; the emitter
; declared these first)
define JsxEmitNone: int 0
define JsxEmitPreserve: int 1
define JsxEmitReactNative: int 2
define JsxEmitReact: int 3
define JsxEmitReactJSX: int 4
define JsxEmitReactJSXDev: int 5

; ── JsxElementLink and SourceFileLink (slice 119) ───────────────────────────
;
; c.jsxElementLinks, the same mint-on-miss idiom a NINTH time and keyed on the JSX
; NODE. Three of its four slots are memos of a lookup that reports, which is what
; makes them load-bearing rather than a saving: `getIntrinsicTagSymbol` emits
; TS7026 on a miss, and SymbolNodeLink's rule — **a stop must not change how many
; times a reporting routine runs** — reads the same way for a routine that
; ANSWERS with a report.
;
; ★★ `jsx_namespace` HAS THREE STATES AND NOT TWO, and the third is what makes the
; global fallback run once. Upstream stores `c.unknownSymbol` to mean *this node
; has been asked and no namespace was found in scope*, so the next visit skips the
; resolveName and drops straight to the global lookup; nil means *not yet asked*.
; The port carries the same three states with the same sentinel.
define JsxElementLink
(
    node: ref[AstNode]?
    jsx_flags: int
    resolved_jsx_element_attributes_type: ref[Type]?
    jsx_namespace: ref[Symbol]?
    jsx_implicit_import_container: ref[Symbol]?
)
{
    init(n: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        set node: n
        set jsx_flags: JsxFlagsNone
        set resolved_jsx_element_attributes_type: null
        set jsx_namespace: null
        set jsx_implicit_import_container: null
    }
}

; c.sourceFileLinks, keyed on the SOURCE FILE node — and only the four JSX slots
; of it. Upstream this table is general (it carries the file's typed-node cache and
; its `@jsx` pragma state alike); here it exists because `getJsxNamespace` writes
; the file's local factory into it and `getJSXFragmentType` memoises on it, and a
; slot no arm of this port writes would be a claim that the rest of the table is
; ported.
;
; ★ The two NAME slots are `Slice[char]` and the empty slice means *not computed*,
; which is the reference's own `""` test at both readers.
define SourceFileLink
(
    node: ref[AstNode]?
    jsx_fragment_type: ref[Type]?
    local_jsx_namespace: Slice[char]
    local_jsx_factory: ref[AstNode]?
    local_jsx_fragment_namespace: Slice[char]
    local_jsx_fragment_factory: ref[AstNode]?
    ; identifierCheckNodes, null until the first registration
    identifier_check_nodes: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
)
{
    init(n: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        set node: n
        set jsx_fragment_type: null
        set local_jsx_namespace: Slice[char]()
        set local_jsx_factory: null
        set local_jsx_fragment_namespace: Slice[char]()
        set local_jsx_fragment_factory: null
        set identifier_check_nodes: null
    }
}

; ── SignatureFlags (slice 84) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's whole const block, in its order, and WHOLE for the reason
; TypeSystemPropertyName's note gives: these are the bits of ONE word, and a
; partial declaration is an invitation to renumber the rest when the fourth
; writer arrives. Three of the nine have a writer in this slice (HasRestParameter,
; HasLiteralTypes, Construct and Abstract — four), two have a READER and no writer
; here (the two call-chain bits, read by getReturnTypeOfSignature's tail and
; written only by resolveCallExpression's optional-chain arms), and the remaining
; three name their writer at their line.
define SignatureFlagsNone: int 0
define SignatureFlagsHasRestParameter: int 1 << 0  ; last parameter is a rest parameter
define SignatureFlagsHasLiteralTypes: int 1 << 1   ; the signature is specialized
define SignatureFlagsConstruct: int 1 << 2         ; a construct signature
define SignatureFlagsAbstract: int 1 << 3          ; from an abstract class or constructor type
; The two call-chain bits. ★★★THE NOTE HERE SAID THEY HAVE NO WRITER, and that
; stopped being true at slice 139: resolveCallExpression's fork and
; getOptionalCallSignature's clone are both in this file, so both bits are set on
; every optional call and the two arms in get_return_type_of_signature are the
; ones that put the `| undefined` back.
define SignatureFlagsIsInnerCallChain: int 1 << 4
define SignatureFlagsIsOuterCallChain: int 1 << 5
; Written by getSignatureFromDeclaration upstream and NOT here — see that
; function's header for the one conjunct this port cannot ask.
define SignatureFlagsIsUntypedSignatureInJSFile: int 1 << 6
; Writers: getTypeOfSymbol's non-inferrable arm, and resolveCall's failure path.
define SignatureFlagsIsNonInferrable: int 1 << 7
define SignatureFlagsIsSignatureCandidateForOverloadFailure: int 1 << 8
define SignatureFlagsPropagatingFlags: int SignatureFlagsHasRestParameter | SignatureFlagsHasLiteralTypes | SignatureFlagsConstruct | SignatureFlagsAbstract | SignatureFlagsIsUntypedSignatureInJSFile | SignatureFlagsIsSignatureCandidateForOverloadFailure
define SignatureFlagsCallChainFlags: int SignatureFlagsIsInnerCallChain | SignatureFlagsIsOuterCallChain

; ── CompositeSignature (slice 155) ──────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's `CompositeSignature`: the signature set a UNION or INTERSECTION
; of signature lists was folded from, plus the bit saying which fold it was.
;
; ★★★ IT IS THE SLOT FOUR STANDING NOTES ARGUED THEMSELVES DEAD AGAINST. Signature's
; own header, cloneSignature, someSignature, isResolvingReturnTypeOfSignature,
; getReturnTypeOfSignature and getTypePredicateOfSignature each said the composite
; arm *"has no input"* / *"is provably dead for every signature this file can
; mint"* — true, and true only because getUnionSignatures stopped. Every one of
; the six arrives in this slice with the producer, which is §3.5gh's rule read
; forwards: **when a slice adds a PRODUCER, grep for every sentence that rested on
; there not being one.**
define CompositeSignature
(
    is_union: bool
    signatures: ref[Array[ref[Signature]?]]?
)

; ── Signature (slice 84) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's *Signature, minus the five slots whose only writers are chapters
; this port has not opened. Every one of the five is named here with the writer
; that would fill it, which is §3.5ao's standard for a deferred field:
;
;   resolved_type_predicate  createTypePredicateFromTypePredicateNode, i.e.
;                            getTypePredicateOfSignature — the stop
;                            check_type_predicate now carries.
;   composite                getUnionOrIntersectionType over a set of signatures.
;                            ★ARRIVED IN SLICE 155 with getUnionSignatures, its
;                            producer — see CompositeSignature.
;   isolated_signature_type  getIsolatedSignatureType, the emit resolver's.
;
; ★★★ `target` AND `mapper` ARRIVED IN SLICE 86 AND THE NOTE THAT DEFERRED THEM
; NAMED THE WRONG WRITER. It said *"getSignatureInstantiation; a generic signature
; instantiated at a call site"*, and the writer that actually landed first is
; `instantiate_signature`, reached from resolveObjectTypeMembers' instantiating
; branch — a chapter away from a call site. **A deferral names a reader it can
; see, and the one that arrives may be another.**
;
; ★★★ AND THEY ARE NOT A SLOT PAIR WITH NO READER: get_return_type_of_signature's
; `sig.target` arm lands in the same slice, because without it an instantiated
; signature would answer the return type of its TARGET's annotation with the
; mapper never applied — a silently wrong type rather than a stop. ★The sentence
; that followed — *"`composite` is still nil for every signature this file can
; mint, so THAT arm remains provably dead"* — held until slice 155 gave it a
; producer, and the arm is written there.
;
; ★ `resolved_min_argument_count` IS carried even though nothing reads it yet, and
; that is not the same claim: it is a MEMO of min_argument_count, initialised to -1
; by newSignature itself, so a record without it would have to invent a sentinel
; the day getMinArgumentCount lands.
; ── the TYPE PREDICATE (slice 123) ──────────────────────────────────────────
;
; checker.TypePredicate and its four kinds. ★The parameter NAME is a (data, len)
; pair into the source, the shape every name in this file travels in, and it is
; only ever compared — `parameterName` upstream is read by the printer, which
; this port reports at.
;
; ★★ `parameter_index` is -1 when the predicate names a parameter the signature
; does not have (`function f(a): b is T`), which is a program the grammar accepts
; and checkTypePredicate reports on. Every reader tests `>= 0` before indexing,
; the way the reference does, so the sentinel is carried rather than repaired.
; One row of c.narrowedTypes — getNarrowedType's memo, keyed on the pair plus the
; two booleans. ★An association list rather than a hash for TypeInstantiation's
; reason: the key is four values and the table holds one row per (reference,
; candidate) the unit narrows, which is a handful even in a large file.
define NarrowedTypeRow
(
    source: ref[Type]
    candidate: ref[Type]
    flags: int
    narrowed: ref[Type]
)

; ── the switch statement's links (slice 123) ───────────────────────────────
;
; checker.SwitchStatementLinks: the three things a switch is asked about, all
; memoised on the statement — its clause TYPES, its typeof WITNESSES, and whether
; it is EXHAUSTIVE.
;
; ★ `witnesses_usable` is the reference's nil slice: one case expression that is
; not a string literal disqualifies the whole switch for the typeof reading, and
; a null ROW inside the list is its empty string — a default clause, or a case
; whose text an earlier case already used.
define ExhaustiveStateUnknown: int 0
define ExhaustiveStateComputing: int 1
define ExhaustiveStateTrue: int 2
define ExhaustiveStateFalse: int 3

define SwitchWitness
(
    text: Slice[char]
)

define SwitchStatementLink
(
    node: ref[AstNode]?
    exhaustive_state: int
    witnesses_computed: bool
    witnesses_usable: bool
    witnesses: ref[Array[ref[SwitchWitness]?]]
    switch_types_computed: bool
    switch_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
)
{
    init(n: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        set node: n
        set exhaustive_state: ExhaustiveStateUnknown
        set witnesses_computed: false
        set witnesses_usable: false
        set witnesses: Array[ref[SwitchWitness]?]^this()
        set switch_types_computed: false
        set switch_types: null
    }
}

; One row of c.assignmentReducedTypes (slice 123) — getAssignmentReducedType's
; memo, keyed on the (declared, assigned) pair the way the reference keys it on
; the two type ids.
define AssignmentReducedRow
(
    declared: ref[Type]
    assigned: ref[Type]
    reduced: ref[Type]
)

; narrow_type_by_discriminant's five closures, as a TAG — see the function's own
; header for why this is the one site in the chapter where §3.5e's flag has to
; carry more than a boolean.
define NarrowKindNarrowedType: int 0
define NarrowKindOptionality: int 1
define NarrowKindTruthiness: int 2
define NarrowKindLiteralExpression: int 3
define NarrowKindEquality: int 4
define NarrowKindSwitchOnDiscriminant: int 5

; ── slice 124: SignatureCheckMode ───────────────────────────────────────────
;
; compareSignaturesRelated's mode word. ★★The two CALLBACK bits are one mask
; (`SignatureCheckModeCallback`) whose meaning is *we are relating two callback
; parameters*, and the recursive call in the parameter loop is the only writer:
; a callback's parameters relate CO-variantly, not bi-variantly, because a
; callback parameter is an OUTPUT position.
define SignatureCheckModeNone: int 0
define SignatureCheckModeBivariantCallback: int 1 << 0
define SignatureCheckModeStrictCallback: int 1 << 1
define SignatureCheckModeIgnoreReturnTypes: int 1 << 2
define SignatureCheckModeStrictArity: int 1 << 3
define SignatureCheckModeStrictTopSignature: int 1 << 4
define SignatureCheckModeCallback: int SignatureCheckModeBivariantCallback | SignatureCheckModeStrictCallback

; The comparer compareSignaturesRelated is handed. ★★★§3.18's rule — *a func value
; whose call sites are NOT static: the tag is the flag* — and there are exactly two
; upstream: `compareTypesAssignable`, a plain relation test, and the RELATER's own
; recursive `isRelatedToWorker`, which reports into the error chain. Only the first
; has a caller in this port (isSignatureAssignableTo); the second arrives with
; signatureRelatedTo, and until then it is a stop rather than a silent alias for
; the first — the two differ in whether they report, which is a `C` line.
define SignatureComparerAssignable: int 0
define SignatureComparerRelaterWorker: int 1

; The three compareSignaturesIdentical takes — `compareTypesIdentical`,
; `compareTypesSubtypeOf` and the RELATER's `isRelatedToSimple`. ★The first two are
; plain relation tests on §3.18's rule; the third is the relater's recursion at
; IntersectionStateNone, which differs from the WORKER arm above only in the
; intersection state it carries, and that difference is the reason both exist.
define SignatureComparerIdentical: int 2
define SignatureComparerSubtypeOf: int 3
define SignatureComparerRelaterSimple: int 4
; compareTypesEqual — plain type IDENTITY, and it is a comparer rather than an
; inline test because compareProperties takes one. Its only caller is
; createUnionOrIntersectionProperty's *are these two the same instantiation*
; question (slice 127), where the relation would be the wrong question: two
; DIFFERENT instantiations of one generic can be mutually assignable.
define SignatureComparerTypesEqual: int 5

define TypePredicateKindThis: int 0
define TypePredicateKindIdentifier: int 1
define TypePredicateKindAssertsThis: int 2
define TypePredicateKindAssertsIdentifier: int 3

define TypePredicate
(
    kind: int
    parameter_index: int
    parameter_name: Slice[char]
    t: ref[Type]?
)

define Signature
(
    id: int
    flags: int
    min_argument_count: int
    resolved_min_argument_count: int
    declaration: ref[AstNode]?
    type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    parameters: ref[Array[ref[Symbol]?]]?
    this_parameter: ref[Symbol]?
    resolved_return_type: ref[Type]?

    ; The instantiation pair, slice 86. They take no init PARAMETER for
    ; `resolved_min_argument_count`'s reason — the one producer never passes them,
    ; and instantiate_signature writes them straight after the construction, which
    ; is the reference's own shape (`result.target = sig; result.mapper = m`).
    target: ref[Signature]?
    mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?

    ; getTypePredicateOfSignature's slot, slice 123, and it takes no init
    ; parameter for the pair above's reason: no producer passes one, and the
    ; resolver writes both straight after it computes them. ★The `computed` flag
    ; is the reference's `noTypePredicate` sentinel — *computed, none* has to
    ; differ from *not computed*, and a boolean says it with one fewer object.
    resolved_type_predicate: ref[TypePredicate]?
    type_predicate_computed: bool

    ; getOrCreateTypeFromSignature's memo, slice 134 — deferred at this record's
    ; header as *"getIsolatedSignatureType, the emit resolver's"*, and the writer
    ; that arrived first is another one: the ES decorator's getter and setter
    ; wrapper types, which need the object type a bare call signature denotes.
    ; **A deferral names a reader it can see, and the one that arrives may be
    ; another** — the same correction `target`/`mapper` cost in slice 86, three
    ; paragraphs up. ★It is a real memo and not an optimisation: the type is
    ; anonymous, so identity is the only thing that tells two of them apart.
    isolated_signature_type: ref[Type]?

    ; getUnionSignatures' slot (slice 155), and it takes no init parameter for the
    ; instantiation pair's reason: createUnionSignature and
    ; combineUnionOrIntersectionMemberSignatures both write it straight after the
    ; construction, which is the reference's own shape.
    composite: ref[CompositeSignature]?

    ; ★★★ THE SIGNATURE'S OWN CACHE ROWS (slice 227): upstream keeps a signature's
    ; instantiations, erased, canonical, base and optional-call twins in
    ; SignatureLinks PER SIGNATURE; this port kept every row of every signature in
    ; ONE array and scanned it whole on each ask — 63 % of the run of
    ; `performanceComparisonOfStructurallyIdenticalInterfacesWithGenericSignatures`
    ; (95 s against the reference's 2 s) in that scan.
    cache_rows: ref[Array[ref[CachedSignature]?]]?
)
{
    init(sid: int, f: int, decl: ref[AstNode]?, tps: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?, tp: ref[Symbol]?, ps: ref[Array[ref[Symbol]?]]?, rrt: ref[Type]?, minac: int)
    {
        set id: sid
        set flags: f
        set min_argument_count: minac
        set resolved_min_argument_count: 0 - 1
        set declaration: decl
        set type_parameters: tps
        set parameters: ps
        set this_parameter: tp
        set resolved_return_type: rrt
        set target: null
        set mapper: null
        set resolved_type_predicate: null
        set type_predicate_computed: false
        set isolated_signature_type: null
        set composite: null
        set cache_rows: null
    }
}

; One row of c.signatureLinks — the same association-list idiom a FIFTH time,
; keyed on the signature DECLARATION node. Upstream the record carries three more
; slots (resolvedSignature's neighbours on SignatureLinks: `effectsSignature` and
; the JSX ones); one slot is what this file's four other links records have and
; what the memo needs.
;
; ★★ ITS MEMO IS OBSERVABLE AND THE OBSERVER IS IDENTITY, exactly as
; value_symbol_links' note says: a signature carries a mutable
; `resolved_return_type` cell, so two asks that answered two different Signature
; objects would memoise the return type twice and the cycle guard would never see
; the second frame as the same target.
define SignatureLink
(
    resolved_signature: ref[Signature]?

    ; ★★★ THE STOP BIT (slice 89). getResolvedSignature memoises through the slot
    ; above, and this port has a THIRD state upstream does not: the resolution
    ; STOPPED. Caching the nil alone would let the second ask answer it without
    ; moving `unported_mark`, and every incompleteness guard in checker.scaly is a
    ; mark delta — slice 88 paid for that once already, in type_node_links, where
    ; the same bit is called `expression_stopped`.

    ; getEffectsSignature's slot (slice 123). Upstream it sits on the SAME
    ; signatureLinks record, keyed on the CALL node rather than on a signature
    ; declaration — two node populations that cannot collide — and it is kept here
    ; for that reason rather than given a table of its own. ★The `computed` flag is
    ; the stop bit above under another name: *asked, no effects* has to differ from
    ; *not asked*.
    effects_signature: ref[Signature]?

    ; getDecoratorCallSignature's slot (slice 134), keyed on the DECORATED node —
    ; `c.signatureLinks.Get(decorator.Parent)` — which is a third node population
    ; on this table beside the signature declaration and the call.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT NEEDS NO STOP BIT AND THE REFERENCE SAYS WHY: the slot is filled with
    ; `c.anySignature` BEFORE the switch runs, and every arm that declines to build
    ; one leaves that marker in place — so *asked, no signature* is already a value
    ; here rather than a second bit, and the reader translates the marker back to
    ; null. That is the same three-state problem `signature_stopped` solves one
    ; slot up, solved upstream by a sentinel.
    decorator_signature: ref[Signature]?
    signature_stopped: bool
    effects_computed: bool
)

; ── ast.CheckFlags: the ONE member this port can set (slice 86) ─────────────
;
; The reference's table has twenty-two members and every one of them is written
; by the CHECKER; the binder sets none. This port creates exactly one kind of
; transient symbol — instantiateSymbol's — so exactly one bit is declared, on the
; discipline the ObjectFlags table's header states: a name for a bit nothing
; writes is a claim about the future.
;
; ★ The four the reference's own `getTypeOfSymbol` switch reads are Instantiated,
; DeferredType, Mapped and ReverseMapped. Three of them still have no writer here
; and that switch still leaves them out rather than reporting — a report is a
; promise that an input can reach the line.
define CheckFlagsNone: int 0
define CheckFlagsInstantiated: int 1 << 0

; ★★ TWO MORE OF ast/checkflags.go's twenty-two, added with slice 91 because
; isReadonlySymbol and getDeclarationModifierFlagsFromSymbol name them. Neither
; has a WRITER here — this port mints exactly one transient symbol kind, the
; instantiated one — so both tests are provably false today; they are written as
; the reference's tests rather than folded away, and a CONSTANT with a reader is
; not the field-with-no-reader §3.5be forbids. ★`Synthetic` is upstream's
; SyntheticProperty|SyntheticMethod, the union-and-intersection property maker's
; mark, which is why the arm that reads it REPORTS rather than answering zero.
; ── AssignmentKind (slice 91) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; checker/utilities.go's three-value iota. It is not a flag set: the values are
; ORDINALS and every reader compares for equality, which is why Definite is 1 and
; not a bit.
;
; ★★★ THEY ARE DEFINED HERE BECAUSE THE COMPILER DOES NOT SAY WHEN THEY ARE NOT.
; The first draft of get_assignment_target_kind spelled all three names with no
; `define` anywhere in the package and `scalyc -c` answered rc 0 — an unknown
; identifier that is the WHOLE of a `return` is neither resolved nor diagnosed,
; where the same name beside a binary operator is a hard rc-4 (root CLAUDE.md's
; *unknown names are hard errors* covers the operand position only). The function
; would have answered garbage for every identifier in the corpus. See §3.5ek.
define AssignmentKindNone: int 0
define AssignmentKindDefinite: int 1
define AssignmentKindCompound: int 2

; ast.AccessKind — another three-value iota, and the same rule applies: they are
; ORDINALS compared for equality, and the `define`s are here because the compiler
; does not say when they are missing (§3.5ek, one paragraph up).
define AccessKindRead: int 0
define AccessKindWrite: int 1
define AccessKindReadWrite: int 2

define CheckFlagsSyntheticProperty: int 1 << 1
define CheckFlagsSyntheticMethod: int 1 << 2
define CheckFlagsReadonly: int 1 << 3
define CheckFlagsSynthetic: int CheckFlagsSyntheticProperty | CheckFlagsSyntheticMethod
; ── the SYNTHESIZED-PROPERTY bits, slice 127 ────────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ THEY ARE WRITTEN AS A SET BECAUSE THEY ARE ONE RECORD'S FIELDS.
; createUnionOrIntersectionProperty computes all nine in one walk and every
; reader asks a combination of them — `Partial` gates the union lookup,
; `NonUniformAndLiteral | HasNeverType` is isDiscriminantWithNeverType's exact
; mask, and the three accessibility bits are the *private or protected in one
; constituent* test. A half table is what §3's own note calls an invitation to
; guess the other half.
define CheckFlagsReadPartial: int 1 << 4
define CheckFlagsWritePartial: int 1 << 5
define CheckFlagsHasNonUniformType: int 1 << 6
define CheckFlagsHasLiteralType: int 1 << 7
define CheckFlagsContainsPublic: int 1 << 8
define CheckFlagsContainsProtected: int 1 << 9
define CheckFlagsContainsPrivate: int 1 << 10
define CheckFlagsContainsStatic: int 1 << 11
define CheckFlagsHasNeverType: int 1 << 17
define CheckFlagsNonUniformAndLiteral: int CheckFlagsHasNonUniformType | CheckFlagsHasLiteralType
define CheckFlagsPartial: int CheckFlagsReadPartial | CheckFlagsWritePartial
; isDiscriminantProperty's memo pair — computed once per SYNTHESIZED property and
; stored on it, which is why they are check flags and not a table.
define CheckFlagsIsDiscriminantComputed: int 1 << 21
define CheckFlagsIsDiscriminant: int 1 << 22
; CheckFlagsLate — the bit a LATE-BOUND symbol for a computed property carries.
; Its writer is the late-binding chapter and this port has none; its reader
; arrives in slice 95, in getNameOfSymbolAsWritten's computed-property fork, where
; it is the question that separates *a name this printer cannot make* from *a name
; the late table already made*. A bit with a reader and no writer is the honest
; shape here: the fork answers the first for every symbol, which is what stops.
; ── the two PARAMETER bits (slice 155) ──────────────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ THEY ARRIVE WITH A PRODUCER, AND THE NOTE THAT SAID THEY HAD NONE NAMED THE
; WRONG ONE. write_parameter_declaration's header calls both *"written by exactly
; one producer — getExpandedParameters' tuple expansion — and that producer cannot
; run"*, which was true of getExpandedParameters and never a complete list:
; combineUnionOrIntersectionParameters writes both, and it runs as of this slice.
; The two disjuncts that stood dead in the printer are written with them.
define CheckFlagsOptionalParameter: int 1 << 14
define CheckFlagsRestParameter: int 1 << 15
; CheckFlagsIndexSymbol — the bit a SYNTHESIZED property standing in for an index
; signature carries. Its writer is getApplicableIndexSymbol (slice 162); its
; readers are the printer and the symbol-declaration accessors, which use it to
; tell such a property from one a declaration actually names.
define CheckFlagsIndexSymbol: int 1 << 23

define CheckFlagsLate: int 1 << 12
; CheckFlagsStripOptional — *strip optionality in mapped property*, slice 125. It
; is set by resolve_mapped_type_members when `-?` meets a property the modifiers
; type declares optional, and read by get_type_of_mapped_symbol, which removes
; `undefined` from the answer. Writer and reader arrive together.
define CheckFlagsStripOptional: int 1 << 19
; CheckFlagsDeferredType — *calculation of the type of this symbol is deferred due
; to processing costs*. Its writer is the `arguments` synthesis inside a function
; body, which this port does not build; its READER arrives in slice 100, in
; assignContextualParameterTypes' rest-parameter guard, where it is the second
; disjunct — the first (a value declaration with no annotation) is the one this
; corpus can reach. A bit with a reader and no writer, on CheckFlagsLate's own
; convention.
define CheckFlagsDeferredType: int 1 << 16
; CheckFlagsMapped — *property of mapped type*, and its reader is slice 124's
; override check: a base property that came out of a mapped type is exempt from the
; property/accessor KIND agreement, because a mapped type's members carry the
; modifiers of the source and not of a declaration. A bit with a reader and no
; writer, on CheckFlagsLate's convention — the writer is the mapped chapter.
define CheckFlagsMapped: int 1 << 18
; CheckFlagsReverseMapped — *property of reverse-inferred homomorphic mapped
; type*, and slice 160 gives it both halves at once: resolve_reverse_mapped_type_
; members sets it on every property it synthesizes, get_type_of_symbol dispatches
; on it into get_type_of_reverse_mapped_symbol. It is the bit that answers *is
; this a reverse-mapped property* for the two readers upstream keeps its own links
; table for, which is the argument the three slots on ValueSymbolLink carry.
define CheckFlagsReverseMapped: int 1 << 13
; MAX_REVERSE_MAPPED_NESTING_INSPECTION_DEPTH — the printer's budget for
; condition (3) of shouldUsePlaceholderForProperty (slice 160).
define MaxReverseMappedNestingInspectionDepth: int 3

; ★★★ `Unresolved` GETS A WRITER IN SLICE 114, and until then the note in
; get_type_from_type_alias_reference proved its arm unreachable from the one
; producer the reference has. That producer is
; getUnresolvedSymbolForEntityName, which is now here — so the bit is a bit and
; the arm is code.
define CheckFlagsUnresolved: int 1 << 20
; the scope walk of the accessible symbol chain (slice 221): per table
define ScopeTableLocalNameLookup: int 1
define ScopeTableGlobals: int 2

; ── VarianceFlags (slice 122) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; internal/checker/types.go. Only the mask and the contravariant bit have a
; reader here — inferFromTypeArguments asks exactly `variances[i] & VarianceMask
; == Contravariant` — but the set is written whole because it is a flags table and
; a half table invites the next reader to add the missing half by guess.
define VarianceFlagsInvariant: int 0
define VarianceFlagsCovariant: int 1 << 0
define VarianceFlagsContravariant: int 1 << 1
define VarianceFlagsBivariant: int VarianceFlagsCovariant | VarianceFlagsContravariant
define VarianceFlagsIndependent: int 1 << 2
define VarianceFlagsVarianceMask: int VarianceFlagsInvariant | VarianceFlagsCovariant | VarianceFlagsContravariant | VarianceFlagsIndependent
define VarianceFlagsUnmeasurable: int 1 << 3
define VarianceFlagsUnreliable: int 1 << 4
define VarianceFlagsAllowsStructuralFallback: int VarianceFlagsUnmeasurable | VarianceFlagsUnreliable
; VarianceFlagsUnmeasured — NOT a reference flag. It is this port's sentinel for
; *the measurement hit a wall*, and it is a bit the reference's mask cannot
; produce (bits 0-4 are the five it uses), so no measured variance can collide
; with it. See measure_variance for why a stop under the comparison may not be
; folded into a variance.
define VarianceFlagsUnmeasured: int 1 << 5

; relate_variances' four answers — see the function for why there are four.
; ★★★ THE FOURTH IS THE `varianceCheckFailed` LATCH, SLICE 187. The reference's
; closure answers `(Ternary, bool)` and writes the latch into a LOCAL of
; structuredTypeRelatedToWorker, which the object tail reads after a SUCCESSFUL
; structural comparison: a variance check that failed makes that success answer
; false anyway. Three of the fallbacks return before the latch is set and their
; structural success STANDS (Unmeasurable/Unreliable, a covariant `void`
; argument, no variance information); the invariant one sets it, and collapsing
; the two into one `Structural` answer reports a TS2322 the reference does not on
; `varianceReferences` or loses the two the reference does report on
; `varianceAnnotations` — which are the same answer read from opposite ends.
define RelateVariancesFalse: int 0
define RelateVariancesTrue: int 1
define RelateVariancesStructural: int 2
define RelateVariancesStructuralLatched: int 3

; discriminate_type_by_discriminable_items' three-state include vector — see the
; function for why a boolean cannot carry it.
define DiscriminateFalse: int 0
define DiscriminateMaybe: int 1
define DiscriminateTrue: int 2

; ── the TypeDiscriminator's two implementations (slice 146) ──────────────────
;
; ★★★ THE NOTE ON discriminate_type_by_discriminable_items PREDICTED THIS AND
; NAMED THE MECHANISM: *the discriminator is a record rather than an interface …
; checker.go's implementation reads a JSX/object-literal member instead and
; arrives with its own chapter — the tag is what will tell them apart.* This is
; that chapter and that tag. Upstream the two are a Go interface with three
; methods (`len`, `name`, `matches`); here they are one record with a kind, which
; is §3.1's shape for an interface with a closed implementation set.
define DiscriminatorKindRelation: int 0
define DiscriminatorKindObjectLiteral: int 1

; ── RelationComparisonResult, the two bits the variance worker reads (slice 130)
;
; The reference's enum, and since slice 172 the whole of it has a reader: the
; note that stood here said the other four *belong to the cached-relation
; chapter, which stores a result rather than a boolean* — that chapter is the
; relation's result cache and it is written now, so a cached row is a WORD and
; not a verdict.
;
; ★★★ AND THE TOP TWO BITS RIDE IN IT, WHICH IS THE HALF THAT IS NOT AN
; OPTIMISATION. `getVariancesWorker` reads `c.reliabilityFlags` after its
; comparisons; a comparison answered FROM THE CACHE performs none, so unless the
; row carries what the original comparison reported, the variance measurement
; silently loses it. Measured: with the cache in and the flags left out, exactly
; three stage-2 units left MATCH and all three are named after this —
; `varianceRepeatedlyPropegatesWithUnreliableFlag` and the two
; `varianceProblingAndZeroOrderIndexSignatureRelationsAlign`. **A cache is not
; semantically neutral when the thing it remembers had a side channel.**
define RelationComparisonResultSucceeded: int 1 << 0
define RelationComparisonResultFailed: int 1 << 1
define RelationComparisonResultReportsUnmeasurable: int 1 << 3
define RelationComparisonResultReportsUnreliable: int 1 << 4
define RelationComparisonResultReportsMask: int RelationComparisonResultReportsUnmeasurable | RelationComparisonResultReportsUnreliable

; ★★★ THE TWO OVERFLOW BITS, SLICE 200 — and they are what a row written by the
; DEPTH LIMITER means, as against a row written by a comparison that ran. The
; distinction is load-bearing in exactly one place: `recursive_type_related_to`
; redoes a cached FAILURE when it has an error node, so that the message can be
; built — and a failure that came out of the limiter cannot be rebuilt, because
; redoing it would overflow again. Upstream's condition is
; `reportErrors && entry&Failed != 0 && entry&Overflow == 0`, i.e. the overflow
; bit is what turns the redo off, and the message is then REPLAYED off the row.
define RelationComparisonResultComplexityOverflow: int 1 << 5
define RelationComparisonResultStackDepthOverflow: int 1 << 6
define RelationComparisonResultOverflow: int RelationComparisonResultComplexityOverflow | RelationComparisonResultStackDepthOverflow

; ── inference: the priorities, the flags and the three records (slice 122) ──
;
; internal/checker/inference.go, plus the InferenceContext / InferenceInfo /
; IntraExpressionInferenceSite triple internal/checker/checker.go declares beside
; it. They stand BEFORE the TypeMapper because the Inference mapper arm holds a
; context and the backreference arm holds a list of type parameters.
;
; ★★★ THE PRIORITIES ARE A BIT SET AND `Circularity` IS -1, WHICH IS WHY EVERY
; COMPARISON IN THIS CHAPTER IS SIGNED. `n.priority < inference.priority` decides
; whether a candidate set is thrown away and started again, and -1 has to lose to
; every real priority for the circular case to be the weakest answer there is;
; `MaxValue` is the seed at the other end, so an untouched inference compares
; greater than any priority a real inference can carry.
define InferencePriorityNone: int 0
define InferencePriorityNakedTypeVariable: int 1 << 0
define InferencePrioritySpeculativeTuple: int 1 << 1
define InferencePrioritySubstituteSource: int 1 << 2
define InferencePriorityHomomorphicMappedType: int 1 << 3
define InferencePriorityPartialHomomorphicMappedType: int 1 << 4
define InferencePriorityMappedTypeConstraint: int 1 << 5
define InferencePriorityContravariantConditional: int 1 << 6
define InferencePriorityReturnType: int 1 << 7
define InferencePriorityLiteralKeyof: int 1 << 8
define InferencePriorityNoConstraints: int 1 << 9
define InferencePriorityAlwaysStrict: int 1 << 10
define InferencePriorityMaxValue: int 1 << 11
define InferencePriorityCircularity: int -1
; The three priorities that mean *combine every candidate* rather than *pick one*.
define InferencePriorityPriorityImpliesCombination: int InferencePriorityReturnType | InferencePriorityMappedTypeConstraint | InferencePriorityLiteralKeyof

define InferenceFlagsNone: int 0
define InferenceFlagsNoDefault: int 1 << 0
define InferenceFlagsAnyDefault: int 1 << 1
define InferenceFlagsSkippedGenericFunction: int 1 << 2

; ★★★ THE SECOND TypeComparer ENUM IS RETIRED (slice 145), AND THE NOTE THAT
; STOOD HERE IS WHY IT HAD TO BE. It read *only `Assignable` is ever passed today
; — the two callers that pass another (instantiateSignatureInContextOf and the
; relater's own inference for signature parameters) are both stops — and a flag
; makes the day one arrives a compile error rather than a silent default.*
; instantiateSignatureInContextOf is the day, and the comparer it forwards is
; compareSignaturesRelated's OWN — which is a `SignatureComparer` tag, a six-value
; set this file already had. Two enums for one func value would have been worse
; than a default: `CompareKindSubtype` is 1 and `SignatureComparerRelaterWorker` is
; 1 too, so passing the relater's comparer through unmapped would have compiled and
; silently asked for the SUBTYPE relation. So the InferenceContext carries a
; `SignatureComparer` and compare_types_by_comparer answers for it; `CompareKind`
; had one writer and no second value.

; The reference's *InferenceInfo. `candidate_depths` is a PARALLEL int array and
; not a slot on the candidate, exactly as upstream: the pair is inserted and
; deleted together in inferFromTypes' type-variable arm, and the depth belongs to
; the (inference, candidate) pair rather than to the type.
define InferenceInfo
(
    type_parameter: ref[Type]?
    candidates: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
    candidate_depths: ref[Array[int]]
    contra_candidates: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
    inferred_type: ref[Type]?
    priority: int
    top_level: bool
    is_fixed: bool
    implied_arity: int
)

; The reference's IntraExpressionInferenceSite.
define IntraExpressionSite
(
    node: ref[AstNode]?
    t: ref[Type]?
)

; The reference's *InferenceContext. `compare_types` is the flag above;
; `inferred_type_parameters` is carried because getSignatureInstantiation reads it
; off the context the call resolution built.
define InferenceContext
(
    inferences: ref[Array[ref[InferenceInfo]?]]
    signature: ref[Signature]?
    flags: int
    compare_kind: int
    mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?
    non_fixing_mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?
    return_mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?
    outer_return_mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?
    inferred_type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    intra_expression_sites: ref[Array[ref[IntraExpressionSite]?]]
)

; The reference's InferenceContextInfo — one entry of c.inferenceContextInfos,
; the stack getInferenceContext searches from the inside out.
define InferenceContextInfo
(
    node: ref[AstNode]?
    context: ref[InferenceContext]?
)

; One row of c.cachedSignatures. The reference's key is
; `{sig, getTypeListKey(typeArguments)}` — a signature and a RENDERING of the
; type-argument list; here the list itself is the key and the comparison is on
; the list, which is exact where the rendering is a bet.
define CachedSignature
(
    source: ref[Signature]?
    kind: int
    type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    instantiation: ref[Signature]?
)

; c.instantiationExpressionTypes' row (slice 181). ★Upstream keys the map on
; `InstantiationExpressionKey{nodeId, typeId}`; here the NODE and the TYPE are
; borrowed references and `=` on either is an identity test, so the pair is the
; key without a counter in between. The cache is not decoration: without it a
; second visit to the same `typeof f<T>` mints a SECOND object type, and every
; identity comparison downstream — and the printer's own alias search — then sees
; two types where the reference has one.
define InstantiationExpressionCacheRow
(
    node: ref[AstNode]?
    source: ref[Type]?
    result: ref[Type]?
)

; The reference's CachedSignatureKey.key — either a rendering of the type
; argument list or one of two SENTINEL strings. Here the list is the key for the
; instantiation rows and the two sentinels are their own kinds, so a base
; signature and an instantiation with no type arguments cannot collide.
define SignatureCacheKindInstantiation: int 0
define SignatureCacheKindBase: int 1
define SignatureCacheKindErased: int 2
; getOptionalCallSignature's two rows, slice 139 — the reference's SignatureKeyInner
; and SignatureKeyOuter, which it likewise keeps in `cachedSignatures` beside the
; other two.
define SignatureCacheKindOptionalInner: int 3
define SignatureCacheKindOptionalOuter: int 4
; getCanonicalSignature's row, slice 145 — the reference's SignatureKeyCanonical,
; the third sentinel of the same table.
define SignatureCacheKindCanonical: int 5

; The reference's *InferenceState — the per-inferTypes scratch the walk threads.
;
; ★★★ THERE IS NO FREE LIST AND THAT IS A DELIBERATE OMISSION, NOT A GAP.
; getInferenceState / putInferenceState upstream recycle the record and its three
; growable slices through `c.freeinferenceState`, which exists because a Go
; allocation is a heap allocation; here the state is built on the caller's region
; page and dies with the frame that made it, so the pool would be machinery with
; nothing to save. ★The reason it is worth a note rather than silence: the pool is
; also what makes upstream's `visited` map survive a call, and nothing in this
; port may rely on that — every field below is initialised per call.
;
; ★★ `visited` IS THREE PARALLEL ARRAYS rather than a map keyed on a pair. The
; reference keys `map[InferenceKey]InferencePriority` on (source id, target id);
; this file's standing answer to a small per-call map is an association list
; (see the intern tables), and this one is smaller than those — it holds the
; invokeOnce pairs of ONE inference, which is a handful.
define InferenceState
(
    inferences: ref[Array[ref[InferenceInfo]?]]
    original_source: ref[Type]?
    original_target: ref[Type]?
    priority: int
    inference_priority: int
    contravariant: bool
    bivariant: bool
    expanding_flags: int
    propagation_type: ref[Type]?
    visited_source: ref[Array[int]]
    visited_target: ref[Array[int]]
    visited_priority: ref[Array[int]]
    source_stack: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
    target_stack: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
    depth: int
)

; ── applyToParameterTypes / applyToReturnTypes: the CALLBACK (slice 145) ──
;
; ★★★ THE TAG NAMES THE CALLER, AND EACH BODY STILL HOLDS ITS OWN CALLEE. The two
; functions take a `callback func(s, t *Type)` upstream and are called from three
; places with three different pairs, so §3.5e's flag is now owed — but the pair
; passed at one call site is not one function: inference.go's
; `inferFromSignature` hands applyToParameterTypes
; `inferFromContravariantTypesIfStrictFunctionTypes` and applyToReturnTypes
; `inferFromTypes`, which are two different callees for ONE caller. So the tag
; cannot name a callee; it names the caller, and the parameter body and the return
; body each resolve it to their own.
;
; ★ The THIRD caller — getTypeOfExpression's contextual-generic block, still a stop
; — passes `inferTypes(inferences, s, t, InferencePriorityNone, contravariant)` on
; both sides with the contravariant flag TRUE for parameters and FALSE for
; returns, which is why the flag is a field here and not baked into the kind.
define ApplyCallbackKindInferFromSignature: int 0
define ApplyCallbackKindInferTypes: int 1

define ApplyCallback
(
    kind: int
    ; ApplyCallbackKindInferFromSignature's one capture.
    state: ref[InferenceState]?
    ; ApplyCallbackKindInferTypes' three.
    inferences: ref[Array[ref[InferenceInfo]?]]?
    priority: int
    contravariant: bool
)

; ── the TypeMapper (slice 86) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; internal/checker/mapper.go, which upstream is an INTERFACE with eight
; implementations and here is a union with two arms — and the cut is a statement
; about writers rather than about effort. Six of the eight cannot be MADE by
; anything this port has: ArrayToSingle and Deferred belong to inference,
; Function to the permissive/restrictive instantiations, Inference to
; InferenceContext, and Merged/Composite are `combineTypeMappers`' product, whose
; two callers — instantiateSymbol's already-instantiated branch and
; instantiateSignatureEx's fresh-type-parameter branch — are both stops in this
; slice. An arm with no writer is a claim about the future (§3.5be), so the two
; that have one are the two that exist.
;
; ★★★ ALL EIGHT ARE HERE SINCE SLICE 151, AND EVERY ONE OF THE SIX ARRIVED WITH
; THE WRITER THIS PARAGRAPH NAMED FOR IT — ArrayToSingle and Deferred in 122 and
; 136, Inference in 122, Merged in 122, Composite in 139 and Function in 151. The
; rule the paragraph states is what made the sequence checkable: an arm was added
; the slice its writer was, never the slice somebody read the reference's list.
;
; ★★ THE `Map` METHOD IS A `choose` AND NOT A DELEGATE, which is the shape this
; file already gives every other Go interface it has met (TypeData, NodeData):
; the reference's dispatch is over a closed set the compiler can see, and a
; closed `choose` is what makes a missing arm a compile error rather than a null
; call.
;
; ★★★ `MapsThisOnly` IS THE WHOLE REASON THE SLICE IS CHEAP, and it is worth
; naming here because it is invisible at every call site. instantiateSymbol's
; first test is `m.MapsThisOnly() && isThisless(symbol)`, and for a NON-GENERIC
; class the mapper is exactly `{thisType -> t}` — so every member whose
; annotation is free of `this` comes back as ITSELF and no symbol is minted at
; all. The minting branch needs a TRANSIENT symbol, which this port cannot make
; (see get_type_of_symbol's note on the four CheckFlags arms); that it is a stop
; rather than a wall is a property of this predicate.
define SimpleTypeMapperData
(
    source: ref[Type]?
    target: ref[Type]?
)

define ArrayTypeMapperData
(
    sources: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    targets: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
)

; ArrayToSingleTypeMapper (slice 122) — every source maps to ONE target. Two
; writers: newBackreferenceMapper, which erases forward references to later type
; parameters to `unknown` while a default is instantiated, and createErasedSignature.
define ArrayToSingleTypeMapperData
(
    sources: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    target: ref[Type]?
)

; DeferredTypeMapper (slice 136) — sources plus a THUNK per source, where the
; reference holds `targets []func() *Type`.
;
; ★★★ THE THUNK IS SPELLED AS ITS ONE SHAPE, AND THAT IS A DIVERGENCE WORTH
; NAMING. `newDeferredTypeMapper` has exactly ONE caller in the whole reference —
; getInferredTypeParameterConstraint's type-reference arm — and every one of its
; thunks is the same expression with a different index:
; `getEffectiveTypeArgumentAtIndex(parent, typeParameters, i)`. So this arm carries
; the NODE instead of a list of closures and computes the i-th argument on demand,
; which preserves the laziness exactly (that is what the deferral BUYS: a
; constraint mentioning one type parameter must not force the type of every
; argument). ★The day a second caller with a different thunk appears, this arm has
; to grow a discriminator rather than a second node slot.
define DeferredTypeMapperData
(
    sources: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    node: ref[AstNode]?
)

; MergedTypeMapper (slice 122) — `m2.Map(m1.Map(t))`, a PLAIN composition of the
; two maps. Its sibling CompositeTypeMapper instantiates through m2 instead of
; mapping through it, and slice 139 gives that one its writers: the two this
; header named as stops — instantiate_symbol's already-instantiated branch and
; instantiate_signature's fresh-type-parameter branch — are the whole of
; combine_type_mappers' caller list.
define MergedTypeMapperData
(
    m1: ref[TypeMapper]?
    m2: ref[TypeMapper]?
)

; CompositeTypeMapper (slice 139) — the same two slots as Merged and a DIFFERENT
; composition, which is the entire point of carrying both.
;
; ★★★ MERGED MAPS THROUGH m2, COMPOSITE INSTANTIATES THROUGH IT. Upstream:
; `t1 := m.m1.Map(t); if t1 != t { return m.c.instantiateType(t1, m.m2) }; return
; m.m2.Map(t)`. So when m1 answered something NEW, m2 is applied to the whole
; result RECURSIVELY (a type reference's arguments, a signature's parameters)
; instead of being asked about the top-level type alone — and that is what makes
; a two-stage substitution correct: the fresh type parameters instantiate_signature
; mints are the TARGETS of m1, and every constraint that mentions them has to be
; rewritten by m2 wherever it occurs, not only when it IS the type.
define CompositeTypeMapperData
(
    m1: ref[TypeMapper]?
    m2: ref[TypeMapper]?
)

; InferenceTypeMapper (slice 122) — the arm that makes an InferenceContext usable
; as a mapper: a type parameter maps to whatever has been inferred for it.
;
; ★★★ THE `fixing` BIT IS AN EFFECT AND NOT A MODE. On a fixing mapper the first
; hit COMMITS the inference — it drains the intra-expression sites, clears every
; cached inferred type and sets `is_fixed` — so mapping a type through
; `context.mapper` changes the context, while `context.non_fixing_mapper` reads it.
; That is why the two exist as separate mappers over one context rather than as a
; parameter of Map.
define InferenceTypeMapperData
(
    context: ref[InferenceContext]?
    fixing: bool
)

; ReliabilityTypeMapper (slice 130) — reportUnreliableMapper and
; reportUnmeasurableMapper, the reference's two `TypeMapperFunc`s.
;
; ★★★ IT IS THE IDENTITY MAP AND ITS PRODUCT IS AN EFFECT. Every type goes
; through unchanged; what the mapper does is NOTICE that a MARKER type passed
; through it and set the corresponding bit on `c.reliabilityFlags`. The two are
; instantiated in exactly the places where the relation cannot account for a
; type argument linearly — a mapped type's constraint under `-?`
; (unmeasurable) and every other mapped constraint (unreliable) — so the bit
; says *the variance measured through this comparison may not be trusted*, and
; the worker turns it into the Unmeasurable/Unreliable bit of the answer.
;
; ★ A `TypeMapperFunc` upstream, an arm here for the reason the Inference arm is
; one: the union is how this port spells a mapper, and both bodies need the
; Checker anyway.
define ReliabilityTypeMapperData
(
    unmeasurable: bool
)
{
    init(u: bool)
        set unmeasurable: u
}

; FunctionTypeMapperData (slice 151) — the reference's `FunctionTypeMapper`, whose
; whole content is a `func(*Type) *Type`. It carries a BOOL rather than a delegate
; for the reason the Reliability arm does: upstream mints five of these and this
; port has two of them, the restrictive and the permissive worker, which are one
; test on `TypeFlagsTypeParameter` apart.
;
; ★★★ THE MAPPER IS AN IDENTITY AND NOT ONLY A FUNCTION, and that is why the two
; live on the Checker as fields rather than being minted per ask.
; `instantiateSignatureEx`'s third argument is `m == c.permissiveMapper` — a
; POINTER comparison — so a freshly built permissive mapper would erase no type
; parameter and the erasure would be silently absent.
;
; ★ The two workers this port does NOT have are the reliability pair, which
; already has an arm of its own. The THIRD name that stood here —
; `getUniqueLiteralTypeForTypeParameter` — landed in slice 166 as the mapper
; behind isReducibleIntersection, which is why the slot is a kind and not a bool.
define FunctionMapperRestrictive: int 0
define FunctionMapperPermissive: int 1
define FunctionMapperUniqueLiteral: int 2

define FunctionTypeMapperData
(
    worker: int
)
{
    init(w: int)
        set worker: w
}

define TypeMapperData union
(
    Simple: SimpleTypeMapperData
    Array: ArrayTypeMapperData
    ArrayToSingle: ArrayToSingleTypeMapperData
    Deferred: DeferredTypeMapperData
    Merged: MergedTypeMapperData
    Composite: CompositeTypeMapperData
    Inference: InferenceTypeMapperData
    Reliability: ReliabilityTypeMapperData
    Function: FunctionTypeMapperData
)

; The reference's `TypeMapper` wrapper, which exists there to give the interface
; a stable address and here to give the union a place to live behind a `ref`.
define TypeMapper
(
    data: TypeMapperData
)
{
    init(d: TypeMapperData)
        set data: d
}

; ── the ACTIVE-MAPPER memo (slice 163) ──────────────────────────────────────
;
; `c.activeMappers` and `c.activeTypeMappersCaches`: upstream a stack of mappers
; and a parallel stack of (type, alias) → type maps, indexed by the same
; position. ONE list of records here (§3.35) — `findActiveMapper` answers an
; INDEX into both and every reader of that index reads the pair, so a record per
; level is the same data with the pairing made structural. Upstream's own reason
; for two slices is to keep a popped map's allocation for reuse, which is an
; allocator question RBMM answers by itself.
;
; ★★★ WHAT IT IS FOR IS NOT A CYCLE BUT A RE-VISIT. `instantiation_depth` bounds
; NESTING; this memo answers *I am already instantiating under this very mapper,
; and this type under this alias came past once before*, which collapses repeated
; work rather than breaking a loop.
;
; ★★★ AND IT IS INERT ON THIS CORPUS, MEASURED, WHICH IS THE HONEST THING TO SAY
; ABOUT IT. It was ported in slice 163 because slices 151/158 had named it as the
; mechanism whose absence forced a deliberate divergence at
; `get_object_type_instantiation` — and that attribution turned out to be wrong
; (the blocker was the printer's dropped `symbolDepth` guard). Kept anyway: it is
; the reference's own line, and the two things a cache can be judged on were both
; measured against a build with the lookup disabled — the whole stage-1 corpus,
; 1674 units, **byte-identical on every artifact**, and 2.4 s of wall against
; 2.4 s. A faithful mechanism that changes no answer and costs nothing stays;
; what may not stay is a claim that it did work it did not do.
;
; ★★★ AND IT IS ONLY WRITTEN FOR A MAPPER THAT WAS ALREADY ACTIVE, which is the
; reference's own asymmetry and not an optimisation: a level PUSHED by this call
; is popped again on the way out, so nothing it memoised could be read; a level
; found ALREADY on the stack is one an outer frame still owns, and the entry is
; what that frame's next ask reads.
define MapperCacheEntry
(
    t: ref[Type]?
    alias_symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    alias_type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    instantiated: ref[Type]?
)

define ActiveMapper
(
    mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?
    entries: ref[Array[ref[MapperCacheEntry]?]]?
)

; ── the printer's symbolDepth (slice 163) ───────────────────────────────────
;
; `visitAndTransformType`'s OTHER guard, and the reference's own comment says what
; it is for: *"Since instantiations of the same anonymous type have the same
; symbol, tracking symbols instead of types allows us to catch circular references
; to instantiations of the same anonymous type"*.
;
; ★★★ IT IS THE HALF `visited_types` CANNOT DO, and the difference is measurable
; rather than theoretical. `visitedTypes` is keyed on the type ID, so it closes a
; cycle only where the SAME type object comes round again; a walk that mints a
; fresh instantiation at every level — `foo<typeof y>()` inside `foo`, where each
; level's type argument is the previous level's type — never repeats an id and
; recurses until the stack is gone. The symbol is what stays the same.
;
; ★★ A `ref[Symbol]` AND NOT AN ID, which is §3.31 applied rather than a new
; decision — and here the second reason is sharper than the collision one.
; Upstream needs a hashable key and takes `ast.GetSymbolId(t.symbol)`, which
; ASSIGNS a number on first request; drawing one from a PRINTER would mint ids
; in a phase that has no business minting them, and this port's counter is per
; file (§3.10) and observable through the private-name minter. A linear scan
; compares the symbol itself, which is the identity the id stands for.
;
; ★ `is_constructor_object` is upstream's own first field: a class's constructor
; object and the class type share a symbol, and the two are printed differently,
; so the pair is the identity and not the symbol alone.
;
; ★★★ AND THE THIRD FIELD ARRIVED IN SLICE 173, BECAUSE THE SENTENCE THAT SAID IT
; WAS NOT NEEDED WAS WRONG. It read: *the other two shapes of upstream's
; CompositeSymbolIdentity — a type reference's NODE and a conditional type's root
; node — belong to visitAndTransformType call sites this printer does not have;
; its one transform is createTypeNodeFromObjectType.* The conditional arm IS such
; a call site upstream (`visitAndTransformType(t, conditionalTypeToTypeNode)`) and
; this printer has had it since slice 121 — unbracketed. Nothing pointed at that
; while a `record_unported` further down the same walk unwound every recursive
; conditional before it could close; slice 173 wrote the SUBSTITUTION arm, that
; stop stopped firing, and `type C1<T> = [T extends string ? C1<T> : never][0]`
; — two lines of the reference's own `recursiveConditionalCrash1` — took the
; process down with a stack overflow. **A stop is a brake wherever it sits, and
; removing one is a claim about every loop that ran through it.**
;
; ★ A `ref[AstNode]` and not a node id, for the symbol field's reason one
; paragraph up: upstream's `ast.GetNodeId` ASSIGNS on first request, and a printer
; may not mint ids. The comparison is the node itself.
define PrinterSymbolFrame
(
    is_constructor_object: bool
    symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    node: ref[AstNode]?
)

; ── IndexInfo (slice 85) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's *IndexInfo, minus `components`. That slot holds the member
; DECLARATIONS a synthesised index info was combined out of; its two writers are
; getObjectLiteralIndexInfo and getUnionIndexInfos, neither of which exists here,
; and its reader is the elaboration that points a report at the original member.
; Named rather than declared, per §3.5ao.
;
; ★ The four that ARE carried are the four an index signature DECLARATION fills,
; which is the only producer this slice has.
define IndexInfo
(
    key_type: ref[Type]?
    value_type: ref[Type]?
    is_readonly: bool
    declaration: ref[AstNode]?
    ; ★★★ getApplicableIndexSymbol's MEMO, and it is a slot on the info rather
    ; than a table because upstream writes it back through the info pointer it was
    ; handed (`info.indexSymbol = symbol`). The symbol it holds is SYNTHESIZED — a
    ; property named `__index` standing in for the signature — so a second ask must
    ; answer the same one or a caller comparing symbols gets two different answers
    ; for one index signature.
    index_symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    ; the reference's `components` (slice 251): the computed-name declarations
    ; an object-literal index info was derived from, which the declaration
    ; emitter writes back as properties when every name is visible
    components: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
)

; ── the resolved members of a structured type (slice 85) ────────────────────
;
; The reference's `StructuredType`: the five slots resolveStructuredTypeMembers
; writes and getPropertiesOfType / getSignaturesOfType / getIndexInfosOfType
; read.
;
; ★★★ IT IS A RECORD BEHIND A REFERENCE RATHER THAN FIVE SLOTS ON EACH PAYLOAD
; ARM, AND THE REASON IS MUTATION, NOT SPACE. Upstream `t.AsStructuredType()`
; hands back a pointer INTO the type and every writer stores through it; here a
; `choose t.data when d: Interface` binding is a COPY (the root CLAUDE.md's
; `when`-binding rule), so five slots per arm would turn every write into a
; copy/mutate/write-back dance in three separate places — and
; resolveObjectTypeMembers writes twice, the second time after a loop. One
; reference, attached once, is written through everywhere after.
;
; ★★ `signatures` IS ONE LIST WITH A COUNT rather than two lists, exactly as
; upstream: the call signatures come first, the construct ones after, and
; getSignaturesOfStructuredType slices at the count. Two lists would have to be
; re-joined in that order by the relation, which is the next reader.
define StructuredMembers
(
    members: ref[SymbolTable]?
    properties: ref[Array[ref[Symbol]?]]?
    signatures: ref[Array[ref[Signature]?]]?
    call_signature_count: int
    index_infos: ref[Array[ref[IndexInfo]?]]?
    ; `objectTypeWithoutAbstractConstructSignatures` — upstream's slot on
    ; StructuredType, and the only memo the PRINTER owns. It holds the copy of
    ; this object type with the abstract construct signatures filtered out, so
    ; that `abstract class C { static x: number }` names its static side with one
    ; intersection rather than a fresh one per visit; the copy carries the memo
    ; pointing at ITSELF, which is what stops the fork from re-entering.
    without_abstract_construct_signatures: ref[Type]?
    ; `resolved.CallSignatures` / `ConstructSignatures`, split out of `signatures`
    ; once (slice 324): the split used to be a fresh list per question, garbage in
    ; the checker's region; null until asked, reset where `signatures` is written
    call_signatures_memo: ref[Array[ref[Signature]?]]?
    construct_signatures_memo: ref[Array[ref[Signature]?]]?
)

; The InterfaceType half — `declaredMembers`, `declaredCallSignatures`,
; `declaredConstructSignatures`, `declaredIndexInfos`. A SECOND record rather
; than four more slots on the one above, on §3.5n's rule: the slot lists are not
; identical, and only a class or an interface has these at all.
;
; ★★ `declaredMembersResolved` IS NOT A SLOT — the record IS the bit.
; resolve_declared_members allocates it, fills `members`, ATTACHES it, and only
; then computes the three lists, which is the reference's own order and the
; reason the bit exists there: a signature that circularly names the type it
; belongs to closes on the half-built record instead of running forever.
; Upstream needs a separate bit because a Go struct is always present; here the
; absence of the reference says the same thing.
define DeclaredMembers
(
    members: ref[SymbolTable]?
    call_signatures: ref[Array[ref[Signature]?]]?
    construct_signatures: ref[Array[ref[Signature]?]]?
    index_infos: ref[Array[ref[IndexInfo]?]]?
)

; SignatureKind. Two members, and they are the INDEX into one list rather than
; two labels: getSignaturesOfStructuredType answers the head of `signatures` for
; Call and its tail for Construct.
; ── the five signature FORMS a type NAME can contain (slice 111) ────────────
;
; signatureToSignatureDeclarationHelper takes an ast.Kind and switches on it at
; the very end; these are the five the printer turns into a name, and the number
; is what it does with the same three pieces.
;
;   FunctionType       <T>(p: A) => R        ConstructorType    new <T>(p: A) => R
;   CallSignature      <T>(p: A): R          ConstructSignature new <T>(p: A): R
;   MethodSignature    m?<T>(p: A): R
;
; ★ They are OUR numbering and not the reference's KindXxx, because the reference
; reuses ast kinds that this port's Kind table already spends on the SYNTAX nodes
; of the same names — a CallSignature node and a call-signature FORM are not the
; same thing, and one enum for both would make the switch read as a node test.
define SigFormFunctionType: int 0
define SigFormConstructorType: int 1
define SigFormCallSignature: int 2
define SigFormConstructSignature: int 3
define SigFormMethodSignature: int 4
; The two ACCESSOR forms, slice 143. They differ from every form above in three
; ways at once and each is upstream's: the keyword comes FIRST, there is no type
; parameter list (the helper is handed nil for both), and the SETTER writes no
; return type at all.
define SigFormGetAccessor: int 5
define SigFormSetAccessor: int 6

; ── ast.TypePrecedence (slice 115) ──────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The emit printer's lattice, in the reference's own order, and only the levels a
; writer here can answer are named. It is what `wrote_type_precedence` holds; the
; field's header carries the argument for why a boolean could not.
;
; ★ Conditional (0) and JSDoc (1) have no writer in this printer yet and are named
; anyway, because the field is a COMPARISON and a gap in the middle of an ordered
; scale is a defect waiting to be written as a wrong number.
define TypePrecedenceConditional:  int 0
define TypePrecedenceJSDoc:        int 1
define TypePrecedenceFunction:     int 2
define TypePrecedenceUnion:        int 3
define TypePrecedenceIntersection: int 4
define TypePrecedenceTypeOperator: int 5
define TypePrecedencePostfix:      int 6
define TypePrecedenceNonArray:     int 7

define SignatureKindCall: int 0
define SignatureKindConstruct: int 1

; ── Relation (slice 101) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The five relations relater.go compares types under. Upstream they are five
; POINTERS on the checker — `relation == c.assignableRelation` is an identity
; test on a map — and here they are five ids, because what every test in that
; file asks is WHICH relation this is and never what is in the map. The map
; itself is the relation CACHE, whose only writer is the recursion (see
; is_type_related_to's note).
;
; ★★ ONLY `RelationAssignable` IS EVER PASSED, and that is a fact about the
; CALLERS rather than about this table: the three sites wired in slice 101 are
; all `checkTypeAssignableToAndOptionallyElaborate`. The other four are declared
; whole on CheckMode's rule — every relation test below is transcribed as the
; reference writes it, so the next arm reads as a transcription rather than as a
; new decision — and each one that guards an unported helper reports there.
define RelationAssignable: int 0
define RelationComparable: int 1
define RelationSubtype: int 2
define RelationStrictSubtype: int 3
define RelationIdentity: int 4

; ── IntersectionState ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's three-value bit set, declared whole. ★★It became LIVE in slice
; 103 and it was dropped by slice 101 with an argument that had an expiry date:
; the only writer is `unionOrIntersectionRelatedTo` — the arm that slice did not
; port — and its two readers are the excess-property and common-property checks in
; `isRelatedToEx`, which sat behind that same stop. All three arrive together, so
; the parameter is threaded rather than defaulted.
define IntersectionStateNone: int 0
define IntersectionStateSource: int 1 << 0   ; Source type is a constituent of an outer intersection
define IntersectionStateTarget: int 1 << 1   ; Target type is a constituent of an outer intersection

; ── RecursionFlags and ExpandingFlags (slice 118) ───────────────────────────
;
; The reference's two bit sets, declared whole. `RecursionFlags` says which of the
; two type STACKS `recursiveTypeRelatedTo` pushes onto, and it reaches that
; function through `is_related_to_ex` — which is why this slice widened that
; signature at all six of its call sites rather than defaulting it.
;
; ★★★ THE FLAG IS NOT A PERFORMANCE HINT, IT IS THE DEPTH MODEL. A union arm
; relates each constituent against the SAME target, so pushing the target once per
; constituent would make `isDeeplyNestedType` count breadth as depth and the
; relation would give up on a wide union rather than on a deep one. The reference
; passes Source there and Target on the mirror arm for exactly that reason.
define RecursionFlagsNone: int 0
define RecursionFlagsSource: int 1 << 0
define RecursionFlagsTarget: int 1 << 1
define RecursionFlagsBoth: int RecursionFlagsSource | RecursionFlagsTarget

define ExpandingFlagsNone: int 0
define ExpandingFlagsSource: int 1 << 0
define ExpandingFlagsTarget: int 1 << 1
define ExpandingFlagsBoth: int ExpandingFlagsSource | ExpandingFlagsTarget

; ── TypeSystemPropertyName ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's ten-member iota, declared WHOLE and in its order even though
; three of them have a writer here. That is the opposite of the ObjectFlags
; decision two hundred lines up, and the difference is what the values are FOR:
; an ObjectFlag is a bit whose meaning is its reader, so a bit nobody reads is a
; claim about the future; these are the arms of ONE switch, and a partial
; declaration is an invitation to renumber the rest when the fourth arrives.
; Nothing outside this file observes the numbers.
define TypeSystemPropertyNameType: int 0
define TypeSystemPropertyNameResolvedBaseConstructorType: int 1
define TypeSystemPropertyNameDeclaredType: int 2
define TypeSystemPropertyNameResolvedReturnType: int 3
define TypeSystemPropertyNameResolvedBaseConstraint: int 4
define TypeSystemPropertyNameResolvedTypeArguments: int 5
define TypeSystemPropertyNameResolvedBaseTypes: int 6
define TypeSystemPropertyNameWriteType: int 7
define TypeSystemPropertyNameInitializerIsUndefined: int 8
define TypeSystemPropertyNameAliasTarget: int 9

; One entry of c.typeResolutions.
;
; ★★ `TypeSystemEntity` IS `any` IN THE REFERENCE — a Symbol, a Type, a Node or a
; Signature, told apart by the propertyName that goes with it — and it is THREE
; SLOTS here rather than a four-arm union. Identity comparison is what the
; reference does with the field, and three null-or-pointer slots compare exactly
; as one `any` does; the fourth arm (`Node`, for InitializerIsUndefined) is named
; rather than declared, on the rule this record has followed since slice 67.
;
; ★★★ SLICE 84 ADDS THE SIGNATURE SLOT AND DELIBERATELY DOES NOT TAKE THE UNION
; THIS NOTE USED TO PROMISE. The earlier sentence read *the day either lands the
; union is the right shape*, and what makes it wrong is what the field is FOR: the
; only operation on it is `==`, so a union buys exclusivity at the price of a
; two-level `choose` in the cycle search's inner loop, and it can prove nothing a
; reader of `find_resolution_cycle_start_index` cannot already read off the
; property_name — which is what selects the slot upstream too. **A shape promised
; for a future slice is a claim about that slice's needs, and it expires the same
; way a blocker does (§3.5p).**
define TypeResolution
(
    target_symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    target_type: ref[Type]?
    target_signature: ref[Signature]?
    ; The FOURTH target kind (slice 175). Upstream `pushTypeResolution` takes a
    ; TypeSystemEntity, which is a symbol, a type, a signature OR A NODE;
    ; parameterInitializerContainsUndefined is the first question here whose
    ; subject is a declaration rather than one of the other three.
    target_node: ref[AstNode]?
    property_name: int
    result: bool
)

; One entry of the stack getResolvedBaseConstraint threads through its recursion
; (slice 81).
;
; ★★ IT IS THE THIRD TIME THIS FILE ANSWERS A GO `any` WITH SLOTS rather than with
; a union — TypeResolution above and LiteralTypeData's value are the other two. The
; reference constrains this `any` to *ast.Node | *ast.Symbol | *Type through a
; generic helper (`asRecursionId`) and then compares two of them with `==`, which on
; an interface value compares the dynamic TYPE and the pointer — so two
; null-or-pointer slots compare exactly as one `any` does.
;
; ★ The NODE arm IS declared and get_recursion_identity reads it first — the
; deferred type reference, tracked through its AST node. What stood here said the
; opposite (*the `node` slot ReferenceTypeData does not carry … the day that slot
; lands, this record gains the third one*); the slot landed with
; create_deferred_type_reference and the arm came with it, and the line was left
; behind. A closed limit reads exactly like an open one, which is how the same
; premise was still being CITED at two other sites forty slices later.
define RecursionId
(
    target_symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    target_type: ref[Type]?

    ; ★★★ THE THIRD KIND OF IDENTITY, SLICE 160. Two of getRecursionIdentity's
    ; arms answer an AST NODE rather than a symbol or a type — a DEFERRED type
    ; reference is tracked through its own type node, and a CONDITIONAL through
    ; its root's node, *the origin of the conditional type* — and both were
    ; missing while nothing reached them. Without them every instantiation of one
    ; conditional has an identity of its own, the deeply-nested guard can never
    ; count two occurrences, and an expanding chain of instantiations does not
    ; terminate. It is not a diagnostic: it is a SIGSEGV at the default stack and
    ; a hang at 64 MB (`submodule_compiler_recursiveTupleTypeInference`).
    target_node: ref[AstNode]?
)

; ── the STOP LOG's element (slice 75) ───────────────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ WHY A LOG BESIDE A FIRST-WINS TAG. `record_unported` keeps the FIRST stop of
; a unit and `unported_count` counts them, so between the two there is a number and
; a name and no way to get from one to the other: a tag that 27 units reach reads as
; ZERO in the work list, because every one of those units stopped somewhere else
; first. Slice 74 chose its successor off that work list and named
; `isConstructorType` as the extends chapter's first wall; measured over the same
; corpus with this log, `isConstructorType` is the first wall for 2 of 92 arrivals
; and `checkIdentifier` for 78. **A histogram of firsts is not a histogram.**
;
; ★★ IT IS COLLECTED UNCONDITIONALLY and printed only by `--stops`, which is what
; keeps the instrument from having a mode: a stop is a rare event (14 878 over the
; whole of stage 1, i.e. ~11 per unit), so the log costs one small region allocation
; per report and nothing per node. The checksum this owes is that the five artifacts
; stay byte-identical with the log in place — see tests/stops.sh's header.
define StopEvent
(
    tag: Slice[char]
    detail: int
)

; ── the MEMBER LOG (slice 86) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ IT EXISTS BECAUSE THE SLICE'S PRODUCT IS UNREADABLE, AND THAT IS A
; MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A COMPLAINT. Slice 86's first control battery ran
; thirty-three rows against five instruments and TWENTY-NINE of them came back
; completely silent — every one of them for the same reason: what
; resolveObjectTypeMembers builds is a members table, and the only reader that
; exists yet is checkIndexConstraints' `len(getIndexInfosOfType) == 0` test. So a
; row that breaks the MAPPER, the thisless predicate, the minting branch or a list
; walk changes the table and changes nothing an instrument can see. **A battery
; whose rows are silent for one shared reason is not measuring the slice; it is
; measuring the absence of a reader.**
;
; ★★ WHAT IT LOGS IS THE ONE THING THOSE ROWS MOVE: for every
; setStructuredTypeMembers, the type's object flags and counts, then one line per
; PROPERTY carrying its name, its symbol flags, its CHECK flags and its declaration
; count — which is exactly the difference between a member answered unchanged and a
; member MINTED, and between a mint that copied its declarations and one that did
; not. Slice 82 added the stop log as a fifth instrument on the same argument one
; dimension over.
;
; ★ IT IS A PIN AND NOT A YARDSTICK: there is no reference side. The battery records
; our own output on the unpatched tree and diffs it, exactly as the STOPPIN does —
; which is all a control needs, because a control asks whether a line is
; LOAD-BEARING and not whether it is right.
;
; ★ Collected unconditionally like the stop log, printed only by `--members`, and
; it owes the same checksum: the five artifacts stay byte-identical with it in
; place.
; ── TypeRefEvent (slice 95) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ WHY AN EIGHTH INSTRUMENT, AND IT IS SLICE 94's FINDING FOUR ARRIVING ONE
; PRODUCT ON. That slice built the UNIONPIN because *this chapter's product is a
; type no artifact in this directory prints*; the type reference is the same
; again, and worse in one way that decides it. A union is INTERNED, so a table of
; every union the checker made is a complete record of the chapter. A type
; reference is not a table — it is an ANSWER GIVEN AT A NODE — and the two halves
; that can go wrong are the SYMBOL a name resolves to and the NAME the printer
; composes back out of the type. Neither moves a diagnostic, a stop or a union.
;
; ★★★ AND THE STRUCTURAL REASON NO EXISTING ARTIFACT CAN SEE IT: THE T SECTION IS
; ALL-OR-NOTHING PER UNIT. A stop anywhere in a unit makes the whole unit UNPORTED
; and the dump prints one line, so the printer arms this slice adds are invisible
; on every unit that still has any other wall — which at stage 1 is 1 466 of
; 1 480. **A product that only a fully-ported unit can show is a product no
; measurement of this corpus can reach**, and the log is what separates *the
; chapter answered* from *the unit finished*.
;
; ★ It records what the CHECK did rather than what a fresh walk would do: the
; entry is appended where getTypeFromTypeReference answers, so a node the checker
; never reached leaves no row. A walk that asked every TypeReference node
; afterwards would be a different question with the same shape, and the memo would
; make it look identical.
define TypeRefEvent
(
    pos: int
    end: int
    symbol_flags: int
    type_id: int
    t: ref[Type]?
)

; ── PropEvent (slice 96) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ THE NINTH INSTRUMENT, AND SLICE 95's ARGUMENT ONE PRODUCT ON. The T section
; is all-or-nothing per unit, so a chapter whose product is an ANSWER GIVEN AT A
; NODE is invisible on every unit that still has any other wall — 1 480 of 1 494 at
; stage 1. A property access is exactly that shape, and it is worse than the type
; reference in one way: a type reference at least has a NODE KIND a walk could
; revisit, while what this chapter produces is *which SYMBOL a name picked out of a
; members table and what TYPE that symbol then has*, neither of which any artifact
; in this directory prints.
;
; ★★ FIVE COLUMNS, AND THE MIDDLE THREE ARE THE THREE THINGS THAT CAN GO WRONG
; SEPARATELY. `symbol_flags` sees WHICH member the lookup found — a method and a
; property of the same name differ here and nowhere else; `apparent_id` sees what
; the lookup was performed ON, which is where the widening and the apparent-type
; steps land; `type_id` plus the printed NAME see what the access ANSWERS, which is
; where getFlowTypeOfAccessExpression's five forks land. A pin with only the last
; of the three cannot tell a wrong receiver from a wrong narrowing.
;
; ★ It is written only where the function ANSWERS WITH A RESOLVED PROPERTY. The
; any-like arm resolves none — it answers the receiver's own type — so it leaves no
; row, and the log's length is therefore the number of property accesses this port
; RESOLVED rather than the number it met. The number it met is the stop log's job.
define PropEvent
(
    pos: int
    end: int
    symbol_flags: int
    apparent_id: int
    type_id: int
    t: ref[Type]?
)

; ── ThisEvent (slice 97) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ THE TENTH INSTRUMENT, AND IT IS PropEvent's ARGUMENT ON THE NEXT CHAPTER.
; What checkThisExpression produces is *the type a `this` answers, in the container
; the normalisation loop settled on* — an ANSWER GIVEN AT A NODE again, so the T
; section cannot show it on any of the 1 481 stage-1 units that still have another
; wall, and this chapter's own diagnostics are no substitute: four of the five
; reports fire only where the answer is a REPORT rather than a type.
;
; ★★ FOUR COLUMNS PLUS THE SPAN, AND THE MIDDLE TWO ARE WHAT SEPARATES THIS
; CHAPTER'S THREE INDEPENDENT HALVES. `container_kind` is the only product of the
; arrow/computed-name loop and of get_this_container's two flags — a wrong flag
; moves it and nothing else — while `arm` says WHICH of tryGetThisTypeAtEx's four
; answering paths ran, which the container kind alone cannot: a method WITH a
; `this` parameter and a method without one have the same container and different
; arms. `type_id` and the printed name are what the flow walk narrows.
;
; ★★★ THE `arm` COLUMN IS THE INSTRUMENT'S AND NOT THE REFERENCE'S, threaded out
; of tryGetThisTypeAtEx through a cell that upstream has no parameter for. It is
; recorded as such rather than folded into the type id, because the two really do
; move independently: h-rows that break the signature path and the class path both
; take the type to `any` and only this column says which one broke.
;
; ★ One row per `this` expression the chapter ANSWERS, including the `any` fallback
; (arm 0), because that is an answer the reference gives too. A `this` whose walk
; STOPPED leaves no row — the number it met is the stop log's job, exactly as
; PropEvent's header says of the property access.
define ThisEvent
(
    pos: int
    end: int
    container_kind: int
    arm: int
    type_id: int
    t: ref[Type]?
)

; ── WithThisEvent (slice 98) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ THE ELEVENTH INSTRUMENT, AND THE CHAPTER IT MEASURES ANSWERS A TYPE THAT NO
; OTHER ARTIFACT IN THIS DIRECTORY CAN NAME. getTypeWithThisArgument does not
; report, does not resolve a symbol and does not sit at a node — it re-anchors a
; class or interface REFERENCE on a `this` argument, and the answer's printed name
; is the SAME STRING as the input's, because a reference is named after its target.
; So the type printer cannot see this chapter at all: the two things it produces
; that can differ are the type IDENTITY and WHICH ARGUMENT was appended, and both
; are invisible in every artifact run.sh compares.
;
; ★★ FOUR COLUMNS, AND `arg_id` IS THE ONE THE CHAPTER IS ABOUT. `in_id` says what
; was asked, `arm` says which of the function's four answering paths ran, `out_id`
; says whether a NEW type came out or the input came back — and `arg_id` is the id
; of the type actually APPENDED, which is the only column a wrong thisArgument
; moves. A caller that passes null where the reference passes the original type
; still produces a well-formed reference with the right name, the right arm and a
; fresh id; only this column says it is anchored on the wrong thing.
;
; ★ The `arm` column is the instrument's and not the reference's: upstream has one
; return per path and no way to tell them apart from outside. 0 = neither a
; reference nor an intersection, so the input comes back; 1 = the REFERENCE arm
; with the arity matched, the answer being a new reference; 2 = the reference arm
; whose arity did NOT match, where upstream returns `t` unchanged; 3 = the
; needApparentType tail. A row is written only where the function ANSWERS — a call
; that stopped leaves none, which is the stop log's job.
;
; ★★★ SLICE 155 ADDED THE INTERSECTION ARM AS TWO VALUES, 4 AND 5, AND THE SPLIT
; IS THE POINT: upstream's `core.Same(newTypes, types)` fork decides whether a NEW
; intersection is minted or the input comes back, and that is exactly the
; distinction no other artifact can see. 4 = a new intersection, 5 = every
; constituent answered itself. Reading 4 where the reference would answer 5 is a
; fresh type where an identity was owed, which breaks a memo rather than a name.
define WithThisEvent
(
    in_id: int
    arg_id: int
    out_id: int
    arm: int
    t: ref[Type]?
)

; ── ContextualInfo (slice 99) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; One entry of c.contextualInfos — the CONTEXTUAL TYPE STACK, and it is a stack
; rather than a table because the same node can be asked twice with different
; answers: `pushCachedContextualType` records what a node's contextual type WAS
; while its own check runs, and `findContextualNode` walking the stack is what
; makes the answer for a nested literal the OUTER literal's and not a fresh
; computation.
;
; ★★ `is_cache` is the reference's own third slot and it is not decoration: a
; cached entry is consulted only for a request with NO ContextFlags, because the
; flags are what a cached answer cannot honour. This slice pushes only cached
; entries — nothing here pushes an explicit contextual type — so the bit has one
; value today, and it is carried rather than folded away because the arm that
; reads it is written (get_contextual_type's first line) and a folded bit would
; make that arm read as unconditional.
;
; ★ NOT CARRIED: c.inferenceContextInfos, the parallel stack getInferenceContext
; walks. Nothing in this port pushes one — inference is the call dimension — so
; the field would have a reader and no writer, which is §3.5be's forbidden shape.
; instantiate_contextual_type says so at its own line rather than here.
define ContextualInfo
(
    node: ref[AstNode]?
    t: ref[Type]?
    is_cache: bool
)

; ── PatternForType (slice 99) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; One row of c.patternForType, the reference's `map[*Type]*ast.Node`. It is an
; ASSOCIATION LIST for TypeInstantiation's reason and a stronger one: the map has
; at most one row per object literal IN A DESTRUCTURING PATTERN of the unit being
; checked, which over the whole stage-2 corpus is a two-digit number.
;
; ★★★ ITS WRITER IS UNREACHABLE TODAY AND IT IS KEPT ANYWAY, which is the opposite
; of §3.5be and needs its reason said at the line. check_object_literal writes a row
; for a literal that IS an assignment target — and such a literal never arrives
; here: checkBinaryExpression sends it to checkDestructuringAssignment, a row this
; port stops at (`check-destructuring-assignment 211`). The second writer,
; getTypeFromBindingPattern, stops too. So the READER can only answer null.
;
; ★★ The alternative is to freeze `contextual_type_has_pattern` at false, and that
; is exactly slice 97's and slice 98's finding three — a flag frozen at what one
; caller happens to pass. The map is what makes the day the destructuring chapter
; lands a change to ONE stop rather than a hunt through this function.
define PatternForType
(
    t: ref[Type]?
    pattern: ref[AstNode]?
)

; ── ObjLitEvent (slice 99) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ THE TWELFTH INSTRUMENT, AND IT EXISTS BECAUSE THIS CHAPTER'S PRODUCT IS A
; TYPE THE PRINTER FLATTENS. An object literal's type is an ANONYMOUS type: it has
; no name, so `type_to_string` answers the printer's rendering of its MEMBERS —
; and three of the four things this slice can get wrong do not move that string.
; The property SYMBOL's flags (an optional property made by a default value), the
; type's OBJECT FLAGS (fresh, JSLiteral, the pattern bit), and the IDENTITY of the
; type — which is what every later comparison in this checker is made of — are all
; invisible in the T section, and the fourth (the member's TYPE) is visible only
; where the unit's whole check completes.
;
; ★★ FIVE COLUMNS. `pos` is the literal's own start, so a row can be matched to a
; source construct without the printer; `type_id` is the identity; `object_flags`
; is the bit set the three flag decisions land in; `prop_count` is how many
; members reached the table (which a spread bail moves and nothing else does); and
; `arm` says which of createObjectLiteralType's three call sites produced this row
; — 0 the tail, 1 the pre-spread flush, 2 the empty-object remap — plus 3 for the
; expando bail, which builds its type without a properties table at all. A literal
; with no spread writes exactly one row with arm 0.
;
; ★ A row is written where a TYPE IS MADE, not where the function is entered — so
; the EXPANDO BAIL writes one too, under arm 3, and a check that stopped writes
; none, which is the stop log's job.
define ObjLitEvent
(
    pos: int
    type_id: int
    object_flags: int
    prop_count: int
    arm: int
    t: ref[Type]?
)

; ── FnEvent (slice 100) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ THE THIRTEENTH INSTRUMENT, AND IT EXISTS BECAUSE THIS CHAPTER'S PRODUCT IS
; SPLIT ACROSS THREE PLACES NO EXISTING PIN LOOKS AT. The type it answers is the
; SYMBOL's, which slice 86's `--members` already prints; the diagnostics it adds
; are diagcheck's; and the body walk it releases is the stop log's. What none of
; the three can see is the DECISION this chapter makes — whether the function was
; judged context sensitive, whether a contextual signature was found, and which
; of the two parameter-assignment arms ran — and those three bits are exactly
; what a wrong port of getContextualSignature moves without moving anything else.
;
; ★★ SIX COLUMNS. `pos` anchors the row to a source construct; `kind` separates
; the three shapes the chapter serves (219 function expression, 220 arrow, 175
; object-literal method), because they take different arms and a battery that
; could not tell them apart would read one row for three; `sensitive` is
; isContextSensitive's answer, the single most load-bearing predicate here;
; `ctxsig` is 1 when getContextualSignature answered a signature; `arm` says what
; contextuallyCheck did — 0 nothing (no call signature), 1
; assignNonContextualParameterTypes, 2 the inference stop, 3 the arity fork's
; stop; and `type_id` is the identity of the type this chapter ANSWERED.
;
; ★ A row is written where the function's type is ANSWERED, so a check that
; stopped writes none. That is the same rule ObjLitEvent's header states and it
; is what makes the row count a statement about completion rather than arrival —
; the stop log is the other half.
define FnEvent
(
    pos: int
    kind: int
    sensitive: int
    ctxsig: int
    arm: int
    type_id: int
)

; ── RelEvent (slice 101) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ THE FOURTEENTH INSTRUMENT, AND THE ARGUMENT FOR IT IS THAT THIS CHAPTER'S
; MOST COMMON PRODUCT IS AN ABSENCE. A relation that answers TRUE emits no
; diagnostic, writes no type, moves no member table and — since the stop it
; replaced is gone — leaves NOTHING in the stop log either. So the whole
; `is_simple_type_related_to` table, fifteen arms of it, is invisible to every
; instrument this port has: a control that makes an arm answer TRUE where the
; reference answers FALSE moves a diagcheck count only if the comparison also
; happened to be one the port could report on, and a control that breaks an arm
; the other way moves nothing at all.
;
; ★★ FOUR COLUMNS, and the fourth is the one the battery reads. `pos` anchors the
; row to the error node the site handed in — the same anchor the diagnostic would
; carry, so a row and a `C` line can be lined up by eye; `source_flags` and
; `target_flags` are the two TypeFlags words, which is what the arms are written
; over and what tells one population from another without a name table; and
; `verdict` is 1 RELATED, 0 NOT RELATED (the path that reports), 2 COULD NOT
; ANSWER — a stop under the comparison, where the reference has no third state and
; this port must have one. ★`pos` is the error node's OWN position and not the
; diagnostic's span (which `error_range_for_node` narrows to the declaration's
; name), so a row and a `C` line line up by proximity rather than by equality —
; enough to read them side by side, and one less thing to keep in step.
;
; ★ A row is written at the DECISION and not at the answer, which is slice 100's
; finding five arriving as a rule rather than as a repair: every call of
; `check_type_related_to_and_optionally_elaborate` writes exactly one, whatever it
; concluded, because a pin that only records success cannot separate the two ways
; of failing.
define RelEvent
(
    pos: int
    source_flags: int
    target_flags: int
    verdict: int
)

; ── KindEvent (slice 102) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ THE FIFTEENTH INSTRUMENT, AND ITS ARGUMENT IS SLICE 101'S ONE PRODUCT ON.
; The RELPIN writes at `check_type_related_to_and_optionally_elaborate`, which is
; the only entry the three assignment sites use — and NOT the entry this chapter
; uses. `isTypeAssignableToKindEx` reaches the relation through `isTypeRelatedTo`,
; below that pin, and most of its calls never reach the relation at all: the
; `source.flags & kind` fast path answers them, and an answer there writes no
; diagnostic, no type, no relation row and nothing in the stop log. So the ten
; lines this slice is named after were invisible to all fourteen instruments.
;
; ★★★ FIVE COLUMNS, AND THE ARM IS THE ONE THE BATTERY READS. `source_flags` and
; `kind` are the two words the function is written over — a kind is not a type, so
; there is no name to print and the flags ARE the identity. `strict` separates the
; two callers that are one line apart in the reference and answer differently on
; the commonest input there is. `arm` says WHICH of the twelve paths answered: 0
; the flags fast path, 1 the strict refusal, 2…11 the nine relation disjuncts in
; the reference's order, 12 the fall-through. `verdict` is 1 TRUE, 0 FALSE, 2 COULD
; NOT ANSWER — the third state the reference has no room for.
;
; ★★ THE ARM IS WHAT MAKES A CONTROL GATEABLE AT ALL. Breaking a disjunct usually
; moves NOTHING anywhere else: the arms are disjoint tests against one `kind` word,
; the caller reads one bool, and under this port every disjunct that reaches the
; relation stops rather than answering. A verdict column alone would read the same
; for a broken arm and for an arm with no input; the arm column tells them apart.
;
; ★ Written at the DECISION, like the RELPIN and for the same reason (slice 100's
; finding five as a rule): every call writes exactly one row, whatever it concluded.
define KindEvent
(
    source_flags: int
    kind: int
    strict: int
    arm: int
    verdict: int
)

; ── ForkEvent (slice 103) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ THE SIXTEENTH INSTRUMENT, AND ITS ARGUMENT IS THAT THIS SLICE REMOVES THE
; ONLY THING THAT COULD SEE ITS OWN POPULATION. Until now the structured branch
; of `isRelatedToEx` was ONE `record_unported`, so every comparison that reached
; it was a row in the stop histogram and the work list was the instrument. This
; slice answers most of them — and an answer there writes no diagnostic, no type
; and, once the stop is gone, nothing in the stop log either. ★The RELPIN cannot
; stand in: it sits at `check_type_related_to_and_optionally_elaborate`, and the
; largest caller of this branch is `checkArithmeticOperandType`, which enters the
; relation through `isTypeRelatedTo` two levels below that pin. The KINDPIN
; cannot either — that call does not go through `isTypeAssignableToKindEx` at all,
; which is slice 102's own finding one.
;
; ★★★ FIVE COLUMNS, AND `route` IS THE ONE THE BATTERY READS. `source_flags` and
; `target_flags` are the two TypeFlags words the branch is written over — there is
; no node here and therefore no position, exactly as for the KINDPIN. `route` says
; which of the four ways out of the FORK was taken: 0 the excess-property stop,
; 1 the common-property stop, 2 `unionOrIntersectionRelatedTo` — the half this
; slice ports — and 3 `recursiveTypeRelatedTo`, the half it does not. `branch`
; says which arm of `unionOrIntersectionRelatedTo` answered (0 source-union
; comparable, 1 source-union, 2 target-union, 3 target-intersection, 4 the
; comparable intersection hoist, 5 the source-intersection tail, 6 the two origin
; fast paths), and is -1 on every route but 2. `verdict` is 1 TRUE, 0 FALSE, 2
; COULD NOT ANSWER — the third state the reference has no room for.
;
; ★★ THE ROUTE AND THE BRANCH ARE TWO COLUMNS RATHER THAN ONE BECAUSE THEY ANSWER
; DIFFERENT QUESTIONS, and the second is the one slice 102's closing paragraph got
; wrong. The ROUTE says how much of the population this slice actually reaches —
; route 3 is what is left for the recursion, and a count there is the honest price
; of the successor. The BRANCH says whether a ported arm has any INPUT: the arms
; are disjoint tests on two flag words, every one of them returns a plain bool to
; the same caller, and a verdict column alone would read identically for a broken
; arm and for an arm nothing in the corpus takes.
;
; ★ Written at the DECISION and exactly once per call of the branch, whatever it
; concluded — slice 100's finding five as a rule, the same one the RELPIN and the
; KINDPIN carry.
define ForkEvent
(
    source_flags: int
    target_flags: int
    route: int
    branch: int
    verdict: int
)

; ── CallEvent (slice 108) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ THE SEVENTEENTH INSTRUMENT, AND ITS ARGUMENT IS THE ONE THE FORKPIN MADE
; ONE CHAPTER EARLIER: this slice's product is a SIGNATURE, and a signature is
; invisible to every gate this port has. diagcheck does not move — the four codes
; the chapter can emit are all on paths where resolution does NOT happen, and the
; corpus's calls resolve — the checker yardstick cannot carry a call's type until
; getTypeOfNode's expression arm lands, the stop log only shows where the walk
; went NEXT, and the RELPIN sees the argument comparisons without saying which
; call they belonged to or whether it ended up applicable. So the chapter's
; ANSWER needs a row of its own.
;
; ★★★ SIX COLUMNS, AND THE LAST THREE ARE WHAT SEPARATES A BROKEN ARITY CHECK FROM
; A BROKEN RELATION. `pos` is the call node's, which is what a `C` line would
; carry, so a row and a diagnostic line up (the RELPIN's own reason). `kind` tells
; a call from a `new`. `verdict` is the resolution's outcome: 0 COULD NOT ANSWER,
; 1 a real CANDIDATE, 2 `anySignature` — the untyped call — 3 `unknownSignature`,
; the error call, and 4 `silentNeverSignature`. `arg_count`, `param_count` and
; `min_args` are the three numbers hasCorrectArity compares, and they are recorded
; whatever the verdict: a candidate that survives with the wrong arity moves the
; last two, and an arity check that rejects one it should accept moves the verdict
; while leaving all three standing.
;
; ★★★ THE CHECKSUM IS LOAD-BEARING, AND THE PAIR THAT PROVES IT IS g19 AGAINST g21:
; two patches — a non-generic candidate handed type arguments, and a call with too few
; arguments — produce the IDENTICAL five numbers `244 23 4 4 213` and differ only in
; the checksum. ★★★**And g21 against g24 is identical INCLUDING the checksum**, which
; refutes the pair the battery originally named: with the minimum forced to 0 the test
; `argCount >= 0` always passes, which is exactly what removing the test does, so the
; two patches are the same patch for this corpus. A pair claimed as a discriminator is
; a proof that two spellings coincide until a number separates them.
;
; ★★ WRITTEN AT `resolve_signature` AND EXACTLY ONCE PER CALL NODE, which is the
; only place in this chapter where every outcome passes: the memo is one level up
; in get_resolved_signature, so a second ask replays a mark and writes nothing —
; and that is deliberate, because a pin that counted memo HITS would report a
; number about the cache and not about the chapter.
;
; ★★★ AND IT CANNOT SEE WHAT THE CALLER DOES WITH THE ANSWER, which g34 measured on
; this slice's own fix: `check_deprecated_signature` runs in check_call_expression
; AFTER get_resolved_signature has answered, so putting its row back costs every
; resolved call its TYPE and leaves this pin UNMOVED to the checksum. The STOP LOG is
; what gates that half, and a pin written at the resolution is not a substitute for
; it.
; resolveCall's own state, slice 131, completed by slice 138 — the reference's
; CallState minus only `signatureHelpTrailingComma`, which needs a CheckMode the
; language service alone sets. `arg_check_mode` is not the only MUTABLE slot any
; more (the three failure slots below move too), but it is the oldest: chooseOverload
; clears SkipContextSensitive on the first candidate that gets that far, and the
; reference's comment says it must stay cleared *"for any subsequent candidates"* —
; which is why it lives in the state and not in a parameter.
define CallState
(
    node: ref[AstNode]
    args: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    candidates: ref[Array[ref[Signature]?]]
    type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    arg_check_mode: int
    is_single_non_generic_candidate: bool

    ; ── the three FAILURE slots, slice 138 ──────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THEY ARE WRITTEN BY chooseOverload AND READ ONLY AFTER IT HAS FAILED,
    ; which is why they are state and not a return value: the reference's comment
    ; over them says what they are for — *"if overload resolution or type argument
    ; inference fails, we want to report the best error possible. The best error is
    ; one which says that an argument was not assignable to a parameter"* — so a
    ; candidate that got as far as the argument comparison outranks one that failed
    ; on type-argument arity, which outranks one that failed on plain arity.
    ;
    ; ★★ `candidates_for_argument_error` is a LIST because the report needs the
    ; LAST one and the COUNT: more than one is what turns each of that candidate's
    ; own diagnostics into TS2769 (`No overload matches this call`).
    candidates_for_argument_error: ref[Array[ref[Signature]?]]?
    candidate_for_argument_arity_error: ref[Signature]?
    candidate_for_type_argument_error: ref[Signature]?
)

define CallEvent
(
    pos: int
    kind: int
    verdict: int
    arg_count: int
    param_count: int
    min_args: int
)

; ── ObjectLiteralState (slice 99) ───────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ `createObjectLiteralType` IS A CLOSURE IN THE REFERENCE AND IT IS CALLED
; THREE TIMES OVER MUTATING CAPTURES, so §3.9e's rule — one method per call site —
; does not apply: the three calls are the SAME code over DIFFERENT state, which is
; the shape §3.5g and §3.5k already settled the other way. The captures become a
; record and the closure becomes a method taking it.
;
; ★★ WHICH SLOTS ARE HERE IS DECIDED BY THE CLOSURE'S BODY, not by the function's
; locals: `spread`, `member` and `computed_name_type` are re-derived per property
; and never read by the closure, so they stay locals of the loop.
;
; ★ `properties_array` is not nullable and the other table is: the reference sets
; `propertiesArray = nil` at a spread flush and `allPropertiesTable` exists only
; under strictNullChecks. Clearing a Scaly Array in place is what the flush does
; instead, so the slot can promise a table.
define ObjectLiteralState
(
    properties_table: ref[SymbolTable]?
    properties_array: ref[Array[ref[Symbol]?]]
    all_properties_table: ref[SymbolTable]?
    offset: int
    object_flags: int
    pattern_with_computed_properties: bool
    has_computed_string_property: bool
    has_computed_number_property: bool
    has_computed_symbol_property: bool
    contextual_type: ref[Type]?
    contextual_type_has_pattern: bool
    in_destructuring_pattern: bool
    in_const_context: bool
    check_flags: int
)
{
    init()
    {
        set properties_table: null
        set properties_array: &Array[ref[Symbol]?]^this()
        set all_properties_table: null
        set offset: 0
        set object_flags: ObjectFlagsNone
        set pattern_with_computed_properties: false
        set has_computed_string_property: false
        set has_computed_number_property: false
        set has_computed_symbol_property: false
        set contextual_type: null
        set contextual_type_has_pattern: false
        set in_destructuring_pattern: false
        set in_const_context: false
        set check_flags: 0
    }
}

; ── JsxAttributesState (slice 119) ──────────────────────────────────────────
;
; createJsxAttributesTypeFromAttributesProperty's locals, as a record for
; ObjectLiteralState's reason — the s178 alloca-pool rule — and because the
; reference's two CLOSURES (`createJsxAttributesType` and
; `parentHasSemanticJsxChildren`) capture them; Scaly has no closure over a
; caller's locals, so the capture becomes a parameter (§3.5g's shape, one
; dimension over).
; ── the JSX elaboration element (slice 162) ─────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's `JsxElaborationElement`, minus its fourth field: `createDiagnostic
; func(prop) *ast.Diagnostic` is a CODE here, for the reason elaborate_element's
; header gives — a diagnostic in this port is a code and a span, so the closure's
; three interpolation captures have no reader.
;
; ★ An EMPTY element is one whose `error_node` is null (a whitespace-only JsxText),
; and generate_jsx_children never puts one in its list — it counts a member offset
; instead, which is what keeps the tuple positions contiguous.
define JsxElaborationElement
(
    error_node: ref[AstNode]?
    inner_expression: ref[AstNode]?
    name_type: ref[Type]?
    text_child_code: int
    text_child_args: ref[Array[String]]?
)

; The four fixed predicates filter_type_by_jsx_children_kind stands for — §3.18's
; tag, because upstream passes each as a closure and a predicate cannot travel as a
; value here. The pair a caller uses is chosen by whether a global `Iterable` exists
; (see elaborate_jsx_components), never mixed.
; ── slice 174: forEachProperty's four callbacks ─────────────────────────────
;
; §3.18's tag again — upstream passes each as a closure. ★The ValidOverride one is
; not a variant of InClassDerivedFrom: it is asked of the TARGET property and
; answers about the SOURCE, so its body RE-ENTERS the walk on the other symbol,
; which is why the walk cannot be folded into either caller.
define ForEachPropertyValidOverride: int 0
define ForEachPropertyInClassDerivedFrom: int 1
define ForEachPropertyDerivedFromDeclaring: int 2
define ForEachPropertyNonMethodDeclaration: int 3

define JsxChildFilterAssignable: int 0
define JsxChildFilterNotAssignable: int 1
define JsxChildFilterArrayLike: int 2
define JsxChildFilterNotArrayLike: int 3

define JsxAttributesState
(
    attributes_table: ref[SymbolTable]
    all_attributes_table: ref[SymbolTable]?
    attributes_symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    attribute_parent: ref[AstNode]?
    spread: ref[Type]?
    has_spread_any_type: bool
    explicitly_specify_children_attribute: bool
    contextual_type: ref[Type]?
    object_flags: int
    ; ★★★ THE ACCUMULATOR OF THE INVALID SPREADS, AND IT IS NOT A SECOND `spread`.
    ; A spread whose type is not a valid spread type is REPORTED and then folded
    ; into this slot instead, and the tail intersects it with the ordinary spread
    ; result — so an `{...notAnObject}` attribute contributes its own type to the
    ; attributes type rather than being dropped. Nil means *no invalid spread was
    ; seen*, which is a third state neither `spread` nor the any-flag can carry.
    type_to_intersect: ref[Type]?
)
{
    init()
    {
        set attributes_table: SymbolTable.create(Page.get(this))
        set all_attributes_table: null
        set attributes_symbol: null
        set attribute_parent: null
        set spread: null
        set has_spread_any_type: false
        set type_to_intersect: null
        set explicitly_specify_children_attribute: false
        set contextual_type: null
        set object_flags: ObjectFlagsNone
    }
}

; ── ContextFlags (slice 99) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's five-member bit family, declared WHOLE for
; TypeSystemPropertyName's reason: they are the arms of one MASK and a partial
; declaration is an invitation to renumber the rest. Two are read here — None at
; every call site of this slice, and SkipBindingPatterns in
; getContextualTypeForInitializerExpression — and the other three are read by the
; three call sites named at get_apparent_type_of_contextual_type.
define ContextFlagsNone: int 0
define ContextFlagsSignature: int 1 << 0
define ContextFlagsNoConstraints: int 1 << 1
define ContextFlagsIgnoreNodeInferences: int 1 << 2
define ContextFlagsSkipBindingPatterns: int 1 << 3

; ── DeclarationMeaning (slice 99) ───────────────────────────────────────────
;
; checkGrammarObjectLiteralExpression's `seen` map values, declared WHOLE and in
; the reference's order for TypeSystemPropertyName's reason: they are the values of
; ONE map, and a partial declaration is an invitation to renumber the rest.
; PrivateStatic has no writer in this slice — its map is checkGrammarClassLikeDeclaration's
; — and it is declared because the two combined masks below name their neighbours.
define DeclarationMeaningGetAccessor: int 1 << 0
define DeclarationMeaningSetAccessor: int 1 << 1
define DeclarationMeaningPropertyAssignment: int 1 << 2
define DeclarationMeaningMethod: int 1 << 3
define DeclarationMeaningPrivateStatic: int 1 << 4
define DeclarationMeaningGetOrSetAccessor: int DeclarationMeaningGetAccessor | DeclarationMeaningSetAccessor
define DeclarationMeaningPropertyAssignmentOrMethod: int DeclarationMeaningPropertyAssignment | DeclarationMeaningMethod

; One row of checkGrammarObjectLiteralExpression's `seen` map. A LIST rather than
; a SymbolTable because the key is a property NAME the grammar check derives and
; not a symbol name, and because an object literal with enough members for a hash
; to matter does not exist: the largest in the stage-2 corpus has 218.
define SeenPropertyName
(
    name: Slice[char]
    meaning: int
)

define MemberEvent
(
    ; 0 = the type header, 1 = one property of it, 2 = one SIGNATURE of it.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE SIGNATURE LINE IS SLICE 87'S AND IT IS THE SAME ARGUMENT SLICE 86
    ; MADE FOR THE RECORD ITSELF. That slice gave the members table a reader
    ; because nine of its rows were otherwise silent; this one produces a
    ; SIGNATURE list — the synthesized `new C(): C` every class without a
    ; constructor gets — and the header's count column cannot tell one signature
    ; from another. `abstract class C {}`, `class C<T> {}` and `class C {}` resolve
    ; to member logs that are IDENTICAL to the byte under the count alone, so the
    ; Abstract flag and the LocalTypeParameters would have been three more rows
    ; measuring the absence of a reader.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE CAVEAT THE SAME BATTERY FOUND: THIS LOG IS WRITTEN INSIDE
    ; set_structured_type_members, SO AN `R` LINE RECORDS A CALL, NOT A STATE. Two
    ; of slice 87's rows — the early call removed, the inheriting branch forced
    ; taken — come back RED with every `P` and every `G` line identical and one `R`
    ; line more or fewer. **A pin that logs at a call site is part call trace and
    ; part state pin, and the colour cannot say which half fired.** The reading rule
    ; is that an `R`-only diff is evidence the call ran and evidence of nothing
    ; else; a claim about the TABLE needs a `P` or a `G` line to move.
    kind: int
    a: int
    b: int
    c: int
    d: int
    name: Slice[char]
)


; UnionType, slice 94. Two slots, and the second of them has no writer in this
; port — which is a statement about what a union can BE here rather than an
; omission.
;
; ★★★ `origin` IS THE DENORMALIZED UNION AND IT IS WRITTEN SINCE SLICE 135 — THE
; NOTE THAT STOOD HERE PREDICTED ITS OWN EXPIRY AND NAMED THE TELL EXACTLY.
; It read: *"the day a second producer arrives — getUnionType's own `alias`
; parameter is the obvious one — this block stops being dead, and the tell will
; be a union printing its constituents where the reference prints a name."* The
; second producer is get_declared_type_of_enum, which passes the enum's own
; symbol as the alias of the union of its members, and the tell arrived exactly
; as written: `E | F` over two numeric enums printed `E.a | F.c | E.b | F.d`
; (exponentiationOperatorWithEnumUnion, arithmeticOperatorWithEnumUnion) — the
; four members interleaved by value.
;
; So both of addTypesToUnion's marks are live: `t.alias != nil` (an enum) and
; `u.origin != nil` (the intersection cross-product, since slice 121). The
; readers are CompareTypes' union arm, the printer's substitution,
; get_constituent_count, map_type_ex and filter_type's per-origin refilter —
; the last of which is the one deliberate gap, and filter_type_outside_flags
; carries the condition under which its `null` IS the reference's answer.
;
; ★ NOT CARRIED, with its reader: `regularType` — getRegularTypeOfLiteralType's
; union memo, which is `mapType` over the constituents and therefore a chapter
; away (the tag `map-type` already names it in get_widened_literal_type).
; `resolvedProperties`, `resolvedIndexType` and the rest of the reference's
; UnionType are the members dimension.
define UnionTypeData
(
    types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    origin: ref[Type]?

    ; ── slice 127 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The reference's `propertyCache` / `propertyCacheWithoutFunctionPropertyAugment`
    ; (both on UnionOrIntersectionType) and `resolvedReducedType`. The two caches
    ; are NOT an optimisation: getUnionOrIntersectionProperty must answer the SAME
    ; symbol object for the same name, because a synthesized property's identity
    ; is what the relation's caches and `mergedInstantiations` compare.
    property_cache: ref[SymbolTable]?
    property_cache_no_augment: ref[SymbolTable]?
    resolved_properties: ref[Array[ref[Symbol]?]]?
    resolved_reduced_type: ref[Type]?

    ; ★★★ A UNION HAS A StructuredMembers RECORD TOO, and forgetting it is an
    ; exit 21 rather than a wrong answer: set_structured_type_members unwraps
    ; `structured_members_of(t)` right after attaching, and attach_structured_
    ; members had no arm for a union — so the attach was a no-op and the unwrap
    ; met the null. `resolveUnionTypeMembers` fills exactly this record.
    resolved: ref[StructuredMembers]?

    ; ── slice 130: the KEY PROPERTY and its map ─────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The reference's `keyPropertyName` and `constituentMap`, memoised together
    ; because they are computed together — `computeKeyPropertyNameAndMap` answers
    ; both or neither. `key_property_computed` is the memo's *filled* bit, since
    ; the ANSWER may legitimately be *no key property* (upstream's
    ; InternalSymbolNameMissing) and a null name cannot say which of the two it
    ; is. The map is an ASSOCIATION LIST rather than a Go map: it is built once
    ; and read by identity, and a union large enough to have one has at most a
    ; few dozen entries.
    key_property_computed: bool
    key_property: Slice[char]
    constituent_map: ref[Array[ref[ConstituentMapEntry]?]]?
)
{
    init(ts: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?, org: ref[Type]?)
    {
        set types: ts
        set origin: org
        set property_cache: null
        set property_cache_no_augment: null
        set resolved_properties: null
        set resolved_reduced_type: null
        set resolved: null
        set key_property_computed: false
        set key_property: Slice[char]()
        set constituent_map: null
    }
}

; One row of a union's constituentMap — the constituent a given literal key type
; selects. `value` is the reference's `unknownType` sentinel where two
; constituents share the key, which is what makes a duplicate answer *no unique
; constituent* rather than the first of them.
define ConstituentMapEntry
(
    key: ref[Type]?
    value: ref[Type]?
)
; ── IntersectionTypeData (slice 121) ────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's `IntersectionType` embeds `UnionOrIntersectionType`, whose one
; field this port needs is `types`.
;
; ★★ ONE SLOT IS NOT CARRIED AND IT NAMES ITS READER.
; `uniqueLiteralFilledInstantiation` is read only by the inference's
; unique-literal fill, which has no producer here.
;
; ★★★ SLICE 155 RETIRED THE OTHER ONE, AND ITS DEFERRAL WAS ARGUED FROM A STOP.
; The note read *"it is not carried, because getApparentTypeOfIntersectionType's
; memo is keyed on the `this` ARGUMENT and this port's get_type_with_this_argument
; answers without one at the only caller that reaches here"* — and the reason it
; answered without one is that its INTERSECTION arm was a stop, so the sentence
; was a claim about the port's own hole rather than about the reference. §3.5gh's
; class, one record over: a premise that expires when a stop is removed.
define IntersectionTypeData
(
    types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?

    ; The same two caches — see UnionTypeData's note.
    property_cache: ref[SymbolTable]?
    property_cache_no_augment: ref[SymbolTable]?
    resolved_properties: ref[Array[ref[Symbol]?]]?
    resolved: ref[StructuredMembers]?

    ; The intersection's own third slot (slice 155): getApparentTypeOfIntersectionType's
    ; memo for the `t == thisArgument` case, where the reference stores the answer
    ; on the type rather than in cachedTypes.
    resolved_apparent_type: ref[Type]?

    ; The reference's `uniqueLiteralFilledInstantiation` (slice 166) —
    ; isReducibleIntersection's one memo. ★It is on the TYPE and not in a side
    ; table because upstream put it there, and the distinction matters for once:
    ; the slot holds an instantiation of THIS intersection through the unique
    ; literal mapper, so its lifetime is the intersection's.
    unique_literal_filled_instantiation: ref[Type]?
)
{
    init(ts: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?)
    {
        set types: ts
        set property_cache: null
        set property_cache_no_augment: null
        set resolved_properties: null
        set resolved: null
        set resolved_apparent_type: null
        set unique_literal_filled_instantiation: null
    }
}

; ── IntersectionEntry ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; One row of `c.intersectionTypes`, whose key is getIntersectionKey(typeSet,
; flags, alias).
;
; ★★★ IT IS AN ENTRY RATHER THAN A BARE TYPE, WHERE THE UNION TABLE IS A BARE
; TYPE, AND THE REASON IS THAT TWO THIRDS OF THE KEY ARE NOT ON THE ANSWER. The
; `flags` are an argument and nothing records them, and a DISTRIBUTED
; intersection answers a UNION — whose own constituent list is the cross product,
; not the key. So the row carries the key beside the answer.
define IntersectionEntry
(
    key_flags: int
    key_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    t: ref[Type]?
)

; c.subtypeReductionCache, slice 135 — removeSubtypes' memo, keyed on the
; constituent LIST the way this file's other intern tables are (same length, same
; types, same order) because the reference's key is `getTypeListKey`, a string of
; the type ids in order.
;
; ★★★ IT IS NOT AN OPTIMISATION HERE EITHER. removeSubtypes runs the STRICT
; SUBTYPE relation over every ordered pair of constituents, which is the most
; expensive thing this file can ask of a union, and the reference's own depth
; guard exists because the product is quadratic. Without the memo a union that is
; reduced twice pays twice.
define SubtypeReductionRow
(
    key: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    result: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
)

; ── the instantiable payloads (slice 121) ───────────────────────────────────
;
; Six records for the six type kinds the type algebra mints. Each is the
; reference's own struct minus its embedded `ConstrainedType`, whose one field
; (`resolvedBaseConstraint`) is the base-constraint memo — a slot this port
; already keeps beside the type rather than inside it.
;
; ★★★ THEY ARRIVE TOGETHER BECAUSE THE ALGEBRA IS ONE GRAPH AND NOT SIX
; CHAPTERS. `keyof T` is an Index whose target may be a Mapped type whose
; constraint is an Index; a Conditional's extends type may be a TemplateLiteral
; whose spans are IndexedAccesses. Adding one arm at a time would mean a stop in
; the middle of every one of those, i.e. six slices that each answer nothing.

; IndexType — `keyof T`.
define IndexTypeData
(
    target: ref[Type]?
    index_flags: int
)

; IndexedAccessType — `T[K]`. ★`accessFlags` keeps only the PERSISTENT bits, which
; is the reference's own comment on the field.
define IndexedAccessTypeData
(
    object_type: ref[Type]?
    index_type: ref[Type]?
    access_flags: int
)

; TemplateLiteralType — `` `a${T}b` ``. ★`texts` is always ONE longer than
; `types`, and `types` always holds at least one element; both are the
; reference's invariants and the printer leans on the first.
define TemplateLiteralTypeData
(
    texts: ref[Array[ref[String]?]]?
    types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
)

; StringMappingType — `Uppercase<T>` and its three siblings. The intrinsic's
; SYMBOL rides on Type.symbol, which is where the reference keeps it too.
define StringMappingTypeData
(
    target: ref[Type]?
)
{
    init(tgt: ref[Type]?)
        set target: tgt
}

; SubstitutionType — a type known to satisfy a constraint, minted by the
; conditional's true branch and by NoInfer.
define SubstitutionTypeData
(
    base_type: ref[Type]?
    constraint: ref[Type]?
)

; ── IndexedAccessEntry ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; One row of `c.indexedAccessTypes`. It is an ENTRY for IntersectionEntry's
; reason and one more besides: the key holds the FULL access flags while the
; minted type keeps only the PERSISTENT ones, so the key cannot be read back off
; the answer even in principle.
; ── SubstitutionEntry (slice 136) ───────────────────────────────────────────
;
; One row of `c.substitutionTypes`, whose key upstream is the pair of TYPE IDS
; (`SubstitutionTypeKey{baseId, constraintId}`).
;
; ★ It is an ENTRY rather than a memo on the type for the reason IndexedAccessEntry
; is: the key is a PAIR and neither half is recoverable from the answer alone —
; getSubstitutionType folds three cases into `baseType` before the table is ever
; asked, so a minted substitution and a base type handed straight back are
; indistinguishable afterwards.
define SubstitutionEntry
(
    base_type: ref[Type]?
    constraint: ref[Type]?
    t: ref[Type]?
)

define IndexedAccessEntry
(
    key_flags: int
    object_type: ref[Type]?
    index_type: ref[Type]?
    t: ref[Type]?
)

; ── ConditionalRoot and ConditionalTypeData (slice 121) ─────────────────────
;
; The reference's `ConditionalRoot` is the SHARED half of a conditional type —
; one per conditional type NODE — and `ConditionalType` is the per-instantiation
; half. Keeping them apart is what makes the tail recursion in getConditionalType
; possible at all: the loop swaps the ROOT and keeps going.
;
; ★★★ `instantiations` IS AN ASSOCIATION LIST SINCE SLICE 125, AND THE NOTE THAT
; STOOD HERE IS ITS EXPIRY. It read *"IS ONE SLOT AND NOT A MAP … the only reader
; — getConditionalTypeInstantiation — is not ported"*, which was true and named
; the day it stopped being: instantiating a conditional type is how a mapped
; type's template reaches its own arms, and one slot cannot hold two
; instantiations of one root. The key is the reference's own —
; (typeArguments, alias, forConstraint) — held as an entry for §3.16's reason.
define ConditionalRoot
(
    node: ref[AstNode]
    check_type: ref[Type]
    extends_type: ref[Type]
    is_distributive: bool
    infer_type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    outer_type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    alias: ref[TypeAlias]?
    instantiations: ref[Array[ref[ConditionalInstantiation]?]]?
    ; `instantiations` by argument hash (HashChain)
    instantiation_chain: ref[HashChain]?
)
{
    init(n: ref[AstNode], ct: ref[Type], et: ref[Type], dist: bool, ip: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?, otp: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?, al: ref[TypeAlias]?)
    {
        set node: n
        set check_type: ct
        set extends_type: et
        set is_distributive: dist
        set infer_type_parameters: ip
        set outer_type_parameters: otp
        set alias: al
        set instantiations: null
        set instantiation_chain: null
    }
}

; ★★ THREE OF THE REFERENCE'S TEN SLOTS ARE NOT CARRIED AND EACH NAMES ITS
; READER: `resolvedInferredTrueType` (getInferredTrueTypeFromConditionalType,
; whose only caller is the relation), `resolvedDefaultConstraint` and
; `resolvedConstraintOfDistributive` (the two constraint memos, read by
; getConstraintOfConditionalType — the constraint chapter).
define ConditionalTypeData
(
    root: ref[ConditionalRoot]?
    check_type: ref[Type]?
    extends_type: ref[Type]?
    resolved_true_type: ref[Type]?
    resolved_false_type: ref[Type]?

    ; ★ The three CONSTRAINT memos, slice 136. They are separate slots upstream for
    ; the reason the two above are: each is the answer of a different question about
    ; the same conditional, and `resolved_constraint_of_distributive` additionally
    ; uses `noConstraintType` as a THIRD state — *asked, and the answer is nothing* —
    ; which a bare null cannot express.
    resolved_default_constraint: ref[Type]?
    resolved_constraint_of_distributive: ref[Type]?
    resolved_inferred_true_type: ref[Type]?

    mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?
    combined_mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?
)

; EvolvingArrayType — the element types seen so far in an `x.push(value)` or
; `x[n] = value` operation along the control flow graph, and the manifest array
; type they are finalized into. The reference's own comment on getEvolvingArrayType
; is the boundary: an evolving array type NEVER escapes getFlowTypeOfReference.
;
; ★★ `final_array_type` IS A LAZY MEMO ON A PAYLOAD ARM, so it takes §3.14's
; copy/mutate/write-back setter (`set_final_array_type`) rather than a direct
; write — a `when` binding is a copy, and the direct spelling is a silent wrong
; store.
define EvolvingArrayTypeData
(
    element_type: ref[Type]?
    final_array_type: ref[Type]?
)

; ── InstantiationExpressionType (slice 181) ────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's `InstantiationExpressionType` is an ObjectType with ONE extra
; slot, the `typeof X<A>` node the type was minted from. ★It is written as its own
; arm rather than folded into Anonymous because getInstantiationExpressionType
; sets the flag ANONYMOUS|INSTANTIATION_EXPRESSION and newObjectType's switch
; tests the second bit FIRST — the two arms are distinguishable only by order, and
; a fold would make the node slot unreachable for exactly the types that carry it.
;
; ★★★ THE FOUR ObjectType SLOTS ARE NOT DECORATION AND LEAVING THEM OUT COST AN
; `exit 21` ON THE FIRST UNIT THAT BUILT ONE. Upstream EMBEDS ObjectType, so an
; instantiation-expression type has `resolved`, `target`, `mapper` and
; `instantiations` like any other; a one-slot arm here made
; attach_structured_members fall through every `when`, and the very next line —
; `structured_members_of(t) as ref[StructuredMembers]` — met the null it had just
; failed to write. **A new TypeData arm owes an arm to every accessor that
; switches over the union, and the ones that matter are the ones that WRITE.**
define InstantiationExpressionTypeData
(
    resolved: ref[StructuredMembers]?
    target: ref[Type]?
    mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?
    instantiations: ref[Array[ref[ObjectInstantiation]?]]?
    node: ref[AstNode]?
)

; ── UniqueESSymbolType (slice 146) ─────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's `UniqueESSymbolType`, whose whole content is a NAME:
; `__@<symbol name>@<symbol id>`, minted by getESSymbolLikeTypeForNode and read
; by getPropertyNameFromType, tryGetNameFromType and the printer.
;
; ★★★ IT IS AN ARM RATHER THAN A SLOT ON `Type` FOR THE REASON `intrinsic_name`
; IS THE OPPOSITE: that one is asked of ANY type by type_to_string, so a `choose`
; there would have to name every arm to answer *not an intrinsic*; this one has
; three readers and every one of them has already tested
; `flags & TypeFlagsUniqueESSymbol`, so the arm is exactly the reference's
; `AsUniqueESSymbolType()` — a cast that is refused where the bit is not set.
;
; ★ The name is a `Slice[char]` on the CHECKER's page and not a `String`, which
; is what every other synthetic symbol name in this port is (Binder.internal_name,
; Checker.decimal_name): it becomes a property NAME, and a name here is a
; (pointer, length) pair that must outlive the call that made it.
define UniqueESSymbolTypeData
(
    name: Slice[char]
)

; ── TypeAlias ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's `TypeAlias` — the (symbol, typeArguments) pair every producer in
; the type algebra takes as its last parameter and writes onto the type it mints.
;
; ★★★ IT IS A RECORD HERE EVEN THOUGH `Type` KEEPS THE PAIR AS TWO SLOTS, and the
; asymmetry is the reference's own: upstream `Type.alias` IS a `*TypeAlias`, but
; this port's two slots predate the parameter and their note's argument (one
; producer, written straight after the construction) still holds for the STORE.
; What changed is that the alias now has to be PASSED — through getUnionTypeEx,
; getIntersectionTypeEx, getConditionalType, instantiateTypeWithAlias and the two
; intern keys — and a pair of parameters threaded through nine recursions is the
; shape §3.5dj warns about, not a saving.
define TypeAlias
(
    symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
)

define TypeData union
(
    None: NoTypeData
    Interface: ref[InterfaceTypeData]
    Reference: ReferenceTypeData
    TypeParameter: TypeParameterTypeData
    Anonymous: AnonymousTypeData
    Mapped: ref[MappedTypeData]
    ReverseMapped: ref[ReverseMappedTypeData]
    Literal: LiteralTypeData
    Union: ref[UnionTypeData]
    Intersection: ref[IntersectionTypeData]
    Index: IndexTypeData
    IndexedAccess: IndexedAccessTypeData
    TemplateLiteral: TemplateLiteralTypeData
    StringMapping: StringMappingTypeData
    Substitution: SubstitutionTypeData
    Conditional: ref[ConditionalTypeData]
    EvolvingArray: EvolvingArrayTypeData
    UniqueESSymbol: UniqueESSymbolTypeData
    InstantiationExpression: InstantiationExpressionTypeData
)

; ── Type ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's `Type` is a tag struct plus an interface-typed payload
; (IntrinsicType, LiteralType, ObjectType, UnionType, …), which is the shape
; CLAUDE.md §3.1 turns into a sum type — the union above.
;
; ★ `id` is not compared by the yardstick: the dump prints a type's STRING, never
; its identity. It is kept because the reference's ids are assigned in creation
; order and several of its caches are keyed on them, so a port that dropped the
; counter would have to invent one later at every such site.
;
; ★★ `intrinsic_name` STAYS ON THE TYPE rather than moving into an intrinsic arm,
; and that is the same divergence ast.scaly makes for `symbol`/`parent`: the
; reader is type_to_string, which asks it of any type it is handed, and a `choose`
; there would have to name every arm to answer "not an intrinsic". A non-intrinsic
; type carries null, which is what the reference's `AsIntrinsicType()` would refuse
; to give at all — the yardstick is the check, exactly as it is for a node's symbol.
;
; ★ `symbol` is the reference's `Type.symbol`, set by newObjectType and
; newTypeParameter. `checker` is not carried — it is this port's `this`.
;
; ★★★ `alias` IS CARRIED SINCE SLICE 111, AND THE NOTE THAT STOOD HERE — *"read by
; typeToString's alias arm, which reports"* — WAS TRUE UNTIL THAT ARM STOPPED
; REPORTING. The moment the object arm could NAME a type literal, the alias arm
; became the difference between `T` and `{ x: number; }` for every
; `type T = { … }` in the corpus, and a structural name where the reference prints
; an alias is a well-formed wrong answer rather than a missing one. §3.5as: the
; reader that makes a deferred field observable may be inside the port.
;
; ★★ IT IS TWO SLOTS AND NOT A RECORD, because the reference's `TypeAlias` has
; exactly two fields and only one producer here fills them. They take no `init`
; parameter, on Signature.target's precedent: upstream writes
; `t.alias = alias` straight after newObjectType, and doing the same keeps the two
; Type constructions unchanged.
;
; ★★★ ONE PRODUCER FILLS THEM AND THAT IS A BOUNDARY, NOT AN OVERSIGHT.
; getTypeFromTypeLiteralOrFunctionOrConstructorTypeNode is the only site this port
; reaches; the reference sets `alias` at six more —
; createDeferredTypeReference, the mapped type, the template literal, the
; instantiation cache and getUnionType/getIntersectionType — and every one of them
; is behind an arm that still reports. ★The union one is the visible neighbour:
; `type U = string | number` names `U` upstream and stops here as
; `type-alias-union`, which is an honest UNPORTED and stays one.
; ★★★ `Type` HAS NEVER BEEN BUILT THROUGH AN `init`, AND THAT IS WHY IT NO LONGER
; HAS ONE. Both constructions pass a bare `null` where
; `intrinsic_name: Slice[char]` stands, `check_initializer_match` refuses
; that, and the construction has been falling through to the POSITIONAL-TUPLE path
; since slice 66 — which is the path every record in this file uses now. What the
; dead init did buy once was an ARITY disagreement: a seventh field with no
; parameter made the tuple path report *no matching initializer for Type with 6
; argument(s): property alias_symbol would be left uninitialized*, on a record that
; was one field away from filling a reference slot with stack garbage in silence.
; ★★That gate is the tuple path's own (`fill_default_components#` plus
; `report_component_type_mismatch#`), not the init's, so removing the init keeps it.
define Type
(
    id: i32
    flags: u32
    object_flags: u32
    ; (slice 332) the union and the bool sit right after the three i32 so their
    ; odd sizes share one alignment gap: 136 -> 120 bytes
    data: TypeData
    rid_computed: bool
    symbol: ref[Symbol]?

    ; The reference's `alias *TypeAlias` — its `symbol` and its `typeArguments`.
    alias_symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    alias_type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?

    ; ★★★ `ConstrainedType.resolvedBaseConstraint`, AND IT SITS HERE BECAUSE IT IS
    ; EMBEDDED BY TWELVE KINDS UPSTREAM AND NOT BY THE TYPE PARAMETER ALONE. It
    ; stood on TypeParameterTypeData until slice 168, which made
    ; getResolvedBaseConstraint memo-less for every other constrained kind — and a
    ; base constraint that is recomputed is one that can be RE-ENTERED: the second
    ; ask arrives with the first ask's resolution frame still on the stack,
    ; pushTypeResolution answers false, and the type is declared CIRCULAR. See
    ; is_constrained_type for the twelve, and getResolvedBaseConstraint's header
    ; for what the missing memo cost.
    resolved_base_constraint: ref[Type]?

    ; ★ the recursion identity, computed once (slice 227): getRecursionIdentity is
    ; a structural read upstream; here every arm copies a union arm out of `data`,
    ; and isDeeplyNestedType asks it for every stack entry on every call
    rid_symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    rid_type: ref[Type]?
    rid_node: ref[AstNode]?
)

; ── ModuleKind: the values this port can currently name ─────────────────────
;
; core.ModuleKind, and only the members a ported branch reads — the enum has
; fourteen. It is a handful of `define`s rather than a generated module for the
; reason ObjectFlags above is: a table is worth generating when the port reads
; most of it, and a table nobody can verify against a reader is a claim about
; the future. The values are the reference's own numbers
; (internal/core/compileroptions.go), which is what makes them checkable.
;
; ★ ES2022 is the DERIVED default under this harness — see Checker.module_kind
; for the derivation, which is the part that surprises.
;
; ★ Node16 and NodeNext are here as the BOUNDS of a range test rather than as
; branch labels — isCommonJSContainingModuleKind asks `Node16 <= k <= NodeNext`,
; and the two intermediate members (Node18 = 101, Node20 = 102) are inside it and
; are named by nothing this port reads. Adding them would be a table nobody
; verifies against a reader, which is the rule above.
; ── ReferenceHint (slice 110) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; markLinkedReferences' `hint` — the reference's whole iota block, WHOLE for
; TypeSystemPropertyName's reason: these are the arms of one dispatch and a partial
; declaration would invite renumbering. One of the eight has a caller here
; (ExportSpecifier); the other seven name the dispatch arm that would read them.
define ReferenceHintUnspecified: int 0
define ReferenceHintIdentifier: int 1
define ReferenceHintProperty: int 2
define ReferenceHintExportAssignment: int 3
define ReferenceHintJsx: int 4
define ReferenceHintExportImportEquals: int 5
define ReferenceHintExportSpecifier: int 6
define ReferenceHintDecorator: int 7

; checker/types.go ExternalEmitHelpers (slice 249)
define ExternalEmitHelpersRest: int 1
define ExternalEmitHelpersDecorate: int 2
define ExternalEmitHelpersMetadata: int 4
define ExternalEmitHelpersParam: int 8
define ExternalEmitHelpersAwaiter: int 16
define ExternalEmitHelpersAwait: int 32
define ExternalEmitHelpersAsyncGenerator: int 64
define ExternalEmitHelpersAsyncDelegator: int 128
define ExternalEmitHelpersAsyncValues: int 256
define ExternalEmitHelpersExportStar: int 512
define ExternalEmitHelpersImportStar: int 1024
define ExternalEmitHelpersImportDefault: int 2048
define ExternalEmitHelpersMakeTemplateObject: int 4096
define ExternalEmitHelpersClassPrivateFieldGet: int 8192
define ExternalEmitHelpersClassPrivateFieldSet: int 16384
define ExternalEmitHelpersClassPrivateFieldIn: int 32768
define ExternalEmitHelpersSetFunctionName: int 65536
define ExternalEmitHelpersPropKey: int 131072
define ExternalEmitHelpersAddDisposableResourceAndDisposeResources: int 262144
define ExternalEmitHelpersRewriteRelativeImportExtension: int 524288

define ModuleKindNone: int 0
define ModuleKindCommonJS: int 1
; ModuleKindSystem (slice 133) — checkImportMetaProperty's one exemption from the
; `moduleKind < ES2020` report, and its only reader.
define ModuleKindSystem: int 4
define ModuleKindES2015: int 5
define ModuleKindES2020: int 6
define ModuleKindES2022: int 7
define ModuleKindESNext: int 99
define ModuleKindNode16: int 100
; ModuleKindNode18 (slice 129) — the LOW end of SupportsImportAttributes' range,
; which is the one reader either of the two node1x members has here.
define ModuleKindNode18: int 101
define ModuleKindNode20: int 102
define ModuleKindNodeNext: int 199
define ModuleKindPreserve: int 200

; ── ScriptTarget ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; core.ScriptTarget lives in tscaly/ScriptTarget.scaly since slice 140, and the
; move is the module-order rule rather than tidiness: the SCANNER reads these
; constants now — checkRegularExpressionFlagAvailability and the named-capturing-
; group report are the readers — and a `define` read from a module declared LATER
; is a hard `unknown identifier` in operator position and is SILENTLY DROPPED as a
; call argument. The reader list and the deadness arguments moved with it.

; ── SemanticMeaning ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ast.SemanticMeaning: which of the three declaration SPACES a declaration
; declares into. Three bits, no complement, so a hand-port is the whole table.
define SemanticMeaningNone: int 0
define SemanticMeaningValue: int 1 << 0
define SemanticMeaningType: int 1 << 1
define SemanticMeaningNamespace: int 1 << 2
define SemanticMeaningAll: int SemanticMeaningValue | SemanticMeaningType | SemanticMeaningNamespace

; ── ElementFlags ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; checker.ElementFlags: what a TUPLE element is. Four bits, and slice 61 needs
; them for getTupleElementFlags and checkTupleType's ordering loop.
;
; ★★ THE DISTINCTION THAT MATTERS HERE IS REST AGAINST VARIADIC, and it is not a
; syntactic one in general: both are spelled `...T`, and which one it is depends on
; whether `T` is an ARRAY. getTupleElementFlags decides it with
; getArrayElementTypeNode, a purely syntactic probe — so `...T[]` and `...[...U[]]`
; answer Rest without a type, and `...T` answers Variadic and is where this port
; stops.
;
; ★★★ THREE OF THE FOUR COMPOSITES ARE LIVE SINCE SLICE 115, and the note that
; stood here is the expiry §3.5p is about: it read *"the four composites the
; reference defines (Fixed, Variable, NonRequired, NonRest) have no reader in this
; slice and are left out (§3.5be)"* — true of slice 61, and every one of the three
; readers arrived with the tuple TARGET. Variable is what decides whether an
; element gets a numbered PROPERTY at all (createTupleTargetType) and whether
; `length` is `number` or a union of literals; NonRequired is
; createNormalizedTupleTypeEx's whole early exit; Variable again is the printer's
; `...` fork. ★NonRest still has no reader and is still left out, so the count is
; three rather than four — a composite is ported when something asks it.
define ElementFlagsNone:     int 0
define ElementFlagsRequired: int 1 << 0
define ElementFlagsOptional: int 1 << 1
define ElementFlagsRest:     int 1 << 2
define ElementFlagsVariadic: int 1 << 3
; ★ The three composites are written as LITERALS and not as an or-fold of the
; single bits: a global's initializer folds only inside ONE precedence level, and
; `1 << 2 | 1 << 3` mixes the shift with the `|`, which is a hard rc-4. The
; comment carries the derivation the expression cannot.
define ElementFlagsFixed:       int 3     ; Required | Optional
define ElementFlagsVariable:    int 12    ; Rest | Variadic
define ElementFlagsNonRequired: int 14    ; Optional | Rest | Variadic
define ElementFlagsNonRest:     int 11    ; Required | Optional | Variadic

; checker.TupleElementInfo, slice 115 — one element's flags plus the DECLARATION
; its label comes from.
;
; ★★ THE SECOND SLOT IS NOT A NAME, AND THAT IS THE REFERENCE'S DESIGN RATHER THAN
; a deferral: getTupleElementLabel reads the declaration's own name NODE when it is
; asked, so a labelled element carries the node and the label is derived. Keeping a
; string here would make the tuple KEY depend on text where the reference's key
; depends on node identity, and two `[a: T]` in one file would then collide.
;
; ★ `labeled_declaration` is a NamedTupleMember or a ParameterDeclaration and null
; for everything else — getTupleElementInfo's own fork.
define TupleElementInfo
(
    flags: int
    labeled_declaration: ref[AstNode]?
)

; ── DeclarationSpaces ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; checker.DeclarationSpaces, slice 65: which of the three export SPACES one
; DECLARATION occupies. Three bits and no composites, so the table is the port.
;
; ★★★ IT IS NOT ast.SemanticMeaning ABOVE, AND THE BIT LAYOUT IS IDENTICAL, which
; is exactly why they are two tables ten lines apart rather than one shared set:
; the temptation is at its strongest where nothing would break today. The
; reference has both, with the same three bits in the same order — and the
; per-kind ANSWERS differ, which is what makes them two questions.
; getMeaningFromDeclaration gives an EnumMember Value|Type and a non-instantiated
; ModuleDeclaration Namespace alone; getDeclarationSpaces gives an EnumMember
; ExportType|ExportValue and a SourceFile all three, a kind the other function
; answers Namespace|Value for. Folding them would be a same-layout merge of two
; specifications, and the merge is invisible until one of them changes.
define DeclarationSpacesNone:            int 0
define DeclarationSpacesExportValue:     int 1 << 0
define DeclarationSpacesExportType:      int 1 << 1
define DeclarationSpacesExportNamespace: int 1 << 2

; ── slice 73: the duplicate-member map ──────────────────────────────────────
;
; One entry of checkObjectTypeForDuplicateDeclarations' three `map[string]int`s.
;
; ★★★ AN ASSOCIATION LIST RATHER THAN A MAP, for the reason TypeInstantiation's
; note gives above and with a number behind it this time. All three maps are
; GUARDED: `instanceNames` and `staticNames` take an entry only for a member whose
; symbol has more than one declaration — i.e. only for a name that is already an
; error — and `privateNames` only for a member named with a private identifier.
; The widest input the corpus has is privateNameDuplicateField.ts with 64 private
; members in one FILE, spread over several classes, so a linear scan is bounded by
; sixty-four comparisons per member and no hash can pay for itself here.
;
; ★★ THE EMPTY LIST **IS** GO'S NIL MAP, exactly, which is what lets the port
; create all three eagerly where the reference creates each on first write. A read
; of a nil Go map answers the zero value and a read of an empty list answers 0;
; every write goes through set_duplicate_name_state, which the reference guards
; with its own `if names == nil` allocation. An `Array` whose init is empty
; allocates nothing, so the eager creation is also free.
define DuplicateNameEntry
(
    name: Slice[char]
    state: int
)

; One row of checkKindsOfPropertyMemberOverrides' `notImplementedInfo` map.
;
; ★★★ ONLY THE COUNT SURVIVES THE PORT, and that is exact rather than
; approximate: the reference's MemberInfo carries the missed property NAMES and
; two type names, all three of which are message ARGUMENTS — and the dump is
; `C pos end code`. What the names decide is which of six codes is emitted, and
; that fork reads `len(missedProperties)` and `IsClassExpression(errorNode)`
; only. So the record is the node and the number.
define UnimplementedMembers
(
    error_node: ref[AstNode]?
    missed_count: int
    ; the MemberInfo's names (message arguments), copied onto the checker's page
    base_type_name: String
    type_name: String
    missed_names: ref[Array[String]]?
)
{
    init(n: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        set error_node: n
        set missed_count: 0
        set base_type_name: String()
        set type_name: String()
        set missed_names: null
    }
}


; ── slice 93: the flow walk's records ───────────────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ THERE IS NO `FlowType` RECORD HERE, AND THAT IS THIS PACKAGE'S OWN
; CONVENTION RATHER THAN A SIMPLIFICATION. Upstream every arm of the walk answers
; a two-field VALUE `FlowType{t, incomplete}`. No function in this port returns a
; record by value — the grep is `returns [A-Z]` and it finds nothing in 76 000
; lines — and the reason is in the root CLAUDE.md: a constructed struct return
; takes the CALLER's page, so in a walk that recurses once per flow EDGE it is a
; page per edge. The pair is a `ref[Type]?` result and a `ref[bool]` out-cell,
; which is the spelling this tree uses for every other out-parameter.
;
; ★★★ AND THE NIL HALF SURVIVES INTACT, WHICH IS WHAT MADE THE CHOICE SAFE — it
; is the one thing a lowering here could have lost. A nil `t` is a REAL ANSWER at
; three arms: assignment, call and array mutation answer it to mean *this node
; does not affect the reference, keep walking*, and the dispatch reads exactly
; that. It is NOT this port's stop signal. The stop signal is the unported MARK,
; as everywhere else in this file, and every recursion point in the walk compares
; one BEFORE it looks at the answer — because the two are otherwise the same
; null.

; checker.SharedFlow — one row of the per-invocation memo of getTypeAtFlowNode.
; A flow node is SHARED when it is the antecedent of more than one node (slice
; 92's h15 measured the two bits a saturating counter), and the whole point of
; the memo is that such a node is reached more than once per invocation.
define SharedFlow
(
    flow: ref[FlowNode]?
    t: ref[Type]?
    incomplete: bool
)

; The reduce-label stack, `f.reduceLabels` upstream.
;
; ★★★ IT IS A CONS LIST AND NOT AN ARRAY, AND THE REASON IS THE POP. Upstream the
; stack is pushed before a recursion and truncated after it (`f.reduceLabels =
; f.reduceLabels[:len-1]`), and `Array` in this stdlib has `add` and `clear` and
; nothing between them — reaching into its `length` field to truncate would be
; this package writing through a container's internals. A cons list pushes by
; minting a head and pops by USING THE OLD HEAD in the continuation, which is the
; same stack with the truncation deleted rather than worked around.
;
; ★ And it reads in the right direction for free: getBranchLabelAntecedents walks
; the reference's array BACKWARD (`i := len; for i != 0 { i-- }`), because a
; `try` nested in a `try` pushes twice for one target and the INNER push must
; win. The head of this list is the last push.
define ReduceLabelStack
(
    data: ref[AstNode]?
    next: ref[ReduceLabelStack]?
)

; checker.FlowState. Upstream it is recycled through a free list
; (getFlowState/putFlowState) because one is allocated per
; getFlowTypeOfReference; here it is minted on the checker's page and the region
; frees it, so the free list has nothing to buy and is not ported.
;
; ★ `sharedFlows` is a CHECKER-level stack upstream, sliced per invocation
; through `f.sharedFlowStart` and truncated on the way out. A per-invocation
; Array is the same window with no cursor, so the start index is gone and the
; truncation with it. `antecedentTypes` is the same shape one level down and
; lives in getTypeAtFlowBranchLabel for the same reason.
;
; ★ `refKey` is NOT here, and its absence is a claim: its only reader is
; getTypeAtFlowLoopLabel's cache key, and the loop label is this slice's wall.
define FlowState
(
    reference: ref[AstNode]?
    declared_type: ref[Type]?
    initial_type: ref[Type]?
    flow_container: ref[AstNode]?
    depth: int
    reduce_labels: ref[ReduceLabelStack]?
    shared_flows: ref[Array[SharedFlow]]

    ; ★★★ `refKey` IS HERE SINCE SLICE 141, AND THE NOTE THAT REFUSED IT NAMED ITS
    ; OWN EXPIRY: *"`refKey` is NOT here, and its absence is a claim: its only
    ; reader is getTypeAtFlowLoopLabel's cache key, and the loop label is this
    ; slice's wall."* The loop label is ported, so the slot is a field again.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT IS TWO SLOTS BECAUSE `IsZero` IS A THIRD STATE. Upstream asks
    ; `f.refKey.IsZero()` for *not computed yet* and compares against
    ; `nonDottedNameCacheKey` for *computed, and the reference is not a dotted
    ; name*; a null key here means the second, so the first needs the flag.
    ref_key_computed: bool
    ref_key: ref[Xxh3]?
)

; checker.FlowTraceEvent — the row of the FLOWPIN (slice 93).
;
; ★★★ THE BATTERY IS WHY THIS EXISTS, AND SLICE 92'S OWN FINDING IS WHY IT COULD
; BE BUILT. This chapter's product is a flow TYPE, and no instrument in this
; directory can see one: the T section is emitted only for a unit whose check
; COMPLETES, and not one unit that reaches the flow does. So a control that breaks
; the walk and still ANSWERS — the wrong type, silently — moves nothing. Measured:
; the first draft of controls-slice93.sh put 31 rows against five instruments and
; NINETEEN came back ungated, including the Start arm's hop, the reduce-label
; stack and the branch label's collapse.
;
; ★★★ IT IS A PIN AND NOT A YARDSTICK, AND SAYING SO IS THE HONEST PART. There is
; no reference side: what it compares is this port against ITSELF one patch ago,
; the way the STOPPIN and the MEMBERPIN do. It cannot say an answer is RIGHT —
; diagcheck and the DIAGPIN are what say that, and between them they cover the
; three diagnostics this chapter produces. What it says is that the WALK took the
; edges it took, which is the claim every row of the battery is about.
;
;   kind 0  an invocation      a = the reference's pos, b = its end
;   kind 1  a visited node     a = the flow node's FLAGS, b = 0
;   kind 2  the answer         a = the type's id, b = 1 if a stop ended the walk
define FlowTraceEvent
(
    kind: int
    a: int
    b: int
)

; c.flowNodeReachable's row — the Shared-node memo of isReachableFlowNodeWorker,
; keyed on the FLOW NODE and living for the whole check rather than for one
; invocation. It is a SEPARATE table from SharedFlow above and not a second use
; of it: reachability is a property of the graph alone, so it survives across
; references, where a flow TYPE is a property of the reference and cannot.
define FlowReachableLink
(
    flow: ref[FlowNode]?
    reachable: bool
)

; c.flowNodePostSuper's row — the Shared-node memo of isPostSuperFlowNodeWorker.
; A SEPARATE table from FlowReachableLink above for the reason that one is
; separate from SharedFlow: the two walks answer different questions about the
; same node, and one table keyed on the node could hold only one of the answers.
define FlowPostSuperLink
(
    flow: ref[FlowNode]?
    post_super: bool
)

; ── ErrorChain (slice 167) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ `Relater.errorChain` AND THE ONE THING IT IS FOR: THE OUTERMOST LEVEL'S
; MESSAGE WINS. Every level of the relation that fails calls `reportError`, which
; PREPENDS to this list, and `checkTypeRelatedTo` turns the whole list into ONE
; diagnostic at the end by reading its HEAD — so the code a reader sees is the
; LAST one reported, i.e. the outermost level's, and every inner message becomes a
; link the dump cannot see (`D <pos> <end> <code>`).
;
; ★★★ IT REPLACES A LATCH THAT HAD THE POLARITY BACKWARDS. Slices 121–166 stood in
; for this with `rel_reported`, a bool set by the FIRST level to reach the report,
; which kept the INNERMOST message — right for every shape the port could reach
; until slice 166 and wrong in general, as `report_error_results`'
; `relation-error-outer-level-message` stop said in so many words. The latch, the
; `rel_unmatched_*` cells beside it and the `diagnostics.truncate` rewind that took
; an already-emitted line back are all gone with it.
;
; ★★ `args` IS A TYPE PAIR HERE WHERE UPSTREAM CARRIES PRINTED NAMES. The two
; readers are `chainArgsMatch` — which compares the head's two type arguments — and
; the property-name transform in `reportError`, and the port has no writer for any
; of the four messages that transform fires on (2202–2205, the only four the
; reference marks `elidedInCompatibilityPyramid`). So an entry carries the pair by
; IDENTITY, which is slice 127's argument unchanged: two types printing the same
; name are not the same type, and the recorder and the reader are the same relation
; LEVEL. It also keeps the chain free of `type_to_string`, whose stops would
; otherwise be taken on every comparison that fails rather than on the few that
; reach the name fork.
;
; ★ `depth` is `chainDepth` memoised on the node: the reference walks the list for
; it, and the two readers (the shorter-chain preference, and the
; `errorChain == saveErrorState.errorChain` test that gates the structural reports)
; ask it once per comparison.
define ErrorChain
(
    next: ref[ErrorChain]?
    code: int
    arg_source: ref[Type]?
    arg_target: ref[Type]?
    depth: int
    ; the reference's `args ...any`, stringified
    args: ref[Array[String]]?
    ; Relater.relatedInfo as it stood when this entry became the head (the
    ; reference saves and restores the two together; newest first)
    related: ref[RelatedNode]?
)

; one entry of Relater.relatedInfo: createDiagnosticForNode's file, span, code and
; arguments, the list newest first
define RelatedNode (next: ref[RelatedNode]?  file: ref[AstNode]?  pos: int  end: int  code: int  args: ref[Array[String]]?)

; printer.SymbolAccessibilityResult (slice 250, the declaration emitter): how a
; symbol may be named from an enclosing declaration, and which unexported
; aliases the emitter must paint visible to name it (`AliasesToMakeVisible`).
; ErrorSymbolName/ErrorModuleName are message arguments no yardstick observes
; (a diagnostic here is (pos, end, code)); they carry the symbols' bare names.
define SymbolAccessibilityAccessible: int 0
define SymbolAccessibilityNotAccessible: int 1
define SymbolAccessibilityCannotBeNamed: int 2
define SymbolAccessibilityNotResolved: int 3

define SymbolAccessibilityResult
(
    accessibility: int
    aliases_to_make_visible: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    error_symbol_name: Slice[char]
    error_module_name: Slice[char]
    error_node: ref[AstNode]?
)

; a generated identifier and the import specifier it stands for (SetReferencedImportDeclaration)
define NodeDeclPair
(
    node: ref[AstNode]
    declaration: ref[AstNode]
)

; ★ The checker's options are `shared`, not `mutable` (slice 359): a `mutable`
; global is THREAD-LOCAL, and a checker on a worker thread would read these
; initial values instead of the program's options. They are set on the main
; thread before any checker runs and only read while checking.
shared checker_strict_null_checks: bool true
; exactOptionalPropertyTypes: the program's option (slice 252), false by
; default — a plain equality against TSTrue — and set by the dump for an
; emit program before its checker exists (undefinedOrMissingType is chosen
; at construction)
shared checker_exact_optional_property_types: bool false
; the checker's other options (slice 263), their defaults the yardsticks' unset
; configuration's; set by an emit program before its checker exists
shared checker_no_implicit_any: bool true
shared checker_no_implicit_this: bool true
shared checker_use_unknown_in_catch_variables: bool true
shared checker_strict_property_initialization: bool true
shared checker_strict_function_types: bool true
shared checker_strict_bind_call_apply: bool true
shared checker_strict_builtin_iterator_return: bool true
shared checker_check_js: bool false
; checkJs unset (TSUnknown): ast.IsPlainJSFile's `checkJs == TSUnknown`
shared checker_check_js_unset: bool true
shared checker_no_property_access_from_index_signature: bool false
; the resolution options (slice 264): allowImportingTsExtensions,
; allowArbitraryExtensions, rewriteRelativeImportExtensions, and
; `NoImplicitAny.DefaultIfUnknown(Strict).IsTrue()` (GetResolutionDiagnostic's)
shared checker_allow_importing_ts_extensions: bool false
shared checker_allow_arbitrary_extensions: bool false
shared checker_rewrite_relative_import_extensions: bool false
shared checker_no_implicit_any_explicit: bool false
; noEmit: a diagnostic SetSkippedOnNoEmit is left out (the program's FilterNoEmitSemanticDiagnostics)
shared checker_no_emit: bool false
; `slices.Contains(compilerOptions.Lib, "lib.dom.d.ts")`: the explicit lib option names dom
shared checker_lib_option_has_dom: bool false
shared checker_no_implicit_returns: bool false
shared checker_no_unchecked_indexed_access: bool false
; noUnusedLocals / noUnusedParameters (slice 265): checkUnusedIdentifiers' gate
shared checker_no_unused_locals: bool false
shared checker_no_unused_parameters: bool false
; the driver initializes the checker itself, after its options (slice 269)
shared checker_defer_initialize: bool false
; `--generateTrace`: every checker this program builds records the types it mints
shared checker_trace_types: bool false
; allowUnreachableCode / allowUnusedLabels (Tristates: an error only when FALSE, no
; check when TRUE), noFallthroughCasesInSwitch, allowUmdGlobalAccess (slice 266)
shared checker_allow_unreachable_code: int 2
shared checker_allow_unused_labels: int 2
shared checker_no_fallthrough_cases_in_switch: bool false
shared checker_allow_umd_global_access: bool false

; addDeferredDiagnostic's callbacks, as one ordered list of what each closure
; captured: the WeakMap/WeakSet and Reflect collision checks (a node) and the
; not-iterable report (a node, a type and the async flag)
define DeferredCheckKindWeakMapSet: int 0
define DeferredCheckKindReflect: int 1
define DeferredCheckKindNotIterable: int 2
define DeferredCheck (kind: int  node: ref[AstNode]?  t: ref[Type]?  flag: bool)

; One resolution the checker has made (program.GetResolvedModule's answer): the
; file it names and the four facts resolveExternalModule reads, keyed by the
; importing file, the name and the usage mode. Upstream the checker asks the
; program's resolution cache and never resolves; here it resolved per REFERENCE,
; and a resolver with its candidate paths was ~70 MB of vs/editor's allocations.
define ResolutionMemo
(
    importing_index: int
    mode: int
    name: Slice[char]
    resolved_index: int
    package_id: Slice[char]
    alternate_result: Slice[char]
    stop_tag: Slice[char]
    arbitrary_extension: bool
    using_ts_extension: bool
)

define Checker
(
    host: ref[Page]
    file: ref[AstNode]?

    ; The bind. The checker asks it two questions in this slice — is this file a
    ; module, and does it own a symbol — and everything the type machinery will
    ; need (getSymbolOfDeclaration, the tables) comes through it.
    b: ref[Binder]

    ; ── the PARSE, and it is here for exactly one question (slice 78) ────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ A NODE'S JSDoc IS NOT IN THE TREE — it hangs off a side table the PARSER
    ; owns (`jsdoc_infos`), so `checkSourceElementWorker`'s first step,
    ; `node.EagerJSDoc(nil)`, is unanswerable from a file node and a binder alone.
    ; Upstream reads it off the SourceFile because the cache lives there; here it
    ; lives on the Parser, which is why the parser has to survive the parse for the
    ; checker as it already did for the JSDoc yardstick (`Parser.parse_ex`).
    ;
    ; ★ It is asked through `Parser.eager_jsdoc`, never `jsdoc_for_node`: the
    ; checker wants the CACHE and not the lazy parse, and the difference is the
    ; whole of slice 78 — see that function's note.
    p: ref[Parser]

    ; ── slice 113: the LIB ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The second file of the program. `tests/oracle/types.go` builds its Program
    ; out of lib.es5.d.ts plus the unit; this port had the unit alone, which is the
    ; half §3.11 named. Both are parsed and bound by the caller, exactly as the
    ; oracle's `main` does, and the Checker holds the lib's tree and its binder
    ; because a node's SOURCE TEXT is reachable only through the binder that read
    ; it.
    lib_file: ref[AstNode]?
    lib_b: ref[Binder]?
    ; the rest of the lib chain (slice 248): the first lib stays `lib_file`
    extra_lib_files: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    ; extra_lib_index_of's address index over extra_lib_files, rebuilt when the list
    ; has grown, and lib_chain_index_of's rank per lib file (computed once per lib
    ; count): both were linear scans on every node comparison
    extra_lib_index: ref[AddressIndex]?
    extra_lib_indexed: int
    lib_ranks: ref[Array[int]]?
    extra_lib_binders: ref[Array[ref[Binder]?]]?
    ; the WHOLE lib chain's parsers and paths, indexed 0..n-1 beside
    ; (lib_file, lib_b) + extra_lib_* — `select_lib_file` needs the two things
    ; the chain does not otherwise carry. Null until the driver sets them; the
    ; checker's own lib loader (tscaly_types) never selects a lib.
    all_lib_parsers: ref[Array[ref[Parser]?]]?
    all_lib_paths: ref[Array[Slice[char]]]?
    ; slice 248: the program — every unit of a case with its binder and parser,
    ; the table the module resolver answers over; null in the single-unit runs
    program: ref[ProgramFiles]?
    ; the resolver's two options (EmitOptions.get_module_resolution_kind,
    ; get_resolve_json_module), set by the driver after construction
    module_resolution_kind: int
    resolve_json_module: bool
    ; `importHelpers`: checkExternalEmitHelpers returns at once without it, and
    ; the tslib lookup it does with it is a said stop (slice 248)
    import_helpers: bool
    ; sourceFileLinks.requestedExternalEmitHelpers / externalHelpersModule of the
    ; file being checked (reset per unit by select_program_file)
    requested_external_emit_helpers: int
    external_helpers_module: ref[Symbol]?
    ; the resolver's further options (slice 249): allowJs, the two package.json
    ; map tristates, whether outDir/rootDir are set
    allow_js: bool
    resolve_package_json_exports: int
    resolve_package_json_imports: int
    out_dir: Slice[char]
    root_dir: Slice[char]

    ; ★ THE RE-SCANNING SCANNER IS THE BINDER'S, AND THAT IS AN ARGUMENT RATHER
    ; THAN A SAVING. grammarErrorOnFirstToken reports at
    ; scanner.GetRangeOfTokenAtPosition, which the reference builds a FRESH
    ; scanner for; the binder ported that in slice 31 as
    ; `range_of_token_at_position` and wrote out why a shared scanner is the same
    ; answer — it carries no diagnostic sink, `skip_trivia_at` does not touch
    ; scanner state, and the language variant is set from the file name at
    ; construction. All three still hold when the sharer is one phase further on,
    ; and the binder is finished with it by the time a checker exists. A second
    ; scanner over the same text would be a second thing to keep in step.

    ; sourceFile.IsDeclarationFile, read by the checkUnusedRenamedBindingElements
    ; guard.
    is_declaration_file: bool
    is_mts_or_cts_file: bool

    ; ── the three compiler options the grammar checks read (slices 49, 50) ───
    ;
    ; ★★ THEY ARE FIELDS RATHER THAN LITERAL `false`s, and the difference is what
    ; a later reader sees. The harness sets SkipDefaultLibCheck and
    ; NoErrorTruncation and nothing else, so ExperimentalDecorators and
    ; VerbatimModuleSyntax are both unset and both of these are false — but a
    ; branch written as `if false` reads as dead code somebody should delete,
    ; while a branch behind a named option reads as the option it is waiting for.
    ; When an options record arrives, these two are the first substitution and the
    ; reports behind them (grammar-legacy-accessor-decorators,
    ; grammar-verbatim-module-syntax) become the work list for it.
    ; ★ node-can-be-decorated-parameter was the third until slice 174, which wrote
    ; that arm rather than leaving it to the option's arrival — the decorator
    ; predicates it belongs to needed it, and both halves its note named were
    ; already in this file.
    ;
    ; ★ `c.legacyDecorators = c.compilerOptions.ExperimentalDecorators ==
    ; core.TSTrue` — the reference computes it once in NewChecker, and so does
    ; create() below.
    legacy_decorators: bool
    verbatim_module_syntax: bool

    ; ★ compilerOptions.EmitDecoratorMetadata (slice 134) — the THIRD option of
    ; that pair's kind, and it gates markDecoratorAliasReferenced's whole body:
    ; the reference asks `IsFalseOrUnknown()`, so an unset option makes the
    ; serialized-metadata walk unreachable. The field is what the day of the
    ; options record fills; see mark_decorator_alias_referenced.
    emit_decorator_metadata: bool

    ; ★ compilerOptions.IsolatedModules (slice 91) — the OTHER half of the pair
    ; preserve_const_enums is derived from, given a name of its own because
    ; onSuccessfullyResolvedSymbol's last block reads it DIRECTLY and says in its
    ; own comment why it must not go through GetIsolatedModules. Unset here, so
    ; both readers answer false; see get_isolated_modules for the derived form.
    isolated_modules: bool

    ; ★ Slice 50's addition, and it is the one of the three whose branch is
    ; FULLY PORTED rather than reported. `shouldCheckErasableSyntax` is
    ; `ErasableSyntaxOnly.IsTrue() && !IsInJSFile(node)` and its consequence is a
    ; plain c.error on the node — no program, no resolver — so the arms of
    ; checkImportEqualsDeclaration and checkExportAssignment that read it carry
    ; live code behind a false option instead of a report. That is the difference
    ; the note above is drawing: a report belongs where the MACHINERY is missing,
    ; not where the option merely happens to be off.
    erasable_syntax_only: bool

    ; ★★★ compilerOptions.ShouldPreserveConstEnums(), slice 65 — and it is a
    ; DERIVATION, not a plain option read: `PreserveConstEnums.IsTrue() ||
    ; IsolatedModules.IsTrue()`. Both are unset here, so the answer is false — but
    ; the pair is why it is a field rather than an omitted term: the day either
    ; option arrives, isInstantiatedModule starts counting a CONST-ENUM-ONLY module
    ; as instantiated, and the four reports behind that gate come alive together.
    ; ★It is the one of this slice's two option readings where the naive answer is
    ; also the right one, which is worth saying because the other one (module_kind,
    ; four fields up) is the reason the derivation is written out at all.
    preserve_const_enums: bool

    ; ★★★ compilerOptions.NoUncheckedSideEffectImports, slice 58 — AND IT IS
    ; **TRUE** WHEN UNSET, which is the second of this slice's two option
    ; surprises and the sharper one. The reference reads it through
    ; `IsTrueOrUnknown()`, and a Tristate's zero value is TSUnknown — so the
    ; branch behind it is taken for every side-effect-only `import "m"` under this
    ; harness, and reading "unset" as false would have silently dropped a whole
    ; population out of the histogram.
    ;
    ; ★ It is a field and not a `true` literal for the same reason the four
    ; options above are fields: what the next reader needs to know is WHICH option
    ; the branch is waiting for, and here also that the answer is not the one the
    ; name suggests.
    no_unchecked_side_effect_imports: bool

    ; ★★★ c.moduleKind, slice 58 — AND IT IS NOT ZERO, WHICH IS THE WHOLE REASON
    ; IT IS A FIELD WITH A DERIVATION RATHER THAN AN OMITTED TERM. The harness
    ; leaves `Module` unset, and the obvious reading of that — the three options
    ; above — is *the option is off, so the branch behind it is dead*. Here the
    ; option being unset does NOT mean ModuleKindNone: NewChecker asks
    ; `compilerOptions.GetEmitModuleKind()`, which falls through to the TARGET when
    ; Module is unset, and an unset target is `ScriptTargetLatestStandard` =
    ; ES2025 = 12, which is `>= ScriptTargetES2022` — so the answer is
    ; **ModuleKindES2022**, and every branch that tests moduleKind is LIVE.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND NO INSTRUMENT IN THIS PACKAGE CAN TELL ES2022 FROM None TODAY, which
    ; is said here rather than left for the next reader to discover. The one
    ; ported reading is checkGrammarImportClause's `!= ESNext && != Preserve`, and
    ; ModuleKindNone fails that test exactly as ES2022 does — so the naive answer
    ; is RIGHT here by accident, and the battery's proof of that is a row that
    ; substitutes None and predicts nothing moves. The value is defended from the
    ; reference, not from a measurement; the first reading that would separate them
    ; is checkModuleExportName's `== ES2015 || == ES2020`, which is unported.
    ; §3.5cw's readings of an unset option get one more here, and it is the first
    ; where the option's own value is not the question at all.
    ;
    ; ★ Stored rather than recomputed, exactly as the reference stores it, and set
    ; from a named constant so the derivation is stated once. The day an options
    ; record arrives this is the line that reads it.
    module_kind: int

    ; ★★★ c.languageVersion, slice 59 — `compilerOptions.GetEmitScriptTarget()`,
    ; which is ScriptTargetLatestStandard = **ES2025 = 12** when Target is unset.
    ; It is the third derived default in this record and the one whose consequence
    ; is largest: the collision family reads it three times and all three reads
    ; come out the same way, so checkCollisionWithGlobalPromiseInGeneratedCode
    ; returns at its own first line (`>= ES2017`) and the two
    ; recordPotentialCollisionWith… functions register nothing (`<= ES2021`).
    ;
    ; ★★ SO THREE OF THE FIVE SUB-CHECKS OF checkCollisionsForDeclarationName ARE
    ; PROVABLY INERT UNDER THIS HARNESS, and each is ported as its guard plus a
    ; report rather than dropped — the shape check_class_declaration's
    ; legacy_decorators branch already has. A target arriving later is then a
    ; substitution and the reports become the work list for it.
    language_version: int

    ; ★★★ program.GetEmitModuleFormatOfFile, slice 59 — AND THIS ONE IS **NOT** THE
    ; DERIVED ANSWER, WHICH IS THE WHOLE REASON IT IS A SEPARATE FIELD FROM
    ; module_kind. The oracle's stub program answers **ModuleKindNone** for every
    ; file (tests/oracle/types.go), while the option-derived value one field up is
    ; ES2022 — so the two disagree, and the yardstick's reference is the STUB's
    ; answer. §3.5cy's rule (*the answer of a PROGRAM is not an option default*)
    ; read in the direction that bites: here it is not a hole, it is a VALUE, and
    ; getting it from the option would silently flip two branches.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND BOTH FLIPS ARE OBSERVABLE, IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS, which is what
    ; makes this the slice's load-bearing constant.
    ; checkCollisionWithRequireExportsInGeneratedCode returns at `format >= ES2015`
    ; — with ES2022 the whole check would be dead, with None it is LIVE — and
    ; checkClassNameCollisionWithObject reports at `format < ES2015`, so `class
    ; Object {}` in a module REPORTS here and would be silent under the option's
    ; own value. The two CommonJS readings (the global-object report, the two
    ; import emit-helper branches) are dead either way, None being neither.
    emit_module_format: int

    ; ★★★ program.GetSourceFileMetaData(path).ImpliedNodeFormat, slice 64 — A
    ; THIRD program answer, and a THIRD field, even though the oracle's stub gives
    ; it the same ModuleKindNone as emit_module_format one line up. The field above
    ; carries the argument in general form and this is the case that tests it: two
    ; program methods that coincide today are still two questions, and folding them
    ; would make a later slice's `@module: node16` flip a branch it does not name.
    ; ★Its one reader is checkGrammarForInOrForOfStatement's Node label, which is
    ; dead under this harness — so the field is written for the DAY the option
    ; arrives, and that is exactly when a shared field would be found to be wrong.
    implied_node_format: int
    ; Program.GetImpliedNodeFormatForEmit (slice 271), the export= check's reading
    implied_node_format_for_emit: int

    ; ★★★ compilerOptions.GetUseDefineForClassFields(), slice 74 — AND IT IS
    ; **TRUE**, WHICH MAKES THE WHOLE HEAD OF THE WORK LIST INERT. The option is
    ; a Tristate and unset, so the reference falls through to
    ; `GetEmitScriptTarget() >= ScriptTargetES2022`, and that is
    ; ScriptTargetLatestStandard = ES2025 = 12 against 9 — so
    ; checkClassForStaticPropertyNameConflicts returns at its own first line for
    ; every class in this corpus, on BOTH sides (tests/oracle/types.go builds its
    ; CompilerOptions with SkipDefaultLibCheck and NoErrorTruncation and nothing
    ; else). This is the fourth derived default in this record and the first whose
    ; consequence is a whole reference function rather than a branch.
    ;
    ; ★ A field rather than the literal `true` for the reason module_kind is one:
    ; what the next reader needs is WHICH option the guard waits for. The day an
    ; options record arrives, or a `@target: es5` case reaches the checker, this is
    ; the line that flips and the report behind the guard becomes the work list.
    use_define_for_class_fields: bool

    ; ★★ compilerOptions.GetEmitStandardClassFields — `UseDefineForClassFields !=
    ; TSFalse && GetEmitScriptTarget() >= ES2022`, a SECOND derivation off the same
    ; option and not a synonym for the field above it: the two agree only because
    ; the option is unset here, and upstream `useDefineForClassFields: false` with
    ; an ES2022 target makes them differ. Its readers are the two parameter-property
    ; arms of isBlockScopedNameDeclaredBeforeUse, where the field decides whether a
    ; `this.bar` in a field initializer is a use-before-declaration at all.
    emit_standard_class_fields: bool

    ; ★★ compilerOptions.NoImplicitOverride, slice 74 — read through `IsTrue()`,
    ; so an unset Tristate is FALSE and this one is not a surprise. Its single
    ; reader is checkMemberForOverrideModifier's early return, and what the false
    ; value buys is exactly the reachable half of that function: a member with no
    ; `override` modifier is not the checker's business, so the only report the
    ; family can raise here is the one about a member that HAS the modifier.
    no_implicit_override: bool

    ; c.diagnostics — the checker's OWN collection, which is neither the parse's
    ; nor the bind's. Appended through add_diagnostic_direct: the reference's
    ; addDiagnostic has no dedup (that is the PARSER's parseErrorAtRange), and its
    ; serializationLevel guard DOES fire — see type_to_string_serialized (slice 213);
    ; the note that stood here said the opposite.
    diagnostics: ref[DiagnosticList]?
    ; the diagnostics with no location, counted (slice 254)
    global_diagnostic_count: int
    ; the global diagnostics' codes (slice 268), for the program's GetGlobalDiagnostics
    global_diagnostic_codes: ref[Array[int]]
    ; the same diagnostics with their message arguments
    global_diagnostic_list: ref[DiagnosticList]
    ; the program's module specifier host, for the module names messages print
    ; (null outside a program: the names stay as written)
    message_specifiers: ref[SpecifierHost]?
    ; the keys of the reporting global resolvers already asked (their memo)
    checked_global_reports: ref[Array[Slice[char]]]
    ; patternAmbientModuleAugmentations: a module name augmenting a pattern ambient
    ; module, and the augmentation merged over the pattern (two parallel lists)
    pattern_augmentation_names: ref[Array[Slice[char]]]
    pattern_augmentation_symbols: ref[Array[ref[Symbol]?]]

    ; nextTypeId, in creation order.
    type_count: int
    ; resolve_external_module's resolutions, with an open-addressed index (1-based
    ; row, 0 empty) keyed on (importing file, name, mode)
    resolution_memos: ref[Array[ref[ResolutionMemo]?]]?
    resolution_memo_buckets: ref[Vector[int]]?
    resolution_memo_mask: int
    ; what TSCALY_STATS reports beside type_count: the checker's own nodes, flow
    ; nodes and transient symbols
    nodes_allocated: int
    flow_nodes_allocated: int
    symbols_allocated: int
    ; `--generateTrace`'s type list, in CREATION order — null unless the driver
    ; asked for a trace (see dump_types_text)
    trace_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?

    ; ── the intrinsic types, in the reference's own creation order ───────────
    ;
    ; NewChecker builds these before anything else, and the ORDER is theirs
    ; because the ids are assigned along it. What is missing from the run is named
    ; at initialize_intrinsic_types.
    any_type: ref[Type]
    auto_type: ref[Type]
    wildcard_type: ref[Type]
    blocked_string_type: ref[Type]
    error_type: ref[Type]
    unresolved_type: ref[Type]
    non_inferrable_any_type: ref[Type]
    intrinsic_marker_type: ref[Type]
    unknown_type: ref[Type]
    undefined_type: ref[Type]
    undefined_widening_type: ref[Type]
    missing_type: ref[Type]
    undefined_or_missing_type: ref[Type]
    optional_type: ref[Type]
    null_type: ref[Type]
    null_widening_type: ref[Type]
    string_type: ref[Type]
    number_type: ref[Type]
    bigint_type: ref[Type]
    es_symbol_type: ref[Type]
    void_type: ref[Type]
    never_type: ref[Type]
    silent_never_type: ref[Type]
    implicit_never_type: ref[Type]
    unreachable_never_type: ref[Type]
    non_primitive_type: ref[Type]
    unique_literal_type: ref[Type]

    ; ── c.emptyTypeLiteralType (slice 80) ───────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The FIRST of NewChecker's fifteen empty/marker object types to be minted
    ; here, and it is minted because it has a reader:
    ; getTypeFromTypeLiteralOrFunctionOrConstructorTypeNode answers it for `{}`.
    ; Slice 81 mints THREE more (below); the remaining eleven (emptyObjectType,
    ; emptyJsxObjectType, emptyGenericType, anyFunctionType, the five marker type
    ; parameters, …) still have no reader, so they stay named in
    ; initialize_intrinsic_types' header rather than written (§3.5be).
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 99 IS WHERE emptyObjectType WOULD HAVE ARRIVED AND IT DELIBERATELY
    ; DID NOT. It is the SEED of checkObjectLiteral's spread fold, and its only
    ; reader there is `spread != c.emptyObjectType` — a test behind the spread arm,
    ; which reports. So the field would have had a writer, a comment and no live
    ; reader, which is §3.5be's forbidden shape exactly; it arrives with getSpreadType.
    ;
    ; ★ It is optional where its neighbours are not, and that is new_object_type's
    ; signature rather than a claim: the Anonymous arm cannot answer null, and the
    ; one reader of this field returns `ref[Type]?` anyway, so the Option costs
    ; nothing and inventing a non-null cast here would.
    empty_type_literal_type: ref[Type]?

    ; ── emptyObjectType and emptyGenericType (slice 115) ─────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ TWO MORE OF THE FIFTEEN, AND BOTH ARRIVE WITH A LIVE READER, which is
    ; what slice 99 refused to do without: `empty_generic_type` is getGlobalType's
    ; answer when the globals table has no `Array` at arity one, and
    ; `empty_object_type` is what createTypeFromGenericGlobalType and
    ; getTupleTargetType answer in front of that sentinel. Both are compared by
    ; IDENTITY and nothing else, exactly like the three markers below.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE REFERENCE HANGS AN INSTANTIATION MAP OFF emptyGenericType
    ; (`AsObjectType().instantiations = make(...)`) and this port does not: the map
    ; is read by createTypeReference, and the ONE caller that could send this type
    ; there — createTypeFromGenericGlobalType — tests for it first and answers
    ; emptyObjectType instead. So the map has no reader here, and giving it one
    ; would be a claim about a path the sentinel exists to prevent.
    ;
    ; ★ They stand at the reference's POSITIONS relative to the types this port
    ; mints — emptyObjectType above emptyTypeLiteralType, emptyGenericType below it
    ; and above the three markers — for the reason emptyTypeLiteralType's own note
    ; gives about ids.
    empty_object_type: ref[Type]?

    ; c.emptyJsxObjectType and c.emptyFreshJsxObjectType (slice 119) — the SEED of
    ; a JSX attributes spread and the type an opening FRAGMENT's synthetic argument
    ; carries. Both are minted where the reference mints them, right after
    ; emptyObjectType, so nothing below moves. ★The fresh one has no reader in this
    ; slice (its only caller is getEffectiveCallArguments' synthetic-expression arm,
    ; which reports); it is minted anyway, because leaving it out would move every
    ; type id after it and the ORDER is what the reference's order buys.
    empty_jsx_object_type: ref[Type]?
    empty_fresh_jsx_object_type: ref[Type]?
    empty_generic_type: ref[Type]?

    ; ── anyFunctionType (slice 141) ──────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS THE ANSWER OF THE SkipContextSensitive EARLY-OUT, and until slice
    ; 141 the port had no writer for the CheckMode bit that reaches it, which is
    ; why the field's absence was recorded at the mint (initialize_intrinsic_types'
    ; noConstraintType note) rather than here. The comment there said the three
    ; markers *"stand where the reference puts them — after anyFunctionType, which
    ; this port does not mint"*; that sentence is discharged, and the ids of every
    ; type minted after this one move by one.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference's own line is `newAnonymousType(nil, nil, nil, nil, nil)`
    ; followed by `objectFlags |= ObjectFlagsNonInferrableType`, and the flag is
    ; what the whole type is FOR: it is what makes a contextually typed argument
    ; whose function expression was skipped non-inferrable, so an inference from it
    ; does not fix a type parameter to `any`.
    any_function_type: ref[Type]?

    ; ── globalArrayType and globalReadonlyArrayType (slice 115) ──────────────
    ;
    ; ★★ THE MEMO, NOT THE TYPE. getGlobalType is called on every array type node,
    ; and its cost is a globals lookup plus an arity test on a declared type; these
    ; two fields hold the answer for the unit. ★They are filled LAZILY where the
    ; reference fills them in initializeChecker, and the difference is the report:
    ; upstream's eager resolution is what owes TS2318 for a missing global, which
    ; is §3.11's chapter and not a name (see get_global_generic_type's header).
    global_array_type: ref[Type]?
    global_readonly_array_type: ref[Type]?
    ; §3.11's table, one more entry. ★It is the first NON-generic global this port
    ; asks for, and it arrives with the relation because two of the relation's own
    ; guards name it — the excess check's `isTypeSubsetOf(globalObjectType, target)`
    ; and the common-property check's `source != globalObjectType`.
    global_object_type: ref[Type]?

    ; §3.11's table again, slice 123. globalFunctionType is what
    ; narrow_type_by_type_name answers for `typeof x === "function"`, what
    ; is_type_derived_from's function arm compares against, and one of the two
    ; types narrow_type_by_type_predicate refuses to narrow `any` to.
    global_function_type: ref[Type]?
    global_callable_function_type: ref[Type]?
    global_newable_function_type: ref[Type]?

    ; globalImportMetaType (slice 133), lazy for the reason the five apparent
    ; types below are: `import.meta` is the only reader and most units have none.
    global_import_meta_type: ref[Type]?

    ; §3.11's table, slice 124 — the five APPARENT types of the primitives.
    ; getApparentType maps a StringLike to `String`, a NumberLike to `Number` and
    ; so on, and until they existed every primitive receiver stopped there. ★The
    ; reference fills three of them eagerly in initializeChecker and asks for the
    ; other two through getGlobalBigIntType / getGlobalESSymbolType; here all five
    ; are lazy, for the reason the two array memos give one screen up.
    global_string_type: ref[Type]?
    global_number_type: ref[Type]?
    global_boolean_type: ref[Type]?
    global_big_int_type: ref[Type]?
    global_es_symbol_type: ref[Type]?
    ; The node builder's `ctx.inferTypeParameters` — the conditional root's own
    ; infer parameters, set while the EXTENDS operand is written and nowhere else.
    ; It is a field rather than a parameter because the printer recurses through
    ; twenty writers between the conditional and the type-parameter arm that reads
    ; it, which is exactly what upstream's node-builder CONTEXT is for.
    printer_infer_type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?

    ; c.getGlobalImportCallOptionsType's memo — the UNCHECKED resolver of the pair,
    ; and it is a SECOND slot beside the checked one on purpose: upstream memoises
    ; the two resolvers separately, and when the lib has no `ImportCallOptions` they
    ; do not answer the same thing (one reports, the other answers the empty object
    ; type), so one slot would let whichever ran first decide for both.
    global_import_call_options_type_unchecked: ref[Type]?

    ; c.unknownUnionType and c.unknownEmptyObjectType (slice 123). Upstream both
    ; are minted in initializeChecker; here they are lazy, because the first one's
    ; only readers are the two narrowing arms that meet an `unknown` reference and
    ; the second exists only to be unioned into it. See get_unknown_union_type.
    unknown_union_type: ref[Type]?
    unknown_empty_object_type: ref[Type]?

    ; anyArrayType and autoArrayType, memoised the same way — see their accessors.
    any_array_type: ref[Type]?
    auto_array_type: ref[Type]?
    ; anyReadonlyArrayType, slice 120's — isArrayLikeType asks the ASSIGNABILITY
    ; relation against it for every non-array type it is handed.
    any_readonly_array_type: ref[Type]?

    ; ── slice 128: the ITERATION and ASYNC globals (§3.11's table) ───────────
    ;
    ; Twenty-one entries, and the number is the chapter rather than an appetite:
    ; the reference builds ONE `IterationTypesResolver` per direction out of eight
    ; global-type getters each, and both directions are needed because a
    ; for-await-of looks for `[Symbol.asyncIterator]` FIRST and then falls back to
    ; `[Symbol.iterator]`. So the sync half is not the async half's sibling — it is
    ; the same routine's second argument.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE `_CHECKED` TWINS ARE NOT DUPLICATES AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE REPORT.
    ; Upstream `getGlobalIterableTypeChecked` is `getGlobalTypeResolver("Iterable",
    ; 3, reportErrors: TRUE)`, i.e. the SAME lookup that additionally owes TS2318 /
    ; TS2317 when the lib does not declare it — §3.11's deferred chapter — so here
    ; they resolve to the same type and the report is the one thing still missing.
    ; They are kept as separate NAMES rather than folded, because the day §3.11
    ; lands the two must diverge and a folded reader would have to be found again.
    ; c.globalRegExpType — `getGlobalType("RegExp", 0, reportErrors)`, and the
    ; only global this port asks for at arity ZERO. It is what a regular
    ; expression LITERAL's type is, and nothing else reads it.
    global_reg_exp_type: ref[Type]?

    ; c.valueSymbolLinks.Get(c.argumentsSymbol).resolvedType — `getGlobalType(
    ; "IArguments", 0, reportErrors)`, the SECOND global this port asks for at
    ; arity zero. It is lazy where the reference's is eager, for the reason every
    ; other global here is (get_global_generic_type's header), and the memo is a
    ; field rather than the symbol's link because the seed has to be able to say
    ; *not asked yet* apart from *asked and absent*.
    global_i_arguments_type: ref[Type]?

    ; ★★★ THE CHECKER OWNS A SECOND SCANNER, and it is not an optimization: the
    ; parser's is long finished and its state belongs to a completed parse, while
    ; this one is pointed at a regular expression literal, run over its body and
    ; then pointed at the next. Upstream keeps exactly this field
    ; (`c.regExpScanner`) and builds it on first use, which is what `null` is
    ; here. ★ Its error rule is not the parser's either — see
    ; Scanner.begin_regexp_scan.
    reg_exp_scanner: ref[Scanner]?

    global_iterator_type: ref[Type]?
    global_iterable_type: ref[Type]?
    global_iterable_iterator_type: ref[Type]?
    global_iterator_object_type: ref[Type]?
    global_generator_type: ref[Type]?
    global_async_iterator_type: ref[Type]?
    global_async_iterable_type: ref[Type]?
    global_async_iterable_iterator_type: ref[Type]?
    global_async_iterator_object_type: ref[Type]?
    global_async_generator_type: ref[Type]?
    global_iterator_yield_result_type: ref[Type]?
    global_iterator_return_result_type: ref[Type]?
    global_promise_type: ref[Type]?
    global_promise_like_type: ref[Type]?

    ; getGlobalTemplateStringsArrayType's memo, slice 138 — the type of a tagged
    ; template's FIRST argument, which is synthesized rather than written.
    global_template_strings_array_type: ref[Type]?

    ; getGlobalImportCallOptionsTypeChecked's memo, slice 138.
    global_import_call_options_type: ref[Type]?
    ; getGlobalBuiltinIteratorTypes — a LIST per direction, four names for sync
    ; (`ArrayIterator`, `MapIterator`, `SetIterator`, `StringIterator`) and one for
    ; async (`ReadableStreamAsyncIterator`), each at arity ONE. ★They are memoised
    ; as a list because `isReferenceToSomeType` asks the whole list at once, and a
    ; per-name field would make that reader assemble one on every call.
    global_builtin_iterator_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    global_async_builtin_iterator_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]?
    ; getGlobalAwaitedSymbol — a TYPE ALIAS symbol, not a type, which is why it
    ; goes through get_global_type_alias_symbol and not the arity helper.
    ; ★★ THE `OrNil` TWIN IS THE SAME LOOKUP, and here it is literally the same
    ; field: the only difference upstream is TS2318, §3.11 again.
    global_awaited_symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    global_awaited_symbol_asked: bool

    ; getGlobalExtractSymbol — `Extract<T, U>`, arity 2. Same two-field memo as
    ; the one above and for the same reason: a lib without `Extract` answers null
    ; legitimately, so a null field cannot mean *not asked yet*.
    global_extract_symbol: ref[Symbol]?
    global_extract_symbol_asked: bool

    ; ── slice 134: the DECORATOR globals ────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The nine names initializeChecker resolves for decorators, memoised lazily
    ; like every other global here. ★All nine are `getGlobalTypeResolver(...,
    ; reportErrors: TRUE)` upstream, so all nine owe §3.11's TS2318 for a lib that
    ; does not declare them — the same debt the iteration globals above carry, and
    ; the same reason the FAILURE value matters more than the report: an absent
    ; `ClassDecoratorContext` answers emptyGenericType, tryCreateTypeReference
    ; turns that into `unknown`, and the decorator's context parameter is `unknown`
    ; rather than a dangling reference.
    global_typed_property_descriptor_type: ref[Type]?
    ; globalThisType — the `ThisType<T>` marker interface, arity 1 (slice 158).
    global_this_marker_type: ref[Type]?
    global_class_decorator_context_type: ref[Type]?
    global_class_method_decorator_context_type: ref[Type]?
    global_class_getter_decorator_context_type: ref[Type]?
    global_class_setter_decorator_context_type: ref[Type]?
    global_class_accessor_decorator_context_type: ref[Type]?
    global_class_accessor_decorator_target_type: ref[Type]?
    global_class_accessor_decorator_result_type: ref[Type]?
    global_class_field_decorator_context_type: ref[Type]?

    ; ── slice 128: c.iterationTypesCache and two rows of c.cachedTypes ───────
    ;
    ; See IterationTypesLink, AwaitedTypeLink and PromisedTypeLink for what each
    ; row is and why the failure is cached as well as the success.
    iteration_types_cache: ref[Array[ref[IterationTypesLink]?]]
    ; its rows chained by the key's hash, caught up per lookup (slice 333)
    iteration_types_cache_chain: ref[HashChain]
    awaited_type_links: ref[Array[ref[AwaitedTypeLink]?]]
    awaited_type_links_index: ref[AddressIndex]
    promised_type_links: ref[Array[ref[PromisedTypeLink]?]]
    promised_type_links_index: ref[AddressIndex]

    ; c.awaitedTypeStack — getAwaitedTypeNoAliasEx's recursion guard, and the one
    ; piece of state in this chapter that is neither a memo nor a global.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS A STACK AND NOT A SET, and the reference's comment says what it is
    ; for: a promise whose promised type is itself (`interface BadPromise { then(
    ; onfulfilled: (value: BadPromise) => any, ...): BadPromise }`) passes the
    ; PromiseLike test and would unwrap for ever. Both readers are
    ; `slices.Contains`, so a set would answer the same — but the ENTRIES are
    ; pushed and popped around one recursion each, and a set would need a count per
    ; type to pop correctly.
    ;
    ; ★ THE ARRAY NEVER SHRINKS AND THE DEPTH COUNTER IS THE STACK —
    ; `rel_source_stack`'s idiom in this file and not a new one, because `Array` has
    ; `add`, `put` and `get_length` and no `pop`. `contains` must therefore stop at
    ; the DEPTH and not at the length, or a popped entry would still be found.
    awaited_type_stack: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
    awaited_type_depth: int

    ; ── c.cachedTypes[CachedTypeKindArrayLiteralType] (slice 120) ────────────
    ;
    ; widened_type_links' idiom at the SECOND kind this port reads, and the kind
    ; half is again the table's name. createArrayLiteralType's memo is not an
    ; optimisation: the flag it sets is read by the WIDENER, so two asks for the
    ; same array literal type must answer the same OBJECT or the widening of one
    ; is invisible to the other.
    array_literal_type_links: ref[Array[ref[ArrayLiteralTypeLink]?]]
    array_literal_type_links_index: ref[AddressIndex]

    ; ── c.cachedTypes[CachedTypeKindEvolvingArrayType] (slice 141) ───────────
    evolving_array_type_links: ref[Array[ref[EvolvingArrayTypeLink]?]]
    evolving_array_type_links_index: ref[AddressIndex]

    ; ── c.flowTypeCache and c.flowInvocationCount (slice 120) ────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE COUNTER IS WHAT MAKES THE CACHE FAITHFUL. getTypeOfExpression caches
    ; its answer ONLY when the check it ran actually performed control-flow
    ; analysis, and the test for *actually* is that this counter moved — see
    ; get_type_of_expression. It is not a lookup table with a fast path: the cache
    ; decides how many times an expression is CHECKED, and a check reports.
    flow_type_cache: ref[Array[ref[FlowTypeCacheLink]?]]
    ; one HashChain per flow type cache list (the cache is swapped around every
    ; looping antecedent): the list's address -> its row in flow_type_cache_chains
    flow_type_cache_chain_index: ref[AddressIndex]
    flow_type_cache_chains: ref[Array[ref[HashChain]?]]

    ; ── c.contextFreeTypes (slice 141) ───────────────────────────────────────
    context_free_types: ref[Array[ref[ContextFreeTypeLink]?]]
    context_free_types_index: ref[AddressIndex]

    ; ── c.flowLoopCache and c.flowLoopStack (slice 141) ──────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE CACHE IS WHAT MAKES A BACK EDGE TERMINATE, together with the stack,
    ; and neither is an optimisation: the reference's own comment on the stack says
    ; *"it is possible to see an empty array in cases where loops are nested and
    ; the back edge of the outer loop reaches an inner loop that is already being
    ; analyzed"*, and the way out of that is the FIRST antecedent of a loop
    ; junction always being the non-looping path.
    flow_loop_cache: ref[Array[ref[FlowLoopCacheLink]?]]
    ; flow node address -> its first row; the rows of one node are chained
    flow_loop_cache_index: ref[AddressIndex]
    ; an instantiation list's address -> its row in object_row_chain_list: the
    ; list's rows chained by argument hash (the reference's map key)
    object_row_chains: ref[AddressIndex]
    object_row_chain_list: ref[Array[ref[HashChain]]]
    ; the same for a signature's cache rows, chained by type-argument hash
    signature_row_chains: ref[AddressIndex]
    signature_row_chain_list: ref[Array[ref[HashChain]]]
    flow_loop_stack: ref[FlowLoopInfo]?
    flow_invocation_count: int

    ; ── c.assertionLinks (slice 120) ─────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; type_node_link_of's idiom keyed on the NODE — see AssertionLink's header for
    ; why it is not a slot on that table.
    assertion_links: ref[Array[ref[AssertionLink]?]]
    assertion_links_index: ref[AddressIndex]

    ; ── the tuple target cache (slice 115) ───────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; c.tupleTypes, which upstream is keyed by getTupleKey — a keyBuilder hash over
    ; each element's flag CHARACTER and its labelled declaration, plus a `!` for
    ; readonly. This port compares the element-info LISTS themselves, which is the
    ; decision TypeInstantiation already made for the instantiation cache and for
    ; the same reason: a hash key here would be a second mechanism to keep in step
    ; with the reference's, where a structural comparison cannot disagree with
    ; itself. ★The key's own SHAPE is what matters and it is ported exactly — two
    ; tuples with the same flags differ when their labelled declarations differ, so
    ; `[a: string]` and `[b: string]` are two targets.
    tuple_targets: ref[Array[ref[TupleTarget]?]]
    ; its rows chained by tuple_key_hash (getTupleKey's parts), caught up per lookup
    tuple_target_chain: ref[HashChain]

    ; ── the three constraint markers (slice 81) ──────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; c.noConstraintType, c.circularConstraintType and c.resolvingDefaultType, the
    ; next three of the fifteen. They are SENTINELS rather than types: no
    ; expression ever has one, every function that could answer one filters it out
    ; (getBaseConstraintOfType, getConstraintFromTypeParameter,
    ; getDefaultFromTypeParameter), and the only operation performed on them is
    ; identity.
    ;
    ; ★★ THEY ARE NON-OPTIONAL WHERE THEIR SIBLING ABOVE IS OPTIONAL, AND THAT IS
    ; THE OPPOSITE DECISION FOR THE SAME REASON. The Option costs nothing where the
    ; one reader returns an Option anyway; here the readers are seven IDENTITY
    ; comparisons, so an Option would put an unwrap at every one of them. The
    ; `as ref[Type]` at the three minting lines is the whole price, and it is sound
    ; for the reason that note gives: new_object_type's Anonymous arm cannot answer
    ; null.
    no_constraint_type: ref[Type]
    circular_constraint_type: ref[Type]
    resolving_default_type: ref[Type]

    ; ── c.enumNumberIndexInfo and c.anyBaseTypeIndexInfo (slice 87) ──────────
    ;
    ; The reference builds both in NewChecker as struct literals rather than
    ; through newIndexInfo, and both are SENTINELS as much as they are values:
    ; three places elsewhere compare an IndexInfo against one of them by identity
    ; (getApplicableIndexInfo, the inference walk, the emit resolver). Neither
    ; comparison is reachable here yet, so what this slice needs of them is only
    ; the value — but the identity is why they are two fields and not two calls at
    ; the use site.
    ;
    ; ★ They carry no type ID, so their position among the intrinsics does not
    ; move any other type's number; they are written at the END of
    ; initialize_intrinsic_types because they READ numberType, stringType and
    ; anyType, which is also where NewChecker puts them relative to those.
    enum_number_index_info: ref[IndexInfo]?
    any_base_type_index_info: ref[IndexInfo]?

    ; ── the base-constraint recursion stack (slice 81) ───────────────────────
    ;
    ; getResolvedBaseConstraint's `stack []RecursionId`, held as a field plus a
    ; depth rather than threaded as a parameter — and the two are the same
    ; mechanism, which is worth proving rather than asserting. `append(stack,
    ; identity)` hands the CALLEE a longer slice while the caller keeps its own
    ; length, so a sibling's append may overwrite a slot no live reader can see;
    ; one array with a push before the recursive call and a pop after it reproduces
    ; that exactly, because every level appends exactly ONE entry and no level ever
    ; mutates an entry below its own top.
    ;
    ; ★ The array is REUSED across queries the way c.typeResolutions is (put below
    ; the high-water mark, add above it), for the same reason: the depth limits are
    ; 10 and 50, so the array is tiny and its allocations are worth not repeating.
    base_constraint_stack: ref[Array[ref[RecursionId]?]]?
    base_constraint_depth: int

    ; ── the printer's visitedTypes, slice 111 ───────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; `b.ctx.visitedTypes`, which visitAndTransformType adds a type id to for the
    ; duration of one transform and deletes after. It is a push/pop pair over one
    ; reused array for base_constraint_stack's reason, and it is a SET of ids
    ; rather than of types because that is what upstream keys it on.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS NOT AN OPTIMISATION AND IT IS NOT ONLY A GUARD: it is an INPUT to
    ; the name. shouldWriteTypeOfFunctionSymbol's first conjunct is
    ; `flags&UseTypeOfFunction != 0 || ctx.visitedTypes.Has(typeId)`, and
    ; typeToString does NOT set UseTypeOfFunction — so under this entry point the
    ; visited set is the ONLY thing that can make a function symbol print as
    ; `typeof f`. A guard that merely stopped recursion would answer a different
    ; name.
    visited_types: ref[Array[int]]?
    visited_type_depth: int

    ; `b.ctx.symbolDepth` — see PrinterSymbolFrame. A stack rather than a map, and
    ; the two are the same thing here: upstream writes `symbolDepth[id] = depth+1`
    ; on the way in and restores the old value on the way out, so its value at any
    ; moment is the number of ACTIVE frames carrying that identity — which is what
    ; counting occurrences on a stack answers.
    printer_symbols: ref[Array[ref[PrinterSymbolFrame]?]]?
    printer_symbol_count: int

    ; ── `ctx.flags & UseFullyQualifiedType`, slice 183 ───────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS A FLAG AND NOT A PARAMETER BECAUSE UPSTREAM MAKES IT ONE: the
    ; collision pass in mapToTypeNodes sets it on the CONTEXT, re-runs
    ; typeToTypeNode for the colliding entries, and restores it — so every arm
    ; between the list and the symbol sees it without carrying it. Threading it as
    ; a parameter would touch the whole printer for a bit that is off almost
    ; always.
    ;
    ; ★★ ITS DEFAULT IS THE ANSWER THAT EXISTED BEFORE IT. With the flag false
    ; `lookup_symbol_chain` answers the symbol alone, which is exactly what
    ; write_symbol_name did unconditionally — so no name in the corpus can move
    ; except inside a list that printed one name twice.
    printer_fully_qualified: bool
    ; TypeFormatFlagsWriteArrayAsGenericType
    printer_write_array_as_generic: bool
    ; the node builder's enclosingDeclaration — nil for TypeToString, set to a
    ; property's declaration while its computed name is written (slice 221)
    printer_enclosing_declaration: ref[AstNode]?
    ; visitedSymbolTablesMap of the accessible-chain walk: (symbol, table) pairs
    chain_visited_symbols: ref[Array[ref[Symbol]?]]?
    chain_visited_tables: ref[Array[ref[SymbolTable]?]]?
    chain_visited_count: int
    ; emitresolver.go's declarationFileLinks.aliasesMarked (slice 250): the
    ; files PrecalculateDeclarationEmitVisibility has walked
    decl_visibility_marked_files: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?

    ; ── `isIdentifierTypeReference(typeNode)`, slice 183 ─────────────────────
    ;
    ; The name the arm just wrote, when what it wrote was a TypeReferenceNode
    ; whose TypeName is a bare IDENTIFIER — an EMPTY slice for everything else.
    ; The collision pass keys on it, and on nothing else: a qualified name is
    ; already unambiguous, and a keyword or a composed name has no symbol to
    ; collide.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT IS SET BY THE WRITER for wrote_type_precedence's reason — upstream
    ; asks the NODE what kind it is, and the only faithful port is for the arm
    ; that decided the shape to say which shape it decided. Unlike the precedence
    ; it is CLEARED at the top of the worker rather than seeded with a value: the
    ; safe default here is *not an identifier type reference*, and an arm that
    ; forgets therefore under-reports a collision rather than inventing one.
    wrote_type_reference_name: Slice[char]

    ; The length of the chain write_symbol_chain last wrote — one means the entity
    ; name is an Identifier, more means a QualifiedName. A field rather than an
    ; out-parameter because the reader is two frames up, at the arm that knows it
    ; built a TypeReferenceNode.
    wrote_chain_length: int

    ; ── the printer's RECURSION DEPTH, and the depth the name was written at ──
    ;
    ; ★★★ WITHOUT THESE THE NAME IS THE DEEPEST ARM'S AND NOT THE CONSTITUENT'S,
    ; AND THAT OVER-QUALIFIES. A type LITERAL writes no name of its own, so after
    ; `{ foo: M.MyEnum }` the name field still held `MyEnum` — set two levels down
    ; by the member's type — and the collision pass read two constituents as
    ; *the same name for different types* and qualified both. The reference cannot
    ; make that mistake: it asks the NODE it got back, and a TypeLiteralNode is not
    ; an identifier type reference whatever its members are. Measured on
    ; `enumAssignmentCompat4`, which printed `({ foo: M.MyEnum; } | { foo: N.MyEnum; })[]`
    ; where the reference prints the bare `MyEnum` twice.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE DEPTH IS THE CHEAP WAY TO ASK *DID THE ARM I CALLED WRITE THIS?* The
    ; alternative is the precedence field's discipline — every arm states its own
    ; value — and it is the wrong trade here: the precedence has a meaningful
    ; answer for EVERY node, so an arm that forgets is a bug in that arm, while the
    ; name has an answer for three arms out of twenty and the other seventeen would
    ; carry a line saying *not me*. One counter in the single wrapper every
    ; recursion passes through says the same thing and cannot be forgotten.
    printer_depth: int
    wrote_name_depth: int

    ; ── whether a chain DROPPED a container it could not name (slice 183) ────
    ;
    ; `getSymbolChain`'s tail declines to write a parent that is an external
    ; MODULE — upstream's *"we prefer just `x` vs `\"foo/bar\".x`"* — so a symbol
    ; exported from a module and a same-named symbol local to a function BOTH come
    ; back as one element and print the same fully-qualified name. That is fine for
    ; a NAME and fatal for the one question that compares two of them:
    ; `getTypeNameForErrorDisplay`'s equality is what picks TS2719 over TS2322, and
    ; two names equal only because a container was dropped is not evidence that the
    ; types share one. The flag says *this answer is not a complete qualification*,
    ; and the TS2719 fork reports instead of deciding on it.
    printer_chain_dropped_container: bool
    ; lookupSymbolChain's `yieldModuleSymbol`: typeToStringEx WITHOUT
    ; UseAliasDefinedOutsideCurrentScope (getTypeNameForErrorDisplay) keeps a module
    ; container, which then prints as an `import("…")` root
    printer_yield_module_symbol: bool

    ; ── the printed node's PRECEDENCE, slice 111, WIDENED IN SLICE 115 ──────
    ;
    ; The emit printer parenthesizes by precedence — `emitTypeNode(node, prec)`
    ; wraps when `GetTypeNodePrecedence(node) < prec`.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT WAS ONE BIT AND THE ARRAY ARM IS WHY IT CANNOT BE. Slice 111 wrote
    ; `wrote_paren_needing_type: bool` with the argument that *a union constituent is
    ; emitted at TypePrecedenceTypeOperator, and of every node kind THIS printer can
    ; build exactly two sit below it: FunctionType and ConstructorType*. That was a
    ; containment proof over the arms that existed, and slice 115 breaks it from the
    ; OTHER side: an array type emits its ELEMENT at TypePrecedencePostfix, which is
    ; one level ABOVE TypeOperator — so a UNION element must be wrapped, and the bit
    ; says exactly the opposite about a union (the union arm set it FALSE, meaning
    ; *no parens*). The reference's answer is `(A | null)[]`; the bit's answer is
    ; `A | null[]`, a different type. **A boolean standing in for a lattice is
    ; correct only while one question is asked of it.**
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS STILL SET BY THE WRITER AND NOT ASKED OF THE TYPE, which is the half
    ; of slice 111's design that survives: upstream builds a node and the printer
    ; reads its KIND, so the only faithful port is for the arm that decides the
    ; shape to record which shape it decided. A predicate re-deriving the routing
    ; would be a second copy of create_type_node_from_object_type's four-way fork.
    ;
    ; ★★ EVERY ARM THAT WRITES MUST SET IT, and `type_to_string` seeds
    ; TypePrecedenceNonArray on entry — the value that needs no parens anywhere — so
    ; an arm that forgets answers *no parens*, which is the wrong default. There is
    ; no way to make the safe default the silent one, so it is written here instead.
    wrote_type_precedence: int

    ; ── the boolean literal pair, slice 71 ──────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★ FOUR TYPES FOR TWO VALUES, and the asymmetry with every other literal is
    ; the reference's: `false` and `true` are the only literal types built EAGERLY,
    ; in NewChecker, and they are built REGULAR-FIRST-THEN-FRESH rather than lazily
    ; through getFreshTypeOfLiteralType. `c.falseType` is what `case
    ; ast.KindFalseKeyword` answers, and it is the FRESH one.
    ;
    ; ★★★ `booleanType` ARRIVES IN SLICE 94 AND THE LINE THAT USED TO STAND HERE —
    ; *"still missing and still named ... a union is a dimension away"* — IS THE
    ; DEFERRAL NAMING ITS OWN EXPIRY DATE, exactly as slice 66's base-type one did.
    ; It is `getUnionType([regularFalse, regularTrue])`, it is the FIRST union this
    ; port mints, and it is the type that makes three arms live at once:
    ; type_to_string's Boolean keyword, getTypeFromTypeNode's KindBooleanKeyword and
    ; getWidenedLiteralType's boolean arm.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT IS AN OPTION WHERE ITS FOUR NEIGHBOURS ARE NOT, and that is
    ; get_union_type's signature rather than a claim about the value: every union
    ; answer is `ref[Type]?` because the worker can STOP (see the union chapter's
    ; walls), so a non-optional field here would need an `x as ref[Type]` unwrap at
    ; the one place where the value is produced — an exit-21 lie for a call that has
    ; no reachable stop today and might grow one. The three readers all answer
    ; `ref[Type]?` themselves, so the Option costs nothing.
    regular_false_type: ref[Type]
    false_type: ref[Type]
    regular_true_type: ref[Type]
    true_type: ref[Type]
    boolean_type: ref[Type]?

    ; ── c.numberOrBigIntType (slice 102) ────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS MINTED NOW BECAUSE IT HAS A READER NOW, which is the rule the four
    ; intrinsic unions were held back under (§3.5be, and the note at the minting
    ; site says so in the reference's own order). `checkArithmeticOperandType` asks
    ; `isTypeAssignableTo(t, numberOrBigIntType)` and NOTHING ELSE decides TS2362 and
    ; TS2363, so the field and its two reports arrive together.
    ;
    ; ★ An Option for boolean_type's reason exactly: `get_union_type_pair` answers
    ; `ref[Type]?` because the union worker can stop, and the one reader unwraps by
    ; testing rather than by an `as`.
    number_or_bigint_type: ref[Type]?

    ; c.stringOrNumberType and c.stringNumberSymbolType, on the same rule and with
    ; the same shape: getIndexTypeEx and getLiteralTypeFromProperties are the only
    ; readers of either, and both arrive in this slice. ★They are minted where the
    ; reference mints them — initialize_intrinsic_types — rather than lazily,
    ; because the union table interns them and a later mint would carry a later id.
    string_or_number_type: ref[Type]?
    string_number_symbol_type: ref[Type]?

    ; c.numericStringType — the template literal `${number}`, slice 136. It arrives
    ; under §3.5be's rule and not before: its two readers are
    ; `is_applicable_index_type` (a `number` index signature applies to it) and the
    ; mapped-type append of `get_conditional_flow_type_of_type`, and both land in
    ; this slice.
    numeric_string_type: ref[Type]?

    ; ── c.typeofType (slice 133) ────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The union of the eight names `typeof x` can answer, and the same rule as its
    ; neighbours brings it: `checkTypeOfExpression` is its reader and arrives with
    ; it. ★★★ THE MEMBERS ARE `typeofNEFacts`' KEYS IN GO'S SORTED ORDER
    ; (`slices.Sorted(maps.Keys(...))`) — bigint, boolean, function, number,
    ; object, string, symbol, undefined — which is not the order that table is
    ; WRITTEN in and not the order a union prints in either; the union sorts its
    ; own constituents. Reading the map's literal order instead would be invisible
    ; here and wrong the day the sort is the thing that matters.
    ;
    ; ★★ It is minted in NewChecker's own position rather than lazily for
    ; string_or_number_type's reason, one notch stronger: eight STRING LITERAL
    ; types are interned by this mint, and a lazy one would give each of them an
    ; id that depends on which unit is being checked.
    typeof_type: ref[Type]?

    ; c.emptyStringType / c.zeroType / c.zeroBigIntType, slice 135 — the three
    ; DEFINITELY FALSY parts of the three primitive domains, which is the whole of
    ; getDefinitelyFalsyPartOfType's answer for `string`, `number` and `bigint`.
    ; ★They are minted in NewChecker's own position, immediately before
    ; typeof_type, because a literal type's id is its creation order and this
    ; file's union ORDER falls back to the id.
    empty_string_type: ref[Type]?
    zero_type: ref[Type]?
    zero_big_int_type: ref[Type]?

    ; The Index-type half of c.cachedTypes — getIndexTypeForGenericType's memo,
    ; whose key is (StringsOnly, type id). Its rows carry the key on the TYPE
    ; (target and index_flags are both payload slots), so unlike the intersection
    ; table this one needs no entry record.
    index_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]

    ; c.indexedAccessTypes — the deferred `T[K]` intern table.
    indexed_access_types: ref[Array[ref[IndexedAccessEntry]?]]
    ; its rows chained by the key's hash, caught up per lookup (slice 333)
    indexed_access_types_chain: ref[HashChain]

    ; c.substitutionTypes — slice 136.
    substitution_types: ref[Array[ref[SubstitutionEntry]?]]

    ; ── the union intern table, slice 94 ────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; c.unionTypes. The reference keys a Go map on a HASH of the constituent type
    ; ids (getUnionKey); this is an association list scanned linearly and compared
    ; on the LIST — same length, same types in the same order — which is what the
    ; hash stands in for and is exact where a hash is a bet.
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHAT THE TABLE BUYS IS IDENTITY, AND HERE — UNLIKE THE THREE LITERAL
    ; TABLES — IT IS OBSERVABLE ON THE DAY IT LANDS. `getOptionalType` answers `t`
    ; unchanged when `t.Types()[0]` is already the undefined type, `formatUnionTypes`
    ; asks whether a constituent pair IS booleanType, and `containsType` and
    ; CompareTypes compare types by POINTER throughout. A union minted twice would
    ; make each of those answer no, silently.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference's second cache, `unionOfUnionTypes`, is NOT carried, and the
    ; argument is that its absence is unobservable rather than cheap: it memoises
    ; getUnionTypeWorker for the two-constituent case, and the worker is a pure
    ; function of (constituents, reduction, alias, origin) whose answer is interned
    ; by the table above. So a miss costs a re-walk and cannot cost a second type:
    ; the only `new_type` inside the worker is behind this table's own guard.
    ; ★★The clause that used to carry this — *"once alias and origin are out of the
    ; picture, and they are, permanently"* — expired in slice 135; the memo's own
    ; guard upstream is `origin == nil`, so it is a fast path and not the reason.
    union_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]

    ; ★★★ THE INTERN TABLE'S INDEX, AND ITS KEY IS getUnionKey'S OWN FOUR CASES.
    ; The scan below it is O(unions) per ask and the note under find_union_type
    ; says what upstream does instead — `c.unionTypes[getUnionKey(...)]`, a map.
    ; It was never priced because no unit made enough unions;
    ; `recursiveReverseMappedType` does once the deferred reference stops walling
    ; the instantiation off, and a `sample` reads 71 % of that run inside
    ; union_key_matches and the two accessors it calls. ★★★The first version of
    ; this index hashed the CONSTITUENT LIST and used it only where the ask
    ; carried no origin — and moved nothing, because every `getUnionType([E, F])`
    ; mints a FRESH origin (the note under union_key_matches says so) so the
    ; origin-free ask is the rare one. `union_key_hash` therefore mirrors
    ; union_key_matches arm for arm: no origin hashes the constituents, a UNION or
    ; INTERSECTION origin hashes the ORIGIN's constituents under a tag — the
    ; normalized set is not in that key at all — and an INDEX origin hashes the
    ; origin's own id and the constituents. The alias stays out and is filtered by
    ; union_key_matches, which every probe still runs.
    union_type_buckets: ref[Vector[int]]?
    union_type_mask: int

    ; c.intersectionTypes (slice 121). Its rows are ENTRIES rather than types —
    ; see IntersectionEntry for why two thirds of the key are not on the answer.
    intersection_types: ref[Array[ref[IntersectionEntry]?]]
    intersection_chain: ref[HashChain]

    ; c.subtypeReductionCache (slice 135) — see SubtypeReductionRow.
    subtype_reduction_cache: ref[Array[ref[SubtypeReductionRow]?]]
    ; its rows chained by the key's type_list_hash, caught up per lookup
    subtype_reduction_chain: ref[HashChain]

    ; ── the three intern tables, slice 71 ───────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; c.stringLiteralTypes, c.numberLiteralTypes, c.bigintLiteralTypes. The
    ; reference keys three Go maps on the VALUE; these are association lists
    ; scanned linearly, which is `reported_ambient`'s idiom and this file's
    ; standing answer to a small per-file map.
    ;
    ; ★★ WHAT THE TABLE BUYS IS IDENTITY, NOT AN ANSWER — two occurrences of `"a"`
    ; in one file must be the SAME type, because every relation the checker will
    ; grow (isTypeIdenticalTo, containsType, the union reducer) compares types by
    ; pointer. Nothing in this slice can observe a hit, and that is exactly why the
    ; table is built now rather than when a reader appears: a literal type minted
    ; twice is indistinguishable from one minted once until the day it is not.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE NUMBER TABLE IS KEYED ON THE BIT PATTERN AND THEREFORE NEEDS NO
    ; SEPARATE NaN CELL, which is a divergence from the reference that is provably
    ; the same answer (§3.5ag). `c.nanType` exists upstream because NaN != NaN in
    ; IEEE-754 and a Go map lookup on a NaN key therefore always misses; a bit
    ; comparison does not miss, and every NaN this port can make is jsnum's single
    ; canonical BITS_NAN. ★The sibling fact is why the arm is unreachable from this
    ; slice at all: a NumericLiteral's text is a number the scanner accepted, so
    ; `jsnum_from_string` of it is neither NaN nor negative zero — the two inputs a
    ; value-keyed table and a bit-keyed table would disagree about.
    string_literal_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
    ; ★ the reference's `stringLiteralTypes[value]` is a map; a linear scan over
    ; every string literal type was 90 % of `hugeDeclarationOutputGetsTruncatedWithError`
    ; (slice 227): open-addressed buckets of row index + 1, 0 empty, as SymbolTable
    string_literal_buckets: ref[Vector[int]]?
    string_literal_mask: int

    ; c.templateLiteralTypes (slice 126). ★A LINEAR table like the three literal
    ; ones and like union_types, and for find_union_type's reason: the key is
    ; recoverable off the answer, since a template literal type carries exactly its
    ; texts and its types.
    template_literal_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]

    ; c.stringMappingTypes (slice 171). ★A LINEAR table for the same reason:
    ; the key is (symbol, target) and both halves are recoverable off the answer.
    string_mapping_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]

    ; c.templateConstraintType — `string | number | boolean | bigint | null |
    ; undefined`. ★It is built LAZILY rather than in initializeChecker, because
    ; get_union_type_ex is not callable that early here (the reference builds it in
    ; its own constructor); the memo is what makes the two the same value.
    template_constraint_type: ref[Type]?
    number_literal_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
    number_literal_buckets: ref[Vector[int]]?
    number_literal_mask: int
    bigint_literal_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]

    ; c.nodeLinks[node].hasReportedStatementInAmbientContext, as a SET of the
    ; nodes that have it. The reference keeps a links record per node and this
    ; slice reads exactly one bit off it, so a set is the whole of it; the record
    ; arrives with the first arm that needs a second field.
    ;
    ; ★ Linear membership, deliberately. The set only ever holds nodes in an
    ; AMBIENT context that have already reported, which is a handful per file even
    ; in a `.d.ts` — and the reference's own map is keyed by pointer identity,
    ; which is what this compares.
    reported_ambient: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]

    ; c.renamedBindingElementsInTypes — the list checkVariableLikeDeclaration's
    ; binding-element block appends to and checkUnusedRenamedBindingElements drains
    ; at the end of the file. Slice 56 is the slice that gives it a WRITER, which is
    ; what turns the empty reader at the other end into a report.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT IS A LIST AND NOT A COUNTER EVEN THOUGH ONE READER ASKS ONLY WHETHER IT
    ; IS EMPTY, because the reader the reference has walks it and reports per
    ; ELEMENT — with the element's own name and property name in the message — so a
    ; counter would be a shape the next slice has to undo. §3.5be's argument runs
    ; the other way here: what may not be built is machinery no instrument can
    ; reach, and this one is reached the moment a `.ts` file carries a renamed
    ; binding element in a body-less signature.
    renamed_binding_elements_in_types: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]

    ; sourceFileLinks.identifierCheckNodes (slice 265), one list over every file;
    ; the drain picks a file's nodes by their source file
    identifier_check_nodes: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]

    ; c.sourceFileLinks[file].deferredNodes — the nodes checkNodeDeferred parks so
    ; that a check needing a type runs after every declaration is bound into the
    ; walk. Slice 60 gives it its first writer (a type parameter) and its first
    ; reader (check_deferred_nodes).
    ;
    ; ★ The reference's is a SET (ordered, deduplicating) and this is a list, which
    ; is the same thing for the one caller there is: check_type_parameter runs once
    ; per type parameter and a node is therefore added once. A second caller that
    ; can reach the same node twice is what makes the distinction matter, and the
    ; reference's own type says so.
    deferred_nodes: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]

    ; ── c.declaredTypeLinks ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The reference's per-symbol links table, as an association LIST keyed on the
    ; symbol itself, scanned linearly — `reported_ambient`'s idiom, for the same
    ; reason and with the same cost profile (a file's classes and interfaces).
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS NOT KEYED ON THE SYMBOL ID, and that is a correctness point rather
    ; than a preference. `Symbol.id` is drawn LAZILY on first request (slice 41),
    ; and the numbers are observable through GetSymbolNameForPrivateIdentifier — so
    ; a table that asked every class's symbol for its id would make every later
    ; class's private-name mangling shift. The pointer is the identity the
    ; reference's map has anyway.
    ;
    ; ★★ NOTHING IN THIS SLICE CAN OBSERVE A HIT. Each of the two call sites asks
    ; once per symbol, and isThislessInterface's recursive ask is behind a report —
    ; so the memo is written for fidelity and its READER is named: the slice that
    ; ports getBaseTypes, where an interface's declared type is asked for a second
    ; time through its base, and the identity of the answer is what makes
    ; `t.AsInterfaceType().thisType` the same object on both routes.
    declared_type_links: ref[Array[ref[DeclaredTypeLink]?]]
    declared_type_links_index: ref[AddressIndex]

    ; ── c.cachedTypes, at the one kind in use (slice 112) ───────────────────
    ;
    ; declared_type_links' idiom keyed on the TYPE pointer. Upstream's key is
    ; (kind, typeId) over one map; CachedTypeKindWidened is the only kind this
    ; port reads, so the kind half is the table's name.
    widened_type_links: ref[Array[ref[WidenedTypeLink]?]]
    widened_type_links_index: ref[AddressIndex]

    ; the property-name text of a NUMBER literal type, made once on the checker's
    ; page and keyed on the type pointer (slice 351, get_property_name_from_type)
    number_names: ref[Array[Slice[char]]]
    number_names_index: ref[AddressIndex]

    ; ── c.cachedTypes[CachedTypeKindRegularObjectLiteral] (slice 146) ────────
    ;
    ; The same idiom at the kind get_regular_type_of_object_literal reads.
    regular_object_literal_type_links: ref[Array[ref[RegularObjectLiteralTypeLink]?]]
    regular_object_literal_type_links_index: ref[AddressIndex]

    ; ── c.cachedTypes[CachedTypeKindApparentType] (slice 155) ───────────────
    ;
    ; The same idiom at the kind get_apparent_type_of_intersection_type reads.
    apparent_type_links: ref[Array[ref[ApparentTypeLink]?]]
    apparent_type_links_index: ref[AddressIndex]

    ; ── c.cachedTypes at the two indexed-access simplification kinds (slice 166)
    ;
    ; The same idiom at the pair get_simplified_indexed_access_type reads — see
    ; SimplifiedIndexedAccessLink for why both kinds sit on one row.
    simplified_indexed_access_links: ref[Array[ref[SimplifiedIndexedAccessLink]?]]
    simplified_indexed_access_links_index: ref[AddressIndex]

    ; ── c.cachedTypes at the three instantiation kinds (slice 151) ───────────
    ;
    ; See InstantiationCacheLink for why the three share a row.
    instantiation_cache_links: ref[Array[ref[InstantiationCacheLink]?]]
    instantiation_cache_links_index: ref[AddressIndex]

    ; ── c.restrictiveMapper / c.permissiveMapper (slice 151) ────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THEY ARE FIELDS BECAUSE THEIR IDENTITY IS READ, not because minting one
    ; is expensive: instantiate_signature_ex's erasure argument is the POINTER
    ; comparison `m = permissive_mapper`, so a mapper built at the call site would
    ; erase nothing and say nothing about it.
    restrictive_mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?
    permissive_mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?

    ; ── c.uniqueLiteralMapper (slice 166) ───────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The third FunctionTypeMapper. ★Unlike the pair above its identity is NOT
    ; read anywhere — it is a field because the reference makes it once in
    ; NewChecker and a mapper minted per ask would be a fresh object on every
    ; reducible-intersection question.
    unique_literal_mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?

    ; ── c.discriminatedContextualTypes (slice 146) ───────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The same idiom keyed on the PAIR — see DiscriminatedContextualTypeLink.
    discriminated_contextual_type_links: ref[Array[ref[DiscriminatedContextualTypeLink]?]]
    discriminated_chain: ref[HashChain]

    ; ── c.typeAliasLinks (slice 105) ────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; declared_type_links' idiom, keyed on the SYMBOL POINTER for the same reason.
    ; ★★ ITS MEMO IS OBSERVABLE and the observer is the resolution stack:
    ; typeResolutionHasProperty's DeclaredType arm asks whether THIS row is
    ; filled, so a second table answering for it would break the cycle search
    ; rather than an optimisation.
    type_alias_links: ref[Array[ref[TypeAliasLink]?]]
    type_alias_links_index: ref[AddressIndex]

    ; ── c.valueSymbolLinks (slice 67) ───────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; declared_type_links' idiom exactly, and keyed on the SYMBOL POINTER for the
    ; same reason: Symbol.id is drawn lazily and its numbers are observable
    ; through private-name mangling.
    ;
    ; ★★ THIS TABLE'S MEMO IS OBSERVABLE WHERE THE OTHER ONE'S WAS NOT, and the
    ; difference is a good miniature of what a memo is for. declared_type_links'
    ; note says "nothing in this slice can observe a hit"; here
    ; getTypeOfFuncClassEnumModule is asked once per class by
    ; checkClassLikeDeclaration and again by checkClassExpression, and the two
    ; must answer the SAME object — a class's static type is compared by identity
    ; against the base's in checkMembersForOverrideModifier.
    value_symbol_links: ref[Array[ref[ValueSymbolLink]?]]

    ; `c.symbolReferenceLinks`' one slot, referenceKinds, by symbol link ordinal
    ; (slice 360). It stood on the Symbol since slice 182 (§3.37: written once per
    ; resolution, so a field and not a scanned table); it is the CHECKER's again
    ; because parallel checkers share the binder's symbols, and the unused-
    ; identifier checks must see this checker's references, not whichever
    ; checker ran first. A flat array keeps the field's O(1).
    symbol_reference_kinds: ref[Array[u32]]

    ; the checker's own scanner for error ranges and token questions, and the
    ; binder whose text it is pointed at (slice 363, scanner_for)
    error_scanner: ref[Scanner]?
    error_scanner_binder: ref[Binder]?

    ; ★★★ AND IT IS THE ONE LINKS TABLE WITH AN INDEX, MEASURED. Every table in
    ; this family is a LINEAR SCAN where upstream keeps a map, which is a property
    ; nobody had to price while no unit filled one — slice 182 measured 86 % of a
    ; run inside `symbol_node_link_of` and left the shape alone. The deferred
    ; reference retired in this slice put enough work through both: a `sample` of
    ; `recursiveReverseMappedType` reads 86 % in THIS function and one of
    ; `binaryArithmeticControlFlowGraphNotTooLarge` 97 % in symbol_node_link_of,
    ; both units MATCH before and over the runner's 60 s afterwards. The index is
    ; `rel_result_buckets`' idiom unchanged — an open-addressed Vector[int] of
    ; row+1, 0 the empty marker by Vector's memset — keyed on the row's own
    ; ADDRESS, which is what upstream's map is keyed on too. The row store stays
    ; the array it was: nothing else reads it, and identity is unaffected. Since
    ; slice 319 it is an AddressIndex like the other link tables: a probe compares
    ; the key in the slot and never loads the row to ask.
    ; what a ValueSymbolLink without its extra record reads: never written
    empty_value_symbol_extra: ref[ValueSymbolExtra]

    ; ── c.typeNodeLinks (slice 80) ──────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The same idiom a third time, keyed on the type NODE. Its memo IS
    ; observable, and by the same argument value_symbol_links gives: an anonymous
    ; type carries no name a printer could compare, so the only thing that tells
    ; two of them apart is identity — and getTypeFromTypeNode is asked twice about
    ; one annotation as soon as a second reader of that annotation lands.
    type_node_links: ref[Array[ref[TypeNodeLink]?]]
    ; The four node-keyed tables (type_node, node, symbol_node, signature) are
    ; DENSE per checker since slice 369: the node's link ordinal is process-wide,
    ; so a table indexed by it directly grew to the whole program's length in
    ; every checker (6.5 M slots, 4.5 % filled, ~270 MB per checker with the
    ; doubling). `node_local` maps the ordinal to this checker's own index + 1.
    ; Only in a checker POOL: a lone checker asks for nearly every ordinal itself,
    ; so its own numbering IS the ordinal and the map would be pure cost (+60 MB,
    ; +0.5 % on the whole of VS Code). The driver sets `node_local_on`.
    node_local: ref[Array[u32]]
    node_local_count: int
    node_local_on: bool

    ; c.mergedSymbols (slice 137) — the same idiom, keyed on the symbol that was
    ; merged AWAY. It is the table `get_merged_symbol` has read since slice 113
    ; while nothing could write it, which is the state that function's header calls
    ; out by name: *a lookup that always misses and a table that is never filled are
    ; the same code*.
    merged_symbols: ref[Array[ref[MergedSymbolLink]?]]
    merged_symbols_index: ref[AddressIndex]

    ; c.nodeLinks (slice 83) — the same idiom a fourth time. One row per accessor
    ; PAIR of the unit, so the scan is shorter than type_node_links'.
    node_links: ref[Array[ref[NodeLink]?]]

    ; c.enumMemberLinks (slice 116) — the same idiom a SIXTH time, keyed on the
    ; enum MEMBER node. Upstream this is its own table rather than a slot on
    ; nodeLinks, and it is transcribed as one for the reason the others are kept
    ; apart: getEnumMemberValue reads it and nothing else does.
    enum_member_links: ref[Array[ref[EnumMemberLink]?]]
    enum_member_links_index: ref[AddressIndex]

    ; c.enumLiteralTypes (slice 116) — the literal-type table's fourth family,
    ; beside string_literal_types, number_literal_types and bigint_literal_types,
    ; and keyed on a PAIR for the reason those three are keyed on a value: two
    ; enums may declare the same member value and their types must differ.
    enum_literal_types: ref[Array[ref[EnumLiteralRow]?]]

    ; c.signatureLinks (slice 84) — the same idiom a FIFTH time, keyed on the
    ; signature DECLARATION node. One row per function-like declaration whose
    ; signature is asked for, which in this port is a `return` statement's
    ; container, a type predicate's parent and an accessor.
    signature_links: ref[Array[ref[SignatureLink]?]]

    ; ast.SyntheticExpression's payload table (slice 134) — see
    ; SyntheticExpressionLink for why the type lives beside the node.
    synthetic_expression_links: ref[Array[ref[SyntheticExpressionLink]?]]
    synthetic_expression_links_index: ref[AddressIndex]

    ; c.cachedTypes at the four DecoratorContext kinds (slice 134).
    decorator_context_override_links: ref[Array[ref[DecoratorContextOverrideLink]?]]

    ; ── c.contextualBindingPatterns (slice 134) ─────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The stack getTypeFromBindingPattern pushes on while it computes a pattern's
    ; implied type. An Array plus a DEPTH counter, which is contextual_infos'
    ; idiom one screen down and for its reason: a `remove_last` this stdlib does
    ; not have, and a stack whose rows are reused costs no allocation per push.
    contextual_binding_patterns: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
    contextual_binding_pattern_depth: int

    ; ── the flow walk's checker-level state (slice 93) ──────────────────────
    ;
    ; c.flowAnalysisDisabled — set by the depth guard and read at
    ; getFlowTypeOfReferenceEx's first line. It is a LATCH: once the walk has
    ; overflowed inside one container the reference stops analysing for the rest
    ; of the check, which is why the flag is on the checker and not on a state.
    flow_analysis_disabled: bool

    ; ★ c.flowInvocationCount is NOT a field here. Upstream it feeds the tracer
    ; and getFlowCacheKey's staleness test; this port has neither, and a counter
    ; nothing prints is a number nobody can be wrong about (§3.5be). What says how
    ; often this chapter runs is the stop log, which is an instrument.

    ; c.flowNodeReachable, c.lastFlowNode, c.lastFlowNodeReachable — the two
    ; memos of isReachableFlowNodeWorker, transcribed rather than dropped.
    ;
    ; ★★ A MEMO OF A PURE FUNCTION LOOKS DROPPABLE AND THIS ONE IS NOT. Without
    ; the Shared-node table the branch-label arm re-walks every antecedent of
    ; every junction it meets, which is exponential in the nesting depth — the
    ; reference's own reason for the table. The one-entry `lastFlowNode` cache in
    ; front of it is an optimisation and is transcribed with its invalidation
    ; (the reduce-label arm clears it, because a label whose antecedents have
    ; been swapped is a different question under the same identity).
    ; c.flowTrace — the FLOWPIN's log; see FlowTraceEvent for why it exists.
    flow_trace: ref[Array[ref[FlowTraceEvent]?]]

    flow_node_reachable: ref[Array[ref[FlowReachableLink]?]]
    flow_node_reachable_index: ref[AddressIndex]
    flow_node_post_super: ref[Array[ref[FlowPostSuperLink]?]]
    flow_node_post_super_index: ref[AddressIndex]
    last_flow_node: ref[FlowNode]?
    last_flow_node_reachable: bool

    ; c.symbolNodeLinks (slice 91) — the same idiom a SIXTH time, keyed on the
    ; identifier NODE. One row per identifier the checker resolves, which is why
    ; the scan is the longest of the six and still linear: see the record's own
    ; note for why the memo is load-bearing rather than a saving.
    symbol_node_links: ref[Array[ref[SymbolNodeLink]?]]

    ; Indexed like value_symbol_links, for the measurement in that field's header.

    ; c.aliasSymbolLinks and c.moduleSymbolLinks (slice 110) — the same idiom a
    ; SEVENTH and EIGHTH time, both keyed on the SYMBOL pointer. See each record's
    ; own header for why the first one's memo may not use nil as *uncomputed*.
    alias_symbol_links: ref[Array[ref[AliasSymbolLink]?]]
    alias_symbol_links_index: ref[AddressIndex]

    ; c.unresolvedSymbols — the path-keyed table getUnresolvedSymbolForEntityName
    ; mints into. It is a SymbolTable and not an association list because its key
    ; IS a name: `A.B.C` as bytes, which is what a SymbolTable is keyed on.
    unresolved_symbols: ref[SymbolTable]

    ; c.undefinedProperties — getUndefinedProperty's memo, keyed by the property
    ; NAME exactly as upstream's `map[string]*ast.Symbol` is (slice 154).
    undefined_properties: ref[SymbolTable]

    ; c.errorTypes — one errorType per (alias symbol, type arguments). An
    ; ASSOCIATION LIST, for TypeInstantiation's reason: upstream keys a hash and
    ; buys a collision question with it, and this list holds one entry per
    ; unresolved name in a unit.
    error_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
    module_symbol_links: ref[Array[ref[ModuleSymbolLink]?]]
    module_symbol_links_index: ref[AddressIndex]

    ; c.membersAndExportsLinks and c.lateBoundLinks (slice 129) — the same
    ; mint-on-miss idiom an ELEVENTH and TWELFTH time; see each record's header.
    members_and_exports_links: ref[Array[ref[MembersAndExportsLink]?]]
    members_and_exports_links_index: ref[AddressIndex]
    late_bound_links: ref[Array[ref[LateBoundLink]?]]
    late_bound_links_index: ref[AddressIndex]

    ; c.jsxElementLinks and c.sourceFileLinks (slice 119) — the same mint-on-miss
    ; idiom a NINTH and TENTH time; see each record's own header.
    jsx_element_links: ref[Array[ref[JsxElementLink]?]]
    jsx_element_links_index: ref[AddressIndex]
    source_file_links: ref[Array[ref[SourceFileLink]?]]
    source_file_links_index: ref[AddressIndex]

    ; c._jsxNamespace and c._jsxFactoryEntity — the checker-wide fallback the file
    ; links stand in front of. ★They are a MEMO WITH A SIDE EFFECT and not a
    ; cache: `getJsxNamespace`'s tail mints the factory entity `React.createElement`
    ; the first time it runs and hands the SAME node out afterwards, and
    ; `getJsxFactoryEntity` answers it. An empty name means *not yet computed*.
    jsx_namespace_name: Slice[char]
    jsx_factory_entity: ref[AstNode]?
    ; the JSX transformer's generated runtime-import names → their specifiers (slice 246)
    jsx_import_refs: ref[Array[NodeDeclPair]]?
    ; compilerOptions.Jsx, JsxFactory, ReactNamespace, JsxFragmentFactory and
    ; JsxImportSource (slice 255). The checker yardstick's harness sets none of
    ; them — JsxEmitNone and empty is that harness; the emit driver sets the case's.
    jsx_emit: int
    jsx_factory_option: Slice[char]
    react_namespace_option: Slice[char]
    jsx_fragment_factory_option: Slice[char]
    jsx_import_source_option: Slice[char]
    ; addDeferredDiagnostic's two collision checks (slice 258): the nodes whose
    ; check runs in produce_deferred_diagnostics, after the markers are set
    deferred_checks: ref[Array[DeferredCheck]]?

    ; ── NewChecker's four singleton signatures (slices 89 and 108) ──────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ ALL FOUR ARE MINTED SINCE SLICE 108, AND THE ORDER IS THE REFERENCE'S
    ; because the ids are assigned along it. Slice 89 minted `resolving_signature`
    ; alone and named the other three at the function that reads each; this slice
    ; ports those functions — resolveUntypedCall answers `any_signature`,
    ; resolveErrorCall answers `unknown_signature`, and the silentNeverType arm of
    ; resolveCallExpression answers `silent_never_signature` — so §3.5be's list is
    ; closed. ★The `silent-never-signature` row was ZERO in both stages and is
    ; removed anyway: the singleton is one line, and a stop kept beside a minted
    ; sibling is a wall that is no longer there.
    any_signature: ref[Signature]
    unknown_signature: ref[Signature]
    resolving_signature: ref[Signature]
    silent_never_signature: ref[Signature]

    ; c.SignatureCount — nextSignatureId, in creation order. It is a COUNTER and
    ; not a length, for `type_count`'s reason: a signature is minted into the
    ; region and the table that holds it is keyed on the declaration, not on the
    ; id, so nothing can recover the count from a container.
    signature_count: int

    ; ── c.typeResolutions (slice 67) ────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The resolution stack, as a backing array plus a DEPTH. Scaly's Array has no
    ; pop, so the array only ever grows and `type_resolution_count` is the logical
    ; length; a push past the high-water mark adds and one below it overwrites.
    ; The reference clears the popped entry "to avoid memory leaks" and the
    ; overwrite is the same statement under RBMM.
    type_resolutions: ref[Array[ref[TypeResolution]?]]
    type_resolution_count: int

    ; ── the VARIANCE machinery (slice 130) ──────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; c.varianceLinks, c.markerTypes and the three MARKER type parameters, which
    ; are what makes a variance measurable at all: the worker instantiates the
    ; generic type once with `markerSubType` and once with `markerSuperType` —
    ; two type parameters in a known subtype relation, since markerSubType's
    ; CONSTRAINT is markerSuperType — and reads the variance off which way round
    ; the two instantiations are assignable.
    variance_links: ref[Array[ref[VarianceLink]?]]
    variance_links_index: ref[AddressIndex]
    marker_types: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
    ; the same set by address (slice 335): the membership test scanned the list
    marker_type_index: ref[AddressIndex]
    marker_super_type: ref[Type]?
    marker_sub_type: ref[Type]?
    marker_other_type: ref[Type]?

    ; c.markerSuperTypeForCheck / c.markerSubTypeForCheck and c.varianceTypeParameter
    ; — the FOURTH and FIFTH marker and the parameter they are named after. They
    ; are a separate pair from the three above and not a reuse of them: the worker
    ; MEASURES a variance and may run under any other comparison, while these two
    ; CHECK a declared one and are excluded from the relation's own type-parameter
    ; error (reportRelationError's `target != markerSuperTypeForCheck` conjunct).
    ; That exclusion is not transcribed: the arm it guards is the first of the
    ; three report_relation_error names as having NO INPUT, because a
    ; TypeParameter target is Instantiable and that function is reached only when
    ; neither side is. The day the arm gets input, the conjunct comes with it.
    marker_super_type_for_check: ref[Type]?
    marker_sub_type_for_check: ref[Type]?
    variance_type_parameter: ref[Type]?

    ; c.reliabilityFlags and c.inVarianceComputation. The flags are set by the two
    ; reliability MAPPERS (type_mapper_map's Reliability arm) when a marker type
    ; passes through an instantiation the relation cannot account for linearly;
    ; the worker reads them straight after its two comparisons and turns them into
    ; the Unmeasurable/Unreliable bits of the answer. `in_variance_computation` is
    ; read by type_arguments_related_to, which propagates the unreliable flag only
    ; while a measurement is in progress.
    reliability_flags: int
    in_variance_computation: bool

    ; The member log, slice 86 — see MemberEvent for why it exists.
    member_log: ref[Array[ref[MemberEvent]?]]
    ; whether the pin logs above and beside it (member, typeref, prop, this, with,
    ; objlit, fn, kind, fork, call, rel, and the flow trace) record at all: TypeDump reads them, the
    ; driver does not, and on a large program they are gigabytes (stop_log is not
    ; one of them — the driver reports its stops through it)
    record_events: bool

    ; The TYPEREF PIN's log — see TypeRefEvent's header.
    typeref_log: ref[Array[ref[TypeRefEvent]?]]

    ; The PROPPIN's log — see PropEvent's header.
    prop_log: ref[Array[ref[PropEvent]?]]

    ; The THISPIN's log (slice 97) — see ThisEvent's header.
    this_log: ref[Array[ref[ThisEvent]?]]

    ; The WITHPIN's log (slice 98) — see WithThisEvent's header.
    with_log: ref[Array[ref[WithThisEvent]?]]

    ; ── c.contextualInfos (slice 99) ────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The contextual-type stack, as a backing array plus a DEPTH — type_resolutions'
    ; idiom and for its reason (Scaly's Array has no pop).
    contextual_infos: ref[Array[ref[ContextualInfo]?]]
    contextual_depth: int

    ; c.patternForType (slice 99) — see PatternForType's header for why it is an
    ; association list and why it has a reader.
    pattern_for_types: ref[Array[ref[PatternForType]?]]
    ; its rows chained by the key's hash, caught up per lookup (slice 333)
    pattern_for_types_chain: ref[HashChain]

    ; The OBJPIN's log (slice 99) — see ObjLitEvent's header.
    objlit_log: ref[Array[ref[ObjLitEvent]?]]

    ; The FNPIN's log (slice 100) — see FnEvent's header.
    fn_log: ref[Array[ref[FnEvent]?]]

    ; The RELPIN's log (slice 101) — see RelEvent's header.
    rel_log: ref[Array[ref[RelEvent]?]]

    ; The KINDPIN's log (slice 102) — see KindEvent's header.
    kind_log: ref[Array[ref[KindEvent]?]]

    ; The FORKPIN's log (slice 103) — see ForkEvent's header.
    fork_log: ref[Array[ref[ForkEvent]?]]

    ; ── the relation's RECURSION state (slice 118) ───────────────────────────
    ;
    ; Upstream these are fields of a `Relater` allocated per top-level comparison;
    ; here the relation is threaded as parameters, so they live on the checker and
    ; are reset by `check_type_related_to` at the entry of every comparison.
    ;
    ; ★★★ `rel_maybe_*` IS A STACK AND NOT A SET, AND SINCE SLICE 172 THAT IS A
    ; DECISION RATHER THAN A CONSEQUENCE. Upstream `maybeKeys` has TWO jobs: it
    ; detects a cycle (*these two are already being compared, assume they
    ; relate*) and it records the keys a later success promotes into the shared
    ; cache. The second job is what makes it a growing list with a `maybeStart`
    ; watermark. The cache exists now — but this port promotes NOTHING into it
    ; from here: `rel_maybe_hits` decides whether a frame's own TRUE was definite,
    ; so only that one key is written and the stack keeps only the first job.
    ; ★★★THE SECOND JOB WAS TRIED AND MEASURED WORSE: with the keys kept to a
    ; watermark, the stack holds a whole successful subtree and the linear scan
    ; below is over it — the file this chapter is about went from 81 s to over ten
    ; minutes, and a tombstone SET beside it did not recover the time either.
    ; ★The ANSWER is unaffected: Maybe is read as *related* at every reader.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE KEY IS THE ID TRIPLE and not `getRelationKey`'s hash. That function's
    ; two branches differ only for a type reference with GENERIC arguments, where
    ; it builds a key out of the type parameters so that two instantiations can
    ; share a cache row; the plain branch it falls back to is `source.id`,
    ; `target.id` and the intersection state, which is this triple exactly. Sharing
    ; rows is a cache concern, so the branch that exists for it is not written —
    ; and the direction it errs in is the safe one: two comparisons this port keeps
    ; apart are two comparisons upstream might have merged, so a cycle is detected
    ; no LATER than the depth limit, never earlier than upstream.
    ; ★★ THE ARRAYS NEVER SHRINK AND A DEPTH COUNTER IS THE STACK, which is
    ; `base_constraint_stack`'s idiom in this file and not a new one: `Array` has
    ; `add`, `put` and `get_length` and no `pop`, so a push writes at the depth and
    ; grows only when the depth has never been reached before.
    rel_maybe_source: ref[Array[int]]
    rel_maybe_target: ref[Array[int]]
    rel_maybe_state: ref[Array[int]]
    rel_maybe_depth: int
    rel_source_stack: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
    rel_source_depth: int
    rel_target_stack: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
    rel_target_depth: int
    ; ★★★ ONE RELATER PER checkTypeRelatedTo (slice 228). Upstream allocates a
    ; fresh Relater — empty maybe keys, empty source/target stacks — for every
    ; check, and a check NESTED in a relation (the variance measurement's
    ; isTypeAssignableTo) sees none of the outer one's state. This port keeps ONE
    ; set of arrays and used to reset the three depths to 0 for the nested check,
    ; which made it OVERWRITE the outer check's entries from index 0: the outer
    ; frames then scanned clobbered maybe keys and a clobbered deep-nesting
    ; stack. The nested check now starts at the current depth and these bases
    ; say where its own view begins.
    rel_maybe_base: int
    rel_source_base: int
    rel_target_base: int
    rel_expanding_flags: int
    rel_overflow: bool

    ; ── the relation's RESULT CACHE (slice 172) ────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE NOTE AT is_type_related_to SAID THIS MAP HAD NO WRITER — *the only
    ; writer in relater.go is `recursiveTypeRelatedTo`, the recursion this slice
    ; does not have* — and that recursion landed in slice 155. Measured on
    ; `FlattenKeys<{ a: 1 }>` alone: **5 000 000 recursive_type_related_to calls
    ; over 2 459 DISTINCT keys**, 1 813 types minted — a repetition rate near
    ; 2000:1, and 81 s where the reference answers the whole file in 0.19 s. The
    ; comparison tree is re-walked instead of remembered.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE FIRST DIAGNOSIS OF THAT NUMBER WAS WRONG. Slice 171 cached only
    ; FAILED results, saw 599 hits in 4 000 000 calls over 45 distinct failing
    ; keys, and concluded the calls were mostly DISTINCT PAIRS. They are not:
    ; 99 % of them answer TRUE, so a failure-only cache measures the 1 % and says
    ; nothing about the rest. **A hit rate is a statement about the half a cache
    ; holds, never about the population.**
    rel_result_entries: ref[Array[RelationCacheRow]]
    rel_result_buckets: ref[Vector[int]]?
    rel_result_mask: int

    ; ── the COMPLEXITY BUDGET (slice 200) ──────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; `Relater.relationCount`, set to `(16_000_000 - relation.size()) / 8` at the
    ; head of every top-level comparison and spent one unit per cache row the
    ; comparison writes. When it runs out the comparison is abandoned exactly as
    ; the hundred-frame limit abandons one, and the two are told apart only by
    ; which of them tripped — `Excessive_complexity_comparing_types` against
    ; `Excessive_stack_depth_comparing_types`.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS THE ONLY BRAKE ON A COMPARISON THAT IS *WIDE* RATHER THAN DEEP,
    ; and its absence is measurable rather than theoretical: five stage-2 units
    ; ran past the runner's sixty-second silence limit and were scored TIMEOUT,
    ; and `relationComplexityError` — sixteen lines, the whole of whose point is
    ; this budget — took **15.5 s** to answer with the two reports missing. The
    ; depth limiter cannot see any of it: every one of those frames is shallow.
    ;
    ; ★★ THE SIZE IS PER RELATION, so a single shared table needs a counter per
    ; relation id. The five ids are dense (RelationAssignable..RelationIdentity),
    ; so the counter is an Array indexed by the id and not a map.
    rel_relation_count: int
    ; the nesting of isRelatedTo inside one checkTypeRelatedTo: the head message is
    ; the TOP call's parameter, nil in every recursive one
    rel_related_nesting: int
    rel_result_counts: ref[Array[int]]

    ; ★★★ THE TERNARY'S SUBSTITUTE, AND IT IS EXACT. Upstream's result is a
    ; TERNARY and its write-back needs the distinction: a definite True is
    ; recorded at once, a MAYBE only once the stacks are empty, because a Maybe is
    ; an ASSUMPTION and caching one at depth would make it permanent. This port's
    ; result is a `bool` with Maybe collapsed into true — but a Maybe has exactly
    ; TWO sources in this relation (the maybe-stack hit and the ExpandingFlagsBoth
    ; brake), so a MONOTONE COUNTER of them answers the question the type cannot:
    ; a frame whose subtree left the counter unchanged used no assumption, and its
    ; TRUE is definite. Slice 171's other attempt — keeping the maybe keys to a
    ; watermark and promoting them at the outermost frame, which is upstream's own
    ; shape — needs the stack to hold a whole successful subtree and made the same
    ; file slower than the 81 s it was meant to fix. This needs no stack at all.
    rel_maybe_hits: int
    ; ★★★ THE THIRD TERNARY VALUE (slice 228). Upstream's Ternary has FOUR members
    ; and this port's bool-plus-counter had modelled three: False, True, and Maybe
    ; (a true that moved `rel_maybe_hits`). The fourth, `TernaryUnknown`, is what a
    ; RECURSIVE variance measurement answers (`getVariances`/`getAliasVariances`
    ; returning the empty list), and it is not a Maybe: `recursiveTypeRelatedTo`
    ; records a Maybe as Succeeded when it reaches depth 0 and NEVER records an
    ; Unknown. The two sentinels answered a plain `true` here, i.e. a DEFINITE
    ; True, and the frame wrote a Succeeded row the reference never writes.
    rel_unknown_hits: int

    ; ── the reverse-mapped family's own state (slice 160) ───────────────────
    ;
    ; `reverseMappedCache`, `reverseHomomorphicMappedCache`,
    ; `reverseMappedSourceStack`, `reverseMappedTargetStack` and
    ; `reverseExpandingFlags`. The two stacks are the THIRD pair of this kind —
    ; the relater's above, inference's on its own state — and is_deeply_nested_
    ; type_in was written with a stack PARAMETER for exactly this arrival.
    ;
    ; ★ They live on the Checker and not on an inference state because the
    ; reference puts them there, and the reason is visible in the call graph:
    ; infer_reverse_mapped_type is reached from resolve_reverse_mapped_type_
    ; members, i.e. from a MEMBER RESOLUTION long after the inference that minted
    ; the type has finished, where no inference state is in hand.
    reverse_mapped_cache: ref[Array[ref[ReverseMappedRow]?]]
    reverse_homomorphic_cache: ref[Array[ref[ReverseMappedRow]?]]
    reverse_mapped_source_stack: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
    reverse_mapped_target_stack: ref[Array[ref[Type]?]]
    reverse_expanding_flags: int

    ; The PRINTER's reverse-mapped stack — upstream's `b.ctx.reverseMappedStack`,
    ; which lives on the node builder's context. This port's printer is the
    ; Checker's own, so the stack is a field beside the other three; it is pushed
    ; only across a reverse-mapped property's type and is empty everywhere else.
    printer_reverse_mapped_stack: ref[Array[ref[Symbol]?]]

    ; ★★★ THE ERROR CHAIN, SLICE 167 — see `define ErrorChain`. Every level that
    ; fails PREPENDS here and `check_type_related_to_ex` emits the head once, which
    ; is `Relater.errorChain` and `createDiagnosticChainFromErrorChain` between
    ; them. It replaces `rel_reported` / `rel_reported_unmatched` /
    ; `rel_reported_mark` / `rel_unmatched_*`, the latch that kept the INNERMOST
    ; message.
    rel_error_chain: ref[ErrorChain]?
    ; getTypeOfDottedName's `diagnostic` (the index it adds related information to,
    ; -1 for none), a cell rather than a parameter threaded through the recursion
    explicit_type_diagnostic: int

    ; ★★★ `NodeBuilderContext.mapper`, SLICE 187 — and the note that said this
    ; port has none was an ARGUMENT ABOUT THE WRONG FIELD. It read *`ctx.mapper`
    ; is nil for the same reason [enclosingDeclaration is nil]*, which is true of
    ; the accessibility half and false of this one: the mapper is set by
    ; `enterSignatureScope`, i.e. by printing an INSTANTIATED signature, and
    ; `typeToString` prints those constantly. Its one reader upstream is
    ; `b.getTypeFromTypeNode(node, false)` — the node-REUSE site's identity test
    ; and its per-node fallback — plus `tryVisitTypeReference`'s refusal for a type
    ; parameter the context remaps. Without it `K2 extends keys2<T, K1>` printed
    ; `K2 extends Extract<Foo[K1] extends object ? keyof Foo[K1] : never, string>`:
    ; the ALIAS a constraint is written with survives only through the reused NODE,
    ; because the instantiated type genuinely loses it (measured — the same alias
    ; in a RETURN position prints expanded on BOTH sides).
    printer_type_mapper: ref[TypeMapper]?

    ; `Relater.errorNode`. It was a PARAMETER threaded down every level, which is
    ; what this port used in place of the reference's `reportErrors` bool (a null
    ; node is a level that must not report). The cell is owed by the chain rather
    ; than by the parameter: `report_excess_property` MOVES the node — `r.errorNode
    ; = name`, so TS2353 lands on the property's own name — and with the emission
    ; at the tail there has to be somewhere for the move to land. The parameter
    ; stays; it still says whether a level reports.
    rel_error_node: ref[AstNode]?
    ; the reference's `diagnosticOutput` for isSignatureApplicable: the INDICES of the
    ; diagnostics the relation machinery added while `relation_capture_on` was set —
    ; side effects of checking the arguments (TS2454 on an unassigned variable) stay
    ; outside it, so re-coding the last candidate's errors under TS2769 no longer
    ; re-codes them (slice 209; `incompatibleTypes`)
    relation_capture_on: bool
    ; serializationLevel — the TypeToString invocations in progress; see
    ; type_to_string_serialized and add_diagnostic_direct (slice 213).
    serialization_level: int
    ; approximateLength's stand-in: the characters of the constituent pieces a print has
    ; produced but not yet appended to their parent builder (write_constituent_list),
    ; so the truncation budget sees the WHOLE print and not the current builder alone.
    printer_emitted: int
    ; ★★★ THE REFERENCE'S TRUNCATION BUDGET (slice 225): `approximateLength` is an
    ; ESTIMATE the node builder adds to at ~49 sites (keyword widths, name
    ; lengths, fixed costs per node kind), consulted by checkTruncationLength at
    ; SIX places only (type-argument and constituent lists, the members of an
    ; object type, a conditional type), and STICKY once past 1 000 000 (the
    ; harness's noErrorTruncation). The port counted emitted characters and
    ; stopped at the wall; both units past the wall are decided by where the
    ; estimate crosses it, not by the characters.
    printer_approximate_length: int
    printer_truncating: bool
    relation_capture: ref[Array[int]]

    ; `saveErrorState.errorChain`'s DEPTH, taken at structuredTypeRelatedTo and
    ; read by `reportStructuralErrors` inside its worker — see that function's
    ; header for why a depth stands for the identity there. It is a cell rather
    ; than a local because the port's worker is a separate function and the value
    ; belongs to the comparison, exactly as upstream's `saveErrorState` does.
    rel_structural_chain_depth: int
    rel_structural_chain: ref[ErrorChain]?

    ; ★★★ THE HEAD MESSAGE, SLICE 124 — and it is a CELL rather than a parameter
    ; for the reason upstream makes it a Relater FIELD: the message belongs to the
    ; comparison as a whole and is consulted exactly once, at the outermost level
    ; that reports (which the chain's HEAD now selects). Threading it through
    ; is_related_to_ex's recursion would put a parameter on forty call sites so
    ; that one of them could read it.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS A CODE AND THEREFORE OBSERVABLE. §3.5's note at chooseOverload says
    ; a head message *would be a parameter with a writer and no reader* — true then
    ; and false now: the class heritage checks hand TS2415 / TS2417 / TS2420 to the
    ; relation with a non-nil error node, so the emitted `C` line's code IS the
    ; head. ★And it SUPPRESSES the whole default fork, TS2719 and the
    ; string-literal suggestion included, exactly as `if message == nil` does
    ; upstream — which also means the head path never runs `type_to_string` and so
    ; cannot stop where the default path can.
    rel_head_code: int

    ; ★★★ THE COMPARER'S CAPTURES, SLICE 126. `signatureRelatedTo` hands
    ; compareSignaturesRelated a closure over the relation and over its own
    ; `intersectionState`; a flag carries neither, so the two travel here and the
    ; comparer reads them. ★They are SAVED AND RESTORED around every call that can
    ; recurse — the comparison walks back into is_related_to_ex, which reaches
    ; signatures_related_to again under a different relation, and a cell that is
    ; only written is a cell that answers for the wrong comparison.
    rel_cur_relation: int
    rel_cur_intersection_state: int

    ; The CALLPIN's log (slice 108) — see CallEvent's header.
    call_log: ref[Array[ref[CallEvent]?]]

    ; The two cells contextuallyCheck writes and check_function_expression_or_
    ; object_literal_method reads. ★They are STATE and not a return value because
    ; the reference's function returns nothing and the pin has to see what it did
    ; — the same shape slice 97's `arm` column took, threaded through a cell
    ; upstream has no parameter for.
    fn_pin_arm: int
    fn_pin_ctxsig: int

    ; c.instantiationDepth (slice 86) and c.instantiationCount.
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 154: THE SECOND HALF WAS A NOTE THAT OUTLIVED ITS REASON. It read
    ; *the reference pairs it with `instantiationCount`, a 5 000 000 budget reset
    ; per statement by checkSourceElement — a line this port does not have, so the
    ; counter is named here rather than carried and the depth limiter is the whole
    ; guard*. checkSourceElement has been in this file since slice 47 and
    ; checkExpressionEx — the reference's other reset — since slice 70, so the line
    ; the note is waiting for had already landed twice; the budget was a **dropped
    ; disjunct** of `instantiationDepth == 100 || instantiationCount >= 5_000_000`,
    ; and §3.5gi's rule is exactly this shape: a conjunct dropped for want of a
    ; reader is not revisited when the reader arrives, because it has no tag, no
    ; stop and no row on any frontier.
    instantiation_depth: int
    instantiation_count: int
    ; TotalInstantiationCount: never reset, the Instantiations row of --diagnostics
    total_instantiation_count: int

    ; c.activeMappers + c.activeTypeMappersCaches, as one stack — see
    ; ActiveMapper for why the two lists are one here and what the memo answers.
    ; ★ A LIST PLUS A COUNT, which is this file's idiom for a stack
    ; (`type_resolutions` / `type_resolution_count`) and is upstream's own shape
    ; here too: `popActiveMapper` shortens the slice but keeps the popped map in
    ; its capacity so the next push can reuse it, and reusing the row is exactly
    ; what a count does.
    active_mappers: ref[Array[ref[ActiveMapper]?]]?
    active_mapper_count: int

    ; c.conditionalConstraintDepth — computeBaseConstraint's own limiter, and it is
    ; a SEPARATE budget from instantiation_depth because the recursion it bounds is
    ; a different one: a conditional's constraint is another conditional's input,
    ; and the walk can be unbounded without instantiating anything.
    conditional_constraint_depth: int

    ; c.currentNode — the node checkSourceElement, checkDeferredNode and
    ; checkExpressionEx save, set and restore around their work.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT IS THE ERROR LOCATION OF EVERY LIMITER, and that is why it arrives with
    ; the counter above. Both the instantiation-depth limit and the conditional
    ; type's 1000-iteration tail limit report `Type_instantiation_is_excessively_
    ; deep_and_possibly_infinite` AT this node, and both stood as stops whose
    ; comment read *there is no currentNode in this port — the field is set by
    ; checkSourceElement, which is a chapter away*.
    ; ★A null here reports nothing, which is the reference's own behaviour:
    ; NewDiagnosticForNode(nil, …) builds a diagnostic with no file, and
    ; GetDiagnostics answers per file.
    current_node: ref[AstNode]?

    ; c.withinUnreachableCode — the SECOND register checkSourceElement saves and
    ; restores, and slice 154 left it out with a reason that has now expired.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE REASON WAS *the reachability check produces nothing this yardstick
    ; compares*, AND IT PROVED THE WRONG HALF. Its own diagnostic is a suggestion
    ; and is genuinely invisible — 0 of 64 148 reference C lines carry TS7027, this
    ; oracle leaving AllowUnreachableCode unset so `addErrorOrSuggestion(false, …)`
    ; takes the suggestion branch. What the chapter DOES produce is the second
    ; visit to every node under a flow-call node: is_source_element_unreachable
    ; asks is_reachable_flow_node, whose Call arm asks get_effects_signature,
    ; whose expression-statement arm asks get_type_of_dotted_name, whose super arm
    ; CALLS check_super_expression — which reports. Twelve units carried a missing
    ; `super` diagnostic for exactly that reason and for no other. §3.5hm's shape
    ; one slice on: the note proved the claim about the DIAGNOSTIC and asserted the
    ; claim about the CHAPTER.
    within_unreachable_code: bool

    ; c.resolutionStart. It has a READER here (findResolutionCycleStartIndex) and
    ; no writer, which is the reverse of §3.5be's forbidden shape and is why it is
    ; a field rather than a literal 0: both of its writers are named
    ; (getResolvedSignature, and the relater's recursion guard), each temporarily
    ; resets the stack floor around a nested resolution, and a literal here would
    ; become a silent divergence the day either lands.
    resolution_start: int

    ; The unported report, in the parser's and the binder's shape: the FIRST
    ; construct this slice does not implement, its tag, and a detail (a kind, in
    ; almost every case). The dump is suppressed once this is set — an incomplete
    ; port that answered a well-formed dump would be reporting agreement it has
    ; not earned.
    unported_pos: int
    unported_tag: Slice[char]
    unported_detail: int

    ; ★★★ THE COUNT OF REPORTS, AND SLICE 69 IS WHAT MADE IT NECESSARY. The FLAG
    ; answers *has this unit stopped*, which is what every caller before this slice
    ; needed — they compare it before and after a call to decide whether to carry
    ; on. It cannot answer *did THIS call stop*, because record_unported is
    ; first-wins: after the first report the flag no longer moves, and
    ; check_source_elements deliberately keeps walking (slice 49's expiry).
    ;
    ; ★★★ THAT DIFFERENCE ONLY CHANGES AN ANSWER WHERE A NULL IS AMBIGUOUS, and
    ; slice 69 is the first place it is: getTypeForVariableLikeDeclaration answers
    ; nil BOTH for *nothing can be inferred* — which the widener turns into `any`
    ; plus an implicit-any diagnostic — and for a stop. Measured: with the flag
    ; comparison, every declaration after a file's first report INVENTED a TS7005,
    ; and diagcheck went red on 377 units. The count is monotone, so `mark before,
    ; mark after` is exact.
    unported_count: int

    ; ── c.suggestionDiagnostics, as a COUNT (slice 70) ──────────────────────
    ;
    ; The reference keeps a second diagnostic list for CategorySuggestion, which
    ; GetDiagnostics does not read — so a suggestion must never reach the C
    ; section, and this port keeps only what makes a suggestion SITE provable:
    ; how many fired. checkGrammarNumericLiteral is the first of them.
    ;
    ; ★ A list would be a field with no reader (§3.5be); the counter has one, and
    ; it is a control rather than the port — which is the honest description of
    ; what a suggestion is worth to this yardstick.
    suggestion_count: int

    ; ── c.argumentsSymbol (slice 79) ────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; NewChecker mints this eagerly, beside `c.requireSymbol`, and the name resolver
    ; answers it for `arguments` inside any function-like container. It is a
    ; TRANSIENT symbol in no table, so it draws no symbol id (Symbol.id is lazy) and
    ; appears in no dump — which is what makes adding it observable only through
    ; resolve_name.
    ;
    ; ★★★ `c.requireSymbol` IS MINTED SINCE SLICE 143 AND IT HAS TWO READERS, which
    ; is what turned the note here from a §3.5be claim about the future into an
    ; expired one: the JS require fallback at the tail of resolve_name ANSWERS it,
    ; and getTypeOfVariableOrParameterOrPropertyWorker's second line answers `any`
    ; for it — which is the whole reason that worker's value-declaration assertion
    ; was reachable at all.
    arguments_symbol: ref[Symbol]
    require_symbol: ref[Symbol]

    ; ── c.unknownSymbol (slice 91) ──────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; `c.newSymbol(SymbolFlagsProperty, "unknown")`, minted in NewChecker beside
    ; the one above. It is the value getResolvedSymbol parks in its links slot
    ; when the lookup finds nothing, and checkIdentifier's SECOND line compares
    ; against it — so it is a sentinel with two readers rather than a field with
    ; none (§3.5be). ★It is transient and in no table, so it draws no symbol id and
    ; no dump can see it, exactly as arguments_symbol cannot.
    unknown_symbol: ref[Symbol]

    ; ── c.globals, c.undefinedSymbol, c.globalThisSymbol (slice 104) ────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE TABLE IS HALF OF THE REFERENCE'S AND THE HALF IT IS MISSING IS THE
    ; LIB. `initializeChecker` merges the top-level locals of every NON-MODULE file
    ; of the program into one table; the oracle's program is lib.es5.d.ts plus the
    ; unit, and this Checker has the unit alone. So `Array`, `String` and `console`
    ; are still not here — that is §3.11 — while a SCRIPT file's own top-level
    ; names now are, which is exactly what lookup_globals' own note called *a
    ; strict improvement and still a subset of upstream's table*.
    ;
    ; ★★ IT STAYS A SUBSET AND THAT IS WHAT KEEPS EVERY CALLER'S REPORT A
    ; SUBSEQUENCE. Both sides run the same tree walk and then consult a table;
    ; ours holds fewer names than theirs and never a different one, so a name we
    ; answer is a name they answer, and a name we miss is a report we do not make.
    globals: ref[SymbolTable]

    ; `c.newSymbol(SymbolFlagsProperty, "undefined")`, minted in NewChecker beside
    ; arguments_symbol and unknown_symbol — and unlike those two it is put INTO a
    ; table, by addUndefinedToGlobalsOrErrorOnRedeclaration. Its type is assigned
    ; eagerly there too, which is why `undefined` in an expression position gets a
    ; type from this slice and `arguments` does not: the latter's is
    ; `getGlobalType("IArguments")` and lives in the lib.
    undefined_symbol: ref[Symbol]

    ; `c.newSymbolEx(SymbolFlagsModule, "globalThis", CheckFlagsReadonly)`. It is
    ; seeded into the table before anything is merged, and its EXPORTS slot IS the
    ; globals table rather than a copy of it (Symbol.set_exports says why).
    global_this_symbol: ref[Symbol]

    ; c.assignmentReducedTypes (slice 123) — see AssignmentReducedRow.
    assignment_reduced_types: ref[Array[ref[AssignmentReducedRow]?]]
    ; its rows chained by the key's hash, caught up per lookup (slice 333)
    assignment_reduced_types_chain: ref[HashChain]

    ; c.markedAssignmentSymbolLinks (slice 123) — see MarkedAssignmentLink.
    marked_assignment_links: ref[Array[ref[MarkedAssignmentLink]?]]
    marked_assignment_links_index: ref[AddressIndex]

    ; c.switchStatementLinks (slice 123) — see SwitchStatementLink.
    switch_statement_links: ref[Array[ref[SwitchStatementLink]?]]
    switch_statement_links_index: ref[AddressIndex]

    ; c.narrowedTypes (slice 123) — getNarrowedType's memo; see NarrowedTypeRow.
    narrowed_types: ref[Array[ref[NarrowedTypeRow]?]]

    ; c.inlineLevel (slice 123) — narrowType's aliased-conditional budget. The
    ; reference inlines up to FIVE levels of `const c = a === "x"` so that
    ; `if (c)` narrows `a`, and the counter is what bounds it.
    inline_level: int

    ; ── inference (slice 122) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; c.inferenceContextInfos — the stack of (node, context) pairs a nested call
    ; searches for the context it is inside; c.cachedSignatures — the
    ; (signature, type arguments) intern table getSignatureInstantiation needs so
    ; that one call site's instantiation is ONE type.
    inference_context_infos: ref[Array[ref[InferenceContextInfo]?]]

    ; c.instantiationExpressionTypes (slice 181) — see InstantiationExpressionCacheRow.
    instantiation_expression_types: ref[Array[ref[InstantiationExpressionCacheRow]?]]

    ; ── the stop log (slice 75) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; Every stop in the order it happened, where `unported_tag` is the first and
    ; `unported_count` the total. See StopEvent for why both are needed.
    stop_log: ref[Array[ref[StopEvent]?]]
)
{
    ; ── slice 113: the LIB, read the way the oracle's program reads it ───────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE HALF OF §3.11 THAT WAS LEFT IS THIS, AND IT IS NOT A LANGUAGE
    ; QUESTION AFTER ALL. The heading said the lib is *"a second tree that has to
    ; outlive a per-unit region without being copied into it"* — which is a
    ; statement about a compiler that checks many units in one process. Every
    ; program in this package checks ONE unit and owns ONE region, so the lib's
    ; tree is allocated on the same host page as the unit's and outlives nothing.
    ; The RBMM question is real and it arrives with the day a driver checks two
    ; units; it was never in the way of the table.
    ;
    ; ★ Answers false rather than reporting, and the caller turns that into the
    ; unit's whole answer. The lib parses and binds BYTE-IDENTICALLY to the
    ; reference today (measured: `oracle_ast` and `oracle_symbols` against ours over
    ; lib.es5.d.ts, zero differing lines), so a false here is a port regression and
    ; not a gap — it reddens every unit at once, which is the loudest shape
    ; available and the reason this is not a stop.
    ; tsoptions.GetLibFileName: a lib NAME (`es2015`, `dom.iterable`, `es6`) to its file
    function lib_file_name(host: ref[Page], name: Slice[char]) returns Slice[char]
    {
        let sb StringBuilder^host()
        var i 0
        while i < (name.length as int)
        {
            var ch name[i as size_t] as int
            if (ch >= ("A" as char) as int) and (ch <= ("Z" as char) as int)
                set ch: ch + 32
            sb.append(ch as char)
            set i: i + 1
        }
        let lower String^host(sb.to_string())
        let lower_view Slice[char](lower.get_length() as int, lower.get_buffer() as pointer[char])
        if lower_view.ends_with(".d.ts")
            return lower_view
        ; tsoptions.GetLibFileName: LibMap first, so an alias such as
        ; `esnext.asynciterable` names lib.es2018.asynciterable.d.ts
        let mapped TsOptionDecls.enum_value(TsOptionDecls.index_of("lib"), lower_view)
        if (mapped.length as int) > 0
            return mapped
        let out StringBuilder^host()
        out.append("lib.")
        if lower_view.equals("es6")
            out.append("es2015")
        else
        {
            if lower_view.equals("es7")
                out.append("es2016")
            else
                out.append(lower_view.data, lower_view.length as size_t)
        }
        out.append(".d.ts")
        let s String^host(out.to_string())
        Slice[char](s.get_length() as int, s.get_buffer() as pointer[char])
    }

    ; the bundled lib directory (TSCALY_LIB_DIR overrides)
    function lib_dir(host: ref[Page]) returns String
    {
        let env getenv("TSCALY_LIB_DIR")
        if env <> null
            return String^host(env as pointer[char])
        String^host("packages/tscaly/_submodules/typescript-go/internal/bundled/libs")
    }

    ; the lib chain of a list of names: each file's own `/// <reference lib>`s first
    ; (the program's order), every file once; answers false when one cannot be read
    ; fileloader.go getLibraryNameFromLibFileName: lib.dom.d.ts → @typescript/lib-dom,
    ; lib.dom.iterable.d.ts → @typescript/lib-dom/iterable,
    ; lib.es2015.symbol.wellknown.d.ts → @typescript/lib-es2015/symbol-wellknown
    function library_name_from_lib_file_name(host: ref[Page], file_name: Slice[char]) returns Slice[char]
    {
        let sb StringBuilder^host()
        sb.append("@typescript/lib-")
        var index 0
        var start 0
        var i 0
        while i <= (file_name.length as int)
        {
            var at_dot i = (file_name.length as int)
            if at_dot = false
                set at_dot: (file_name[i as size_t] as int) = ("." as char) as int
            if at_dot
            {
                let component file_name.subslice(start as size_t, i as size_t)
                if index = 1
                    sb.append(component.data, component.length)
                if index >= 2
                {
                    if (component.length as int) = 0
                        break
                    if Checker.name_bytes_are(component, "d")
                        break
                    if index = 2
                        sb.append("/")
                    else
                        sb.append("-")
                    sb.append(component.data, component.length)
                }
                set index: index + 1
                set start: i + 1
            }
            set i: i + 1
        }
        let out sb.to_string()
        slice_copy_on(host, Slice[char](out.get_length(), out.get_buffer() as pointer[char]))
    }

    ; the lib chain under `libReplacement` (and with a program's own lib reference
    ; directives): pathForLibFile resolves `@typescript/lib-<name>` from the
    ; configuration's directory and takes the resolved program file for the lib
    procedure load_lib_chain_replacing(host: ref[Page], names: ref[Array[Slice[char]]], out_files: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]], out_binders: ref[Array[ref[Binder]?]], seen: ref[Array[Slice[char]]], pf: ref[ProgramFiles], replace: bool, module_resolution: int, resolve_from_directory: Slice[char]) returns bool
    {
        var i 0
        while i < (names.get_length() as int)
        {
            let file_name Checker.lib_file_name(host, names[i as size_t])
            set i: i + 1
            var already false
            var k 0
            while k < (seen.get_length() as int)
            {
                let sk seen[k as size_t]
                if sk.equals(file_name)
                    set already: true
                set k: k + 1
            }
            if already
                continue
            var replaced 0 - 1
            if replace and (Checker.name_bytes_are(file_name, "lib.d.ts") = false)
            {
                let library_name Checker.library_name_from_lib_file_name(host, file_name)
                let resolve_from combine_paths(host, resolve_from_directory, join_slices(host, join_slices(host, "__lib_node_modules_lookup_", file_name), "__.ts"))
                ; the resolution fills ProgramFiles' lazy caches: under
                ; enter_shared (slice 366)
                enter_shared()
                let resolver ModuleResolver.create(host, pf, module_resolution, false, ModuleKindCommonJS, false, 2, 2, Slice[char](), Slice[char]())
                let r resolver.resolve(library_name, resolve_from)
                leave_shared()
                if (r >= 0) and ((resolver.stop_tag.length as int) = 0)
                {
                    if (pf.file_at(r) <> null) and (pf.binder_at(r) <> null)
                        set replaced: r
                }
            }
            if replaced < 0
            {
                let one &Array[Slice[char]]^host()
                one.add(names[(i - 1) as size_t])
                if Checker.load_lib_chain_once(host, one, out_files, out_binders, seen, pf, replace, module_resolution, resolve_from_directory) = false
                    return false
                continue
            }
            seen.add(file_name)
            let rf pf.file_at(replaced) as ref[AstNode]
            let refs AstNode.lib_references_of(rf)
            if refs <> null
            {
                if Checker.load_lib_chain_replacing(host, refs as ref[Array[Slice[char]]], out_files, out_binders, seen, pf, replace, module_resolution, resolve_from_directory) = false
                    return false
            }
            out_files.add(rf)
            out_binders.add(pf.binder_at(replaced))
        }
        true
    }

    ; one bundled lib file and its references, the references through the
    ; replacing loader again
    procedure load_lib_chain_once(host: ref[Page], names: ref[Array[Slice[char]]], out_files: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]], out_binders: ref[Array[ref[Binder]?]], seen: ref[Array[Slice[char]]], pf: ref[ProgramFiles], replace: bool, module_resolution: int, resolve_from_directory: Slice[char]) returns bool
    {
        let file_name Checker.lib_file_name(host, names[0])
        seen.add(file_name)
        let dir Checker.lib_dir(host)
        let sb StringBuilder^host()
        sb.append(dir.get_buffer(), dir.get_length())
        sb.append("/")
        sb.append(file_name.data, file_name.length as size_t)
        let path String^host(sb.to_string())
        if File.exists(path) = false
            return false
        let raw File.read_to_string(path)
        let text String^host(raw)
        var unported 0 - 1
        var unported_tag: Slice[char] ""
        var unported_detail 0
        let script_kind Parser.supported_script_kind(path)
        let lp Parser.parse_ex(host, path, text, script_kind, &unported, &unported_tag, &unported_detail)
        if unported >= 0
            return false
        let f Parser.source_file_of(lp)
        if f = null
            return false
        let lf f as ref[AstNode]
        let refs AstNode.lib_references_of(lf)
        if refs <> null
        {
            if Checker.load_lib_chain_replacing(host, refs as ref[Array[Slice[char]]], out_files, out_binders, seen, pf, replace, module_resolution, resolve_from_directory) = false
                return false
        }
        let is_external Parser.external_module_indicator(lf, AstNode.statements_of(lf))
        let lb Binder.bind_source_file(host, lf, text, path, is_external)
        if Binder.unported_at(lb) >= 0
            return false
        out_files.add(lf)
        out_binders.add(lb)
        true
    }

    procedure load_lib_chain(host: ref[Page], names: ref[Array[Slice[char]]], out_files: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]], out_binders: ref[Array[ref[Binder]?]], seen: ref[Array[Slice[char]]]) returns bool
    {
        var i 0
        while i < (names.get_length() as int)
        {
            let file_name Checker.lib_file_name(host, names[i as size_t])
            set i: i + 1
            var already false
            var k 0
            while k < (seen.get_length() as int)
            {
                let sk seen[k as size_t]
                if sk.equals(file_name)
                    set already: true
                set k: k + 1
            }
            if already
                continue
            seen.add(file_name)
            if getenv("TSCALY_DEBUG_LIBS") <> null
            {
                let dbg String^host(file_name.data as pointer[const_char], file_name.length)
                Console.println(dbg.to_c_string())
            }
            let dir Checker.lib_dir(host)
            let sb StringBuilder^host()
            sb.append(dir.get_buffer(), dir.get_length())
            sb.append("/")
            sb.append(file_name.data, file_name.length as size_t)
            let path String^host(sb.to_string())
            if File.exists(path) = false
                return false
            let raw File.read_to_string(path)
            let text String^host(raw)
            var unported 0 - 1
            var unported_tag: Slice[char] ""
            var unported_detail 0
            let script_kind Parser.supported_script_kind(path)
            let lp Parser.parse_ex(host, path, text, script_kind, &unported, &unported_tag, &unported_detail)
            if unported >= 0
                return false
            let f Parser.source_file_of(lp)
            if f = null
                return false
            let lf f as ref[AstNode]
            ; its own references first
            let refs AstNode.lib_references_of(lf)
            if refs <> null
            {
                if Checker.load_lib_chain(host, refs as ref[Array[Slice[char]]], out_files, out_binders, seen) = false
                    return false
            }
            let is_external Parser.external_module_indicator(lf, AstNode.statements_of(lf))
            let lb Binder.bind_source_file(host, lf, text, path, is_external)
            if Binder.unported_at(lb) >= 0
                return false
            out_files.add(lf)
            out_binders.add(lb)
        }
        true
    }

    procedure load_lib(host: ref[Page], out_file: ref[ref[AstNode]?], out_b: ref[ref[Binder]?]) returns bool
    {
        var path0 String("packages/tscaly/_submodules/typescript-go/internal/bundled/libs/lib.es5.d.ts")
        let env getenv("TSCALY_LIB")
        if env <> null
            set path0: String(env as pointer[char])
        ; Pinned for the text's reason: the binder keeps the file NAME too.
        let path String^host(path0)
        if File.exists(path) = false
            return false
        ; ★★★ THE TEXT IS PINNED ON `host` AND THAT IS NOT TIDINESS. `read_to_string`
        ; builds its String on ITS CALLER's page, which here is this function's own;
        ; the BINDER keeps that buffer as its trivia source and hands slices of it out
        ; through `source_slice` and `skip_trivia_at` for the rest of the run, so a
        ; buffer that dies at this return is read for as long as the process lives.
        ; The unit's own text is a local of `main` and never had the question.
        let raw File.read_to_string(path)
        let text String^host(raw)
        var unported 0 - 1
        var unported_tag: Slice[char] ""
        var unported_detail 0
        let script_kind Parser.supported_script_kind(path)
        let lp Parser.parse_ex(host, path, text, script_kind, &unported, &unported_tag, &unported_detail)
        if unported >= 0
            return false
        let f Parser.source_file_of(lp)
        if f = null
            return false
        let lf f as ref[AstNode]
        let is_external Parser.external_module_indicator(lf, AstNode.statements_of(lf))
        let lb Binder.bind_source_file(host, lf, text, path, is_external)
        if Binder.unported_at(lb) >= 0
            return false
        set out_file: lf
        set out_b: lb
        true
    }

    ; ── entry ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; checker.NewChecker, minus the forty-method Program: the oracle's stub answers
    ; every module-resolution question with nothing, and this side has no program
    ; layer to answer them from either, so the two agree by having the same hole.
    ; ★ Since slice 113 the one thing the stub program DOES carry is the file set,
    ; and it is the oracle's own: lib.es5.d.ts plus the unit.
    procedure create(host: ref[Page], file: ref[AstNode], file_name: String, b: ref[Binder], p: ref[Parser], lib_file: ref[AstNode]?, lib_b: ref[Binder]?) returns ref[Checker]
        Checker.create_ex(host, file, file_name, b, p, lib_file, lib_b, null, null, null)

    ; the same with the REST of the lib chain (slice 247): the extra libs must be
    ; in hand when initialize_checker merges the globals at the end of this
    ; constructor — set afterwards they were never merged, and a chain whose
    ; first file was a referenced lib (lib.decorators.d.ts before lib.es5.d.ts)
    ; checked the unit against thirteen globals
    procedure create_ex(host: ref[Page], file: ref[AstNode], file_name: String, b: ref[Binder], p: ref[Parser], lib_file: ref[AstNode]?, lib_b: ref[Binder]?, extra_files: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?, extra_binders: ref[Array[ref[Binder]?]]?, program: ref[ProgramFiles]?) returns ref[Checker]
    {
        let c host.allocate(sizeof Checker, alignof Checker) as pointer[Checker]
        set c.host: host
        set c.file: file
        set c.b: b
        set c.p: p
        set c.lib_file: lib_file
        set c.lib_b: lib_b
        set c.extra_lib_files: extra_files
        set c.extra_lib_index: null
        set c.extra_lib_indexed: 0
        set c.lib_ranks: null
        set c.extra_lib_binders: extra_binders
        set c.all_lib_parsers: null
        set c.all_lib_paths: null
        set c.program: program
        set c.module_resolution_kind: ModuleResolutionKindUnknown
        ; GetResolveJsonModule under the unset configuration: bundler resolution, so
        ; true (slice 264: the not-found fork reads it); an emit driver sets it
        set c.resolve_json_module: true
        set c.import_helpers: false
        set c.requested_external_emit_helpers: 0
        set c.external_helpers_module: null
        set c.allow_js: false
        set c.resolve_package_json_exports: 2
        set c.resolve_package_json_imports: 2
        set c.out_dir.data: null
        set c.out_dir.length: 0
        set c.root_dir.data: null
        set c.root_dir.length: 0
        set c.is_declaration_file: is_declaration_file_name(file_name)
        ; The one question the arrow-function grammar check asks of the FILE NAME
        ; (slice 100). Derived at construction, on is_declaration_file's own
        ; convention: the Checker keeps the answer, never the name.
        set c.is_mts_or_cts_file: file_extension_is_mts_or_cts(file_name)
        set c.legacy_decorators: false
        set c.emit_decorator_metadata: false
        set c.verbatim_module_syntax: false
        set c.isolated_modules: false
        set c.erasable_syntax_only: false
        set c.preserve_const_enums: false
        set c.module_kind: ModuleKindES2022
        set c.no_unchecked_side_effect_imports: true
        set c.language_version: ScriptTargetES2025
        set c.emit_module_format: ModuleKindNone
        set c.implied_node_format: ModuleKindNone
        set c.implied_node_format_for_emit: ModuleKindNone
        set c.use_define_for_class_fields: ScriptTargetES2025 >= ScriptTargetES2022
        set c.emit_standard_class_fields: ScriptTargetES2025 >= ScriptTargetES2022
        set c.no_implicit_override: false
        let dl &DiagnosticList^host(host)
        set c.diagnostics: dl
        set c.global_diagnostic_count: 0
        let gdc &Array[int]^host()
        set c.global_diagnostic_codes: gdc
        let gdl &DiagnosticList^host(host)
        set c.global_diagnostic_list: gdl
        set c.message_specifiers: null
        let cgr &Array[Slice[char]]^host()
        set c.checked_global_reports: cgr
        let pan &Array[Slice[char]]^host()
        set c.pattern_augmentation_names: pan
        let pas &Array[ref[Symbol]?]^host()
        set c.pattern_augmentation_symbols: pas
        set c.type_count: 0
        set c.resolution_memos: null
        set c.resolution_memo_buckets: null
        set c.resolution_memo_mask: 0
        set c.nodes_allocated: 0
        set c.flow_nodes_allocated: 0
        set c.symbols_allocated: 0
        set c.trace_types: null
        if checker_trace_types
        {
            let tt &Array[ref[Type]?]^host()
            set c.trace_types: tt
        }
        let ra &Array[ref[AstNode]?]^host()
        set c.reported_ambient: ra
        let rb &Array[ref[AstNode]?]^host()
        set c.renamed_binding_elements_in_types: rb
        let icn &Array[ref[AstNode]?]^host()
        set c.identifier_check_nodes: icn
        let dn &Array[ref[AstNode]?]^host()
        set c.deferred_nodes: dn
        let dt &Array[ref[DeclaredTypeLink]?]^host()
        set c.declared_type_links: dt
        set c.declared_type_links_index: AddressIndex.create(host)
        let wtl &Array[ref[WidenedTypeLink]?]^host()
        set c.widened_type_links: wtl
        set c.widened_type_links_index: AddressIndex.create(host)
        let nnames &Array[Slice[char]]^host()
        set c.number_names: nnames
        set c.number_names_index: AddressIndex.create(host)
        let roltl &Array[ref[RegularObjectLiteralTypeLink]?]^host()
        set c.regular_object_literal_type_links: roltl
        set c.regular_object_literal_type_links_index: AddressIndex.create(host)
        let atl &Array[ref[ApparentTypeLink]?]^host()
        set c.apparent_type_links: atl
        set c.apparent_type_links_index: AddressIndex.create(host)
        let sial &Array[ref[SimplifiedIndexedAccessLink]?]^host()
        set c.simplified_indexed_access_links: sial
        set c.simplified_indexed_access_links_index: AddressIndex.create(host)
        let dctl &Array[ref[DiscriminatedContextualTypeLink]?]^host()
        set c.discriminated_contextual_type_links: dctl
        set c.discriminated_chain: HashChain.create(host)
        let altl &Array[ref[ArrayLiteralTypeLink]?]^host()
        set c.array_literal_type_links: altl
        set c.array_literal_type_links_index: AddressIndex.create(host)
        let eatl &Array[ref[EvolvingArrayTypeLink]?]^host()
        set c.evolving_array_type_links: eatl
        set c.evolving_array_type_links_index: AddressIndex.create(host)
        let asl &Array[ref[AssertionLink]?]^host()
        set c.assertion_links: asl
        set c.assertion_links_index: AddressIndex.create(host)
        let ftc &Array[ref[FlowTypeCacheLink]?]^host()
        set c.flow_type_cache: ftc
        set c.flow_type_cache_chain_index: AddressIndex.create(host)
        let ftcc &Array[ref[HashChain]?]^host()
        set c.flow_type_cache_chains: ftcc
        let cft &Array[ref[ContextFreeTypeLink]?]^host()
        set c.context_free_types: cft
        set c.context_free_types_index: AddressIndex.create(host)
        let flc &Array[ref[FlowLoopCacheLink]?]^host()
        set c.flow_loop_cache: flc
        set c.flow_loop_cache_index: AddressIndex.create(host)
        set c.object_row_chains: AddressIndex.create(host)
        set c.object_row_chain_list: &Array[ref[HashChain]]^host()
        set c.signature_row_chains: AddressIndex.create(host)
        set c.signature_row_chain_list: &Array[ref[HashChain]]^host()
        set c.flow_loop_stack: null
        set c.flow_invocation_count: 0
