        ; candidatesOutArray == nil`, and BOTH halves are constants here: the flag
        ; is written only by services.go, and no caller in this port passes an out
        ; array. A synthetic `[Symbol.hasInstance]` call (`left instanceof right`)
        ; heads its argument reports.
        var failure_head head_code
        if (failure_head = 0) and (AstNode.kind_of(node) = KindBinaryExpression)
            set failure_head: DiagThe_left_hand_side_of_an_instanceof_expression_must_be_assignable_to_the_first_argument_of_the_right_hand_side_s_Symbol_hasInstance_method
        this.report_call_resolution_errors(node, state, signatures as ref[Array[ref[Signature]?]], failure_head)
        if this.unported_mark() <> mark
            return null
        fallback
    }
    ; ── THE FAILURE PASS, slice 138 ──────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ROW `report-call-resolution-errors` WAS ONE STOP FOR TWO DIFFERENT
    ; JOBS, and only one of them is a report. `getCandidateForOverloadFailure`
    ; answers a SIGNATURE — the call's type after a failed resolution, which every
    ; caller of get_resolved_signature then reads — while
    ; `reportCallResolutionErrors` produces the diagnostics. A port that had
    ; neither answered `null` for the whole resolution, so a wrong call lost its
    ; TYPE as well as its message: that is why the row was worth twelve units and
    ; not just twelve diagnostics.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE PORT NEEDS NO DIAGNOSTIC-CHAIN OBJECT FOR IT. Upstream re-heads
    ; a diagnostic with `ast.NewDiagnosticChain`, which keeps the file and the LOC
    ; and replaces the outermost MESSAGE — so for a dump of (pos, end, code) the
    ; chain is a change of CODE and nothing else. Where the code is known before
    ; the report, the head is chosen at the report (`var code msg; if head_code <>
    ; 0 set code: head_code`); where the relation is what reports, the codes are
    ; rewritten afterwards through `DiagnosticList.recode_from`.
